window.__SEARCH_INDEX__=[{"id":"vol0","t":"From Zero to Your First Trade","v":"Vol 0","x":"The part nobody writes down: how a person with a laptop, some savings and no experience gets to a real, correctly sized, risk-controlled first trade — without being ruined by the attempt. Every other volume tells you how markets work. This one tells you what to do on Monday. It names no brokers and no products, because those change and depend on where you live; it gives you the tests that tell a good one from a bad one anywhere. Read this before Volume 00, even though 00 is numbered first Volume 00 teaches you how ","s":1},{"id":"the-reference-in-pictures","t":"The whole reference in 83 pictures","v":"Vol &mdash;","x":">The whole reference in 83 pictures ¶ The shortest complete route through this document. Every diagram carries one idea and states its own conclusion.","s":"the whole reference in 83 pictures figure index diagrams visual the three ways money is made &mdash; and only three be explicit about which one you are attempting. what your account size actually lets you hold the rule of thumb behind the whole chart: the annual risk carried by one unit must be no more than about 2.5% of your account. why the same skill wins at one size and loses at another below the shaded band the strategy can work. the leverage you did not notice margin is what the broker needs to let you trade. every position size is one division both boxes are measurable. what each order type does when the price moves nothing you can trade at retail size requires immediacy. the runway &mdash; four weeks, and what each gate is for the waiting is not padding. why re-reading feels like learning and is not the tested curve feels worse at every point &mdash; you are made to fail, on purpose. interleaving: the finding that feels wrong this is why the review queue mixes volumes rather than letting you drill one at a time. the derivative is a limit of slopes you can actually draw everything later &mdash; it&ocirc;, the greeks, gradients &mdash; is this picture with more indices. taylor series: replacing a curve with a polynomial you can compute almost every approximation in this reference is this one: keep two terms, and know how wrong you are when the move is large. an eigenvector is a direction the matrix does not turn finding those directions is the same act as finding where your risk actually is &mdash; which is why the same decomposition appears in pca, in risk models and in volume 11a. known at time t every lookahead bug is this picture: an arrow pointing backwards. diversification has a floor, and the floor is correlation. at average correlation 0.4 you reach about 13% with twenty names and nothing beyond helps — and the floor rises exactly when correlations rise, which is when you needed t why &ldquo;how many standard deviations&rdquo; misleads on returns every risk number that assumes normality &mdash; most var, most option pricing, most position sizing &mdash; is wrong in the same direction, and the error is worst exactl the one-bar bug that turns a losing strategy into a spectacular one no dataset states this reliably and both conventions are common. known at time t every lookahead bug is this picture: an arrow pointing backwards. where the extra term in it&ocirc;&rsquo;s lemma comes from for a smooth path the moves are small enough that the curvature vanishes. square the random part and you get δt back — a first-order term. over a short interval a price’s random move is of size √δt, not δt. the four processes you will actually fit to data fitting the wrong one is not a modelling nicety. why quoting a spread is not free money it also explains the fill model in volume 16.2. the limit order book filling a limit order the moment the price “touches” your level assumes you were first in the queue. what a round trip actually costs, in order of size sized for a liquid instrument at retail scale. doubling the order multiplies the cost by 1.41×, not 2×. a straight-line model charges too little for small orders and far too much for large ones — which is how a workable strategy comes to look uninvestable at size. charge the part that stays on the position; the part that fades, on the trade. impact peaks when you stop trading, then most of it fades — but not all. two wandering prices, one steady spread both prices wander with no tendency to return anywhere, so forecasting either is hopeless. how a cointegrated spread is actually traded volume 3.6 lists the three ways this stops working. returns are unpredictable; their size is not this is the entire commercial content of volume 4, and the reason volatility targeting in volume 13.2 does most of the work in trend following. what a posterior gives you that a point estimate does not sizing on the peak while the width is this large is how a position ends up several times larger than the evidence supports. what a factor model actually splits apart a strategy that looks profitable and turns out to be four-fifths market exposure has not found an edge; it has found leverage with extra steps. labelling a trade by what would actually have happened whichever barrier the path touches first decides the label — so the label describes a position you could actually have held, not one you would have been stopped out of on fractional differencing: the trade-off nobody has to make whole the window exists because the two requirements decay at different rates. why ordinary cross-validation leaks on financial data overlapping observations put near-duplicates on both sides of a random split, so the model is graded on data it saw. where deep learning earns its cost, and where it does not deep learning's honest role here is representation &mdash; turning text, filings and raw books into a number you then put in a simple model. an option&rsquo;s payoff, and why hedging turns it into a volatility bet which is why the only genuinely contested input in black&ndash;scholes is volatility. the volatility smile &mdash; in equity indices, mostly a skew it is not a failure of the formula so much as a place to keep the corrections: fat tails, jumps, stochastic volatility and the simple fact that people pay up for insurance. the two directions the surface moves in knowing which of the two you are trading decides the model. the numbers that settle most arguments about speed read once, this rules out most latency folklore. why the mean latency is the least useful number you have the same asymmetry appears in volume 17.6: monitoring an average hides exactly the events the monitoring exists to catch. how fast to unwind: the whole trade-off in one picture not risk aversion &mdash; alpha decay. the one question that decides whether rl belongs near your problem the mistake is not using reinforcement learning. why the optimiser produces portfolios nobody would hold nothing was wrong wit"},{"id":"0-1-what-trading-actually-is","t":"0.1 What trading actually is","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.1 What trading actually is ¶ Strip away the screens and the vocabulary and a trade is one thing: you agree to swap money for a claim, with someone who wants the opposite swap. You buy a hundred shares because someone else sold a hundred shares. At that instant you and they looked at the same price and drew opposite conclusions. One of you is going to be more right. Everything difficult about this follows from that sentence. There is a person on the other side. That person may be a pension fund rebalancing, an al","s":"0.1 what trading actually is >0.1 what trading actually is ¶ strip away the screens and the vocabulary and a trade is one thing: you agree to swap money for a claim, with someone who wants the opposite swap. you buy a hundred shares because someone else sold a hundred shares. at that instant you and they looked at the same price and drew opposite conclusions. one of you is going to be more right. everything difficult about this follows from that sentence. there is a person on the other side. that person may be a pension fund rebalancing, an al be explicit about which one you are attempting. they need different evidence, different patience, and only the middle one requires you to be clever. three panels: a risk premium path rising through a deep drawdown, an edge path drifting gently up, and a luck path drifting down towards minus costs"},{"id":"0-2-the-four-questions","t":"0.2 The four questions every trade answers","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.2 The four questions every trade answers ¶ A trade is not an opinion. An opinion is “I think oil goes up.” A trade is a set of four answers, all four of which must exist before you touch anything: # Question A vague answer (worthless) A real answer 1 What exactly am I buying or selling? The precise instrument, not the theme. “Oil.” “One micro WTI crude futures contract, the December expiry, on this exchange, worth about $6,500 of notional exposure.” 2 Which way , and why now rather than last month? “Long, oil lo","s":1},{"id":"0-3-the-readiness-test","t":"0.3 The readiness test","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.3 The readiness test ¶ Five conditions. They are not a motivational exercise; each one, if unmet, changes your behaviour in a way that reliably destroys accounts. Read them as engineering constraints. Condition The test What happens if it is not met Money you can genuinely lose If this entire amount vanished tomorrow, would anything in your life change materially — rent, food, a dependant, a plan? If yes, the amount is too large. You will size positions by fear rather than by rule: too small when the signal is g","s":1},{"id":"0-4-capital-bands","t":"0.4 What your capital actually buys","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.4 What your capital actually buys ¶ Account size does not change how much you can make in percentage terms — it changes which strategies are physically possible , because costs are partly fixed and instruments come in indivisible lumps. Below a certain size, entire categories of trading are arithmetically closed to you, no matter how good you are. This is the table nobody shows beginners. Capital Realistically available Closed to you, and why Costs as a share of capital The correct goal at this size Under $500 F","s":"0.4 what your capital actually buys >0.4 what your capital actually buys ¶ account size does not change how much you can make in percentage terms — it changes which strategies are physically possible , because costs are partly fixed and instruments come in indivisible lumps. below a certain size, entire categories of trading are arithmetically closed to you, no matter how good you are. this is the table nobody shows beginners. capital realistically available closed to you, and why costs as a share of capital the correct goal at this size under $500 f the rule of thumb behind the whole chart: the annual risk carried by one unit must be no more than about 2.5% of your account. that single line rules futures out of small accounts and etfs in. horizontal logarithmic bars showing the minimum account size at which each instrument's smallest tradeable unit fits inside a ten percent risk target, from sixty dollars for a fractional etf to 1.6 million dollars for a standard index future below the shaded band the strategy can work. above it, the account is paying more to trade than the strategy can possibly earn — and no amount of skill moves the curve. a curve of annual trading cost as a share of the account, falling steeply from twenty-one percent at a one thousand dollar account to under half a percent at fifty thousand, crossing a shaded band representing a good strategy's entire gross return"},{"id":"0-5-choosing-what-to-trade","t":"0.5 Choosing what to trade","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.5 Choosing what to trade ¶ Beginners choose an instrument by which one they have heard of, or which one a video was about. The correct way is to work down four filters in order: can I afford a sensible position in it? can I get honest data on it? what is the worst thing it can do to me? and how much does a round trip cost? Only after those four does the question of what you find interesting matter. Instrument In plain words Smallest sane account Round-trip cost, order of magnitude What can actually ruin you Shar","s":"0.5 choosing what to trade >0.5 choosing what to trade ¶ beginners choose an instrument by which one they have heard of, or which one a video was about. the correct way is to work down four filters in order: can i afford a sensible position in it? can i get honest data on it? what is the worst thing it can do to me? and how much does a round trip cost? only after those four does the question of what you find interesting matter. instrument in plain words smallest sane account round-trip cost, order of magnitude what can actually ruin you shar margin is what the broker needs to let you trade. notional is what the market can do to you. sizing by the first is the most common way a small futures account ends. two boxes: a small green box showing 1,400 dollars of margin posted next to a much wider red box showing 25,000 dollars of notional exposure controlled"},{"id":"0-6-choosing-a-broker","t":"0.6 Choosing a broker","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.6 Choosing a broker ¶ Your broker holds your money. That is the entire relationship, and everything else — the interface, the app, the promotions — is decoration on top of the single question of whether you get your money back. Firms are named nowhere in this reference because the right answer depends on where you live and changes yearly. What does not change are the tests.","s":1},{"id":"the-nine-disqualifiers","t":"The nine disqualifiers — any one is enough to walk away","v":"Vol 0","x":">The nine disqualifiers — any one is enough to walk away ¶ # Disqualifier How to check it, concretely 1 No verifiable licence from a regulator in a jurisdiction with real enforcement. Take the licence number from the firm's footer. Go to the regulator's own website — typed by hand, never via a link from the broker — and search the register. Confirm the entity name matches exactly , and that the licence covers the activity you intend. Clone firms copying a real licence number are common. 2 Client funds are not segre","s":1},{"id":"twelve-things-to-verify","t":"Then the twelve things to verify on a survivor","v":"Vol 0","x":">Then the twelve things to verify on a survivor ¶ Check What good looks like Commission structure Published per-share, per-contract or per-trade, with the tiers stated. A per-contract futures commission of $0.50–$2.50 all-in is normal; $10 is not. Currency conversion Stated as a spread over the interbank rate. 0.02–0.2% is competitive; 0.5–2% is a large, silent, recurring tax if you fund in a different currency from the one you trade in. Financing / margin interest A published rate tied to a benchmark plus a spread","s":1},{"id":"0-7-opening-and-funding","t":"0.7 Opening and funding, step by step","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.7 Opening and funding, step by step ¶ Expect this to take one to three weeks end to end, mostly waiting. Nothing here is difficult; the value of the list is that it stops you discovering a blocker after you have committed. Step What happens The thing that catches people 1 · Account type Choose individual cash or individual margin. A cash account cannot borrow; a margin account can, and enables short selling and futures. Start with cash unless you specifically need margin. A margin account permits you to lose mor","s":1},{"id":"0-8-data-that-does-not-lie","t":"0.8 Data that does not lie to you","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.8 Data that does not lie to you ¶ Volume 15 is the full treatment and is, per the preface, the volume most likely to make you money. This is the short version: every backtest is a claim about the past, and free data routinely misrepresents the past in ways that make strategies look profitable when they are not. The failures are systematic and they all bias in the same direction — towards flattering you. The flaw What it does to your backtest The 10-minute check Survivorship — the dataset contains only things tha","s":1},{"id":"0-9-automated-or-not","t":"0.9 Automated, semi-automatic or discretionary","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.9 Automated, semi-automatic or discretionary ¶ This is the decision that shapes everything else you build, and it is usually made by accident. The three options are not points on a scale of sophistication — they are different jobs with different failure modes. Here they are side by side, honestly. Discretionary You decide each trade Semi-automatic Code decides, you execute Fully automated Code decides and executes What you actually do daily Watch, judge, decide, place orders. Run one script. Read the output. Pla","s":1},{"id":"0-10-your-first-strategy","t":"0.10 Your first strategy, and why it is boring","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.10 Your first strategy, and why it is boring ¶ Your first strategy is a piece of training equipment. Its job is to be simple enough that when something goes wrong you can find out why, and slow enough that costs do not consume it while you are still making mistakes. It is not supposed to be good. Six criteria: Criterion Why Daily or slower You can inspect every decision by hand. Intraday strategies fail in ways you cannot see without infrastructure you do not have. Fewer than four parameters Every parameter is a","s":1},{"id":"a-first-strategy-fully-specified","t":"A first strategy, fully specified","v":"Vol 0","x":">A first strategy, fully specified ¶ This is written out completely so there is nothing left to guess. It is a slow, diversified, volatility-targeted trend follower — the strategy Volume 13 identifies as the most realistic starting point for an individual, and the one Carver's framework is built around. It is offered as a worked specification to study and criticise, not as a recommendation. Component The rule Why this and not something else Universe Six to ten liquid instruments spanning at least four asset groups ","s":1},{"id":"0-11-paper-trading","t":"0.11 Paper trading: what it does and does not teach","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.11 Paper trading: what it does and does not teach ¶ Paper trading is a simulated account with fake money and a real interface. It is genuinely useful for a narrow set of things and actively misleading about the rest, and knowing which is which saves months. It teaches, reliably It cannot teach, and pretends to The mechanics of the order ticket — which field is which, what each order type does, what a partial fill looks like. Realistic fills. Simulators fill you at the touch, instantly, in full. Real markets do n","s":1},{"id":"0-12-position-sizing","t":"0.12 Position sizing, worked in numbers","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.12 Position sizing, worked in numbers ¶ This is the most important section in this volume, and the arithmetic is school arithmetic. The idea it replaces is the one everybody starts with: “how much can I afford to buy?” That question has no useful answer. The right question is “how much risk does one unit of this thing carry, and how much risk am I willing to hold?” Divide the second by the first and you have your position size. Everything else is detail.","s":"0.12 position sizing, worked in numbers >0.12 position sizing, worked in numbers ¶ this is the most important section in this volume, and the arithmetic is school arithmetic. the idea it replaces is the one everybody starts with: “how much can i afford to buy?” that question has no useful answer. the right question is “how much risk does one unit of this thing carry, and how much risk am i willing to hold?” divide the second by the first and you have your position size. everything else is detail. both boxes are measurable. neither has anything to do with how much cash is in the account, which is the number beginners size by. a division: the risk you will accept, being account times target, divided by the risk one unit carries, being its volatility times its value, giving 0.19 units and therefore no position at all"},{"id":"method-a-risk-per-trade","t":"Method A — risk per trade","v":"Vol 0","x":">Method A — risk per trade, when you use a stop ¶ Decide in advance the most you will lose on one position if it goes wrong: a fixed small fraction of the account, typically 0.25% to 1% . Then: The formula, and the same thing in words Units = (Account × Risk fraction) ÷ (Distance from entry to stop, per unit) In words: the money I am willing to lose, divided by the money I lose per share if I am wrong, is the number of shares. Account Risk fraction Money at risk Entry / stop Risk per share Position Position as % of","s":1},{"id":"method-b-volatility-targeting","t":"Method B — volatility targeting","v":"Vol 0","x":">Method B — volatility targeting, when you hold a portfolio ¶ This is what the specification in 0.10 uses, and what Volume 13.2 shows does most of the measured work in trend following. Instead of a stop distance, you use the instrument's own volatility as the measure of how much risk one unit carries. The formula, and the same thing in words Units = (Account × Target volatility × Weight × IDM) ÷ (Instrument volatility × Value of one unit) In words: the top is how much annual risk, in money, I want this one instrume","s":1},{"id":"the-minimum-capital-test","t":"The minimum-capital test","v":"Vol 0","x":">The minimum-capital test — run this before you choose anything ¶ Rearranging the same formula answers the question that actually decides your instrument choice: how large must my account be before one unit of this fits? Minimum account = (Instrument volatility × Value of one unit) ÷ (Target volatility × Weight × IDM) Instrument (illustrative — check current values) Value of one unit Annual vol Risk per unit Minimum account at 10% target, 8 instruments Broad equity ETF, one share $50 15% $7.50 $300 Broad equity ETF","s":1},{"id":"0-13-the-first-order","t":"0.13 The first order, field by field","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.13 The first order, field by field ¶ An order ticket looks intimidating and is not. It is seven or eight fields, each answering an obvious question. Here is every one, what it does, and how it goes wrong. Field What it means How it goes wrong Symbol / instrument Exactly what you are trading. Nearly identical tickers on different exchanges; the same ticker for a company and an unrelated fund; for futures, the wrong expiry month . Always confirm the description and the exchange, not just the letters. Side Buy, Sel","s":"0.13 the first order, field by field >0.13 the first order, field by field ¶ an order ticket looks intimidating and is not. it is seven or eight fields, each answering an obvious question. here is every one, what it does, and how it goes wrong. field what it means how it goes wrong symbol / instrument exactly what you are trading. nearly identical tickers on different exchanges; the same ticker for a company and an unrelated fund; for futures, the wrong expiry month . always confirm the description and the exchange, not just the letters. side buy, sel nothing you can trade at retail size requires immediacy. the urgency that makes people send market orders is manufactured by the screen, not by the market. a price path with a decision point marked, beside three cards describing market, limit and stop orders and what each one costs you"},{"id":"what-happens-after-you-click","t":"What happens after you click","v":"Vol 0","x":">What happens after you click ¶ Your order goes to the broker, is checked for margin and permissions, and is routed to a venue. Then one of five things happens: it sits working in the book; it is partially filled , leaving a remainder working; it is filled completely; it is cancelled by you or by expiry; or it is rejected , with a reason that is usually accurate and worth reading. When it fills, do one thing immediately, before anything else: write down the price you got and the price that was showing when you deci","s":1},{"id":"0-14-the-first-month","t":"0.14 The first month","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.14 The first month ¶ The first month has one job, and it is not making money. It is establishing that the routine is something you actually do, that your numbers match your broker's numbers, and that you can tolerate the experience. Profit in the first month is noise; the routine is signal. Rhythm What you do Time Daily, at a fixed time Run the signal script. Compare its intended positions with your actual positions. Place any orders it names, using the pre-trade checklist. Log every fill: time, instrument, side","s":1},{"id":"0-15-what-it-actually-costs","t":"0.15 What it actually costs","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.15 What it actually costs ¶ Beginners count commission. Commission is often the smallest item on this list. Here is every cost, including the ones that never appear on a statement. Cost What it is Typical size Visible? Commission What the broker charges per trade. $0–$1 per equity trade; $0.50–$2.50 per futures contract. Yes, on the confirmation. Bid–ask spread The gap between what you can buy at and sell at. You pay half of it going in and half coming out. 1–5 bp on liquid ETFs; 10–50 bp on small stocks; 200–1,","s":1},{"id":"0-16-how-you-will-lose-the-money","t":"0.16 How you will actually lose the money","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.16 How you will actually lose the money ¶ Not “the market went down.” These are the specific mechanisms, in rough order of how many accounts each one ends. For each, the control that actually works — and note how few of them are about market knowledge. # Mechanism What it feels like from the inside The control 1 Position too large Reasonable. You sized by what you could afford, and the number looked fine. Section 0.12, applied every time without exception. This one mechanism accounts for more ruin than all the o","s":1},{"id":"0-17-kill-criteria","t":"0.17 Kill criteria, decided in advance","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.17 Kill criteria, decided in advance ¶ Write these down before your first trade, because your ability to write them honestly decreases monotonically from that moment. The purpose is not to protect capital — position sizing does that. It is to remove the decision from the moment when you are least able to make it. Trigger The pre-committed response Account down 10% from its high-water mark No change to positions. Write one page on what happened. Continue. This level exists specifically so that the first alarming ","s":1},{"id":"0-18-the-runway-checklist","t":"0.18 The runway: a gated checklist","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.18 The runway: a gated checklist ¶ Everything above, in order, as things you have either done or not done. This is deliberately slower than you will want it to be. The waiting is not padding — it is the mechanism by which an impulse to trade becomes a decision to trade, and the two produce different outcomes. Volume 0 runway — tick only what you have actually done Week 1 · Readiness. I have passed all five conditions in 0.3, and I have written down the exact amount I am risking and confirmed I can lose all of it","s":"0.18 the runway: a gated checklist >0.18 the runway: a gated checklist ¶ everything above, in order, as things you have either done or not done. this is deliberately slower than you will want it to be. the waiting is not padding — it is the mechanism by which an impulse to trade becomes a decision to trade, and the two produce different outcomes. volume 0 runway — tick only what you have actually done week 1 · readiness. i have passed all five conditions in 0.3, and i have written down the exact amount i am risking and confirmed i can lose all of it the waiting is not padding. it is the mechanism by which an impulse to trade becomes a decision to trade, and the two produce different outcomes. four weekly stages &mdash; readiness, broker, data and strategy, rehearsal &mdash; chained left to right into a fifth box marked first trade"},{"id":"0-19-where-to-go-next","t":"0.19 Where to go next","v":"Vol 0","x":">0.19 Where to go next ¶ The first trade is not an achievement; it is the point at which learning finally has consequences attached and therefore starts working. The reference is arranged so that you can go deep exactly where your own results start hurting. What just happened Go here Nothing yet — I want to build before I read 26 (the Forge) — build a strategy in this page in twenty minutes, then 18 to find out why the result is not real I need the mathematics and the code from nothing 0A then 0B — and 0B.12 is a c","s":1},{"id":"vol00","t":"The Study System","v":"Vol 00","x":"Every other volume in this reference is content. This one is method , and it is placed first because the method changes how much of the content you keep by more than the content changes. The uncomfortable finding underneath it: reading is close to the least effective way to learn technical material, and it feels like the most effective . That gap between how well studying feels and how well it works is not a minor calibration error — it is the reason most self-directed technical projects produce a sense of familiar","s":1},{"id":"00-1-why-reading-this-will-not-work","t":"00.1 Why reading this will not work","v":"Vol 00","x":">00.1 Why reading this will not work ¶ Consider what happens when you re-read a section you have read before. It goes down easily. Nothing surprises you. You finish with a strong sense that you understand it. Every one of those sensations is produced by processing fluency — the ease of processing familiar text — and fluency is a measure of how recently you encountered something, not of whether you could produce it. The experiment worth knowing Roediger and Karpicke's 2006 studies are the canonical demonstration. St","s":1},{"id":"00-2-the-five-mechanisms-that-do-work","t":"00.2 The five mechanisms that do work","v":"Vol 00","x":">00.2 The five mechanisms that do work ¶ Mechanism What it is Why it works Cost Retrieval practice producing an answer from memory, not recognising it the act of retrieval is itself a memory-modifying event; it strengthens the trace far more than re-exposure does feels harder and slower; you will get things wrong Spacing reviewing at increasing intervals rather than in one block each retrieval after partial forgetting is more effortful and more consolidating than one from a fresh memory requires a schedule you do n","s":"00.2 the five mechanisms that do work >00.2 the five mechanisms that do work ¶ mechanism what it is why it works cost retrieval practice producing an answer from memory, not recognising it the act of retrieval is itself a memory-modifying event; it strengthens the trace far more than re-exposure does feels harder and slower; you will get things wrong spacing reviewing at increasing intervals rather than in one block each retrieval after partial forgetting is more effortful and more consolidating than one from a fresh memory requires a schedule you do n the tested curve feels worse at every point — you are made to fail, on purpose. that failure is the mechanism, which is why the strategy that feels weakest is the one that works. two recall curves over thirty days: one from a single re-reading decaying to near zero, and one repeatedly reset by tests at widening intervals, staying high"},{"id":"desirable-difficulties","t":"Desirable difficulties","v":"Vol 00","x":">Desirable difficulties ¶ The unifying idea, due to Robert Bjork, is that conditions which slow acquisition often improve long-term retention and transfer . Spacing, interleaving, testing, and even reading slightly degraded text all make learning feel harder and measurably work better. The practical implication is a rule for interpreting your own experience: during study, difficulty is weak evidence that it is working, and ease is weak evidence that it is not. Not perfect evidence — confusion is also what being los","s":1},{"id":"00-3-how-this-reference-implements-each","t":"00.3 How this reference implements each","v":"Vol 00","x":">00.3 How this reference implements each ¶ Mechanism Where it lives How to use it Retrieval practice 479 cards , one deck, anchored to the section that teaches each the review queue below, or the panel at the end of each volume Spacing an SM-2 scheduler that sets the next interval from how well you recalled review when cards are due; the badge in the sidebar tells you Interleaving the global queue shuffles across volumes; the per-volume panel does not use the global queue for review, the per-volume panel only when ","s":"00.3 how this reference implements each >00.3 how this reference implements each ¶ mechanism where it lives how to use it retrieval practice 479 cards , one deck, anchored to the section that teaches each the review queue below, or the panel at the end of each volume spacing an sm-2 scheduler that sets the next interval from how well you recalled review when cards are due; the badge in the sidebar tells you interleaving the global queue shuffles across volumes; the per-volume panel does not use the global queue for review, the per-volume panel only when  this is why the review queue mixes volumes rather than letting you drill one at a time. two pairs of bars: during practice blocked study scores far higher than mixed, and a week later the ordering reverses sharply in favour of mixed"},{"id":"00-4-your-dashboard","t":"00.4 Your dashboard","v":"Vol 00","x":">00.4 Your dashboard ¶ Everything below is computed from your own review history, stored in this browser only. Nothing is transmitted. Export progress writes a JSON file you can keep or move to another machine. — loading What the numbers mean Cards due — what the scheduler says you are about to forget. This is the queue, and it is the only number that should drive a session. Consolidated — cards whose interval has passed 21 days, the conventional line between still-learning and genuinely retained. Sections marked —","s":1},{"id":"00-5-a-session-template","t":"00.5 A session template","v":"Vol 00","x":">00.5 A session template ¶ Ninety minutes, five days a week, as Volume 25 specifies. Within the session, the ordering matters and is not the obvious one. Phase Minutes Why here 1 Review due cards — the global queue, interleaved 10–15 first, while attention is freshest, and because skipping it is how the schedule collapses 2 Read the new section once , without notes 15 one pass to build the shape; note where you got lost and keep moving 3 Close it and reconstruct the main result on a blank page 15 this is the retrie","s":1},{"id":"00-6-when-a-card-keeps-failing","t":"00.6 When a card keeps failing","v":"Vol 00","x":">00.6 When a card keeps failing ¶ A card you have marked Again four or five times is not telling you that you have a bad memory. It is telling you one of four things, and they have different fixes. Diagnosis Sign Fix The card is too big the answer has three separate ideas in it the underlying section needs re-reading, not more repetitions — go back to the source link on the card You never understood it you cannot reconstruct the answer even with the section open this is a prerequisite gap. Follow the cross-referenc","s":1},{"id":"vol0a","t":"Mathematics from Zero","v":"Vol 0A","x":"Volume 1 opens with the sentence “a probability space is a sample space, a σ-algebra of measurable events, and a measure”. If that sentence is not already comfortable, nothing after it will be, and no amount of rereading Volume 1 will fix it. This volume exists to make that sentence ordinary. It assumes school algebra and nothing else: you can rearrange and you know what a square root is. It ends exactly where Volume 1 begins. Everything in between is derived rather than asserted, because a result you have seen der","s":1},{"id":"0a-1-how-to-use-this-volume","t":"0A.1 How to use this volume","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.1 How to use this volume ¶ Read with a pen. Every section ends with a small number of exercises marked ▢ ; they are not optional garnish, they are the mechanism by which the material moves from recognisable to usable. The difference between the two is the difference between a candidate who nods along in an interview and one who can answer. There is a specific failure mode to name now, because it is the one that ends most self-directed mathematical projects: reading forward when you do not understand, on the the","s":1},{"id":"notation-used-throughout","t":"Notation used throughout","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Notation used throughout ¶ Symbol Read as Means in membership: says is an element of the set subset of every element of the first set is in the second for all the statement holds for every value named there exists at least one value makes the statement true implies if the left is true, the right must be if and only if each side implies the other; they are equivalent the reals all decimal numbers, including irrational ones R n lists of real numbers; a portfolio lives here sum product expectation of X the probabilit","s":1},{"id":"0a-2-sets-functions-and-relations","t":"0A.2 Sets, functions and relations","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.2 Sets, functions and relations ¶ A set is a collection of distinct objects, written in braces: . Order does not matter and repetition does not count. Sets can be infinite ( , the counting numbers) or defined by a property rather than a list: read as “the set of real such that ”, which is the interval . The colon means “such that”. This set-builder notation is used constantly and is worth being fluent in.","s":1},{"id":"operations","t":"Operations","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Operations ¶ Union — everything in either set. Intersection — everything in both. Complement — everything in the ambient set that is not in . Difference — in but not . Cartesian product — all ordered pairs . is : the plane. These are not decoration. In Volume 1 a σ-algebra is defined as a collection of sets closed under complement and countable union; if those two words are not automatic, that definition is noise.","s":1},{"id":"functions","t":"Functions","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Functions ¶ A function assigns to each element of (the domain ) exactly one element of (the codomain ). The word “exactly one” is the whole content: a rule that sometimes gives two answers is not a function, and this matters when you invert things. Injective (one-to-one): different inputs give different outputs. is; on is not. Surjective (onto): every element of the codomain is hit by something. Bijective : both. Exactly the functions with a well-defined inverse . Composition — apply , then . Read right to left. T","s":1},{"id":"the-functions-you-will-meet-constantly","t":"The functions you will meet constantly","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The functions you will meet constantly ¶ Function Definition Why it appears the unique function equal to its own derivative continuous compounding; every solution of a linear differential equation inverse of , defined for log returns are additive across time, which simple returns are not the standard normal cumulative distribution Black–Scholes; every normal-tail probability the positive part, written an option payoff is exactly this 1 if event happened, else 0 the indicator: turns events into numbers so you can t","s":1},{"id":"0a-3-sequences-limits-and-continuity","t":"0A.3 Sequences, limits and continuity","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.3 Sequences, limits and continuity ¶ Almost every definition in analysis is a limit, and a limit is a promise about how close you can get. The formal statement is worth reading slowly once, because the pattern recurs everywhere: In words: name any tolerance , however small; I can name a point in the sequence beyond which every term is within that tolerance of . The order of the quantifiers is the whole content. is allowed to depend on ; if it were not, the definition would be far stronger (that is uniform conve","s":1},{"id":"the-limit-you-must-know-cold","t":"The limit you must know cold","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The limit you must know cold ¶ This is compounding. Ten percent a year compounded annually is ; compounded monthly, ; compounded continuously, . The gap between monthly and continuous is five basis points, and the fact that it is that small is why continuous-time models are usable at all: the mathematical convenience is enormous and the approximation error is a rounding difference.","s":1},{"id":"continuity","t":"Continuity","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Continuity ¶ is continuous at if — the limit exists and agrees with the value. Informally: you can draw it without lifting the pen. It matters because continuous functions on closed bounded intervals attain their maxima (so optimisation problems have solutions), and because price paths are continuous but not differentiable , which is the single structural fact that makes stochastic calculus a separate subject rather than an application of ordinary calculus. The fact that generates Volume 1 A Brownian path is conti","s":1},{"id":"0a-4-the-derivative-built-from-nothing","t":"0A.4 The derivative, built from nothing","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.4 The derivative, built from nothing ¶ The derivative of at is the limit of the average rate of change as the interval shrinks: The quotient is the slope of the straight line through and — the average rate of change over a window of width . Shrink the window and, if the limit exists, you get the instantaneous rate. Do it once by hand for , because doing it once is worth reading it ten times: Note what happened: the term survived division by as an , then vanished in the limit. In ordinary calculus, squared incre","s":"0a.4 the derivative, built from nothing >0a.4 the derivative, built from nothing ¶ the derivative of at is the limit of the average rate of change as the interval shrinks: the quotient is the slope of the straight line through and — the average rate of change over a window of width . shrink the window and, if the limit exists, you get the instantaneous rate. do it once by hand for , because doing it once is worth reading it ten times: note what happened: the term survived division by as an , then vanished in the limit. in ordinary calculus, squared incre everything later — itô, the greeks, gradients — is this picture with more indices. volume 1 exists because for a price path the secants never settle. a parabola with three secant lines drawn from a fixed point to points that get progressively closer, their slopes converging on the tangent line"},{"id":"the-rules-and-where-each-one-is-used","t":"The rules, and where each one is used","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The rules, and where each one is used ¶ Rule Statement Used for Power everything Exponential continuous compounding; the reason is special Logarithm log returns; log-likelihoods Sum portfolio derivatives are sums of position derivatives Product Itô's product rule adds a third term to this Quotient ratios, spreads Chain the most important rule in the book","s":1},{"id":"why-the-chain-rule-dominates","t":"Why the chain rule dominates","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Why the chain rule dominates ¶ Because almost everything you differentiate is a composition. A P&L is a function of positions, which are a function of signals, which are a function of prices. Backpropagation in a neural network (Volume 7) is the chain rule applied mechanically across layers. Delta hedging (Volume 8) is the chain rule applied to an option's dependence on the underlying. Itô's lemma is the chain rule with one extra term. Learn it until it is reflex. ▢ Differentiate . (Answer: — the negative logistic","s":1},{"id":"0a-5-taylor-series-the-approximation-everything-ru","t":"0A.5 Taylor series: the approximation everything runs on","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.5 Taylor series: the approximation everything runs on ¶ If is smooth near , it is well approximated by a polynomial built from its derivatives at : Truncating after the linear term is linearisation ; after the quadratic term you have captured curvature. Essentially every practical formula in finance is one of these two truncations, and knowing which one you are using tells you exactly when it will fail. Where it shows up The truncation What the neglected term costs you Duration and convexity (Volume 13A) bond p","s":"0a.5 taylor series: the approximation everything runs on >0a.5 taylor series: the approximation everything runs on ¶ if is smooth near , it is well approximated by a polynomial built from its derivatives at : truncating after the linear term is linearisation ; after the quadratic term you have captured curvature. essentially every practical formula in finance is one of these two truncations, and knowing which one you are using tells you exactly when it will fail. where it shows up the truncation what the neglected term costs you duration and convexity (volume 13a) bond p almost every approximation in this reference is this one: keep two terms, and know how wrong you are when the move is large. an exponential curve with its first-order and second-order taylor approximations, matching closely near the expansion point and diverging away from it"},{"id":"0a-6-integration-and-the-fundamental-theorem","t":"0A.6 Integration and the fundamental theorem","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.6 Integration and the fundamental theorem ¶ An integral is a limit of sums. Partition into pieces, multiply the function value on each piece by the piece's width, add them up, and let the pieces shrink: That is the definition, and it is worth carrying rather than the “area under the curve” picture, because the sum-of-products form is what generalises. A trading P&L is exactly a sum of (position) × (price change); as the rebalancing interval shrinks it becomes , a stochastic integral. The whole of Volume 1.4 is ","s":1},{"id":"the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus","t":"The fundamental theorem of calculus","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The fundamental theorem of calculus ¶ Differentiation and integration undo each other. The practical consequence is that you compute integrals by recognising antiderivatives, and the practical trap is that the theorem requires to be differentiable — which Brownian paths are not, which is why Itô's lemma has an extra term where the fundamental theorem would predict none.","s":1},{"id":"the-three-techniques-worth-having","t":"The three techniques worth having","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The three techniques worth having ¶ Technique Form Where you need it Substitution the chain rule run backwards; change of variables in densities By parts moment calculations; the Itô product rule is its stochastic cousin Change of variable in a density converting between price and log-price distributions; normalising flows","s":1},{"id":"the-integral-you-should-memorise","t":"The integral you should memorise","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The integral you should memorise ¶ This is why the normal density carries a : it is the constant that makes the total probability one. The standard proof squares the integral, converts to polar coordinates and evaluates — a two-dimensional trick for a one-dimensional problem, and a classic interview question (Volume 23). ▢ Compute by parts. (Answer: ; multiply by and you have the mean of an exponential distribution, which is the waiting time between Poisson arrivals — the model for order flow in Volume 2.7.)","s":1},{"id":"0a-7-several-variables-gradients-hessians-and-jaco","t":"0A.7 Several variables: gradients, Hessians and Jacobians","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.7 Several variables: gradients, Hessians and Jacobians ¶ A portfolio return depends on many weights at once, so single-variable calculus is not enough. The partial derivative differentiates with respect to one variable holding the others fixed. Collect them into a vector and you have the gradient : Three facts about the gradient are worth knowing precisely rather than vaguely. It points in the direction of steepest increase , and its magnitude is the rate of increase in that direction. Gradient descent therefor","s":1},{"id":"the-hessian","t":"The Hessian","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The Hessian ¶ The matrix of second partials describes curvature. It is symmetric whenever the second partials are continuous (Clairaut's theorem), which is essentially always in practice. At a critical point: Hessian is The point is In portfolio terms positive definite a local minimum the risk function has a unique local best negative definite a local maximum the utility surface peaks here indefinite a saddle point improvable in some directions, worse in others singular inconclusive at second order a flat directio","s":1},{"id":"the-jacobian-and-the-multivariate-chain-rule","t":"The Jacobian and the multivariate chain rule","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The Jacobian and the multivariate chain rule ¶ For a vector-valued , the Jacobian is the matrix of all first partials, and the chain rule becomes matrix multiplication: . Backpropagation is precisely this product, evaluated right to left because that ordering is cheaper when the output is scalar. ▢ For show (using symmetry of ). Then confirm that setting the gradient of to zero reproduces the Markowitz weights.","s":1},{"id":"0a-8-vectors-and-what-a-portfolio-actually-is","t":"0A.8 Vectors, and what a portfolio actually is","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.8 Vectors, and what a portfolio actually is ¶ A vector in is an ordered list of numbers. A portfolio of assets is a weight vector ; a day's returns are a vector . Almost everything you do is then an operation on these two objects. Operation Formula What it means here Inner product the portfolio's return — the single most used expression in the book Norm size of the position vector; the ridge penalty is its square norm gross exposure; the lasso penalty; turnover when applied to Angle correlation between two cent","s":1},{"id":"linear-independence-span-and-basis","t":"Linear independence, span and basis","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Linear independence, span and basis ¶ Vectors are linearly independent if the only combination is the one with every . Their span is the set of all combinations. A basis is an independent spanning set, and its size is the dimension . The trading content: if your factors are not linearly independent, the regression that attributes returns to them has no unique answer. Value and quality overlap; momentum and low-vol overlap; if you include enough of them you will hit exact or near collinearity and your factor loadin","s":1},{"id":"0a-9-matrices-as-linear-maps","t":"0A.9 Matrices as linear maps","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.9 Matrices as linear maps ¶ A matrix is best understood not as a grid of numbers but as a function that takes vectors to vectors and respects addition and scaling: and . Every such function is a matrix and every matrix is such a function. Matrix multiplication is composition of these functions, which is why it is associative ( ) and why it is not commutative — doing then is not the same as doing then , exactly as with any two operations.","s":1},{"id":"the-matrices-you-meet","t":"The matrices you meet","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The matrices you meet ¶ Matrix Structure Where it appears Covariance symmetric, positive semi-definite risk; the Hessian of the portfolio variance Design , observations by features every regression; every factor model Projection symmetric, idempotent fitted values; hedging out factor exposure Orthogonal rotations; preserves lengths and angles; the of a QR decomposition Diagonal off-diagonals zero independent scaling; idiosyncratic risk in a factor model Loadings , assets by factors — the factor-model covariance","s":1},{"id":"transpose-trace-and-determinant","t":"Transpose, trace and determinant","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Transpose, trace and determinant ¶ Transpose . Reverses order under products: . Getting this backwards is the single most common algebra slip. Trace . Cyclic: . Equals the sum of eigenvalues, which is why is total variance. Determinant . The factor by which the map scales volume; zero exactly when the map collapses a dimension, i.e. when is singular. Equals the product of eigenvalues. A rule you will break exactly once Never compute by forming . Solve the system instead: np.linalg.solve(A, b) , not np.linalg.inv(A","s":1},{"id":"0a-10-linear-systems-rank-and-the-meaning-of-singu","t":"0A.10 Linear systems, rank and the meaning of singularity","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.10 Linear systems, rank and the meaning of singularity ¶ Solving asks: which input does the map send to ? There are exactly three possible answers, and knowing which one you are in explains most numerical trouble you will meet. Case Condition Trading interpretation Unique solution square, full rank the hedge is determined No solution outside the column space no combination of hedges reproduces the exposure; least squares gives the closest Infinitely many columns linearly dependent several hedges are equivalent;","s":1},{"id":"condition-number","t":"Condition number","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Condition number ¶ The ratio of largest to smallest singular value. It bounds how much a relative error in can be amplified in : roughly, you lose digits of accuracy. Double precision gives you about 16 digits, so leaves four. Print the condition number of every matrix you invert. It costs one line and it will one day save you a month. Python Copy import numpy as np def check_conditioning (Sigma, name= \"Sigma\" , warn_at= 1e10 ): \"\"\"Print the diagnostics that decide whether an inverse is meaningful. A covariance ma","s":1},{"id":"0a-11-eigenvalues-eigenvectors-and-the-spectral-th","t":"0A.11 Eigenvalues, eigenvectors and the spectral theorem","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.11 Eigenvalues, eigenvectors and the spectral theorem ¶ An eigenvector of is a direction the map does not rotate, only stretches; the stretch factor is its eigenvalue : For the symmetric matrices that dominate finance, the situation is as good as it can possibly be. The spectral theorem says: every real symmetric can be written with all real and the eigenvectors (columns of ) mutually orthogonal. Read the decomposition as a recipe: rotate into the eigenbasis, scale each axis independently, rotate back. Any symm","s":"0a.11 eigenvalues, eigenvectors and the spectral theorem >0a.11 eigenvalues, eigenvectors and the spectral theorem ¶ an eigenvector of is a direction the map does not rotate, only stretches; the stretch factor is its eigenvalue : for the symmetric matrices that dominate finance, the situation is as good as it can possibly be. the spectral theorem says: every real symmetric can be written with all real and the eigenvectors (columns of ) mutually orthogonal. read the decomposition as a recipe: rotate into the eigenbasis, scale each axis independently, rotate back. any symm finding those directions is the same act as finding where your risk actually is — which is why the same decomposition appears in pca, in risk models and in volume 11a. two panels: on the left a vector that the matrix both rotates and stretches, on the right an eigenvector that is stretched along its own line without turning"},{"id":"what-this-buys-you-immediately","t":"What this buys you immediately","v":"Vol 0A","x":">What this buys you immediately ¶ PCA is this theorem. Applied to a covariance matrix, the eigenvectors are the principal components and the eigenvalues are the variance each explains. The first eigenvector of an equity covariance matrix is almost always close to the market itself. Powers and functions become trivial: , and the same works for , and . Repairing a broken correlation matrix: compute the spectrum, clip negative eigenvalues to a small positive floor, reassemble, rescale the diagonal to one. This is the ","s":1},{"id":"singular-value-decomposition","t":"Singular value decomposition","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Singular value decomposition ¶ For non-square or non-symmetric matrices the corresponding tool is the SVD, , with orthogonal and diagonal with non-negative entries. It always exists, for every matrix, which makes it the single most reliable numerical tool in linear algebra. Least squares, pseudo-inverses, low-rank approximation, PCA on the data matrix directly (without forming the covariance, which squares the condition number) — all are SVD. Python Copy import numpy as np def pca_returns (R, k= None ): \"\"\"PCA on ","s":1},{"id":"0a-12-positive-definiteness-cholesky-and-simulatin","t":"0A.12 Positive definiteness, Cholesky and simulating correlated returns","v":"Vol 0A","x":"A covariance matrix is a claim about how a portfolio's risk depends on its weights. Positive definiteness is the claim that no combination of weights produces negative risk — which would be nonsense, and which estimation error nevertheless produces routinely. Cholesky is the machine that both checks >0A.12 Positive definiteness, Cholesky and simulating correlated returns ¶ A symmetric is positive definite if for every non-zero , and positive semi-definite (PSD) if the inequality is merely . Equivalent characterisations, all worth recognising: every eigenvalue is positive (non-negative); the Cholesky factorisation exists; every leadi","s":"0a.12 positive definiteness, cholesky and simulating correlated returns >0a.12 positive definiteness, cholesky and simulating correlated returns ¶ a symmetric is positive definite if for every non-zero , and positive semi-definite (psd) if the inequality is merely . equivalent characterisations, all worth recognising: every eigenvalue is positive (non-negative); the cholesky factorisation exists; every leading principal minor is positive. why it matters: is the variance of the portfolio . a covariance matrix that is not psd is asserting that some portfolio has negative variance, which  a covariance matrix is a claim about how a portfolio's risk depends on its weights. positive definiteness is the claim that no combination of weights produces negative risk — which would be nonsense, and which estimation error nevertheless produces routinely. cholesky is the machine that both checks the claim and, as a by-product, hands you the recipe for manufacturing correlated random numbers, which is how every simulation in this reference builds a market."},{"id":"cholesky","t":"Cholesky","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Cholesky ¶ The Cholesky factor is the “square root” you want in practice: it is twice as fast as an eigendecomposition, numerically excellent, and it fails loudly — if is not positive definite the factorisation raises rather than returning nonsense. Use its success as your PSD test. Its main use is simulating correlated draws. If is a vector of independent standard normals, then has covariance Python Copy import numpy as np def correlated_normals (Sigma, n_draws, rng= None , jitter= 1e-10 ): \"\"\"Draw n_draws x n sa","s":1},{"id":"0a-13-matrix-calculus-the-seven-identities-you-wil","t":"0A.13 Matrix calculus: the seven identities you will reuse","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.13 Matrix calculus: the seven identities you will reuse ¶ These come up constantly and are tedious to re-derive under time pressure. Learn them as facts; each can be verified by writing out one component. Expression Derivative with respect to Appears in expected return of a portfolio if symmetric portfolio variance ridge penalty w.r.t. the OLS normal equations w.r.t. Gaussian log-likelihood; MLE for covariance w.r.t. trace tricks in covariance estimation w.r.t. a scalar sensitivity of an optimiser to its inputs","s":1},{"id":"0a-14-probability-from-first-principles","t":"0A.14 Probability from first principles","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.14 Probability from first principles ¶ Probability is built from three objects, and Volume 1 will name all three in its opening sentence. They are worth meeting slowly. Sample space the set of everything that could happen. For one coin flip, . For a price path over a year, the set of all continuous functions on — which is why the machinery has to be careful. σ-algebra the collection of subsets of to which we are willing to assign probabilities, closed under complement and countable union. On finite you can take","s":1},{"id":"the-rules-derived-rather-than-listed","t":"The rules, derived rather than listed","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The rules, derived rather than listed ¶ Complement: , since and are disjoint and cover . Inclusion–exclusion: — add the pieces, subtract the double-counted overlap. Monotonicity: if then . Union bound: . Crude but universal, and it is the whole basis of the Bonferroni correction in Volume 18.","s":1},{"id":"conditional-probability-and-bayes","t":"Conditional probability and Bayes","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Conditional probability and Bayes ¶ Rearranging gives the multiplication rule , and applying it both ways gives Bayes' theorem : The calculation that should change how you read a backtest Suppose one in twenty strategy ideas is genuinely profitable — a generous prior for retail-accessible ideas. Your validation procedure detects a real edge 80% of the time (power) and falsely blesses a worthless idea 10% of the time (size). An idea passes. What is the probability it is real? Seventy percent of everything that pass","s":1},{"id":"0a-15-random-variables-distributions-and-densities","t":"0A.15 Random variables, distributions and densities","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.15 Random variables, distributions and densities ¶ A random variable is a measurable function from outcomes to numbers — it attaches a number to whatever happened. “Measurable” means that is in for every , so that asking “what is the probability ?” is a legitimate question. The cumulative distribution function is the primary object. It always exists, is non-decreasing, right-continuous, and runs from to . If it is differentiable, its derivative is the density , and A density is not a probability A density can e","s":1},{"id":"joint-marginal-conditional","t":"Joint, marginal, conditional","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Joint, marginal, conditional ¶ Object Definition Reading Joint the full description of two quantities together Marginal integrate out what you do not care about Conditional the distribution of once is known Independence knowing one tells you nothing about the other The decomposition is the reason copulas exist: it says a joint distribution splits into marginals plus a dependence structure, and Volume 12A shows that the split can be made exact and canonical. It is also why “the marginals looked fine” is never a def","s":1},{"id":"transformations","t":"Transformations","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Transformations ¶ If with monotone and differentiable, the change-of-variables formula gives The Jacobian factor is not optional bookkeeping; forgetting it is the most common error in hand-rolled likelihoods. Concretely: if log returns are normal then prices are log-normal with density , and the out front is exactly that Jacobian. It is also why the median and the mean of a log-normal differ by , which is the same volatility-drag term from 0A.3 wearing a different hat.","s":1},{"id":"0a-16-expectation-variance-and-covariance","t":"0A.16 Expectation, variance and covariance","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.16 Expectation, variance and covariance ¶ Expectation is a probability-weighted average, and the second form — the “law of the unconscious statistician” — says you can average against 's own density without first finding the density of .","s":1},{"id":"the-properties-and-the-two-that-are-not-properties","t":"The properties, and the two that are not properties","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The properties, and the two that are not properties ¶ Statement True? Note Always linearity needs no independence — the most useful fact in probability Only if uncorrelated independence is sufficient, not necessary Always why volatility scales with , not Only if uncorrelated in general add Only if is affine Jensen: for convex , for concave Essentially never a special case of Jensen that costs people real money Jensen's inequality is the reason for half the results in this book For convex , . Consequences you will ","s":1},{"id":"covariance-and-correlation","t":"Covariance and correlation","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Covariance and correlation ¶ Correlation is covariance rescaled to be unit-free, and by 0A.8 it is a cosine. It measures linear association only: with symmetric about zero has correlation exactly zero and total dependence. Volume 12A is largely about what to use when linear association is not the right question.","s":1},{"id":"portfolio-variance-which-is-the-point-of-all-of-th","t":"Portfolio variance, which is the point of all of this","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Portfolio variance, which is the point of all of this ¶ Read the double sum: with assets there are variance terms and covariance terms. For that is 50 versus 2450. Portfolio risk is almost entirely a statement about correlations, not about individual volatilities , and it is why diversification stops working precisely when correlations rise, which is precisely when you need it. Equal-weighting assets with common correlation and common vol gives portfolio variance , which tends to rather than to zero. Diversificati","s":1},{"id":"0a-17-the-distributions-you-actually-need","t":"0A.17 The distributions you actually need","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.17 The distributions you actually need ¶ Distribution Density or mass Mean / Variance Where it appears Bernoulli / a single win/loss; hit rate Binomial / wins in trades; the binomial tree Poisson / order arrivals; jump counts Exponential / waiting time between arrivals; memoryless Normal / the default, and usually wrong in the tails Log-normal / — prices under GBM; strictly positive Student — / returns with fat tails; fits daily equities Chi-squared — / sums of squared normals; variance tests; Johansen Gamma / ","s":"0a.17 the distributions you actually need >0a.17 the distributions you actually need ¶ distribution density or mass mean / variance where it appears bernoulli / a single win/loss; hit rate binomial / wins in trades; the binomial tree poisson / order arrivals; jump counts exponential / waiting time between arrivals; memoryless normal / the default, and usually wrong in the tails log-normal / — prices under gbm; strictly positive student — / returns with fat tails; fits daily equities chi-squared — / sums of squared normals; variance tests; johansen gamma /  every risk number that assumes normality — most var, most option pricing, most position sizing — is wrong in the same direction, and the error is worst exactly when it matters. a normal density beside a fat-tailed density of the same variance, with the region beyond four standard deviations shaded to show how much more probability the fat-tailed one puts there"},{"id":"the-relationships-worth-knowing","t":"The relationships worth knowing","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The relationships worth knowing ¶ Sum of independent Bernoullis is Binomial; its limit as with fixed is Poisson. Sum of independent squared standard normals is . Ratio of a standard normal to is — which is exactly why estimating the variance rather than knowing it turns a -statistic into a -statistic. as ; by the difference is cosmetic, which is the origin of the folklore ‘30 observations is enough’. Exponential inter-arrival times Poisson counts. Hawkes processes (Volume 2.7) break this by letting the arrival rat","s":1},{"id":"0a-18-the-two-limit-theorems-and-what-they-do-not","t":"0A.18 The two limit theorems, and what they do not say","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.18 The two limit theorems, and what they do not say ¶","s":1},{"id":"law-of-large-numbers","t":"Law of large numbers","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Law of large numbers ¶ Sample averages converge to the truth. The condition matters: a Cauchy distribution has no mean and its sample average does not converge to anything — it has the same distribution as a single draw, no matter how many observations you collect. Distributions with infinite variance but finite mean converge, but slowly enough that the practical implications are severe.","s":1},{"id":"central-limit-theorem","t":"Central limit theorem","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Central limit theorem ¶ Not only does the average converge, its error is asymptotically normal with standard deviation . This is where every standard error, confidence interval and -statistic in the book comes from. Four things the CLT does not say It says nothing about the tails. Convergence is in distribution and is fastest in the centre. The approximation in the far tail — the only region VaR cares about — can be poor at any sample size you will ever have. The Berry–Esseen bound gives an error of order uniforml","s":1},{"id":"the-consequence-you-will-use-every-day","t":"The consequence you will use every day","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The consequence you will use every day ¶ — where both the Sharpe and are in the same time units. Mixing units here is the most common error in this calculation, so convert once and carefully. With periods per year and years of data, substituting and gives the annualised version, which is the one you want: The correction term is divided by for daily data, so it is negligible and the standard error of an annualised Sharpe ratio is essentially one over the square root of the number of years . That is a fact worth mem","s":1},{"id":"0a-19-estimation-likelihood-bias-and-standard-erro","t":"0A.19 Estimation: likelihood, bias and standard error","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.19 Estimation: likelihood, bias and standard error ¶ An estimator is a function of data used to guess a parameter. It is itself a random variable, and everything about it is a statement about its distribution. Property Definition Comment Bias unbiased is nice, not essential — see below Variance how much it moves across samples MSE the decomposition behind every regularisation argument Consistency as the minimum acceptable property Efficiency attains the Cramér–Rao lower bound no unbiased estimator does better W","s":1},{"id":"maximum-likelihood","t":"Maximum likelihood","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Maximum likelihood ¶ Choose the parameter under which the observed data is most probable. Work with the log because it converts products to sums, which is both numerically necessary (products of thousands of small densities underflow) and analytically convenient. Under regularity conditions the MLE is consistent, asymptotically normal and asymptotically efficient, with The Fisher information is the curvature of the log-likelihood at its peak. A sharply peaked likelihood means the data pin the parameter down; a fla","s":1},{"id":"0a-20-hypothesis-testing-and-what-a-t-statistic-ac","t":"0A.20 Hypothesis testing, and what a t-statistic actually means","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.20 Hypothesis testing, and what a t-statistic actually means ¶ The logic is narrower than most people assume, and the gap between what a test says and what people hear it say is responsible for a large share of the fake strategies in the world. State a null hypothesis — typically ‘this strategy has no edge’. Choose a statistic whose distribution under you know. Compute it on your data. The p-value is . If the p-value is below a pre-chosen , reject . What a p-value is not It is not . That is the quantity you wan","s":1},{"id":"the-two-errors-and-which-one-you-should-fear","t":"The two errors, and which one you should fear","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The two errors, and which one you should fear ¶ true (no edge) false (real edge) Reject Type I — you trade noise. Probability Correct Fail to reject Correct Type II — you skip a good strategy. Probability Convention sets and tolerates . For a trader that trade-off is backwards. A Type II error costs you an opportunity; a Type I error costs you capital, time, and the confidence to run the next system. Given that you will test many ideas and that the prior on any one being real is low, the correct posture is a much ","s":1},{"id":"the-t-statistic","t":"The t-statistic","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The t-statistic ¶ For a return series tested against zero mean, . Now substitute the definition of the Sharpe ratio, per period: The most useful identity in the book A Sharpe ratio is a t-statistic divided by the square root of the number of years. Sharpe 2.0 over one year is — not significant at 5% once you account for having looked at more than one strategy. Sharpe 1.0 over nine years is , which is real evidence. Whenever someone shows you a Sharpe ratio, immediately multiply by the square root of the track reco","s":1},{"id":"0a-21-regression-derived-and-diagnosed","t":"0A.21 Regression, derived and diagnosed","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.21 Regression, derived and diagnosed ¶ Linear regression is the workhorse of the entire field: factor models, hedge ratios, beta estimation, cointegration, event studies and impact calibration are all regressions. You met the derivation in 0A.13; here is what surrounds it.","s":1},{"id":"the-assumptions-and-which-are-negotiable","t":"The assumptions, and which are negotiable","v":"Vol 0A","x":">The assumptions, and which are negotiable ¶ Assumption Needed for Reality in finance Linearity in parameters unbiasedness usually acceptable; you can add non-linear features Exogeneity unbiasedness the critical one ; violated by simultaneity and omitted variables No perfect collinearity existence of a unique near-violated constantly by overlapping factors Homoscedasticity the textbook standard errors false — volatility clusters. Use robust SEs No autocorrelation the textbook standard errors false for overlapping w","s":1},{"id":"0a-22-optimisation-convexity-lagrange-and-kkt","t":"0A.22 Optimisation: convexity, Lagrange and KKT","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.22 Optimisation: convexity, Lagrange and KKT ¶ Portfolio construction, model fitting and execution scheduling are all optimisation problems. The single most useful distinction is convex versus not.","s":1},{"id":"convexity","t":"Convexity","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Convexity ¶ A set is convex if the segment between any two of its points stays inside it. A function is convex if — the chord lies above the graph. Equivalently, for twice-differentiable , the Hessian is positive semi-definite everywhere. Why the distinction is worth everything For a convex objective over a convex set, every local minimum is global , a solution can be certified optimal, and off-the-shelf solvers reach it reliably in polynomial time. Mean–variance with linear constraints is convex. Ridge and lasso ","s":1},{"id":"unconstrained-gradient-descent-and-newton","t":"Unconstrained: gradient descent and Newton","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Unconstrained: gradient descent and Newton ¶ Method Update Cost / behaviour Gradient descent cheap per step; linear convergence; step size is a nuisance Newton quadratic convergence near the optimum; per step Quasi-Newton (BFGS) approximates from gradients the practical default for smooth problems under a few thousand parameters Stochastic gradient gradient on a minibatch the only option when is large; noise acts as regularisation","s":1},{"id":"constrained-lagrange-multipliers","t":"Constrained: Lagrange multipliers","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Constrained: Lagrange multipliers ¶ To minimise subject to , form and set all partial derivatives to zero. The condition says the gradients are parallel at the optimum — you cannot improve without leaving the constraint surface. The multiplier is not an artefact: it is the shadow price , the rate at which the optimum improves as the constraint is relaxed. In a portfolio problem with a leverage constraint, tells you what an extra unit of leverage is worth, which is exactly the number you need to decide whether to p","s":1},{"id":"inequality-constraints-kkt","t":"Inequality constraints: KKT","v":"Vol 0A","x":">Inequality constraints: KKT ¶ With , the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker conditions add complementary slackness : with . Read it as a dichotomy: either a constraint binds ( ) and has a positive shadow price, or it is slack ( ) and its multiplier is zero. In portfolio terms, a position sitting exactly at its cap has a shadow price telling you what relaxing that cap is worth; a position below its cap is unaffected by it. Scanning the multipliers after an optimise is the fastest way to understand what is actually driving a soluti","s":1},{"id":"0a-23-numerical-hygiene-when-the-arithmetic-lies","t":"0A.23 Numerical hygiene: when the arithmetic lies","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.23 Numerical hygiene: when the arithmetic lies ¶ Every number in your system is a 64-bit float with about 15–17 significant decimal digits. That is usually plenty and occasionally catastrophic, and the failures are silent. Hazard What happens Defence Catastrophic cancellation for close loses most digits algebraic rearrangement; Welford for variance Naive variance can return negative Welford's online algorithm, or subtract the mean first Accumulated summation error error grows like over a long series math.fsum o","s":1},{"id":"0a-24-self-test-are-you-ready-for-volume-1","t":"0A.24 Self-test: are you ready for Volume 1?","v":"Vol 0A","x":">0A.24 Self-test: are you ready for Volume 1? ¶ Answer on paper, without notes, in about ninety minutes. This is not a formality — the whole purpose of a gate is that it is possible to fail it. Tick a box only if you could produce the answer on a blank page; the score is recorded on your dashboard in Volume 00. Volume 0A gate — tick each one you could answer on a blank page Write the definition of a σ-algebra, and explain in one sentence why a filtration is the formal statement of “no lookahead”. Derive d/dx of x² ","s":1},{"id":"vol0b","t":"Python, Tooling and the Research Environment from Zero","v":"Vol 0B","x":"Volume 0A gives you the mathematics. This one gives you the machine that runs it. It assumes you have never installed Python, never used a terminal, and never written a backtest, and it ends with a correct end-to-end backtest you wrote yourself and a repository layout that will still make sense in a year. The order matters. Most people learn Python by writing analysis scripts and only later discover environments, testing and version control, by which point their research is a directory of notebooks with names like ","s":1},{"id":"0b-1-the-environment-set-up-once-and-correctly","t":"0B.1 The environment, set up once and correctly","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.1 The environment, set up once and correctly ¶ Python's packaging has a reputation it has earned, but the modern answer is short. Use a recent Python (3.11 or later), one virtual environment per project, and a lockfile. Never install packages into the system Python; that is how you end up unable to run last month's research because this month's project upgraded NumPy. Shell Copy # Install uv -- a fast, single-binary Python package and project manager. curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh # Create the","s":1},{"id":"the-terminal-in-the-eight-commands-you-need","t":"The terminal, in the eight commands you need","v":"Vol 0B","x":">The terminal, in the eight commands you need ¶ Command Does Note pwd print working directory where am I ls -la list files including hidden hidden files start with a dot and matter cd path change directory cd .. goes up one mkdir -p a/b make directories -p creates parents as needed cat f / head -20 f show a file / its first lines head first, always, on a data file grep -rn 'text' . search recursively with line numbers how you find things in your own code python -m module run a module as a script the correct way to ","s":1},{"id":"0b-2-the-language-in-one-pass-for-numerical-work","t":"0B.2 The language, in one pass, for numerical work","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.2 The language, in one pass, for numerical work ¶ You do not need most of Python. You need types, control flow, functions, comprehensions, a working understanding of mutability, and enough object orientation to read other people's code. Python Copy from dataclasses import dataclass from typing import Iterable # --- the types you will actually use ----------------------------------------- x: float = 1.5 # float, not decimal -- see 0A.23 on money n: int = 3 # arbitrary precision, never overflows name: str = \"AAPL","s":1},{"id":"mutability-and-the-copy-you-did-not-make","t":"Mutability, and the copy you did not make","v":"Vol 0B","x":">Mutability, and the copy you did not make ¶ Python Copy import numpy as np a = np. arange ( 5 ) b = a # b is the SAME array; b[0] = 99 changes a c = a[ 1 : 3 ] # a VIEW: writing to c writes to a d = a[ 1 : 3 ]. copy () # an independent copy df_slice = df[df.date > \"2020-01-01\" ] # pandas: may be a view or a copy df_slice[ \"x\" ] = 1 # -> SettingWithCopyWarning; may silently no-op df_slice = df.loc[df.date > \"2020-01-01\" ]. copy () # explicit, unambiguous, correct","s":1},{"id":"0b-3-numpy-the-mental-model-then-the-api","t":"0B.3 NumPy: the mental model, then the API","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.3 NumPy: the mental model, then the API ¶ NumPy exists because a Python loop over a million floats takes seconds and the equivalent array operation takes milliseconds. The reason is that a NumPy array is a contiguous block of identical machine-typed numbers, so the loop happens in compiled code with no per-element Python object overhead. Everything about the library follows from that one fact. Python Copy import numpy as np a = np. array ([ 1.0 , 2.0 , 3.0 ]) print (a.shape, a.dtype, a.nbytes) # (3,) float64 24","s":1},{"id":"the-performance-rules-in-order-of-impact","t":"The performance rules, in order of impact","v":"Vol 0B","x":">The performance rules, in order of impact ¶ Do not loop over rows. A vectorised expression is typically 50–200× faster. This is the whole game and everything else is a rounding error. Preallocate. Appending to a list in a loop and calling np.array at the end is fine; growing an array with np.append inside a loop is quadratic and will hang on real data. Watch dtype. An accidental object dtype loses every speed advantage and is usually caused by a stray None or string in the input. Check .dtype when something is mys","s":1},{"id":"0b-4-pandas-the-safe-parts-and-the-parts-that-will","t":"0B.4 pandas: the safe parts and the parts that will hurt you","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.4 pandas: the safe parts and the parts that will hurt you ¶ pandas gives you labelled axes, which is exactly what you want for time series: alignment by timestamp rather than by position removes an entire category of off-by-one bug. It also has a large surface area and several genuinely dangerous defaults. Python Copy import pandas as pd import numpy as np df = pd. read_parquet ( \"prices.parquet\" ) # parquet, not CSV: typed, compressed, fast df[ \"ts\" ] = pd. to_datetime (df[ \"ts\" ], utc= True ) # ALWAYS utc=Tru","s":1},{"id":"when-to-leave-pandas","t":"When to leave pandas","v":"Vol 0B","x":">When to leave pandas ¶ pandas is a good research tool and a poor production one. Its memory overhead is large, its copy semantics are ambiguous, and operations that look identical can differ by an order of magnitude in cost. Once a computation is settled, port it to NumPy or Polars for the production path and keep the pandas version as a reference implementation to test against. Volume 16.4 makes this concrete.","s":1},{"id":"0b-5-polars-and-when-the-switch-is-worth-it","t":"0B.5 Polars, and when the switch is worth it","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.5 Polars, and when the switch is worth it ¶ Polars is a columnar dataframe library with a lazy query engine and no index. Two consequences matter: it is typically 5–30× faster than pandas on grouped operations over large panels, and its lazy API lets the engine see the whole query before executing, so it can push filters down and avoid materialising intermediate frames. Python Copy import polars as pl # Lazy: nothing executes until .collect(). The optimiser sees the whole plan. q = ( pl. scan_parquet ( \"bars/*.","s":1},{"id":"0b-6-time-timezones-and-the-bug-that-silently-inva","t":"0B.6 Time, timezones and the bug that silently invalidates backtests","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.6 Time, timezones and the bug that silently invalidates backtests ¶ Time handling causes more silent backtest corruption than any other category of error, because the failures do not raise — they quietly shift a signal by a few hours and improve the results. Store everything in UTC. Convert to a local exchange timezone only for display. A naive timestamp — one with no timezone attached — is an ambiguous number and should not exist in your system. Know your bar convention. Does the timestamp on a bar mark its op","s":"0b.6 time, timezones and the bug that silently invalidates backtests >0b.6 time, timezones and the bug that silently invalidates backtests ¶ time handling causes more silent backtest corruption than any other category of error, because the failures do not raise — they quietly shift a signal by a few hours and improve the results. store everything in utc. convert to a local exchange timezone only for display. a naive timestamp — one with no timezone attached — is an ambiguous number and should not exist in your system. know your bar convention. does the timestamp on a bar mark its op no dataset states this reliably and both conventions are common. it is the single most valuable twenty minutes of checking in the whole research process. two interpretations of the same daily bar timestamp: read as the bar's open it lets a strategy act on information from the future, read as the bar's close it does not"},{"id":"0b-7-getting-data-in-files-apis-and-a-cache-that-s","t":"0B.7 Getting data in: files, APIs and a cache that saves you","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.7 Getting data in: files, APIs and a cache that saves you ¶ Format Use for Avoid because CSV interchange with humans untyped, slow, no compression, ambiguous dates Parquet the default for everything — HDF5 legacy interop concurrency is painful; the ecosystem has moved on Arrow IPC zero-copy between processes not a long-term archive format SQLite / DuckDB a queryable local store SQLite is row-oriented; DuckDB is the analytics answer Python Copy import hashlib, json, time from pathlib import Path import pandas as","s":1},{"id":"0b-8-testing-numerical-code","t":"0B.8 Testing numerical code","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.8 Testing numerical code ¶ Numerical code needs a different testing style from ordinary software, because the answer is rarely an exact value you can assert on. Four techniques cover almost everything. Python Copy import numpy as np import pytest from hypothesis import given, strategies as st, settings # --- 1. Closed-form comparison: test against a case you can solve by hand ---- def test_ou_half_life_matches_theory (): \"\"\"An OU process with theta=0.05 has half-life ln(2)/theta. Simulate a long path, fit, and ","s":1},{"id":"0b-9-git-as-a-research-log","t":"0B.9 Git as a research log","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.9 Git as a research log ¶ Version control in research is not primarily about collaboration; it is about being able to answer “what exactly produced this number?” six months later. That makes the commit message the important artefact, not the diff. Shell Copy # The daily loop git status # what changed git diff # read it before committing -- always git add -p # stage selectively, hunk by hunk git commit -m \"...\" # message conventions below git log --oneline --graph -20 # where am I # Branch per research cycle, so","s":1},{"id":"commit-messages-that-are-worth-reading-later","t":"Commit messages that are worth reading later","v":"Vol 0B","x":">Commit messages that are worth reading later ¶ Prefix Means Example exp: an experiment, with its result in the message exp: 60d vol target, Sharpe 0.71 -> 0.79, turnover +14% fix: a correctness fix — say what was wrong fix: signal was not shifted; all results before this are void feat: new capability feat: Newey-West standard errors in ols_hac refactor: no behaviour change — golden tests must pass refactor: extract fill model from engine data: a change to inputs, which invalidates prior results data: switch to poi","s":1},{"id":"0b-10-notebooks-and-modules-a-working-compromise","t":"0B.10 Notebooks and modules: a working compromise","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.10 Notebooks and modules: a working compromise ¶ Notebooks are excellent for looking at things and terrible for anything you will rely on. The out-of-order execution that makes them pleasant to explore in is exactly what makes their state unreproducible: a notebook that runs top to bottom today may not tomorrow, and one that shows a result may have produced it with code that has since been edited above it. The compromise that works, and that scales from your first week to a live system: All logic lives in src/ ","s":1},{"id":"0b-11-profiling-and-the-three-speedups-that-matter","t":"0B.11 Profiling, and the three speedups that matter","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.11 Profiling, and the three speedups that matter ¶ Measure before optimising. Programmer intuition about where time goes is unreliable in general and worse than random on numerical code, where a single accidental dtype conversion can dominate everything else. Shell Copy # Function-level: where does the time go? uv run python -m cProfile -s cumtime -m qr.research.momentum | head -30 # Line-level inside one function uv add --dev line_profiler # decorate with @profile, then: uv run kernprof -lv qr/signals.py # Mem","s":1},{"id":"0b-12-your-first-backtest-end-to-end-and-correct","t":"0B.12 Your first backtest, end to end and correct","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.12 Your first backtest, end to end and correct ¶ Everything above exists so that this is possible. What follows is a complete, honest vectorised backtester: it shifts signals, charges costs on turnover, targets volatility, and reports the statistics that matter rather than the ones that flatter. It is deliberately small enough to read in one sitting and to verify line by line, and it is the direct ancestor of the event-driven engine in Volume 16. Python Copy \"\"\"A minimal but honest vectorised backtester. Delibe","s":1},{"id":"0b-13-a-repository-layout-that-survives-a-year","t":"0B.13 A repository layout that survives a year","v":"Vol 0B","x":">0B.13 A repository layout that survives a year ¶ Shell Copy quant-research/ pyproject.toml # dependencies, tool config, package metadata uv.lock # exact pinned versions -- COMMIT THIS README.md # how to run it, from a cold clone .gitignore # data/, .venv/, __pycache__/, *.parquet .pre-commit-config.yaml # ruff, nbstripout, no-large-files src/qr/ __init__.py config.py # frozen dataclasses; no magic numbers anywhere else data/ loaders.py # raw ingestion; every loader validates (0B.7) universe.py # point-in-time memb","s":1},{"id":"vol1","t":"Stochastic Calculus and the SDEs You Actually Use","v":"Vol 01","x":"Most of stochastic calculus is irrelevant to trading. This volume covers the part that is not: the four processes you will fit to data (Brownian motion with drift, geometric Brownian motion, Ornstein–Uhlenbeck, and jump diffusion), the one lemma that generates every pricing PDE (Itô), the one theorem that lets you change drift for free (G","s":1},{"id":"1-1-filtered-probability-spaces-and-what-a-trading-s","t":"1.1 Filtered probability spaces and what a trading signal formally is","v":"Vol 01","x":"A probability space ( Omega, F, P) is a sample space Omega (the set of possible market paths), a sigma -algebra F of measurable events, and a measure P:F to[0,1] with P( Omega)=1 and countable additivity. This is bookkeeping. The object that matters for trading is the filtration .","s":1},{"id":"1-1-filtered-probability-spaces-and-what-a-trading-s","t":"1.1 Filtered probability spaces and what a trading signal formally is","v":"Vol 01","x":"A filtration F = F_t _t ge 0 is a non-decreasing family of sub- sigma -algebras, F_s subseteq F_t subseteq F for s le t . Read F_t as everything observable by time t . A process X is adapted to F if X_t is F_t -measurable for every t , and predictable if it is measurable with respect to the sigma -algebra generated by left-continuous adap","s":1},{"id":"1-1-filtered-probability-spaces-and-what-a-trading-s","t":"1.1 Filtered probability spaces and what a trading signal formally is","v":"Vol 01","x":"Strip the notation away and this section makes one claim: at any moment you know some things and not others, and a trading rule may only use what you already know. Everything below is the apparatus for saying that precisely enough that code can be checked against it. If you take one word from the se A trading strategy is a predictable process theta_t (position held over (t-dt, t] ), and the P&L is the stochastic integral int_0^T theta_t dS_t . Predictability is the mathematical statement of “you must decide the position before you observe the move.” Every look-ahead bug in a backtest is a violation of predictability — you have used ","s":"1.1 filtered probability spaces and what a trading signal formally is a trading strategy is a predictable process theta_t (position held over (t-dt, t] ), and the p&l is the stochastic integral int_0^t theta_t ds_t . predictability is the mathematical statement of “you must decide the position before you observe the move.” every look-ahead bug in a backtest is a violation of predictability — you have used a strip the notation away and this section makes one claim: at any moment you know some things and not others, and a trading rule may only use what you already know. everything below is the apparatus for saying that precisely enough that code can be checked against it. if you take one word from the section, take predictable — it is the formal name for the discipline that every look-ahead bug violates."},{"id":"1-2-martingales-and-the-two-fundamental-theorems","t":"1.2 Martingales and the two fundamental theorems","v":"Vol 01","x":"An adapted process M with E|M_t| < infty is a martingale if E[M_t mid F_s] = M_s for all s le t ; a supermartingale if le , a submartingale if ge . A martingale is a formalised fair game: the best forecast of the future value is the current value.","s":1},{"id":"1-2-martingales-and-the-two-fundamental-theorems","t":"1.2 Martingales and the two fundamental theorems","v":"Vol 01","x":"FTAP I. A market admits no free lunch with vanishing risk if and only if there exists a probability measure Q sim P (equivalent: same null sets) under which discounted asset prices are local martingales. FTAP II. The market is complete — every contingent claim is replicable — if and only if that Q is unique.","s":1},{"id":"1-2-martingales-and-the-two-fundamental-theorems","t":"1.2 Martingales and the two fundamental theorems","v":"Vol 01","x":"The practical content: equivalent means P and Q agree on what is impossible, not on what is likely. Changing measure changes drifts, never volatility. This is why an option price is a statement about volatility and not about your directional view, and why “the risk-neutral probability of default” is not a forecast of default.","s":1},{"id":"1-3-brownian-motion-and-quadratic-variation","t":"1.3 Brownian motion and quadratic variation","v":"Vol 01","x":"W = W_t _t ge0 is a standard Brownian motion if (i) W_0 = 0 a.s.; (ii) increments are independent: W_t - W_s perp F_s for s<t ; (iii) W_t - W_s sim N(0, t-s) ; (iv) paths are continuous a.s.","s":1},{"id":"1-3-brownian-motion-and-quadratic-variation","t":"1.3 Brownian motion and quadratic variation","v":"Vol 01","x":"The single most important property is the quadratic variation . For a partition Pi_n of [0,T] with mesh to 0 ,","s":1},{"id":"1-3-brownian-motion-and-quadratic-variation","t":"1.3 Brownian motion and quadratic variation","v":"Vol 01","x":"Contrast a differentiable path, whose quadratic variation is zero. Brownian paths are of unbounded first variation and finite non-zero second variation — this is exactly why ordinary calculus fails and why the Itô correction term exists. In shorthand:","s":1},{"id":"1-3-brownian-motion-and-quadratic-variation","t":"1.3 Brownian motion and quadratic variation","v":"Vol 01","x":"Brownian motion is the mathematical idealisation of a path that is jagged at every scale: continuous, so it never jumps, yet nowhere smooth, so it has no speed at any instant. The property that matters commercially is the last one below. Add up the squares of the little moves and you get a number th Quadratic variation is not an abstraction: it is what a realised-volatility estimator measures. Summing squared intraday log-returns over a day is a discrete estimate of [X,X] over that day. This is the formal justification for the HAR-RV models in Volume 4 and for variance swap replication in Volume 8 — a variance swap pays the realised","s":"1.3 brownian motion and quadratic variation quadratic variation is not an abstraction: it is what a realised-volatility estimator measures. summing squared intraday log-returns over a day is a discrete estimate of [x,x] over that day. this is the formal justification for the har-rv models in volume 4 and for variance swap replication in volume 8 — a variance swap pays the realised  brownian motion is the mathematical idealisation of a path that is jagged at every scale: continuous, so it never jumps, yet nowhere smooth, so it has no speed at any instant. the property that matters commercially is the last one below. add up the squares of the little moves and you get a number that does not shrink as you look more finely — an ordinary curve gives zero. that non-zero number is where the extra term in itô's lemma comes from, and therefore where option pricing comes from."},{"id":"1-4-the-it-integral-and-it-isometry","t":"1.4 The Itô integral and Itô isometry","v":"Vol 01","x":"For a predictable integrand theta with E int_0^T theta_t^2 dt < infty , the Itô integral I_T( theta)= int_0^T theta_t dW_t is defined as the L^2 limit of left-endpoint Riemann sums sum_i theta_t_i(W_t_i+1-W_t_i) . The left endpoint is not a convention of convenience — it is what makes I a martingale, and it is the mathematical image of pr","s":1},{"id":"1-4-the-it-integral-and-it-isometry","t":"1.4 The Itô integral and Itô isometry","v":"Vol 01","x":"id=\"1-4-the-it-integral-and-it-isometry\" data-t=\"1.4 The Itô integral and Itô isometry\">1.4 The Itô integral and Itô isometry ¶ For a predictable integrand with , the Itô integral is defined as the limit of left-endpoint Riemann sums . The left endpoint is not a convention of convenience — it is what makes a martingale, and it is the mathematical image of predictability. Itô isometry This is the workhorse variance calculation. Applied to a trading strategy it says: the variance of your P&L is the expected time-inte","s":1},{"id":"1-4-the-it-integral-and-it-isometry","t":"1.4 The Itô integral and Itô isometry","v":"Vol 01","x":"This is the workhorse variance calculation. Applied to a trading strategy it says: the variance of your P&L is the expected time-integral of your squared position-times-volatility. Every volatility-targeting rule in Volume 13 is a discrete attempt to hold that integrand constant.","s":1},{"id":"1-5-it-s-lemma","t":"1.5 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 01","x":"Let X satisfy dX_t = mu_t dt + sigma_t dW_t and let f(t,x) in C^1,2 . Expanding f to second order and applying (dW)^2 = dt :","s":1},{"id":"1-5-it-s-lemma","t":"1.5 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 01","x":"The multidimensional version, with dX^i = mu^i dt + sum_j sigma^ij dW^j and d[X^i,X^k]_t = ( sigma sigma^ top)^ik dt :","s":1},{"id":"1-5-it-s-lemma","t":"1.5 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 01","x":"Ordinary calculus rests on the assumption that if you zoom in far enough on a curve, it looks like a straight line. Price paths never do — zoom in and they stay exactly as jagged. Itô's lemma is the chain rule repaired for that fact, and the repair is exactly one extra term: the second derivative mu Apply Itô to f(x)= log x with dS/S = mu dt + sigma dW : the drift of log S is mu - tfrac12 sigma^2 , not mu . That - tfrac12 sigma^2 is volatility drag , and it is the reason a strategy with positive expected arithmetic return can have negative expected log return and go broke almost surely. It is the same term that makes leveraged ETFs ","s":"1.5 itô's lemma apply itô to f(x)= log x with ds/s = mu dt + sigma dw : the drift of log s is mu - tfrac12 sigma^2 , not mu . that - tfrac12 sigma^2 is volatility drag , and it is the reason a strategy with positive expected arithmetic return can have negative expected log return and go broke almost surely. it is the same term that makes leveraged etfs d ordinary calculus rests on the assumption that if you zoom in far enough on a curve, it looks like a straight line. price paths never do — zoom in and they stay exactly as jagged. itô's lemma is the chain rule repaired for that fact, and the repair is exactly one extra term: the second derivative multiplied by the variance. almost everything in derivative pricing is a consequence of that single correction. for a smooth path the moves are small enough that the curvature vanishes. for a price path they are not, because the squared moves add up to something finite. that is the entire content of volume 1. a parabola with equal up and down moves from its minimum, both of which raise the function value, showing the curvature term that does not cancel"},{"id":"geometric-brownian-motion","t":"Geometric Brownian motion","v":"Vol 01","x":"dS_t = mu S_t dt + sigma S_t dW_t . Applying Itô to log S and integrating gives the exact solution — note this is exact, so GBM never needs to be discretised approximately:","s":1},{"id":"ornstein-uhlenbeck-the-mean-reversion-workhorse","t":"Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: the mean-reversion workhorse","v":"Vol 01","x":"dX_t = kappa( theta - X_t) dt + sigma dW_t . Multiply by the integrating factor e^ kappa t and note d(e^ kappa tX_t) = e^ kappa t(dX_t + kappa X_t dt) = kappa theta e^ kappa tdt + sigma e^ kappa tdW_t :","s":1},{"id":"ornstein-uhlenbeck-the-mean-reversion-workhorse","t":"Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: the mean-reversion workhorse","v":"Vol 01","x":"By Itô isometry the conditional law is Gaussian with","s":1},{"id":"ornstein-uhlenbeck-the-mean-reversion-workhorse","t":"Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: the mean-reversion workhorse","v":"Vol 01","x":"The half-life of a shock, t_1/2 = ln 2/ kappa , is the single number that decides whether a statistical-arbitrage spread is tradeable: it must be short relative to your capital's patience and long relative to your transaction costs. Volume 3 estimates kappa from data.","s":1},{"id":"arithmetic-brownian-motion","t":"Arithmetic Brownian motion","v":"Vol 01","x":"dX_t = mu dt + sigma dW_t . Trivially X_t sim N(X_0+ mu t, sigma^2 t) . Used for spreads (which can go negative, unlike prices), for the Bachelier option model that fixed-income desks reverted to when rates went negative, and as the base case for first-passage results in Volume 23.","s":1},{"id":"merton-jump-diffusion","t":"Merton jump diffusion","v":"Vol 01","x":"dfracdS_tS_t^- = ( mu - lambda k) dt + sigma dW_t + (Y_t-1) dN_t , where N is a Poisson process of intensity lambda , jump sizes satisfy log Y sim N( mu_J, sigma_J^2) , and k = E[Y-1] compensates the drift. This is the minimum honest model of an equity: continuous diffusion punctuated by gaps you cannot hedge through. Its existence is the","s":1},{"id":"1-7-girsanov-buying-a-drift-change","t":"1.7 Girsanov: buying a drift change","v":"Vol 01","x":"Let Lambda be a predictable process satisfying Novikov's condition E exp tfrac12 int_0^T Lambda_t^2 dt < infty , and define the stochastic exponential","s":1},{"id":"1-7-girsanov-buying-a-drift-change","t":"1.7 Girsanov: buying a drift change","v":"Vol 01","x":"Girsanov answers a strange-sounding question that turns out to be the foundation of option pricing: can you change how likely you consider each future path to be, in a way that alters the price's average drift but leaves its volatility untouched? The answer is yes, at a computable price — and it is  Then widetildeW_t = W_t + int_0^t Lambda_s ds is a Q -Brownian motion. Choosing Lambda = ( mu - r)/ sigma — the Sharpe ratio, i.e. the market price of risk — turns the discounted stock price into a Q -martingale. That is the whole content of risk-neutral pricing: you are not asserting investors are risk-neutral, you are choosing the meas","s":"1.7 girsanov: buying a drift change then widetildew_t = w_t + int_0^t lambda_s ds is a q -brownian motion. choosing lambda = ( mu - r)/ sigma — the sharpe ratio, i.e. the market price of risk — turns the discounted stock price into a q -martingale. that is the whole content of risk-neutral pricing: you are not asserting investors are risk-neutral, you are choosing the measu girsanov answers a strange-sounding question that turns out to be the foundation of option pricing: can you change how likely you consider each future path to be, in a way that alters the price's average drift but leaves its volatility untouched? the answer is yes, at a computable price — and it is the reason an option's value does not depend on whether you personally think the stock is going up."},{"id":"1-8-feynman-kac-sdes-become-pdes","t":"1.8 Feynman–Kac: SDEs become PDEs","v":"Vol 01","x":"If V(t,x) = E^Q [e^-r(T-t) Phi(X_T) middle| X_t=x] with dX = mu(t,X)dt + sigma(t,X)dW^Q , then V solves","s":1},{"id":"1-8-feynman-kac-sdes-become-pdes","t":"1.8 Feynman–Kac: SDEs become PDEs","v":"Vol 01","x":"Substituting GBM coefficients gives the Black–Scholes PDE directly (Volume 8). The equivalence runs both ways and is operationally useful: Monte Carlo when the dimension is high or the payoff is path-dependent, finite differences when the dimension is low and you need stable Greeks or an early-exercise boundary.","s":1},{"id":"1-9-discretisation-and-the-error-you-are-actually-ma","t":"1.9 Discretisation and the error you are actually making","v":"Vol 01","x":"For dX = mu(X)dt + sigma(X)dW , the Euler–Maruyama scheme is","s":1},{"id":"1-9-discretisation-and-the-error-you-are-actually-ma","t":"1.9 Discretisation and the error you are actually making","v":"Vol 01","x":"It has strong order 1/2 (pathwise error E|X_T - X_T^ Delta| = O( Delta t^1/2) ) and weak order 1 (error in E[f(X_T)] is O( Delta t) ). Adding the Milstein correction recovers strong order 1 :","s":1},{"id":"1-9-discretisation-and-the-error-you-are-actually-ma","t":"1.9 Discretisation and the error you are actually making","v":"Vol 01","x":"For plain option pricing you average a payoff, so weak order governs the bias and Euler is usually adequate. For anything path-dependent that you compare pathwise — barrier monitoring, a backtest of a path-dependent strategy, multilevel Monte Carlo — strong order governs and Euler's O(sqrt( Delta t)) will dominate your error budget. Simul","s":1},{"id":"1-10-calibrating-an-ou-process-from-data","t":"1.10 Calibrating an OU process from data","v":"Vol 01","x":"Because the OU transition density is Gaussian, the exact maximum-likelihood estimator reduces to an OLS regression of X_t+ Delta on X_t . If X_t+ Delta = a + b X_t + varepsilon with varepsilon simN(0,s^2) , then","s":1},{"id":"1-10-calibrating-an-ou-process-from-data","t":"1.10 Calibrating an OU process from data","v":"Vol 01","x":"This is exact MLE, not an approximation — a consequence of the OU process being both Gaussian and Markov. The estimator of kappa is nevertheless badly biased upward in short samples, which matters enormously in practice: a spread that is actually a random walk will produce a finite estimated half-life, and you will trade it. Volume 3 pair","s":1},{"id":"1-10-calibrating-an-ou-process-from-data","t":"1.10 Calibrating an OU process from data","v":"Vol 01","x":"Your Openness facets (Imagination 16/20, Intellect 16/20) make this the most seductive volume in the book: it is elegant, self-contained, and infinitely deepenable. It is also the volume with the lowest marginal P&L per hour beyond the level presented here. The honest boundary is this — you need Itô, Girsanov, OU calibration, and discreti","s":1},{"id":"vol2","t":"Market Microstructure and the Limit Order Book","v":"Vol 02","x":"A signal that predicts a 4 bp move is worthless if crossing the spread costs 6 bp. Microstructure is the discipline that tells you which of your alphas survive contact with the exchange. It is also where the majority of retail systematic strategies die — not because the signal was wrong, but because the backtest assumed a fill that the bo","s":1},{"id":"2-1-the-continuous-double-auction","t":"2.1 The continuous double auction","v":"Vol 02","x":"A modern electronic market is a continuous double auction with a price–time priority queue. The limit order book at time t is a pair of sorted ladders,","s":1},{"id":"2-1-the-continuous-double-auction","t":"2.1 The continuous double auction","v":"Vol 02","x":"Before any theory: a market is a queue. Everyone who wants to buy posts the price they will pay; everyone who wants to sell posts the price they will accept. The highest bid and the lowest offer face each other across a gap. You can either wait in the queue at your price and hope someone comes to yo with best bid p^b_1 , best ask p^a_1 , mid M_t= tfrac12(p^b_1+p^a_1) and quoted spread S_t = p^a_1 - p^b_1 . Three order types cover almost all flow: a limit order joins the back of a price queue; a market order crosses and consumes the opposite side from the top; a cancel removes resting quantity. Everything else (iceberg, peg, IOC, FOK","s":"2.1 the continuous double auction with best bid p^b_1 , best ask p^a_1 , mid m_t= tfrac12(p^b_1+p^a_1) and quoted spread s_t = p^a_1 - p^b_1 . three order types cover almost all flow: a limit order joins the back of a price queue; a market order crosses and consumes the opposite side from the top; a cancel removes resting quantity. everything else (iceberg, peg, ioc, fok, before any theory: a market is a queue. everyone who wants to buy posts the price they will pay; everyone who wants to sell posts the price they will accept. the highest bid and the lowest offer face each other across a gap. you can either wait in the queue at your price and hope someone comes to you, or cross the gap and pay for immediacy. every cost in this volume is a consequence of that one choice."},{"id":"the-micro-price-and-why-the-mid-is-a-biased-predicto","t":"The micro-price and why the mid is a biased predictor","v":"Vol 02","x":"The mid ignores the fact that a book with 5,000 shares bid and 100 offered is about to tick up. The size-weighted micro-price corrects this:","s":1},{"id":"the-micro-price-and-why-the-mid-is-a-biased-predicto","t":"The micro-price and why the mid is a biased predictor","v":"Vol 02","x":"The bracketed term is the queue imbalance I_t in[-1,1] . Empirically E[M_t+ delta-M_t mid I_t] is increasing and close to linear in I_t over short horizons, which makes imbalance one of the few genuinely robust high-frequency predictors. Marking a book to the mid rather than the micro-price systematically flatters a market-making backtest","s":1},{"id":"2-2-adverse-selection-glosten-milgrom","t":"2.2 Adverse selection: Glosten–Milgrom","v":"Vol 02","x":"Consider a market maker facing a mix of informed traders (who know the terminal value V in V_L,V_H ) and uninformed noise traders. The maker quotes zero-expected-profit prices conditional on the direction of the incoming order:","s":1},{"id":"2-2-adverse-selection-glosten-milgrom","t":"2.2 Adverse selection: Glosten–Milgrom","v":"Vol 02","x":"Here is the market maker's problem. They offer to buy at one price and sell at a slightly higher one, hoping to earn the difference many times over. But some of the people who accept those offers know something the maker does not, and those people are systematically on the profitable side. The sprea With informed fraction mu and prior pi=P(V=V_H) , Bayes gives a strictly positive spread even with zero inventory cost, zero fees and infinite competition. This is the central result of the field: the spread exists because of information asymmetry, not because of operating costs. It follows that you cannot compete the spread to zero, and","s":"2.2 adverse selection: glosten–milgrom with informed fraction mu and prior pi=p(v=v_h) , bayes gives a strictly positive spread even with zero inventory cost, zero fees and infinite competition. this is the central result of the field: the spread exists because of information asymmetry, not because of operating costs. it follows that you cannot compete the spread to zero, and  here is the market maker's problem. they offer to buy at one price and sell at a slightly higher one, hoping to earn the difference many times over. but some of the people who accept those offers know something the maker does not, and those people are systematically on the profitable side. the spread is therefore not a profit margin — it is a toll charged to everyone, to cover the losses to the few who are informed. it also explains the fill model in volume 16.2. assuming you fill whenever the price touches your level assumes away precisely the traders who make market making hard. a three-box chain: the mix of uninformed and informed traders who take your quote, the resulting small frequent gains against rare large losses, and the conclusion that the spread is a toll"},{"id":"2-3-kyle-s-lambda-and-market-impact","t":"2.3 Kyle's lambda and market impact","v":"Vol 02","x":"Kyle (1985) models a single informed trader submitting order flow x into aggregate flow y = x+u (where u is noise), with a competitive market maker setting a linear price rule P = P_0 + lambda y . Solving the equilibrium gives","s":1},{"id":"2-3-kyle-s-lambda-and-market-impact","t":"2.3 Kyle's lambda and market impact","v":"Vol 02","x":"where Sigma_0=Var(V) and sigma_u^2 is noise-trader variance. lambda is illiquidity : price impact per unit of signed volume, and its reciprocal 1/ lambda is market depth . The estimable version, regressing returns on signed volume, is the standard desk measure:","s":1},{"id":"2-3-kyle-s-lambda-and-market-impact","t":"2.3 Kyle's lambda and market impact","v":"Vol 02","x":"The square-root impact law with Y approx 0.5 – 1 is one of the most robust empirical regularities in finance, holding across asset classes and decades. Note the consequence: impact is concave , so executing 4 times the size costs only about 2 times the impact per share — but it is also super-linear in total cost , since total cost scales ","s":1},{"id":"2-3-kyle-s-lambda-and-market-impact","t":"2.3 Kyle's lambda and market impact","v":"Vol 02","x":"Kyle's model asks what someone who genuinely knew where the price was going would do, and what a market maker who knows such a person exists but cannot identify them would charge. The answer is that price moves in proportion to net order flow, and the constant of proportionality — lambda — has becom id=\"2-3-kyle-s-lambda-and-market-impact\" data-t=\"2.3 Kyle's lambda and market impact\">2.3 Kyle's lambda and market impact ¶ Kyle (1985) models a single informed trader submitting order flow into aggregate flow (where is noise), with a competitive market maker setting a linear price rule . Solving the equilibrium gives where and is noise-","s":"2.3 kyle's lambda and market impact id=\"2-3-kyle-s-lambda-and-market-impact\" data-t=\"2.3 kyle's lambda and market impact\">2.3 kyle's lambda and market impact ¶ kyle (1985) models a single informed trader submitting order flow into aggregate flow (where is noise), with a competitive market maker setting a linear price rule . solving the equilibrium gives where and is noise-trader variance. is illiquidity : price impact per unit of signed volume, and its reciprocal is market depth . the estimable version, regressing returns on signed volume, is the sta kyle's model asks what someone who genuinely knew where the price was going would do, and what a market maker who knows such a person exists but cannot identify them would charge. the answer is that price moves in proportion to net order flow, and the constant of proportionality — lambda — has become the standard measure of how expensive a market is to trade: the price move you cause per unit of net buying."},{"id":"2-4-order-flow-imbalance","t":"2.4 Order Flow Imbalance","v":"Vol 02","x":"Cont, Kukanov and Stoikov (2014) showed that best-quote changes, not trades, drive short-horizon price formation. Define the per-event contribution","s":1},{"id":"2-4-order-flow-imbalance","t":"2.4 Order Flow Imbalance","v":"Vol 02","x":"and aggregate over a bucket k to get OFI_k = sum_n in k e_n . Then","s":1},{"id":"2-4-order-flow-imbalance","t":"2.4 Order Flow Imbalance","v":"Vol 02","x":"with R^2 frequently above 0.65 at 10-second buckets for liquid US equities — extraordinary for a financial regression. Critically, beta scales inversely with depth, so the natural normalisation is OFI_k / barD_k where bar D is average top-of-book depth. This is contemporaneous, not predictive; the tradeable version requires lagging the OF","s":1},{"id":"2-5-avellaneda-stoikov-market-making","t":"2.5 Avellaneda–Stoikov market making","v":"Vol 02","x":"A market maker with inventory q , risk aversion gamma , and horizon T maximises exponential utility of terminal wealth. Solving the HJB equation yields a reservation price — the inventory-adjusted price at which the maker is indifferent to holding:","s":1},{"id":"2-5-avellaneda-stoikov-market-making","t":"2.5 Avellaneda–Stoikov market making","v":"Vol 02","x":"With market-order arrival intensity Lambda( delta) = A e^-k delta at distance delta from the mid, the optimal total spread and its skew are","s":1},{"id":"2-5-avellaneda-stoikov-market-making","t":"2.5 Avellaneda–Stoikov market making","v":"Vol 02","x":"Read the second equation carefully: it is the entire strategy. When you are long ( q>0 ) you quote lower on both sides, making it easier for someone to buy from you and harder to sell to you. Inventory management is not a risk overlay bolted onto market making — it is market making. The first equation says your spread must widen with vola","s":1},{"id":"2-5-avellaneda-stoikov-market-making","t":"2.5 Avellaneda–Stoikov market making","v":"Vol 02","x":"The model assumes your quotes are always at the front of the queue, that fills are independent of information, and that sigma is known. Reality: you are behind a queue whose position you must model, your fills are adversely selected (you get filled precisely when the price is about to move against you — see Glosten–Milgrom above), and sig","s":1},{"id":"2-6-queue-position-the-variable-that-decides-whether","t":"2.6 Queue position: the variable that decides whether you get filled","v":"Vol 02","x":"Under price–time priority, a limit order joining a level with Q_ahead resting shares is filled only after those shares are consumed or cancelled. Model the level's depletion by trades (rate mu ) and cancellations ahead of you (rate nu ). The probability of being filled before the level is wiped by an adverse price move is approximately","s":1},{"id":"2-6-queue-position-the-variable-that-decides-whether","t":"2.6 Queue position: the variable that decides whether you get filled","v":"Vol 02","x":"which decays quickly in Q_ahead . Two operational consequences dominate high-frequency P&L: joining a queue early (when the level is thin) is worth far more than joining a thick level at a better price, and cancel/replace resets your position to the back — so a strategy that re-quotes aggressively on every tick never gets filled on the pa","s":1},{"id":"2-7-hawkes-processes-order-flow-clusters","t":"2.7 Hawkes processes: order flow clusters","v":"Vol 02","x":"Order arrivals are not Poisson — they cluster, because trades beget trades. A mutually exciting Hawkes process captures this with intensity","s":1},{"id":"2-7-hawkes-processes-order-flow-clusters","t":"2.7 Hawkes processes: order flow clusters","v":"Vol 02","x":"where mu_i is the baseline rate and the kernel term is self- and cross-excitation. Stationarity requires the spectral radius of the branching matrix | alpha_ij/ beta_ij | to be below 1; as it approaches 1 the process becomes near-critical and produces the endogenous cascades that look like flash crashes. Fitted branching ratios on equity ","s":1},{"id":"2-8-realistic-cost-accounting","t":"2.8 Realistic cost accounting","v":"Vol 02","x":"Every backtest must price the following, and most retail backtests price none of them:","s":1},{"id":"2-8-realistic-cost-accounting","t":"2.8 Realistic cost accounting","v":"Vol 02","x":"This volume rewards patience with unglamorous detail: queue mechanics, fee schedules, borrow rates. Your Orderliness score (16/20, the highest facet in your profile) is a genuine asset here — building a meticulous cost model is exactly the kind of work you will do well and enjoy. The risk is the opposite one: with Openness facets equally ","s":"2.8 realistic cost accounting this volume rewards patience with unglamorous detail: queue mechanics, fee schedules, borrow rates. your orderliness score (16/20, the highest facet in your profile) is a genuine asset here — building a meticulous cost model is exactly the kind of work you will do well and enjoy. the risk is the opposite one: with openness facets equally  sized for a liquid instrument at retail scale. on a small-cap stock or a single option contract, the spread bars alone are ten to fifty times longer. a ranked bar chart of round-trip trading costs in basis points, with commission smallest and slippage against the decision price and delay largest"},{"id":"vol2a","t":"Impact Beyond Kyle: Propagators, Microprice and Cross-Impact","v":"Vol 2A","x":"Volume 2.3 gives you Kyle's lambda: a linear, permanent, instantaneous impact model. It is the right first model and it is wrong in three specific ways that matter once you trade size. Impact is concave in quantity, not linear. It decays rather than persisting forever. And it is cross-sectional — trading one asset moves correlated assets, which is invisible in any single-name analysis and is the dominant cost for a large portfolio. This volume covers the propagator framework that fixes all three, the microprice as ","s":1},{"id":"2a-1-why-linear-permanent-impact-cannot-be-right","t":"2A.1 Why linear permanent impact cannot be right","v":"Vol 2A","x":">2A.1 Why linear permanent impact cannot be right ¶ Start with an argument that requires no data. Suppose impact is permanent and linear: buying shares moves the price by and it stays moved. Then split the order into child orders of . Total impact is : unchanged. Now buy and immediately sell . Under permanence you have moved the price up by and back down by , at zero cost. That last statement is a round trip in zero time with zero net cost, which is close to an arbitrage and is certainly not what happens. Something","s":1},{"id":"the-square-root-law","t":"The square-root law","v":"Vol 2A","x":">The square-root law ¶ with the daily volatility, the order size, the daily volume, and a dimensionless constant of order one. This is among the most robust empirical regularities in market microstructure: it holds across equities, futures, FX and crypto, across four decades, and across participation rates from to . It has been rediscovered enough times to have several names. Feature of the law Consequence for you Concave in doubling size costs ×, not 2×; large orders are cheaper per share than linear models predic","s":1},{"id":"2a-2-the-propagator-model","t":"2A.2 The propagator model","v":"Vol 2A","x":">2A.2 The propagator model ¶ The fix for permanence is to let each trade's impact decay. The propagator or transient-impact model writes the price as a sum of decaying kicks from every past trade: Here is the sign of the trade at time , its volume, a concave function (typically with ), and the propagator : the kernel describing how a unit kick decays. Empirically is a slowly decaying power law: The one deep fact here Order flow is strongly autocorrelated : the sign of trades has a long-memory autocorrelation decayi","s":1},{"id":"permanent-versus-temporary-and-why-the-split-matte","t":"Permanent versus temporary, and why the split matters","v":"Vol 2A","x":">Permanent versus temporary, and why the split matters ¶ A propagator with has a non-zero long-run limit: some fraction of the impact never decays. Decompose the cost of a metaorder into Permanent impact — the level shift that survives. You pay it once and it is a genuine cost of the position, borne whether you exit or not. Temporary impact — the part that decays after you stop. You pay it only while trading, and it is the part that execution scheduling (Volume 10) can reduce. Empirically, roughly two thirds of pea","s":1},{"id":"2a-3-the-microprice","t":"2A.3 The microprice","v":"Vol 2A","x":">2A.3 The microprice ¶ The mid-price is the obvious state variable and a poor one. When the bid holds 10,000 shares and the ask 100, the next trade is far more likely to lift the ask; the mid is not the market's expectation of the next price. The weighted mid corrects the obvious part: with the spread and the queue imbalance. This is better than the mid and still not a martingale — it over-corrects, because imbalance mean-reverts at short horizons.","s":1},{"id":"stoikov-s-microprice","t":"Stoikov's microprice","v":"Vol 2A","x":">Stoikov's microprice ¶ The microprice is defined as the actual conditional expectation of the future mid, given the current state, in the limit of long horizon: It is estimated by discretising imbalance and spread into states and iterating a dynamic programme until the adjustment converges. The result is a martingale by construction, which is exactly the property that makes it the right anchor for a market-making quote and the right reference price for measuring execution quality. Python Copy import numpy as np im","s":1},{"id":"2a-4-cross-impact","t":"2A.4 Cross-impact","v":"Vol 2A","x":">2A.4 Cross-impact ¶ Trading asset moves asset . For a single-name trader this is a curiosity; for a portfolio it is often the majority of total impact, and it is invisible to every single-name cost model. The natural generalisation replaces the scalar with a matrix: The empirical structure of is close to what a factor model would predict: most cross-impact is transmitted through common factors rather than idiosyncratically. A reasonable and well-behaved parameterisation is with the return covariance and the share ","s":1},{"id":"2a-5-calibrating-impact-from-your-own-fills","t":"2A.5 Calibrating impact from your own fills","v":"Vol 2A","x":">2A.5 Calibrating impact from your own fills ¶ Published parameters are a starting point and not a substitute. Your broker, venue mix, order types and urgency all shift the constant substantially. Once you have a few thousand of your own fills, calibrate. Define the metaorder. Group child fills into parent decisions: same instrument, same sign, gaps below a threshold. Impact is a property of the parent, not the child. Measure impact against the decision-time microprice , not the arrival mid and not the day's VWAP. ","s":1},{"id":"vol3","t":"Statistical Arbitrage, Cointegration and Mean Reversion","v":"Vol 03","x":"Two assets whose prices both wander without bound may nonetheless have a linear combination that does not; if you can establish that combination is stationary, has a short enough half-life, and reverts by more than transaction costs, you have a trade. Every part of that sentence is a hypothesis test, and the discipline of this volume is r","s":1},{"id":"3-1-stationarity-unit-roots-and-the-difference-that-","t":"3.1 Stationarity, unit roots, and the difference that matters","v":"Vol 03","x":"A series is integrated of order d , written I(d) , if it must be differenced d times to become stationary. Asset log-prices are typically I(1) ; log-returns are I(0) . The augmented Dickey–Fuller test regresses","s":1},{"id":"3-1-stationarity-unit-roots-and-the-difference-that-","t":"3.1 Stationarity, unit roots, and the difference that matters","v":"Vol 03","x":"and tests H_0: gamma = 0 (unit root, non-stationary) against H_1: gamma < 0 . The test statistic hat gamma/se( hat gamma) does not follow a t distribution — critical values come from the Dickey–Fuller distribution and are considerably more negative (about -2.86 at 5% for the constant-only case). KPSS reverses the hypotheses ( H_0 : statio","s":1},{"id":"3-1-stationarity-unit-roots-and-the-difference-that-","t":"3.1 Stationarity, unit roots, and the difference that matters","v":"Vol 03","x":"A stationary series is one whose statistical behaviour stays put: same average, same spread, whichever window you look at. Prices are not stationary — they wander off and never return to any particular level, which is exactly why you cannot make money by declaring a price “too high”. The whole of st ADF has notoriously low power against persistent-but-stationary alternatives. With three years of daily data you will fail to reject a unit root for a genuine OU process with a 60-day half-life a large fraction of the time. The asymmetric consequence: a spread you reject is probably genuinely a random walk, but a spread you fail to rejec","s":"3.1 stationarity, unit roots, and the difference that matters adf has notoriously low power against persistent-but-stationary alternatives. with three years of daily data you will fail to reject a unit root for a genuine ou process with a 60-day half-life a large fraction of the time. the asymmetric consequence: a spread you reject is probably genuinely a random walk, but a spread you fail to reject a stationary series is one whose statistical behaviour stays put: same average, same spread, whichever window you look at. prices are not stationary — they wander off and never return to any particular level, which is exactly why you cannot make money by declaring a price “too high”. the whole of statistical arbitrage rests on finding a combination of non-stationary prices that is stationary, and this section is the machinery for telling the two apart."},{"id":"3-2-cointegration-engle-granger-and-johansen","t":"3.2 Cointegration: Engle–Granger and Johansen","v":"Vol 03","x":"Two I(1) series y_1, y_2 are cointegrated if there exists beta such that varepsilon_t = y_1,t - beta y_2,t - alpha is I(0) . Engle–Granger is the two-step estimator: OLS for beta , then ADF on the residual (with adjusted critical values, because beta was estimated). It is simple and it is asymmetric — regressing y_1 on y_2 gives a differe","s":1},{"id":"3-2-cointegration-engle-granger-and-johansen","t":"3.2 Cointegration: Engle–Granger and Johansen","v":"Vol 03","x":"Johansen handles n assets simultaneously and symmetrically by working with the vector error correction model","s":1},{"id":"3-2-cointegration-engle-granger-and-johansen","t":"3.2 Cointegration: Engle–Granger and Johansen","v":"Vol 03","x":"The cointegration rank r = rank( Pi) is the number of independent stationary combinations. beta holds the cointegrating vectors, alpha the speeds of adjustment — how fast each asset corrects toward equilibrium. The trace statistic tests r le r_0 sequentially:","s":1},{"id":"3-2-cointegration-engle-granger-and-johansen","t":"3.2 Cointegration: Engle–Granger and Johansen","v":"Vol 03","x":"Two prices can each wander without limit while the gap between them stays tethered. That is cointegration, and it is the entire foundation of pairs trading. The test asks whether what is left over after regressing one price on the other behaves like something that returns to its average, rather than Johansen's advantages are real: it finds baskets, gives a proper likelihood-ratio test, and the alpha vector tells you which leg does the correcting , which matters for execution — you want to be passive in the leg that adjusts slowly.","s":"3.2 cointegration: engle–granger and johansen johansen's advantages are real: it finds baskets, gives a proper likelihood-ratio test, and the alpha vector tells you which leg does the correcting , which matters for execution — you want to be passive in the leg that adjusts slowly. two prices can each wander without limit while the gap between them stays tethered. that is cointegration, and it is the entire foundation of pairs trading. the test asks whether what is left over after regressing one price on the other behaves like something that returns to its average, rather than something that wanders off on its own account."},{"id":"3-3-from-spread-to-trade-the-ou-layer","t":"3.3 From spread to trade: the OU layer","v":"Vol 03","x":"Fit the spread varepsilon_t to an OU process (Volume 1.10) to get kappa , theta , sigma , and hence the equilibrium standard deviation sigma_eq = sigma/sqrt(2 kappa) and the half-life ln 2/ kappa . The z -score is","s":1},{"id":"3-3-from-spread-to-trade-the-ou-layer","t":"3.3 From spread to trade: the OU layer","v":"Vol 03","x":"and the canonical rule enters at |z| > z_in against the deviation, exits at |z| < z_out , and stops at |z| > z_stop . Note that a stop-loss on a mean-reverting spread is a strange object: you are exiting precisely where the model says the edge is largest. The stop is not there because the model says so, it is there because the model may b","s":"3.3 from spread to trade: the ou layer and the canonical rule enters at |z| > z_in against the deviation, exits at |z| < z_out , and stops at |z| > z_stop . note that a stop-loss on a mean-reverting spread is a strange object: you are exiting precisely where the model says the edge is largest. the stop is not there because the model says so, it is there because the model may b"},{"id":"3-3-from-spread-to-trade-the-ou-layer","t":"3.3 From spread to trade: the OU layer","v":"Vol 03","x":"For an OU spread with proportional cost c per round trip, maximising expected profit per unit time over entry level a (symmetric, exit at the mean) trades off larger profit per trade against a lower trade frequency. The expected profit per trade is a sigma_eq - c , and the expected round-trip time grows roughly like the first-passage time","s":"3.3 from spread to trade: the ou layer for an ou spread with proportional cost c per round trip, maximising expected profit per unit time over entry level a (symmetric, exit at the mean) trades off larger profit per trade against a lower trade frequency. the expected profit per trade is a sigma_eq - c , and the expected round-trip time grows roughly like the first-passage time volume 3.6 lists the three ways this stops working. all three look identical to a good opportunity at the moment they begin. a mean-reverting spread oscillating around zero with entry bands at plus and minus two standard deviations, marked where positions are opened and closed"},{"id":"3-4-dynamic-hedge-ratios-with-the-kalman-filter","t":"3.4 Dynamic hedge ratios with the Kalman filter","v":"Vol 03","x":"A fixed beta from a rolling OLS window is a crude instrument: it lags, it jumps when the window rolls off an outlier, and the window length is a free parameter you will overfit. Model beta as a random walk and filter it instead. State and observation equations:","s":1},{"id":"3-4-dynamic-hedge-ratios-with-the-kalman-filter","t":"3.4 Dynamic hedge ratios with the Kalman filter","v":"Vol 03","x":"The filter recursion is predict, then correct:","s":1},{"id":"3-4-dynamic-hedge-ratios-with-the-kalman-filter","t":"3.4 Dynamic hedge ratios with the Kalman filter","v":"Vol 03","x":"The innovation e_t is the trading spread, and sqrt(S_t) is its model-implied standard deviation, so the z -score e_t/sqrt(S_t) comes out of the filter for free and is automatically volatility-adjusted. The ratio Q/R is the only real parameter: it sets how fast beta is allowed to move. Large Q/R tracks regime changes but eats your signal (","s":1},{"id":"3-5-selecting-pairs-without-discovering-noise","t":"3.5 Selecting pairs without discovering noise","v":"Vol 03","x":"Screening all pairs in an N -name universe means binomN2 tests. For N=500 that is 124,750 hypotheses; at a 5% level you expect roughly 6,200 spurious “cointegrated” pairs by chance alone. This is not a subtlety — it is the single most common way statistical arbitrage research produces strategies that die instantly out of sample.","s":1},{"id":"3-5-selecting-pairs-without-discovering-noise","t":"3.5 Selecting pairs without discovering noise","v":"Vol 03","x":"Three defences, in order of effectiveness:","s":1},{"id":"3-5-selecting-pairs-without-discovering-noise","t":"3.5 Selecting pairs without discovering noise","v":"Vol 03","x":"Restrict the search space on economic grounds before testing. Same sub-industry, shared supply chain, dual-listed share classes, ETF versus its own constituents, futures calendar spreads. This reduces binomN2 by orders of magnitude and — more importantly — supplies a reason for reversion that can be checked when the spread breaks. Control","s":1},{"id":"3-6-why-cointegration-breaks","t":"3.6 Why cointegration breaks","v":"Vol 03","x":"A spread does not usually decay gracefully; it breaks. The causes are almost always structural and almost always knowable in advance if you were watching:","s":1},{"id":"3-6-why-cointegration-breaks","t":"3.6 Why cointegration breaks","v":"Vol 03","x":"A spread at z=3 looks better than at z=2 , so the model says add. A spread at z=5 looks better still. Scaling into a diverging spread is mathematically correct if the model is right and catastrophic if it is not — and the situations in which a spread reaches z=5 are precisely the situations in which the model is most likely to be wrong, b","s":1},{"id":"3-6-why-cointegration-breaks","t":"3.6 Why cointegration breaks","v":"Vol 03","x":"Statistical arbitrage is the strategy class whose failure mode maps most exactly onto your profile. It asks you to hold a losing position that the model says is getting better, under uncertainty, for days — which is a direct load on Anxiety (16/20) and Vulnerability (13/20). And its characteristic blow-up is adding to a loser , which is a","s":1},{"id":"vol4","t":"Time-Series Econometrics, GARCH and Regime Switching","v":"Vol 04","x":"Daily equity returns are close to unforecastable in the mean: the best linear models explain well under 1% of variance, and the honest estimate of tomorrow's expected return is “approximately the risk premium”. Volatility is a different matter — it is strongly autocorrelated, and R^2 of 0.4–0.6 for one-day-ahead realised variance is routi","s":1},{"id":"4-1-the-stylised-facts-any-model-must-reproduce","t":"4.1 The stylised facts any model must reproduce","v":"Vol 04","x":"Returns are approximately unpredictable — autocorrelation of r_t is near zero beyond microstructure effects at very short horizons. Volatility clusters — |r_t| and r_t^2 have slowly decaying, positive autocorrelation out to hundreds of lags. Returns are fat-tailed — unconditional kurtosis far above 3; the tail index is typically between 3","s":"4.1 the stylised facts any model must reproduce returns are approximately unpredictable — autocorrelation of r_t is near zero beyond microstructure effects at very short horizons. volatility clusters — |r_t| and r_t^2 have slowly decaying, positive autocorrelation out to hundreds of lags. returns are fat-tailed — unconditional kurtosis far above 3; the tail index is typically between 3 this is the entire commercial content of volume 4, and the reason volatility targeting in volume 13.2 does most of the work in trend following. a daily return series with visible calm and violent stretches, and beneath it the same returns squared and smoothed, forming a clear persistent signal"},{"id":"4-2-arma-and-why-it-rarely-helps-for-returns","t":"4.2 ARMA and why it rarely helps for returns","v":"Vol 04","x":"An ARMA(p,q) model is phi(L) y_t = theta(L) varepsilon_t with lag polynomials phi(L) = 1- sum phi_i L^i and theta(L)=1+ sum theta_j L^j . Stationarity requires the roots of phi(z)=0 to lie outside the unit circle; invertibility requires the same of theta(z) . Selection by AIC ( -2 ell + 2k ) or BIC ( -2 ell + k ln T ) — BIC is the more co","s":1},{"id":"4-2-arma-and-why-it-rarely-helps-for-returns","t":"4.2 ARMA and why it rarely helps for returns","v":"Vol 04","x":"Applied to daily equity returns, ARMA finds tiny coefficients that are unstable across subsamples. It becomes genuinely useful in two places: intraday returns at horizons where microstructure creates real autocorrelation, and — much more importantly — as the mean equation whose residuals feed the variance model below.","s":1},{"id":"4-3-garch-and-its-asymmetric-extensions","t":"4.3 GARCH and its asymmetric extensions","v":"Vol 04","x":"GARCH(1,1) : with r_t = mu + varepsilon_t , varepsilon_t = sigma_t z_t , z_t sim i.i.d.(0,1) ,","s":1},{"id":"4-3-garch-and-its-asymmetric-extensions","t":"4.3 GARCH and its asymmetric extensions","v":"Vol 04","x":"Covariance stationarity requires alpha+ beta<1 , giving unconditional variance bar sigma^2 = omega/(1- alpha- beta) . Typical daily equity estimates are alpha approx0.06 , beta approx0.92 , so alpha+ beta approx0.98 — extremely persistent, with a shock half-life of ln 2/(- ln( alpha+ beta)) approx 34 days. The multi-step forecast mean-rev","s":1},{"id":"4-3-garch-and-its-asymmetric-extensions","t":"4.3 GARCH and its asymmetric extensions","v":"Vol 04","x":"GJR-GARCH adds the leverage effect with an indicator on negative shocks:","s":1},{"id":"4-3-garch-and-its-asymmetric-extensions","t":"4.3 GARCH and its asymmetric extensions","v":"Vol 04","x":"with gamma>0 almost universally in equity data. EGARCH models ln sigma_t^2 , which removes the positivity constraints on parameters entirely:","s":1},{"id":"4-3-garch-and-its-asymmetric-extensions","t":"4.3 GARCH and its asymmetric extensions","v":"Vol 04","x":"GARCH is one idea wearing notation: today's expected volatility is a weighted blend of three things — a long-run average, yesterday's forecast, and how surprised you were yesterday. That is the whole model; the parameters only say how much weight each of the three receives. It works because markets  GARCH reacts to yesterday's squared return, so it is structurally late to a volatility regime change — exactly when you most need it. Its value in a trading system is comparative, not absolute: it reliably tells you that today is a higher-volatility day than last Tuesday, which is enough to scale positions. Do not use a one-day GARCH for","s":"4.3 garch and its asymmetric extensions garch reacts to yesterday's squared return, so it is structurally late to a volatility regime change — exactly when you most need it. its value in a trading system is comparative, not absolute: it reliably tells you that today is a higher-volatility day than last tuesday, which is enough to scale positions. do not use a one-day garch fore garch is one idea wearing notation: today's expected volatility is a weighted blend of three things — a long-run average, yesterday's forecast, and how surprised you were yesterday. that is the whole model; the parameters only say how much weight each of the three receives. it works because markets genuinely do have calm stretches and violent stretches rather than a constant level of activity."},{"id":"4-4-estimation-quasi-maximum-likelihood","t":"4.4 Estimation: quasi-maximum likelihood","v":"Vol 04","x":"Even when z_t is not Gaussian, the Gaussian QMLE is consistent for the variance parameters provided the variance equation is correctly specified — this is the Bollerslev–Wooldridge result, and it is why Gaussian GARCH remains the default despite everyone knowing returns are fat-tailed. Standard errors must be computed from the sandwich es","s":1},{"id":"4-5-realised-volatility-and-the-har-model","t":"4.5 Realised volatility and the HAR model","v":"Vol 04","x":"With intraday data you can measure rather than infer volatility. Realised variance over day d from M intraday returns is RV_d = sum_i=1^M r_d,i^2 , a consistent estimator of the day's quadratic variation as M to infty — in the absence of microstructure noise. In its presence, RV diverges as sampling frequency rises, which produces the cla","s":1},{"id":"4-5-realised-volatility-and-the-har-model","t":"4.5 Realised volatility and the HAR model","v":"Vol 04","x":"Corsi's HAR-RV model is a heterogeneous autoregression that captures long memory with three coefficients:","s":1},{"id":"4-5-realised-volatility-and-the-har-model","t":"4.5 Realised volatility and the HAR model","v":"Vol 04","x":"where RV^(h) is the average over the past h days. It is a linear regression, it takes five lines of code, and it beats GARCH on out-of-sample realised-variance forecasting almost everywhere. Fitting it in logs is standard and improves the residual behaviour considerably. The HAR-RV-J extension separates the jump component using bipower va","s":1},{"id":"4-6-markov-regime-switching","t":"4.6 Markov regime switching","v":"Vol 04","x":"Hamilton's model lets parameters depend on an unobserved state S_t in 1, dots,K following a Markov chain with transition matrix P_ij = P(S_t=j mid S_t-1=i) :","s":1},{"id":"4-6-markov-regime-switching","t":"4.6 Markov regime switching","v":"Vol 04","x":"The Hamilton filter gives P(S_t = k mid F_t) recursively; the Kim smoother gives the two-sided estimate. A two-state fit to equity index returns almost always recovers the same structure: a high-probability, low-volatility, mildly-positive-drift state with expected duration 1/(1-P_11) of many months, and a low-probability, high-volatility","s":1},{"id":"4-6-markov-regime-switching","t":"4.6 Markov regime switching","v":"Vol 04","x":"The filtered probability P(S_t = crisis mid F_t) rises after volatility rises. It is a sophisticated coincident indicator, not a leading one. Systems that de-risk on filtered crisis probability sell after the drawdown and buy back after the recovery — a systematic negative edge that is easy to miss in a backtest fitted on the same sample ","s":1},{"id":"4-7-vector-autoregressions-and-granger-causality","t":"4.7 Vector autoregressions and Granger causality","v":"Vol 04","x":"A VAR( p ) treats n series symmetrically: y_t = c + sum_i=1^p A_i y_t-i + varepsilon_t . Series x Granger-causes y if lagged x improves the forecast of y beyond lagged y alone — an F -test on the joint significance of x 's lags.","s":1},{"id":"4-7-vector-autoregressions-and-granger-causality","t":"4.7 Vector autoregressions and Granger causality","v":"Vol 04","x":"Two cautions. First, the name is misleading: Granger causality is predictive precedence, not causation, and a common driver observed with different lags produces it trivially. Second, if the series are I(1) and cointegrated, a VAR in levels is misspecified and a VAR in differences discards the long-run relationship — you need the VECM of ","s":1},{"id":"4-8-choosing-a-model-for-a-purpose","t":"4.8 Choosing a model for a purpose","v":"Vol 04","x":"Volatility forecasting is one of the few places in this book where meticulous, repetitive, careful work has a direct and durable payoff, and where the models are stable enough to reward maintenance rather than novelty. That makes it an unusually good fit for you. It is also the model layer that makes every other strategy in this book less","s":1},{"id":"vol4a","t":"Bayesian Inference, MCMC and State-Space Models","v":"Vol 4A","x":"Volume 4 fits models by maximum likelihood and reports point estimates with standard errors. That is the right default and it hides something important: in finance the parameter uncertainty is often as large as the parameter, and a decision made from the point estimate alone throws away the fact. This volume covers three connected things. Bayesian inference , which carries the whole posterior instead of its peak. MCMC , which makes that computable for models with no closed form. And state-space models , where the o","s":1},{"id":"4a-1-why-a-posterior-beats-a-point-estimate-here","t":"4A.1 Why a posterior beats a point estimate here","v":"Vol 4A","x":"A point estimate says “the half-life is 14 days”. A posterior says “the half-life is probably between 6 and 40 days, most likely near 14”. In finance the second sentence is almost always the honest one, because the uncertainty around a parameter is routinely as large as the parameter itself. Sizing  >4A.1 Why a posterior beats a point estimate here ¶ The practical case for the Bayesian framing in finance is not philosophical. It is that financial data is short relative to the number of things you want to estimate, so the likelihood alone is often close to flat in the directions that matter, and a flat likelihood maximised is a numbe","s":"4a.1 why a posterior beats a point estimate here >4a.1 why a posterior beats a point estimate here ¶ the practical case for the bayesian framing in finance is not philosophical. it is that financial data is short relative to the number of things you want to estimate, so the likelihood alone is often close to flat in the directions that matter, and a flat likelihood maximised is a number with no content. four concrete consequences: situation point estimate says posterior says sharpe from 2 years of data 1.4 1.4 ± 0.7; expected returns for optimisation a vector, tr a point estimate says “the half-life is 14 days”. a posterior says “the half-life is probably between 6 and 40 days, most likely near 14”. in finance the second sentence is almost always the honest one, because the uncertainty around a parameter is routinely as large as the parameter itself. sizing a position on the first sentence when the second is true is how a strategy ends up several times larger than its evidence supports. sizing on the peak while the width is this large is how a position ends up several times larger than the evidence supports. a prior density, a likelihood from data, and the narrower posterior that combines them, with the single point estimate marked at the posterior's peak"},{"id":"conjugate-updates-which-cover-more-than-you-expect","t":"Conjugate updates, which cover more than you expect","v":"Vol 4A","x":">Conjugate updates, which cover more than you expect ¶ Likelihood Conjugate prior Posterior update Bernoulli Beta Normal, known Normal precision-weighted average of prior and data Normal, unknown Normal-Inverse-Gamma closed form; the basis of Bayesian regression Poisson Gamma Multivariate normal Normal-Inverse-Wishart the natural prior for a covariance matrix Python Copy import numpy as np from scipy import stats def hit_rate_posterior (wins, losses, prior_a= 8.0 , prior_b= 8.0 ): \"\"\"Beta-Bernoulli posterior for a ","s":1},{"id":"4a-2-mcmc-in-the-amount-of-detail-you-need","t":"4A.2 MCMC, in the amount of detail you need","v":"Vol 4A","x":">4A.2 MCMC, in the amount of detail you need ¶ When there is no conjugate form — which is most interesting models — you sample from the posterior instead of solving for it. Markov chain Monte Carlo constructs a chain whose stationary distribution is the posterior; run it long enough and the samples are draws from . Algorithm How it moves Use when Metropolis–Hastings propose, accept with probability low dimension; a baseline you can write yourself Gibbs sample each parameter from its full conditional conditionals ar","s":1},{"id":"4a-3-state-space-models-and-filtering","t":"4A.3 State-space models and filtering","v":"Vol 4A","x":">4A.3 State-space models and filtering ¶ A state-space model separates an unobserved state from the noisy measurements of it: This is a startlingly general template. Some things it is: Model State Observation Time-varying beta the current hedge ratio the pair of returns Fair value of a spread the unobserved equilibrium level the traded spread Local level / trend the underlying level and slope the noisy price Stochastic volatility log variance squared returns Regime switching a discrete regime label returns (HMM, 4A","s":1},{"id":"4a-4-hidden-markov-models-and-regimes","t":"4A.4 Hidden Markov models and regimes","v":"Vol 4A","x":">4A.4 Hidden Markov models and regimes ¶ When the state is discrete rather than continuous, the state-space model becomes a hidden Markov model: a latent regime evolving by a transition matrix , with returns drawn from a regime-specific distribution. The forward–backward algorithm gives the exact posterior over regimes and the Baum–Welch algorithm (an EM special case) fits the parameters. Two regimes fitted to equity returns reliably recover something like a calm regime (low volatility, slight positive drift, high ","s":1},{"id":"vol5","t":"Cross-Sectional Alpha and Factor Modelling","v":"Vol 05","x":"Time-series strategies ask “is this asset going up?” Cross-sectional strategies ask “which of these assets will beat the others?” The second question is easier, because market direction — the largest and least predictable component — cancels out of a dollar-neutral long/short book. Volume 22's 101 alphas are all answers to the second ques","s":1},{"id":"5-1-the-linear-factor-model","t":"5.1 The linear factor model","v":"Vol 05","x":"Under Arbitrage Pricing Theory, asset returns decompose into common factor exposures and idiosyncratic residuals:","s":1},{"id":"5-1-the-linear-factor-model","t":"5.1 The linear factor model","v":"Vol 05","x":"with B inR^N times K the loading matrix. The covariance structure follows:","s":1},{"id":"5-1-the-linear-factor-model","t":"5.1 The linear factor model","v":"Vol 05","x":"Most of what any single stock does is explained by a handful of things that move whole groups of stocks at once — the market itself, company size, valuation, recent momentum. Strip those out and what remains is specific to that company. A factor model is the arithmetic of that split, and it matters  This is the single most valuable identity in portfolio construction. Estimating Sigma directly for N=1000 assets requires 500,500 parameters from perhaps 500 observations — hopeless, and the resulting matrix is singular. The factor form needs only NK + K(K+1)/2 + N parameters. For K=20 that is about 21,000 — still large, but tractable an","s":"5.1 the linear factor model this is the single most valuable identity in portfolio construction. estimating sigma directly for n=1000 assets requires 500,500 parameters from perhaps 500 observations — hopeless, and the resulting matrix is singular. the factor form needs only nk + k(k+1)/2 + n parameters. for k=20 that is about 21,000 — still large, but tractable and most of what any single stock does is explained by a handful of things that move whole groups of stocks at once — the market itself, company size, valuation, recent momentum. strip those out and what remains is specific to that company. a factor model is the arithmetic of that split, and it matters because you generally want to be paid for the leftover part while holding as little of the common part as you can manage. a strategy that looks profitable and turns out to be four-fifths market exposure has not found an edge; it has found leverage with extra steps. a stock's nine percent monthly return broken into market, size, value and momentum contributions plus a residual, with the common factors bracketed as risk that can be bought cheaply"},{"id":"5-2-signal-hygiene-the-pipeline-every-alpha-must-pas","t":"5.2 Signal hygiene: the pipeline every alpha must pass through","v":"Vol 05","x":"A raw signal is never traded. The standard pipeline, in order, and the reason for each step:","s":1},{"id":"5-2-signal-hygiene-the-pipeline-every-alpha-must-pas","t":"5.2 Signal hygiene: the pipeline every alpha must pass through","v":"Vol 05","x":"Point-in-time alignment. Every input must be lagged to when it was actually knowable — fundamentals to the filing date, not the fiscal period end; index membership as of that date, not today's. This is the single largest source of fake alpha in published research. Universe filter. Drop names below a liquidity floor and a price floor befor","s":1},{"id":"neutralisation-is-a-projection","t":"Neutralisation is a projection","v":"Vol 05","x":"To remove exposure to a set of risk factors X (industry dummies, size, beta), project the signal onto the orthogonal complement of their column space:","s":1},{"id":"neutralisation-is-a-projection","t":"Neutralisation is a projection","v":"Vol 05","x":"M_X is the residual-maker matrix; the operation is exactly the residual of a cross-sectional regression of z on X . Industry neutralisation is the special case where X is a matrix of group dummies, which reduces to demeaning within each group — this is precisely what the IndNeutralize operator in Volume 22 does.","s":1},{"id":"5-3-evaluating-a-signal-before-you-ever-backtest-it","t":"5.3 Evaluating a signal before you ever backtest it","v":"Vol 05","x":"The information coefficient is the cross-sectional correlation between the signal at t and the forward return over [t, t+h] , computed each period:","s":1},{"id":"5-3-evaluating-a-signal-before-you-ever-backtest-it","t":"5.3 Evaluating a signal before you ever backtest it","v":"Vol 05","x":"Use Spearman (rank IC) unless you have a reason not to. What matters is not a single IC but its distribution over time: the information ratio of the signal is IR = overlineIC/ sigma(IC) . Realistic daily rank ICs for equity cross-sections are 0.01–0.05; anything above 0.10 sustained over years should be assumed to be a bug until proven ot","s":1},{"id":"5-3-evaluating-a-signal-before-you-ever-backtest-it","t":"5.3 Evaluating a signal before you ever backtest it","v":"Vol 05","x":"IR approx IC cdotsqrt(BR) cdotTC , where BR is breadth (the number of independent bets per year) and TC is the transfer coefficient (how much of the signal survives constraints and costs). The sqrt(BR) term is why cross-sectional equity works at all: IC=0.03 sounds like noise, but with 500 names traded daily, and even allowing generously ","s":1},{"id":"diagnostics-that-catch-problems-a-sharpe-ratio-hides","t":"Diagnostics that catch problems a Sharpe ratio hides","v":"Vol 05","x":"IC decay curve. Plot mean IC against forward horizon h = 1,2,5,10,20 days. A signal peaking at h=1 and dead by h=3 is a microstructure effect you cannot trade after costs. A slowly decaying curve means you can trade at lower turnover. Quantile monotonicity. Sort into deciles and plot mean forward return. You want monotone. A signal whose ","s":1},{"id":"5-4-the-canonical-factors-and-what-is-left-of-them","t":"5.4 The canonical factors, and what is left of them","v":"Vol 05","x":"Several hundred published equity factors exist. Systematic replication studies have found that a large fraction do not survive out-of-sample testing, a further tranche shrink sharply after publication, and much of the remainder are variants of the handful above. The methodological response is a higher significance bar for new factors — a ","s":1},{"id":"5-5-from-signal-to-portfolio","t":"5.5 From signal to portfolio","v":"Vol 05","x":"The naive mapping — weights proportional to the normalised signal — is only optimal under strong assumptions (equal idiosyncratic variance, no costs, no constraints). The mean–variance-consistent version weights by signal and risk:","s":1},{"id":"5-5-from-signal-to-portfolio","t":"5.5 From signal to portfolio","v":"Vol 05","x":"where z is the normalised signal and sigma the vector of asset volatilities — this is Grinold's forecasting rule, mu_i = IC cdot sigma_i cdot z_i , which converts a unitless score into an expected-return estimate with the right scale. Volume 11 handles the estimation of Sigma^-1 , which is where this becomes dangerous. Volume 10 handles t","s":1},{"id":"5-5-from-signal-to-portfolio","t":"5.5 From signal to portfolio","v":"Vol 05","x":"The construction side of this volume — pipelines, point-in-time discipline, neutralisation, diagnostic batteries — plays to Orderliness 16/20 and is genuinely where careful people beat clever ones. The discovery side is a hazard: searching for new factors is an unbounded, highly stimulating activity with an enormous multiple-testing probl","s":1},{"id":"vol6","t":"Financial Machine Learning: Labels, Leakage and Validation","v":"Vol 06","x":"Off-the-shelf machine learning assumes i.i.d. observations, a stationary data-generating process, and a label that is well defined. Financial data violates all three: observations overlap in time, the process changes regime, and “did the price go up?” depends entirely on the horizon and the path. Most of this volume is about repairing tho","s":1},{"id":"6-1-structuring-data-information-driven-bars","t":"6.1 Structuring data: information-driven bars","v":"Vol 06","x":"Time bars sample the market on a clock, which is the wrong clock: market activity is wildly non-uniform, so a 1-minute bar at 09:31 contains an order of magnitude more information than one at 12:31. Consequences are concrete — time-bar returns have worse normality and stronger serial correlation in variance than alternatives.","s":1},{"id":"6-2-labelling-the-triple-barrier-method","t":"6.2 Labelling: the triple-barrier method","v":"Vol 06","x":"“The return over the next 5 days” is a poor label because it ignores the path: a position that hits a stop on day 2 never sees the day-5 return. The triple-barrier method labels each observation by which of three barriers is touched first — an upper profit-taking barrier, a lower stop-loss barrier, and a vertical time barrier.","s":1},{"id":"6-2-labelling-the-triple-barrier-method","t":"6.2 Labelling: the triple-barrier method","v":"Vol 06","x":"The horizontal barriers should be set in units of local volatility, not in fixed percentages, so that the label means the same thing in calm and volatile regimes:","s":1},{"id":"6-3-meta-labelling-separate-direction-from-size","t":"6.3 Meta-labelling: separate direction from size","v":"Vol 06","x":"Meta-labelling splits the problem in two. A primary model (which may be a simple rule, a technical signal, or a human view) decides direction . A secondary ML model then predicts, for each primary signal, whether it will be profitable — a binary label of 1 for a true positive and 0 for a false positive. The secondary model's predicted pro","s":1},{"id":"6-3-meta-labelling-separate-direction-from-size","t":"6.3 Meta-labelling: separate direction from size","v":"Vol 06","x":"The benefits are structural, not incidental:","s":1},{"id":"6-3-meta-labelling-separate-direction-from-size","t":"6.3 Meta-labelling: separate direction from size","v":"Vol 06","x":"The secondary problem is binary and balanced-ish, which is far easier to learn than a three-class direction problem with near-zero signal. Precision and recall become directly meaningful: the meta-model raises precision (fewer bad trades) at some cost in recall (fewer trades overall), and the F1 trade-off maps onto a real economic trade-o","s":1},{"id":"6-3-meta-labelling-separate-direction-from-size","t":"6.3 Meta-labelling: separate direction from size","v":"Vol 06","x":"A natural sizing map from probability p to position, using the z -score of the probability above the 1/K threshold for K classes:","s":1},{"id":"6-4-fractional-differentiation-stationarity-without-","t":"6.4 Fractional differentiation: stationarity without amnesia","v":"Vol 06","x":"Prices are non-stationary; returns are stationary but have discarded all memory of level. Fractional differentiation interpolates. For d in (0,1) , expand (1-L)^d as a binomial series:","s":1},{"id":"6-4-fractional-differentiation-stationarity-without-","t":"6.4 Fractional differentiation: stationarity without amnesia","v":"Vol 06","x":"Choose the smallest d for which the ADF test rejects a unit root. For most equity price series that d is around 0.3–0.5, and the resulting series retains a correlation above 0.9 with the original price level while being stationary — whereas d=1 (plain returns) has essentially zero correlation with the level. The weights decay hyperbolical","s":"6.4 fractional differentiation: stationarity without amnesia choose the smallest d for which the adf test rejects a unit root. for most equity price series that d is around 0.3–0.5, and the resulting series retains a correlation above 0.9 with the original price level while being stationary — whereas d=1 (plain returns) has essentially zero correlation with the level. the weights decay hyperbolical the window exists because the two requirements decay at different rates. nothing else in financial machine learning gives you something for so little. two curves against differencing order: memory retained falls exponentially while a stationarity test passes sharply near an order of four tenths, leaving a window where both hold"},{"id":"6-5-the-validation-problem-and-why-it-is-the-whole-b","t":"6.5 The validation problem, and why it is the whole ballgame","v":"Vol 06","x":"Standard k -fold cross-validation is invalid on financial data for two independent reasons, and both must be fixed:","s":1},{"id":"6-5-the-validation-problem-and-why-it-is-the-whole-b","t":"6.5 The validation problem, and why it is the whole ballgame","v":"Vol 06","x":"Leakage through overlapping labels. A label at t spanning [t, t+5] shares information with a label at t+2 . Putting one in train and the other in test leaks the answer. Leakage through serial correlation. Features near the train/test boundary are highly correlated with those just across it, even without overlapping labels.","s":1},{"id":"6-5-the-validation-problem-and-why-it-is-the-whole-b","t":"6.5 The validation problem, and why it is the whole ballgame","v":"Vol 06","x":"This section decides whether machine learning helps you or ruins you. Ordinary cross-validation assumes each observation is independent, so hiding some from the model gives an honest test. Financial observations overlap in time and share information, so a model tested this way has usually already se The fix is purging — dropping from the training set any observation whose label interval overlaps the test interval — plus an embargo , dropping a further small fraction of observations immediately after the test set. Combined:","s":"6.5 the validation problem, and why it is the whole ballgame the fix is purging — dropping from the training set any observation whose label interval overlaps the test interval — plus an embargo , dropping a further small fraction of observations immediately after the test set. combined: this section decides whether machine learning helps you or ruins you. ordinary cross-validation assumes each observation is independent, so hiding some from the model gives an honest test. financial observations overlap in time and share information, so a model tested this way has usually already seen the answer — through a neighbour. almost every spectacular machine-learning backtest is this one bug."},{"id":"6-6-sample-weights-not-every-observation-is-worth-th","t":"6.6 Sample weights: not every observation is worth the same","v":"Vol 06","x":"Overlapping labels mean observations are not independent, so they should not receive equal weight. Define the uniqueness of observation i as the average, over the bars its label spans, of the reciprocal of the number of concurrent labels:","s":1},{"id":"6-6-sample-weights-not-every-observation-is-worth-th","t":"6.6 Sample weights: not every observation is worth the same","v":"Vol 06","x":"Weight training samples by bar u_i , optionally multiplied by |r_i| (attribution weighting, so that large moves matter more) and by a time-decay factor. Sequential bootstrap draws samples with probability proportional to uniqueness, producing bags that are far closer to independent than a standard bootstrap.","s":1},{"id":"6-7-feature-importance-without-fooling-yourself","t":"6.7 Feature importance without fooling yourself","v":"Vol 06","x":"Run MDA and clustered MDA on purged folds. If a feature is important in MDI but not in MDA, it is memorising, not predicting.","s":1},{"id":"6-7-feature-importance-without-fooling-yourself","t":"6.7 Feature importance without fooling yourself","v":"Vol 06","x":"Suppose you test 50 model configurations on the same data. Under the null of no skill, the expected maximum Sharpe among 50 independent trials with T=1000 daily observations (just under four years) is about 1.14 annualised — purely by chance. Reporting the best of 50 as if it were the only test is therefore a systematic upward bias of rou","s":1},{"id":"6-7-feature-importance-without-fooling-yourself","t":"6.7 Feature importance without fooling yourself","v":"Vol 06","x":"The specific hazard here is not laziness — it is the opposite. High Intellect and Imagination (both 16/20) generate model variants faster than any honest validation budget can absorb, and Orderliness (16/20) makes it pleasant to run all of them carefully. Careful execution of an overfitting procedure is still overfitting. The countermeasu","s":1},{"id":"vol7","t":"Deep Learning, NLP and Language Models for Market Signals","v":"Vol 07","x":"Deep learning has a poor record on raw price prediction and a good record on representation : turning unstructured input (text, filings, transcripts, images, graphs) into a number you can put in a linear model. The reason is sample size. A decade of daily bars on 500 names is 1.26 million observations with a signal-to-noise ratio near zer","s":1},{"id":"scaled-dot-product-attention","t":"Scaled dot-product attention","v":"Vol 07","x":"The core operation, for queries Q , keys K and values V :","s":1},{"id":"scaled-dot-product-attention","t":"Scaled dot-product attention","v":"Vol 07","x":"The sqrt(d_k) divisor exists because the dot product of two independent d_k -dimensional unit-variance vectors has variance d_k ; without it, softmax saturates and gradients vanish. For financial time series, note that attention is permutation-equivariant — it has no notion of time order except through positional encoding, which is exactl","s":1},{"id":"7-2-financial-nlp-that-is-worth-the-effort","t":"7.2 Financial NLP that is worth the effort","v":"Vol 07","x":"Generic sentiment models perform poorly on financial text because the vocabulary is domain-inverted: “liability”, “tax”, “crude” and “cost” are neutral accounting terms, not negative sentiment. The Loughran–McDonald finance-specific word lists exist for precisely this reason and remain a strong, interpretable baseline. Domain-adapted tran","s":1},{"id":"7-2-financial-nlp-that-is-worth-the-effort","t":"7.2 Financial NLP that is worth the effort","v":"Vol 07","x":"The signals that have historically carried information:","s":1},{"id":"7-2-financial-nlp-that-is-worth-the-effort","t":"7.2 Financial NLP that is worth the effort","v":"Vol 07","x":"Change in language, not level. Year-over-year textual similarity of the risk-factor section of a 10-K: firms that substantially rewrite Item 1A tend to underperform. Level of negativity is largely priced; change is less so. Earnings-call tone asymmetry. Prepared remarks are scripted; the Q&A section is not. Evasion, hedging and answer lat","s":1},{"id":"7-2-financial-nlp-that-is-worth-the-effort","t":"7.2 Financial NLP that is worth the effort","v":"Vol 07","x":"Three specific traps. Timestamp drift: news databases frequently carry the revision timestamp rather than the first-publication timestamp; a story “published” at 09:00 may have been updated at 16:00 with the outcome. Pretraining contamination: a language model trained on text through 2024 already knows what happened to the companies in yo","s":1},{"id":"7-3-graph-neural-networks-on-economic-relationships","t":"7.3 Graph neural networks on economic relationships","v":"Vol 07","x":"Firms are not independent: they share customers, suppliers, ownership and competitors, and shocks propagate along those edges with a lag that markets have historically been slow to price. A graph convolution layer aggregates neighbour representations with a symmetric normalisation:","s":1},{"id":"7-3-graph-neural-networks-on-economic-relationships","t":"7.3 Graph neural networks on economic relationships","v":"Vol 07","x":"The normalisation prevents high-degree nodes from dominating. Two practical warnings: the graph must be point-in-time (a supplier relationship disclosed in 2021 did not exist for your 2018 backtest), and depth is harmful — beyond two or three layers, node representations converge to a constant (over-smoothing), and in an economic graph th","s":1},{"id":"7-4-language-models-as-extraction-tools-not-oracles","t":"7.4 Language models as extraction tools, not oracles","v":"Vol 07","x":"The productive use of an LLM in a systematic stack is as a structured-extraction engine, replacing brittle regex and manual annotation. It converts unstructured text into typed fields that then flow into the ordinary signal pipeline of Volume 5. It is not a forecaster and should never be asked for one.","s":1},{"id":"7-4-language-models-as-extraction-tools-not-oracles","t":"7.4 Language models as extraction tools, not oracles","v":"Vol 07","x":"The validate_extraction step is the important one. A model that occasionally invents a quote is unusable as an oracle but perfectly usable as an extractor, because invented quotes fail an exact-substring test and are silently dropped. Design every LLM component so that its characteristic failure is detectable by a deterministic check.","s":1},{"id":"7-5-regularisation-that-actually-matters-here","t":"7.5 Regularisation that actually matters here","v":"Vol 07","x":"Shrink the parameter count first. The most effective regulariser on financial data is a smaller model. Start with one that underfits and grow it only while purged out-of-sample performance improves. Ensemble across seeds. Run identical architectures on different random seeds and average. The spread across seeds is a free estimate of how m","s":1},{"id":"7-5-regularisation-that-actually-matters-here","t":"7.5 Regularisation that actually matters here","v":"Vol 07","x":"Text and graph inputs pass through a pretrained encoder producing a handful of interpretable features (tone change, novelty, guidance direction, network-propagated shock). Those features join price and fundamental features in the ordinary cross-sectional pipeline. A gradient-boosted tree or a regularised linear model maps features to a sc","s":1},{"id":"7-5-regularisation-that-actually-matters-here","t":"7.5 Regularisation that actually matters here","v":"Vol 07","x":"This is the material with the highest ratio of intellectual stimulation to expected P&L in the entire book, which makes it precisely calibrated to your Openness profile (Imagination 16, Intellect 16, Adventurousness 13). It also has the longest feedback loop: a transformer experiment takes days to run and produces a result you cannot inte","s":1},{"id":"vol8","t":"Options, the Greeks and the Volatility Surface","v":"Vol 08","x":"An option is not a directional bet with leverage. It is a bet on the relationship between the volatility you pay and the volatility that is realised, plus a set of second-order exposures you will be paid or charged for whether or not you understand them. The purpose of this volume is to make each of those exposures explicit, because the s","s":1},{"id":"8-1-black-scholes-from-the-hedging-argument","t":"8.1 Black–Scholes from the hedging argument","v":"Vol 08","x":"Assume dS = mu S dt + sigma S dW . Form a portfolio Pi = V - Delta S and apply Itô:","s":1},{"id":"8-1-black-scholes-from-the-hedging-argument","t":"8.1 Black–Scholes from the hedging argument","v":"Vol 08","x":"Choosing Delta = partial V/ partial S annihilates the stochastic term. A riskless portfolio must earn r , so d Pi = r Pi dt , giving the Black–Scholes PDE:","s":1},{"id":"8-1-black-scholes-from-the-hedging-argument","t":"8.1 Black–Scholes from the hedging argument","v":"Vol 08","x":"Note what vanished: mu . The drift does not appear anywhere in the option price. With terminal condition max(S_T-K,0) the solution is","s":1},{"id":"8-1-black-scholes-from-the-hedging-argument","t":"8.1 Black–Scholes from the hedging argument","v":"Vol 08","x":"Black–Scholes is worth meeting as an argument rather than as a formula. Hold an option, and simultaneously hold the right amount of the underlying in the opposite direction, and the combination stops caring which way the price moves. Something that does not care which way the price moves must earn t Substituting the Greeks into the PDE for a delta-hedged position gives, over a small interval, P &L approx tfrac12 Gamma S^2[(( Delta S)/(S))^ 2 - sigma_imp^2 Delta t]. A delta-hedged long option makes money precisely when realised variance over the interval exceeds the implied variance you paid for. That is the entire economics of volat","s":"8.1 black–scholes from the hedging argument substituting the greeks into the pde for a delta-hedged position gives, over a small interval, p &l approx tfrac12 gamma s^2[(( delta s)/(s))^ 2 - sigma_imp^2 delta t]. a delta-hedged long option makes money precisely when realised variance over the interval exceeds the implied variance you paid for. that is the entire economics of volati black–scholes is worth meeting as an argument rather than as a formula. hold an option, and simultaneously hold the right amount of the underlying in the opposite direction, and the combination stops caring which way the price moves. something that does not care which way the price moves must earn the risk-free rate, or it could be arbitraged. impose that and the option's price falls out, with no room left for opinion about direction. which is why the only genuinely contested input in black–scholes is volatility. the other five you can look up. three expiry payoff diagrams: a long call, a long put, and a long straddle whose payoff rises in both directions away from the strike"},{"id":"8-2-the-greeks-with-their-trading-meaning","t":"8.2 The Greeks, with their trading meaning","v":"Vol 08","x":"Closed forms for a non-dividend-paying call: Delta = Phi(d_1) , Gamma = dfrac phi(d_1)S sigmasqrt(T) , nu = S phi(d_1)sqrt(T) , Theta = - dfracS phi(d_1) sigma2sqrt(T) - rKe^-rT Phi(d_2) . Note Gamma and nu are identical for calls and puts of the same strike — put–call parity is linear in S , so it has no second derivative.","s":1},{"id":"8-3-the-volatility-surface-what-the-smile-is-telling","t":"8.3 The volatility surface: what the smile is telling you","v":"Vol 08","x":"Black–Scholes assumes a single sigma . The market quotes a different implied volatility for every strike and maturity — that surface is not a failure of the model but the market's way of expressing everything the model omits: fat tails, jumps, stochastic volatility, and the supply/demand for crash protection.","s":1},{"id":"8-3-the-volatility-surface-what-the-smile-is-telling","t":"8.3 The volatility surface: what the smile is telling you","v":"Vol 08","x":"Equity index skew is steeply downward-sloping in strike: out-of-the-money puts trade at much higher implied vol than calls. Two reinforcing causes — the leverage effect makes down-moves genuinely more volatile, and there is persistent structural demand for downside protection with limited natural supply. Term structure is usually upward-s","s":"8.3 the volatility surface: what the smile is telling you equity index skew is steeply downward-sloping in strike: out-of-the-money puts trade at much higher implied vol than calls. two reinforcing causes — the leverage effect makes down-moves genuinely more volatile, and there is persistent structural demand for downside protection with limited natural supply. term structure is usually upward-s it is not a failure of the formula so much as a place to keep the corrections: fat tails, jumps, stochastic volatility and the simple fact that people pay up for insurance. a flat line at twenty percent representing the single black-scholes volatility, against the market's downward-sloping smile with implied volatility rising sharply for out-of-the-money puts"},{"id":"arbitrage-free-constraints-a-fitted-surface-must-res","t":"Arbitrage-free constraints a fitted surface must respect","v":"Vol 08","x":"A surface is not just an interpolation problem; there are hard no-arbitrage constraints:","s":1},{"id":"arbitrage-free-constraints-a-fitted-surface-must-res","t":"Arbitrage-free constraints a fitted surface must respect","v":"Vol 08","x":"Butterfly (convexity): partial^2 C/ partial K^2 ge 0 , i.e. the implied risk-neutral density must be non-negative. A fitted smile with too much curvature produces negative probabilities. Calendar: total implied variance w(K,T)= sigma^2_imp T must be non-decreasing in T for fixed log-moneyness. Vertical: call prices must be non-increasing ","s":1},{"id":"arbitrage-free-constraints-a-fitted-surface-must-res","t":"Arbitrage-free constraints a fitted surface must respect","v":"Vol 08","x":"This is why SVI (stochastic volatility inspired) parameterisations are the industry default: they fit total variance per slice as","s":1},{"id":"arbitrage-free-constraints-a-fitted-surface-must-res","t":"Arbitrage-free constraints a fitted surface must respect","v":"Vol 08","x":"with explicit parameter conditions guaranteeing butterfly-arbitrage-free slices, and calendar constraints enforced by ordering across maturities.","s":1},{"id":"dupire-local-volatility","t":"Dupire local volatility","v":"Vol 08","x":"Given a complete arbitrage-free surface of European prices, there is a unique local volatility function reproducing it exactly:","s":1},{"id":"dupire-local-volatility","t":"Dupire local volatility","v":"Vol 08","x":"Local vol calibrates perfectly to vanillas by construction, which is exactly its weakness: it fits today's surface but predicts the wrong dynamics . Under local vol the smile flattens as spot rises, which is the opposite of observed behaviour, so it misprices forward-starting and barrier products.","s":1},{"id":"heston-stochastic-volatility","t":"Heston stochastic volatility","v":"Vol 08","x":"dS = mu S dt + sqrt(v)S dW^1 , dv = kappa( theta - v)dt + xisqrt(v) dW^2 , d langle W^1,W^2 rangle = rho dt . Five parameters with direct interpretations: rho<0 generates skew, xi generates smile curvature, kappa controls how fast the term structure flattens, theta is the long-run variance, v_0 the current level. The Feller condition 2 ka","s":1},{"id":"heston-stochastic-volatility","t":"Heston stochastic volatility","v":"Vol 08","x":"Not from delta. The recurring causes are: short gamma into a gap , where the hedge is executed after the move and the loss is quadratic in the gap size; pin risk at expiry , where delta is genuinely undefined and assignment is uncertain; vanna/volga in a crash , where spot and vol move together so a book that was vega-neutral becomes very","s":1},{"id":"8-5-variance-swaps-and-model-free-replication","t":"8.5 Variance swaps and model-free replication","v":"Vol 08","x":"A variance swap pays realised variance minus a fixed strike. Its fair strike is replicable from the option surface without any volatility model:","s":1},{"id":"8-5-variance-swaps-and-model-free-replication","t":"8.5 Variance swaps and model-free replication","v":"Vol 08","x":"The 1/K^2 weighting is the important structural fact: the strike depends heavily on deep out-of-the-money puts. The VIX is a discretised version of exactly this formula on 30-day SPX options, which is why VIX is not “the market's expectation of volatility” so much as the price of a specific option portfolio dominated by the put wing. Sinc","s":1},{"id":"8-5-variance-swaps-and-model-free-replication","t":"8.5 Variance swaps and model-free replication","v":"Vol 08","x":"There is a genuine structural fit here worth naming. Long-option positions have bounded loss: the maximum you can lose is known and paid at inception. Short-option positions have unbounded loss and a return stream that is small, positive and steady until it is catastrophic. Given Anxiety 16/20 and Vulnerability 13/20, the psychological co","s":1},{"id":"vol8a","t":"Stochastic Volatility: Heston, SABR, Local-Stochastic and Rough Paths","v":"Vol 8A","x":"Volume 8 gives you Black–Scholes, the Greeks, and the empirical fact that implied volatility is not flat. This volume covers the models built to explain that surface, in the order they are actually used, and the one question that decides which you need: are you pricing, hedging, or trading the surface? The three answers are different. Pricing a vanilla needs no model at all beyond an interpolation of the surface. Hedging needs a model whose dynamics are right, because the hedge is a statement about how the surface ","s":1},{"id":"8a-1-what-the-surface-actually-looks-like","t":"8A.1 What the surface actually looks like","v":"Vol 8A","x":"There is no such thing as the volatility of a market. Options at different strikes and different expiries trade at prices implying different volatilities, and the pattern they form is stable, has a recognisable shape, and moves in describable ways. This section is that description. The models in the >8A.1 What the surface actually looks like ¶ Feature Empirical fact What it implies about the model Equity index skew put IV well above call IV; slope steepest at short tenor negative spot-vol correlation, or jumps Term structure usually upward-sloping in calm, inverted in stress mean-reverting variance with a level above spot vol ATM sk","s":"8a.1 what the surface actually looks like >8a.1 what the surface actually looks like ¶ feature empirical fact what it implies about the model equity index skew put iv well above call iv; slope steepest at short tenor negative spot-vol correlation, or jumps term structure usually upward-sloping in calm, inverted in stress mean-reverting variance with a level above spot vol atm skew decay decays roughly like with diffusive stochastic vol gives — too fast vol of vol high, and higher in stress variance itself must be stochastic, not just time-varying smile in  there is no such thing as the volatility of a market. options at different strikes and different expiries trade at prices implying different volatilities, and the pattern they form is stable, has a recognisable shape, and moves in describable ways. this section is that description. the models in the rest of the volume are attempts to produce the shape from assumptions rather than merely fit it. knowing which of the two you are trading decides the model. skew trades need a model that produces skew; term-structure trades need one with mean-reverting variance."},{"id":"8a-2-heston","t":"8A.2 Heston","v":"Vol 8A","x":">8A.2 Heston ¶ Variance follows a mean-reverting square-root (CIR) process, correlated with the spot. The five parameters map cleanly onto surface features, which is a large part of why the model persists: Parameter Controls Typical equity index value speed of variance mean reversion 1–5 per year long-run variance — the level the term structure approaches vol of vol — the convexity of the smile 0.3–1.0 spot–vol correlation — the skew to current variance — the ATM level whatever spot vol is Its decisive practical ad","s":1},{"id":"8a-3-sabr-and-why-it-dominates-in-rates","t":"8A.3 SABR, and why it dominates in rates","v":"Vol 8A","x":">8A.3 SABR, and why it dominates in rates ¶ SABR models the forward directly, with a CEV local-volatility exponent and lognormal vol of vol. Its dominance in rates and FX rests on one thing: Hagan's asymptotic expansion gives implied volatility in closed form , so calibration is instantaneous and a trader can move parameters by hand and see the smile respond. Parameter Moves Convention the overall level solved from the ATM quote, not fitted the backbone — how ATM vol moves when the forward moves fixed by convention","s":1},{"id":"8a-4-local-volatility-lsv-and-rough-volatility","t":"8A.4 Local volatility, LSV and rough volatility","v":"Vol 8A","x":">8A.4 Local volatility, LSV and rough volatility ¶","s":1},{"id":"local-volatility","t":"Local volatility","v":"Vol 8A","x":">Local volatility ¶ Dupire's local volatility is the unique deterministic function that reproduces every European option price exactly: It fits the surface perfectly by construction. Its dynamics are wrong: it predicts that the smile flattens and moves in a way the market does not, so it misprices anything path-dependent or forward-starting, and its hedge ratios are systematically off. Use it to price European exotics consistently with the vanilla surface, never as a description of how volatility behaves.","s":1},{"id":"local-stochastic-volatility","t":"Local-stochastic volatility","v":"Vol 8A","x":">Local-stochastic volatility ¶ LSV multiplies a stochastic variance by a local “leverage” function calibrated so that vanillas still reprice exactly: The leverage function is found by solving a McKean–Vlasov problem — the calibration depends on the law of the process it is calibrating — usually by particle methods. LSV is the production standard for exotics desks because it is the only common framework that fits the surface exactly and has plausible dynamics. It is also complex, slow to calibrate, and unnecessary u","s":1},{"id":"rough-volatility","t":"Rough volatility","v":"Vol 8A","x":">Rough volatility ¶ The empirical starting point is direct: estimate the Hurst exponent of realised log-volatility and you get , far below the of a standard diffusion. Log-volatility is much rougher than Brownian motion. The rough Bergomi model with a fractional Brownian motion, reproduces the observed power-law skew decay with essentially two parameters, and calibrates the whole term structure of skew where diffusive models need a different parameter set per maturity. Where this belongs in your priorities Rough vo","s":1},{"id":"8a-5-choosing-and-the-variance-risk-premium","t":"8A.5 Choosing, and the variance risk premium","v":"Vol 8A","x":">8A.5 Choosing, and the variance risk premium ¶ Your job Model Why Price a vanilla no model — interpolate the surface (SVI) the surface is the data; a model adds error Interpolate arbitrage-free SVI / eSSVI explicitly parameterised to preclude butterfly and calendar arbitrage Hedge a vanilla book SABR or Heston hedge ratios come from dynamics, not from fit quality Price barriers, cliquets LSV needs exact vanilla fit and plausible dynamics Trade short-dated skew rough Bergomi only framework that gets the skew term s","s":1},{"id":"the-variance-risk-premium","t":"The variance risk premium","v":"Vol 8A","x":">The variance risk premium ¶ Implied variance exceeds subsequent realised variance on average, across markets and decades. The gap is a risk premium paid to whoever is short volatility, and it is one of the more durable returns in finance. It is also brutally asymmetric: a short variance swap has a payoff that is slightly positive most months and catastrophically negative occasionally, with skew around and kurtosis in the tens. The Sharpe ratio of a naive short-variance position over a benign decade is high and com","s":1},{"id":"vol9","t":"Low-Latency Systems Engineering","v":"Vol 09","x":"Latency-sensitive trading is a capital-intensive arms race. Competing for queue position at the top of the book against firms with colocated FPGAs, microwave links and custom kernels is not a project an individual can win, and no amount of C++ skill substitutes for being 300 metres closer to the matching engine. This volume is included fo","s":1},{"id":"9-1-the-latency-hierarchy","t":"9.1 The latency hierarchy","v":"Vol 09","x":"Speed matters in a narrow and specific way, and the first useful step is simply knowing the numbers. What follows is the cost of each operation a trading system performs, in nanoseconds. Read it once and a great deal of latency folklore becomes obviously true or obviously false — and, more usefully, Read the last two rows together. The gap between a retail connection and a colocated kernel-bypass stack is roughly a factor of 10^4 . No strategy whose edge depends on reacting faster than other participants survives that gap. Strategies whose edge is statistical and measured in hours or days are entirely unaffected by it — which is the","s":"9.1 the latency hierarchy read the last two rows together. the gap between a retail connection and a colocated kernel-bypass stack is roughly a factor of 10^4 . no strategy whose edge depends on reacting faster than other participants survives that gap. strategies whose edge is statistical and measured in hours or days are entirely unaffected by it — which is the  speed matters in a narrow and specific way, and the first useful step is simply knowing the numbers. what follows is the cost of each operation a trading system performs, in nanoseconds. read it once and a great deal of latency folklore becomes obviously true or obviously false — and, more usefully, you will be able to tell whether speed is your constraint at all. for most strategies at most sizes, it is not. read once, this rules out most latency folklore. it also tells you honestly whether speed is your constraint, and for a daily-frequency strategy it is not. a logarithmic bar chart of operation latencies from one nanosecond for an l1 cache reference to sixty milliseconds for a cross-continent round trip"},{"id":"9-2-mechanical-sympathy-the-three-rules","t":"9.2 Mechanical sympathy: the three rules","v":"Vol 09","x":"Do not allocate on the hot path. malloc can take microseconds and its worst case is unbounded. Pre-allocate every buffer at startup; use object pools and ring buffers. Respect the cache line. 64 bytes is the unit of memory traffic. Two threads writing to different variables in the same line cause false sharing — the line ping-pongs betwee","s":1},{"id":"9-3-an-order-book-that-does-not-chase-pointers","t":"9.3 An order book that does not chase pointers","v":"Vol 09","x":"The dominant design for a fast book is an array indexed by price offset from a base, not a map keyed by price. Lookup becomes a single subtraction and an array index; the top of book is found by scanning a small bitmap or maintaining cached best indices. The trade-off is memory proportional to the price range covered, which is acceptable ","s":1},{"id":"9-4-measure-the-tail-never-the-mean","t":"9.4 Measure the tail, never the mean","v":"Vol 09","x":"Mean latency is close to meaningless for a trading system: the orders that matter are the ones sent during bursts, which is exactly when the tail is worst. Report the full distribution and act on the tail:","s":1},{"id":"9-4-measure-the-tail-never-the-mean","t":"9.4 Measure the tail, never the mean","v":"Vol 09","x":"Record timestamps at every hop — wire arrival (NIC hardware timestamp), decode, strategy decision, order encode, wire departure — and store them as fixed-point integers in a pre-allocated ring, never as strings. Publish p50, p99, p99.9 and max per hop. A system with a 2 μs median and a 400 μs p99.9 is worse than one with a 6 μs median and","s":"9.4 measure the tail, never the mean record timestamps at every hop — wire arrival (nic hardware timestamp), decode, strategy decision, order encode, wire departure — and store them as fixed-point integers in a pre-allocated ring, never as strings. publish p50, p99, p99.9 and max per hop. a system with a 2 μs median and a 400 μs p99.9 is worse than one with a 6 μs median and the same asymmetry appears in volume 17.6: monitoring an average hides exactly the events the monitoring exists to catch. a right-skewed latency distribution with the median, mean, ninety-ninth and ninety-nine-point-nine percentiles marked, and the long right tail shaded"},{"id":"9-5-the-system-level-knobs","t":"9.5 The system-level knobs","v":"Vol 09","x":"Pre-allocate. Keep hot data contiguous and hot paths branch-light. Timestamp everything and report percentiles. Make the fast path allocation-free and syscall-free. These four habits will make your backtester run in minutes instead of hours and your live loop miss fewer signals — which is where the value is for you. The FPGA row in that t","s":1},{"id":"9-5-the-system-level-knobs","t":"9.5 The system-level knobs","v":"Vol 09","x":"Systems optimisation offers something rare and dangerous: an unambiguous, fast, quantitative feedback loop. Every change produces a number that goes down, which is deeply satisfying — and with Orderliness at 16/20 and low Self-Efficacy at 11/20, optimising a measurable thing is a strong attractor precisely when the unmeasurable thing (doe","s":1},{"id":"vol10","t":"Optimal Execution: Almgren–Chriss and the Algorithms","v":"Vol 10","x":"You have decided to buy X shares. Trading them all now costs a large, certain amount of market impact. Trading them slowly reduces impact but exposes you to price drift over the execution horizon. Optimal execution is the resolution of that trade-off, and it has a closed-form answer under reasonable assumptions.","s":1},{"id":"10-1-the-almgren-chriss-framework","t":"10.1 The Almgren–Chriss framework","v":"Vol 10","x":"Liquidate X shares over [0,T] in N intervals of length tau=T/N . Let x_k be the holding remaining after interval k and n_k = x_k-1-x_k the shares traded in that interval. The price process has two impact components:","s":1},{"id":"10-1-the-almgren-chriss-framework","t":"10.1 The Almgren–Chriss framework","v":"Vol 10","x":"Here g is permanent impact (it moves the market and stays), h is temporary impact (it affects only your fill and decays). Taking both linear, g(v)= gamma v and h(v)= epsilon sgn(v) + eta v , the implementation shortfall has","s":1},{"id":"10-1-the-almgren-chriss-framework","t":"10.1 The Almgren–Chriss framework","v":"Vol 10","x":"Note the structure: expected cost is driven by sum n_k^2 (trade fast, pay impact) and variance by sum x_k^2 (trade slow, carry risk). Minimising E[C] + lambdaVar[C] over the trajectory gives an exponential decay:","s":1},{"id":"10-1-the-almgren-chriss-framework","t":"10.1 The Almgren–Chriss framework","v":"Vol 10","x":"You have a large position to unwind. Sell it quickly and your own selling pushes the price down against you. Sell it slowly and you are exposed to the market moving while you wait. Neither extreme is right, so the only question is where in between — and that question has a closed-form answer whose s The urgency kappa scales as sqrt( lambda sigma^2/ eta) . Three consequences follow directly and are worth committing to memory: Higher volatility → trade faster . Risk of waiting has risen. Higher temporary impact eta → trade slower . Cost of haste has risen. lambda to 0 (risk-neutral) → kappa to 0 → the trajectory becomes linear: TWAP i","s":"10.1 the almgren–chriss framework the urgency kappa scales as sqrt( lambda sigma^2/ eta) . three consequences follow directly and are worth committing to memory: higher volatility → trade faster . risk of waiting has risen. higher temporary impact eta → trade slower . cost of haste has risen. lambda to 0 (risk-neutral) → kappa to 0 → the trajectory becomes linear: twap is you have a large position to unwind. sell it quickly and your own selling pushes the price down against you. sell it slowly and you are exposed to the market moving while you wait. neither extreme is right, so the only question is where in between — and that question has a closed-form answer whose shape is the most useful thing in this volume. not risk aversion — alpha decay. a signal with a one-day half-life must be traded today whatever it costs; a signal with a six-month half-life should never pay for immediacy. three execution trajectories showing remaining position against time: an urgent convex one, a balanced one, and a patient near-linear one"},{"id":"the-intraday-volume-profile","t":"The intraday volume profile","v":"Vol 10","x":"Volume follows a persistent U-shape: heavy at the open, a mid-day trough, and a large spike into the close, which in US equities now routinely accounts for 8–12% of the day's volume in a single auction. A VWAP algorithm needs a forecast of this profile; the standard estimator is a blend of a long-run seasonal average and a recent-days ave","s":1},{"id":"10-3-alpha-decay-changes-everything","t":"10.3 Alpha decay changes everything","v":"Vol 10","x":"Almgren–Chriss assumes the price is a martingale over the execution horizon. If you are trading because you have a signal, that assumption is false and the schedule must front-load. With an expected drift alpha(t) = alpha_0 e^-t/ theta decaying at rate 1/ theta , the trade-off gains a third term: waiting now costs you not only risk but ex","s":1},{"id":"10-3-alpha-decay-changes-everything","t":"10.3 Alpha decay changes everything","v":"Vol 10","x":"The practical rule of thumb is a comparison of two time constants. Let theta be the alpha half-life and T^ ast the impact-minimising horizon. If theta ll T^ ast , the signal will be gone before you finish — either trade aggressively and accept the impact, or do not take the trade at all. This is the calculation that kills most high-turnov","s":1},{"id":"10-3-alpha-decay-changes-everything","t":"10.3 Alpha decay changes everything","v":"Vol 10","x":"Filling at the closing price of the bar on which the signal was computed. This assumes you observed the close and traded at it, which is impossible, and it captures the entire bar's move for free. The minimum honest convention is: signal computed on bar t 's close, execution at bar t+1 's open, with slippage. If your strategy's edge disap","s":1},{"id":"10-4-measuring-execution-quality","t":"10.4 Measuring execution quality","v":"Vol 10","x":"Implementation shortfall is the only benchmark that measures what you actually lost relative to the decision:","s":1},{"id":"10-4-measuring-execution-quality","t":"10.4 Measuring execution quality","v":"Vol 10","x":"The three-way decomposition matters because the fixes are different: delay cost is a systems problem, trading cost is an algorithm problem, and opportunity cost is a strategy problem. A desk reporting only “beat VWAP by 2 bp” is hiding all three — VWAP is a self-referential benchmark that an algorithm can beat by simply trading when the a","s":1},{"id":"10-4-measuring-execution-quality","t":"10.4 Measuring execution quality","v":"Vol 10","x":"Additional diagnostics worth tracking per parent order: Reversion after completion. Measure the price 5, 30 and 60 minutes after your last fill. Strong reversion means you paid temporary impact you did not need to pay — slow down. Fill rate on passive orders , split by whether the market subsequently moved with or against you. A high fill","s":1},{"id":"10-5-order-types-and-venue-mechanics","t":"10.5 Order types and venue mechanics","v":"Vol 10","x":"Two mechanics that regularly surprise people: maker–taker fee inversion means the venue with the best displayed price may not offer the best all-in price once rebates and fees are counted, and dark pool adverse selection means a midpoint fill is most likely precisely when someone informed is on the other side. Measure fill quality by post","s":1},{"id":"10-5-order-types-and-venue-mechanics","t":"10.5 Order types and venue mechanics","v":"Vol 10","x":"Execution is the stage of the pipeline where discretion is most tempting and least rewarded. It happens in real time, it produces a visible number immediately, and it invites the thought “the price is moving, I should adjust.” With Immoderation at 15/20 — the facet describing difficulty resisting an urge in the moment — this is your most ","s":1},{"id":"vol10a","t":"Reinforcement Learning for Execution and Control","v":"Vol 10A","x":"Volume 10 solves the execution problem analytically: Almgren–Chriss gives an optimal trajectory under a linear impact model with quadratic risk aversion. The solution is a closed form and it is genuinely useful. It is also the answer to a stylised problem, and the ways it is stylised — linear impact, no order book state, no alpha decay, a fixed horizon, no opportunistic behaviour — are exactly where the remaining money is. Reinforcement learning is the natural framework for what is left: a sequential decision probl","s":1},{"id":"10a-1-execution-as-a-markov-decision-process","t":"10A.1 Execution as a Markov decision process","v":"Vol 10A","x":"Reinforcement learning is the branch of machine learning for problems where you take a sequence of actions and only learn much later whether they were good ones. Execution fits that shape unusually well: you slice an order across an hour, each slice changes the conditions for the next, and the score >10A.1 Execution as a Markov decision process ¶ The formulation is clean, which is part of the appeal. Element In execution Note State remaining quantity, time left, spread, queue imbalance, recent volume, short-term signal the design of this is most of the work Action quantity to send this interval, and aggressiveness (limit offset) dis","s":"10a.1 execution as a markov decision process >10a.1 execution as a markov decision process ¶ the formulation is clean, which is part of the appeal. element in execution note state remaining quantity, time left, spread, queue imbalance, recent volume, short-term signal the design of this is most of the work action quantity to send this interval, and aggressiveness (limit offset) discretise; continuous actions add difficulty for little gain reward negative implementation shortfall for the slice, minus a risk penalty the sign conventions matter and are easy to g reinforcement learning is the branch of machine learning for problems where you take a sequence of actions and only learn much later whether they were good ones. execution fits that shape unusually well: you slice an order across an hour, each slice changes the conditions for the next, and the score is the average price you achieved. the formal statement below is clean; the difficulty lives entirely in the simulator, which is the next section."},{"id":"10a-2-the-algorithms-and-which-to-use","t":"10A.2 The algorithms, and which to use","v":"Vol 10A","x":">10A.2 The algorithms, and which to use ¶ Method Learns Suits execution because Watch out for Tabular Q-learning a table over discrete states interpretable; the right first implementation state space explodes past ~4 dimensions Fitted Q iteration a regression from state to value sample-efficient; works offline on logged data extrapolation outside the logged policy DQN a neural handles a rich state unstable; needs target networks and replay Policy gradient / PPO the policy directly natural for continuous action; sta","s":1},{"id":"10a-3-the-simulator-is-the-hard-part","t":"10A.3 The simulator is the hard part","v":"Vol 10A","x":">10A.3 The simulator is the hard part ¶ RL needs many episodes — typically to — and you cannot get those from live trading. So you train in a simulator, and the agent learns the simulator, not the market . Every shortcut in the simulator becomes a policy that exploits it and then fails live. Simulator shortcut Policy the agent learns Live outcome Limit orders always fill at the touch quote passively, never cross no fills at all; the schedule is missed No queue position modelled post and expect immediate execution b","s":1},{"id":"10a-4-where-rl-wins-and-where-it-does-not","t":"10A.4 Where RL wins, and where it does not","v":"Vol 10A","x":">10A.4 Where RL wins, and where it does not ¶ Application Verdict Reason Order placement over minutes Genuinely wins dense reward, short horizon, huge sample count, effects are local and measurable Choosing limit offsets Genuinely wins a clean trade-off between fill probability and price improvement Market making quote skewing Promising well-posed inventory control; Avellaneda–Stoikov is the analytical baseline Optimal liquidation schedule Marginal Almgren–Chriss is already close; the gain is a few basis points Por","s":"10a.4 where rl wins, and where it does not >10a.4 where rl wins, and where it does not ¶ application verdict reason order placement over minutes genuinely wins dense reward, short horizon, huge sample count, effects are local and measurable choosing limit offsets genuinely wins a clean trade-off between fill probability and price improvement market making quote skewing promising well-posed inventory control; avellaneda–stoikov is the analytical baseline optimal liquidation schedule marginal almgren–chriss is already close; the gain is a few basis points por the mistake is not using reinforcement learning. it is using it where the feedback arrives a hundred times a decade. three problems ranked by how many labelled outcomes each produces: market making and execution with thousands to hundreds of thousands, portfolio allocation with about a hundred and twenty"},{"id":"if-you-deploy-one","t":"If you deploy one","v":"Vol 10A","x":">If you deploy one ¶ Constrain the action space so that no action is catastrophic. The agent should choose among a set of reasonable schedules, never among arbitrary quantities. A bounded action space is worth more than any amount of training. Keep the analytical baseline running in shadow and compare continuously. If the agent underperforms Almgren–Chriss over a rolling window, it reverts automatically. Log the state the agent saw and the action it chose, always. Without this you cannot diagnose anything, and the ","s":1},{"id":"vol11","t":"Portfolio Optimisation: Covariance, Shrinkage and Hierarchy","v":"Vol 11","x":"Markowitz solved portfolio choice in 1952 and the solution is correct. It is also close to useless when applied naively, because it requires inputs — expected returns and a covariance matrix — that must be estimated, and the optimiser behaves as an error-maximiser: it allocates most aggressively to whichever asset's expected return has be","s":1},{"id":"11-1-mean-variance-and-why-it-fails-in-practice","t":"11.1 Mean–variance, and why it fails in practice","v":"Vol 11","x":"Maximise w^ top mu - tfrac lambda2w^ top Sigmaw . The unconstrained solution and the tangency (maximum-Sharpe) portfolio are","s":1},{"id":"11-1-mean-variance-and-why-it-fails-in-practice","t":"11.1 Mean–variance, and why it fails in practice","v":"Vol 11","x":"The failure is arithmetic, not philosophical. Estimating mu from T observations gives a standard error of sigma/sqrt(T) ; with sigma=20 % and ten years of monthly data, se( hat mu) approx 6.3 % — larger than the equity risk premium being estimated. Meanwhile Sigma^-1 amplifies exactly the directions in which Sigma is worst estimated, sinc","s":1},{"id":"11-1-mean-variance-and-why-it-fails-in-practice","t":"11.1 Mean–variance, and why it fails in practice","v":"Vol 11","x":"Mean–variance optimisation is exactly what it sounds like: given expected returns and a covariance matrix, compute the portfolio with the best return for a chosen level of risk. The mathematics is correct and the results are frequently absurd, and the reason is worth understanding before you use it. With N assets and T observations, the sample covariance matrix has rank min(N,T-1) . If N ge T it is singular — not merely ill-conditioned — and Sigma^-1 does not exist. Even at N/T = 0.5 the condition number is severe. The Marchenko–Pastur law gives the eigenvalue support of a pure-noise sample covariance as lambda_ pm = sigma^2(1 pmsqr","s":"11.1 mean–variance, and why it fails in practice with n assets and t observations, the sample covariance matrix has rank min(n,t-1) . if n ge t it is singular — not merely ill-conditioned — and sigma^-1 does not exist. even at n/t = 0.5 the condition number is severe. the marchenko–pastur law gives the eigenvalue support of a pure-noise sample covariance as lambda_ pm = sigma^2(1 pmsqrt mean–variance optimisation is exactly what it sounds like: given expected returns and a covariance matrix, compute the portfolio with the best return for a chosen level of risk. the mathematics is correct and the results are frequently absurd, and the reason is worth understanding before you use it. the optimiser has no way of knowing which of its inputs were measured well. it treats a wild guess with exactly the confidence it gives a solid estimate, and then leans its whole weight on whichever happens to look best. nothing was wrong with the mathematics. the inputs carried error comparable to their size, and the inverse covariance matrix amplifies exactly that error. two weight charts for the same eight assets: unconstrained mean-variance produces huge offsetting long and short positions, while shrunk inputs produce small balanced ones"},{"id":"11-2-shrinkage-ledoit-wolf","t":"11.2 Shrinkage: Ledoit–Wolf","v":"Vol 11","x":"Shrink the sample covariance toward a structured target F :","s":1},{"id":"11-2-shrinkage-ledoit-wolf","t":"11.2 Shrinkage: Ledoit–Wolf","v":"Vol 11","x":"where pi is the sum of asymptotic variances of the sample covariance entries, rho the covariance between sample and target estimation errors, and gamma the squared misspecification of the target. The optimal intensity is derived analytically by minimising expected Frobenius loss — there is no cross-validation and no free parameter. Common","s":1},{"id":"11-2-shrinkage-ledoit-wolf","t":"11.2 Shrinkage: Ledoit–Wolf","v":"Vol 11","x":"An alternative in the same spirit is eigenvalue clipping : decompose S= sum_i lambda_i v_iv_i^ top , replace every lambda_i below the Marchenko–Pastur upper edge with their average, and rebuild. This keeps the few genuinely informative eigenvectors — the market mode and a handful of sector modes — and flattens the noise bulk.","s":1},{"id":"11-3-black-litterman-priors-done-properly","t":"11.3 Black–Litterman: priors done properly","v":"Vol 11","x":"Black–Litterman starts from the market portfolio and asks what expected returns would make it optimal — reverse optimisation :","s":1},{"id":"11-3-black-litterman-priors-done-properly","t":"11.3 Black–Litterman: priors done properly","v":"Vol 11","x":"These implied equilibrium returns become the prior. Views are stated as P mu = q + varepsilon , varepsilon simN(0, Omega) , where each row of P picks out an absolute or relative view. The posterior is the precision-weighted blend:","s":1},{"id":"11-3-black-litterman-priors-done-properly","t":"11.3 Black–Litterman: priors done properly","v":"Vol 11","x":"The practical virtue is that assets you hold no view on stay at their equilibrium weights instead of being driven to a corner by estimation noise, and the strength of each view is explicit in Omega rather than implicit in a point estimate. A standard, defensible choice is Omega_ii = tau (P SigmaP^ top)_ii , which scales view uncertainty t","s":1},{"id":"11-4-hierarchical-risk-parity","t":"11.4 Hierarchical Risk Parity","v":"Vol 11","x":"HRP avoids matrix inversion entirely. Three steps:","s":1},{"id":"11-4-hierarchical-risk-parity","t":"11.4 Hierarchical Risk Parity","v":"Vol 11","x":"Tree clustering. Convert correlation to a distance d_ij= sqrt tfrac12(1- rho_ij) and run hierarchical clustering to build a dendrogram of the assets. Quasi-diagonalisation. Reorder the covariance matrix so that similar assets sit adjacent, concentrating large covariances near the diagonal. Recursive bisection. Split the ordered list in tw","s":1},{"id":"11-4-hierarchical-risk-parity","t":"11.4 Hierarchical Risk Parity","v":"Vol 11","x":"Because Sigma^-1 never appears, HRP is well defined when N>T and is far more stable out of sample. Its allocations are demonstrably not mean–variance optimal if you knew the true parameters — but you do not, and in realistic simulations with estimated inputs HRP frequently delivers a higher realised Sharpe than the “optimal” solution. It ","s":1},{"id":"11-5-the-alternatives-that-keep-winning","t":"11.5 The alternatives that keep winning","v":"Vol 11","x":"The 1/N result deserves emphasis: in a well-known comparison across many datasets, no optimisation method reliably beat equal weighting out of sample once estimation error was accounted for, and the sample length required for mean–variance to win was measured in centuries for realistic asset counts. Treat 1/N as the benchmark your optimis","s":1},{"id":"11-6-constraints-turnover-and-the-cost-of-rebalancin","t":"11.6 Constraints, turnover and the cost of rebalancing","v":"Vol 11","x":"Constraints are usually described as concessions to reality. They are better understood as implicit shrinkage: a long-only constraint has been shown to be equivalent to shrinking the covariance matrix, which is why constrained portfolios often outperform their unconstrained counterparts out of sample. Useful constraints:","s":1},{"id":"11-6-constraints-turnover-and-the-cost-of-rebalancin","t":"11.6 Constraints, turnover and the cost of rebalancing","v":"Vol 11","x":"Position bounds |w_i| le w_ max — the single most effective defence against estimation error. Gross exposure sum|w_i| le G and net exposure | sum w_i| le E . Sector/factor exposure bounds, tying back to Volume 5's neutralisation. Turnover: sum_i |w_i - w_i^prev| le Gamma per rebalance.","s":1},{"id":"11-6-constraints-turnover-and-the-cost-of-rebalancin","t":"11.6 Constraints, turnover and the cost of rebalancing","v":"Vol 11","x":"Rebalancing has a genuine optimum. Trading toward the target every day pays continuous costs to chase a target that is itself mostly noise; never trading lets risk drift. The standard result is a no-trade region around the target — trade only when the portfolio leaves a band whose width scales with the ratio of transaction costs to tracki","s":1},{"id":"11-6-constraints-turnover-and-the-cost-of-rebalancin","t":"11.6 Constraints, turnover and the cost of rebalancing","v":"Vol 11","x":"There is a real argument here that has nothing to do with which optimiser is mathematically superior. A mean–variance portfolio produces concentrated, sometimes bizarre-looking weights that invite second-guessing — and the moment you start adjusting optimiser output by hand you have replaced a tested process with an untested one, at the w","s":1},{"id":"vol11a","t":"Random Matrix Theory and High-Dimensional Covariance","v":"Vol 11A","x":"Volume 11.2 tells you to shrink the covariance matrix and gives you Ledoit–Wolf. This volume tells you why that works, and gives you the tool that says precisely how much of your estimated covariance matrix is signal and how much is noise: random matrix theory. The central result is startling the first time you see it. If you estimate an covariance matrix from observations of pure noise, the eigenvalues do not cluster near one — they spread out over a wide, precisely known interval. Almost every eigenvalue of a rea","s":1},{"id":"11a-1-the-marchenko-pastur-law","t":"11A.1 The Marchenko–Pastur law","v":"Vol 11A","x":"Here is a fact that ought to disturb you. Take a hundred assets that are genuinely, completely unrelated. Measure their correlations from a few years of data. The matrix you get will not look unrelated — it will appear to have strong shared structure, purely because you had a finite amount of data.  >11A.1 The Marchenko–Pastur law ¶ Let be with i.i.d. entries of mean zero and variance , and form . As with ratio fixed, the empirical distribution of eigenvalues converges to a deterministic density: Read those bounds carefully, because they are the whole content. With assets and days (four years of daily data — more than most people us","s":"11a.1 the marchenko–pastur law >11a.1 the marchenko–pastur law ¶ let be with i.i.d. entries of mean zero and variance , and form . as with ratio fixed, the empirical distribution of eigenvalues converges to a deterministic density: read those bounds carefully, because they are the whole content. with assets and days (four years of daily data — more than most people use), and the noise eigenvalues spread from to . pure noise produces eigenvalues varying by a factor of 34. every one of those eigenvalues, in a real dataset, will look like a compone here is a fact that ought to disturb you. take a hundred assets that are genuinely, completely unrelated. measure their correlations from a few years of data. the matrix you get will not look unrelated — it will appear to have strong shared structure, purely because you had a finite amount of data. random matrix theory says precisely what that spurious structure looks like, which lets you subtract it and keep whatever is left."},{"id":"11a-2-cleaning-the-spectrum","t":"11A.2 Cleaning the spectrum","v":"Vol 11A","x":">11A.2 Cleaning the spectrum ¶ The recipe follows directly. Compute the spectrum, identify the eigenvalues that lie above — those carry genuine common structure — and replace everything inside the bulk with a constant that preserves the trace. Python Copy import numpy as np def rmt_clean (returns, keep_trace= True , shrink_bulk= True ): \"\"\"Clean a correlation matrix by clipping its noise bulk. Steps: 1. correlation matrix of standardised returns 2. Marchenko-Pastur edge lambda_+ for this (n, T) 3. eigenvalues above","s":1},{"id":"why-the-inverse-is-where-this-bites","t":"Why the inverse is where this bites","v":"Vol 11A","x":">Why the inverse is where this bites ¶ If then . Inversion turns the smallest eigenvalues into the largest weights. In the 500/1000 example the smallest noise eigenvalue is 0.086, so its inverse is 11.6, against 0.34 for the largest. The optimiser therefore places its largest bets on the directions it has estimated worst. This is the precise mechanism behind the folklore that mean–variance optimisation is an “error maximiser”, and stated this way it is obviously a fixable estimation problem rather than a defect in ","s":1},{"id":"11a-3-shrinkage-and-why-it-works","t":"11A.3 Shrinkage, and why it works","v":"Vol 11A","x":">11A.3 Shrinkage, and why it works ¶ Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage takes a convex combination of the sample covariance and a structured target: with the sample covariance, the target (constant correlation, or a scaled identity, or a single-factor model), and the intensity chosen to minimise expected Frobenius loss. The formula's three terms are the sum of asymptotic variances of the sample entries, their covariance with the target, and the squared distance between and . Seen through the RMT lens, shrinkage is a smooth vers","s":1},{"id":"11a-4-how-many-factors-are-real","t":"11A.4 How many factors are real?","v":"Vol 11A","x":">11A.4 How many factors are real? ¶ The permutation edge answers this directly: count the eigenvalues above it. On broad equity panels the answer is usually smaller than people expect — typically 1 clear market factor, 3–8 components that are stable and interpretable across subsamples, and everything else inside the noise bulk. That is a useful corrective to factor zoos with hundreds of entries. Diagnostic What it tells you Threshold Eigenvalues above the permutation edge the honest count of common factors count th","s":1},{"id":"vol12","t":"Risk Measurement, Tail Modelling and Position Sizing","v":"Vol 12","x":"Returns compound multiplicatively, so losses hurt more than equivalent gains help: a 50% loss requires a 100% gain to recover. Everything in risk management follows from that asymmetry. Position sizing is not a secondary consideration applied after strategy selection — for most retail systematic traders it is a larger determinant of termi","s":1},{"id":"12-1-var-and-its-two-serious-defects","t":"12.1 VaR, and its two serious defects","v":"Vol 12","x":"Value at Risk is the alpha -quantile of the loss distribution: VaR_ alpha = inf ell : P(L> ell) le 1- alpha . Three estimation routes:","s":1},{"id":"12-1-var-and-its-two-serious-defects","t":"12.1 VaR, and its two serious defects","v":"Vol 12","x":"The first defect is that VaR says nothing about the magnitude of losses beyond the threshold. The second is deeper: VaR is not subadditive , so VaR(A+B) can exceed VaR(A)+VaR(B) , meaning it can penalise diversification. It is therefore not a coherent risk measure. Expected shortfall repairs both:","s":1},{"id":"12-1-var-and-its-two-serious-defects","t":"12.1 VaR, and its two serious defects","v":"Vol 12","x":"Value at Risk answers “on a bad day, how much do I lose?” with a specific definition of bad: the loss that a given percentage of days do not exceed. It is the most widely quoted risk number in finance, and it has two defects serious enough to know before you quote it — it says nothing about how bad  ES is coherent (monotone, subadditive, positively homogeneous, translation-invariant), and it is what the Basel framework moved to for exactly this reason.","s":"12.1 var, and its two serious defects es is coherent (monotone, subadditive, positively homogeneous, translation-invariant), and it is what the basel framework moved to for exactly this reason. value at risk answers “on a bad day, how much do i lose?” with a specific definition of bad: the loss that a given percentage of days do not exceed. it is the most widely quoted risk number in finance, and it has two defects serious enough to know before you quote it — it says nothing about how bad the bad days are, and it can claim that combining two portfolios is riskier than holding them separately. the second defect is worse and less known: var can say that combining two portfolios is riskier than holding them apart, which is not a property any risk measure should have. a fat-tailed profit and loss distribution with the ninety-nine percent var marked at the edge of a shaded loss tail, and expected shortfall marked further out at the tail's average"},{"id":"12-2-extreme-value-theory-for-the-part-that-matters","t":"12.2 Extreme value theory for the part that matters","v":"Vol 12","x":"Rather than fitting a distribution to all returns and hoping the tail comes out right, fit the tail directly. The peaks-over-threshold approach uses the Pickands–Balkema–de Haan theorem: for a sufficiently high threshold u , the conditional excess distribution converges to a Generalised Pareto:","s":1},{"id":"12-2-extreme-value-theory-for-the-part-that-matters","t":"12.2 Extreme value theory for the part that matters","v":"Vol 12","x":"The shape parameter xi is the whole story. xi>0 is heavy-tailed (Fréchet); moments of order ge 1/ xi do not exist. Daily equity index losses typically give xi approx 0.2 – 0.35 , implying finite variance but infinite or near-infinite fourth moment — which is why sample kurtosis is unstable and why “a 6-sigma event” is a meaningless phrase","s":1},{"id":"12-2-extreme-value-theory-for-the-part-that-matters","t":"12.2 Extreme value theory for the part that matters","v":"Vol 12","x":"Best practice is a two-stage fit: filter returns through a GARCH model (Volume 4) to get standardised residuals, then fit EVT to the residual tail. This separates the time-varying scale from the fixed tail shape and produces conditional risk estimates that respond to the current volatility regime.","s":1},{"id":"12-3-drawdown-the-risk-measure-that-actually-binds","t":"12.3 Drawdown: the risk measure that actually binds","v":"Vol 12","x":"Volatility is symmetric and forgettable; drawdown is neither. For a strategy with annualised Sharpe S and volatility sigma , the expected maximum drawdown over a long horizon grows roughly like","s":1},{"id":"12-3-drawdown-the-risk-measure-that-actually-binds","t":"12.3 Drawdown: the risk measure that actually binds","v":"Vol 12","x":"so a Sharpe-1 strategy will, with high probability, experience a drawdown comparable to its annual volatility, and will spend a substantial fraction of its life below its prior high-water mark. This is not a sign of failure; it is the base rate. Two numbers worth internalising: a Sharpe-1 strategy has roughly a 16% chance of a losing year","s":1},{"id":"12-3-drawdown-the-risk-measure-that-actually-binds","t":"12.3 Drawdown: the risk measure that actually binds","v":"Vol 12","x":"The probability that a strategy with true Sharpe S shows a negative return over a period of t years is Phi(-Ssqrt(t)) . For S=1 : 16% over one year, 8% over two, 2.3% over four. The corollary is uncomfortable — you cannot distinguish a broken Sharpe-1 strategy from an unlucky one in under several years. Any decision rule of the form “stop","s":"12.3 drawdown: the risk measure that actually binds the probability that a strategy with true sharpe s shows a negative return over a period of t years is phi(-ssqrt(t)) . for s=1 : 16% over one year, 8% over two, 2.3% over four. the corollary is uncomfortable — you cannot distinguish a broken sharpe-1 strategy from an unlucky one in under several years. any decision rule of the form “stop this is why the ladder in volume 0.17 is written in advance. every level of it is reached during the flat period marked here, when the case for stopping feels strongest. an equity curve beneath its running high-water mark, falling thirty percent from a peak, spending a long flat period below it, and slowly recovering, with the time under water marked"},{"id":"12-4-kelly-and-why-you-should-not-use-full-kelly","t":"12.4 Kelly and why you should not use full Kelly","v":"Vol 12","x":"Maximising expected log wealth for a continuous return process with drift mu and volatility sigma gives the optimal leverage","s":1},{"id":"12-4-kelly-and-why-you-should-not-use-full-kelly","t":"12.4 Kelly and why you should not use full Kelly","v":"Vol 12","x":"where S is the Sharpe ratio. The growth rate achieved is r + tfrac12 S^2 . Two properties make full Kelly untradeable in practice:","s":1},{"id":"12-4-kelly-and-why-you-should-not-use-full-kelly","t":"12.4 Kelly and why you should not use full Kelly","v":"Vol 12","x":"The drawdowns are brutal. Under full Kelly, the probability of at some point halving your capital is 50%; of losing 90% it is 10%. These are properties of the optimal strategy, not of bad luck. It is exquisitely sensitive to mu , which you cannot estimate. Overestimating the Sharpe by a factor of two doubles the leverage; beyond 2f^ ast t","s":1},{"id":"12-4-kelly-and-why-you-should-not-use-full-kelly","t":"12.4 Kelly and why you should not use full Kelly","v":"Vol 12","x":"Kelly answers a genuinely well-posed question: what fraction of your money should you stake to make it grow fastest over the long run? The answer is elegant, and the trap is that it depends on knowing your edge exactly, and you do not. What follows is the formula, and then the much more important di The asymmetry is the key insight: the growth-rate curve g(f) = fS sigma - tfrac12 f^2 sigma^2 is a downward parabola peaking at f^ ast . Half Kelly captures 75% of the maximum growth rate with half the volatility and roughly a quarter of the drawdown. Quarter Kelly captures 44% of maximum growth with a quarter of the volatility. Given es","s":"12.4 kelly and why you should not use full kelly the asymmetry is the key insight: the growth-rate curve g(f) = fs sigma - tfrac12 f^2 sigma^2 is a downward parabola peaking at f^ ast . half kelly captures 75% of the maximum growth rate with half the volatility and roughly a quarter of the drawdown. quarter kelly captures 44% of maximum growth with a quarter of the volatility. given est kelly answers a genuinely well-posed question: what fraction of your money should you stake to make it grow fastest over the long run? the answer is elegant, and the trap is that it depends on knowing your edge exactly, and you do not. what follows is the formula, and then the much more important discussion of what happens when the number you feed it is too large."},{"id":"12-5-stress-testing-that-is-not-theatre","t":"12.5 Stress testing that is not theatre","v":"Vol 12","x":"A stress test is only useful if it can change a decision. Three families:","s":1},{"id":"12-5-stress-testing-that-is-not-theatre","t":"12.5 Stress testing that is not theatre","v":"Vol 12","x":"Historical replay. Run the current book through October 1987, August 1998 (LTCM), the August 2007 quant quake, September–October 2008, May 2010, February 2018 (volatility complex), March 2020, and January 2021 (short squeeze). Each has a distinct signature; the quant quake in particular is the one that matters for anyone running crowded f","s":1},{"id":"12-5-stress-testing-that-is-not-theatre","t":"12.5 Stress testing that is not theatre","v":"Vol 12","x":"The empirical regularity is that diversification fails when you need it. Average pairwise equity correlation runs around 0.2–0.3 in calm markets and exceeds 0.7 in crises; cross-asset relationships that are negative for years turn positive inside a week during a liquidation. A portfolio whose risk model uses full-sample correlations is sy","s":1},{"id":"12-6-the-risk-limit-hierarchy","t":"12.6 The risk limit hierarchy","v":"Vol 12","x":"Limits should be layered, automated, and increasingly hard to override as they escalate:","s":1},{"id":"12-6-the-risk-limit-hierarchy","t":"12.6 The risk limit hierarchy","v":"Vol 12","x":"Two properties separate a real limit system from a written one. First, limits are enforced in the order gateway , not in the strategy — so a bug in the strategy cannot exceed them. Second, re-enabling a tripped limit requires an explicit action with a delay, so the decision to resume is never made in the same emotional state that produced","s":1},{"id":"12-6-the-risk-limit-hierarchy","t":"12.6 The risk limit hierarchy","v":"Vol 12","x":"The combination in your profile that most directly threatens a trading account is Immoderation 15/20 (difficulty resisting an in-the-moment urge, orientation to immediate reward) together with low Self-Efficacy 11/20 (doubt in your own competence under pressure) and high Vulnerability 13/20 (panic under stress). That triad describes someo","s":1},{"id":"vol12a","t":"Copulas, Tail Dependence and Joint Extremes","v":"Vol 12A","x":"Volume 12 measures the risk of a single return series and its tail. This volume is about the harder and more consequential question: what happens to several positions at once . Correlation, the standard answer, is a single number summarising linear association in the body of a joint distribution. It says almost nothing about the joint tail, and the joint tail is the only region that matters — a portfolio does not fail because two assets moved together on an average day. Copulas separate the question cleanly: margin","s":1},{"id":"12a-1-sklar-s-theorem","t":"12A.1 Sklar's theorem","v":"Vol 12A","x":"Correlation measures how two things move together on an ordinary day. It has almost nothing to say about whether they crash together, and crashing together is the behaviour that can actually destroy you. A copula separates “how does each thing behave on its own” from “how do they behave at the same  >12A.1 Sklar's theorem ¶ Every joint distribution splits, uniquely, into its marginals and a copula: where each is a marginal CDF and is a distribution function on with uniform marginals. If the marginals are continuous, is unique. The construction is mechanical: apply each variable's own CDF to itself, which maps every marginal to unifo","s":"12a.1 sklar's theorem >12a.1 sklar's theorem ¶ every joint distribution splits, uniquely, into its marginals and a copula: where each is a marginal cdf and is a distribution function on with uniform marginals. if the marginals are continuous, is unique. the construction is mechanical: apply each variable's own cdf to itself, which maps every marginal to uniform by definition, and whatever dependence remains is the copula. why this is the right decomposition it is invariant to monotone transformation. the copula of equals the copula of . correlation measures how two things move together on an ordinary day. it has almost nothing to say about whether they crash together, and crashing together is the behaviour that can actually destroy you. a copula separates “how does each thing behave on its own” from “how do they behave at the same time”, so the second question becomes something you can model, measure and stress deliberately."},{"id":"12a-2-tail-dependence-the-coefficient-that-matters","t":"12A.2 Tail dependence, the coefficient that matters","v":"Vol 12A","x":">12A.2 Tail dependence, the coefficient that matters ¶ In words: given that one asset has had a once-in-a-thousand-days loss, what is the chance the other did too? This is the number a risk manager wants and correlation cannot supply. Copula Lower tail Upper tail Use for Gaussian 0 0 the default, and it says crashes are independent — they are not Student same (symmetric) the sensible default for returns Clayton 0 joint crashes without joint rallies — equities Gumbel 0 joint rallies; commodity spikes Frank 0 0 symme","s":1},{"id":"12a-3-simulating-from-a-copula-and-stressing-it","t":"12A.3 Simulating from a copula, and stressing it","v":"Vol 12A","x":">12A.3 Simulating from a copula, and stressing it ¶ The practical payoff is scenario generation. Fit marginals however you like — empirical, Student- , extreme-value in the tail — fit the copula to ranks, then simulate the copula and push the uniforms back through the marginal quantile functions. Python Copy import numpy as np from scipy import stats def simulate_t_copula (rho, nu, n_sims, marginal_ppf, rng= None ): \"\"\"Draw joint scenarios with t-copula dependence and arbitrary marginals. marginal_ppf: list of call","s":1},{"id":"12a-4-when-not-to-use-a-copula","t":"12A.4 When not to use a copula","v":"Vol 12A","x":">12A.4 When not to use a copula ¶ Copulas are not free, and three limitations are worth stating plainly. They are static. A fitted copula describes unconditional dependence. Real dependence is time-varying and rises in stress. Either fit on rolling windows and watch the parameters move, or use a dynamic conditional specification — but then you are estimating a time-varying high-dimensional object from the same limited data, and Volume 11A applies. They scale badly. Beyond five or six dimensions, elliptical copulas ","s":1},{"id":"vol13","t":"Trend Following, Carry and Systematic Macro","v":"Vol 13","x":"Trend following on liquid futures has three properties almost no other strategy family combines: it has been documented across asset classes and centuries of data, it is low-turnover enough that transaction costs do not dominate, and it is implementable with daily data on a retail platform. Its rules are simple enough to fit on a page — w","s":1},{"id":"13-1-time-series-momentum","t":"13.1 Time-series momentum","v":"Vol 13","x":"The core empirical fact: an asset's own past return over 1–12 months positively predicts its future return over the next 1–3 months, across equity indices, bonds, commodities and currencies, over samples going back more than a century. The canonical signal is a volatility-scaled past return,","s":1},{"id":"13-1-time-series-momentum","t":"13.1 Time-series momentum","v":"Vol 13","x":"with the continuous version generally preferred: it sizes conviction rather than flipping between full long and full short, which reduces turnover and softens the whipsaw around a zero crossing.","s":1},{"id":"moving-average-crossovers-as-trend-filters","t":"Moving-average crossovers as trend filters","v":"Vol 13","x":"The EWMA crossover is the practitioner's standard because it is smooth, has one parameter per leg, and responds to recent data without a hard window edge:","s":1},{"id":"moving-average-crossovers-as-trend-filters","t":"Moving-average crossovers as trend filters","v":"Vol 13","x":"Standard speed pairs are (n_f, n_s) in (2,8),(4,16),(8,32),(16,64),(32,128),(64,256) — a geometric ladder rather than a chosen “best” pair. Averaging across speeds is not a hedge against ignorance; it is a genuine improvement, because different speeds capture trends of different duration and their errors are imperfectly correlated. The fa","s":1},{"id":"13-2-volatility-targeting-the-mechanism-that-does-mo","t":"13.2 Volatility targeting: the mechanism that does most of the work","v":"Vol 13","x":"Position size is inversely proportional to forecast volatility, so that each instrument contributes approximately equal risk:","s":1},{"id":"13-2-volatility-targeting-the-mechanism-that-does-mo","t":"13.2 Volatility targeting: the mechanism that does most of the work","v":"Vol 13","x":"Empirically, volatility targeting improves risk-adjusted returns for trend strategies through a specific channel: volatility is persistent and negatively related to subsequent returns in equities, so scaling down in high-volatility regimes avoids the worst periods rather than merely rescaling them. It also mechanically truncates the left ","s":1},{"id":"13-2-volatility-targeting-the-mechanism-that-does-mo","t":"13.2 Volatility targeting: the mechanism that does most of the work","v":"Vol 13","x":"Implementation details that matter more than the formula: use a blend of a fast (e.g. 20-day) and slow (e.g. 250-day) volatility estimate so that the position does not whip around on a single volatile day; apply a buffer so that small changes in target size do not generate trades; and cap leverage independently, because the formula demand","s":"13.2 volatility targeting: the mechanism that does most of the work implementation details that matter more than the formula: use a blend of a fast (e.g. 20-day) and slow (e.g. 250-day) volatility estimate so that the position does not whip around on a single volatile day; apply a buffer so that small changes in target size do not generate trades; and cap leverage independently, because the formula demand the gain is in risk taken, not in return promised. volume 13.2 is blunt about the consequence: most of trend following's measured risk-adjusted performance comes from this mechanism, not the signal. two equity curves from the same signal: one at fixed position size falling sharply through two shaded high-volatility periods, one volatility-targeted passing through them far more smoothly"},{"id":"13-3-why-trend-following-works-and-when-it-does-not","t":"13.3 Why trend following works, and when it does not","v":"Vol 13","x":"Three explanations, all partially true and none complete: Behavioural. Under-reaction to news followed by over-reaction and delayed herding produces autocorrelated price paths. Structural flow. Rebalancing, hedging, and risk-limit-driven liquidation are price-insensitive and predictable, and they push in the direction of the existing move","s":1},{"id":"13-3-why-trend-following-works-and-when-it-does-not","t":"13.3 Why trend following works, and when it does not","v":"Vol 13","x":"A trend strategy's return profile against the underlying's return is approximately convex and V-shaped: it profits from large moves in either direction and loses a small amount in quiet, range-bound markets. This is the payoff of a long straddle, and the small consistent losses are the premium. The practical consequences follow directly: ","s":1},{"id":"13-4-carry","t":"13.4 Carry","v":"Vol 13","x":"Carry is the return earned if prices do not move — a genuinely different signal from trend, with low correlation to it, which is why the two are usually run together.","s":1},{"id":"13-4-carry","t":"13.4 Carry","v":"Vol 13","x":"In futures the common implementation compares two points on the same curve:","s":1},{"id":"13-4-carry","t":"13.4 Carry","v":"Vol 13","x":"Carry is short a straddle: small, steady, high-hit-rate gains punctuated by sharp losses when the relationship snaps — the classic “picking up pennies in front of a steamroller” profile of the FX carry trade in 2008. Trend and carry are therefore natural complements, not because their correlation is low in normal times (it is), but becaus","s":1},{"id":"13-5-building-a-diversified-managed-futures-book","t":"13.5 Building a diversified managed-futures book","v":"Vol 13","x":"Universe. 25–50 liquid futures across equity indices, government bonds, short rates, FX, energy, metals and agriculture. Diversification across sectors matters far more than count — ten correlated equity index futures are one bet. Signals. A ladder of trend speeds plus carry, each normalised to a comparable scale. Combination. Weighted av","s":1},{"id":"13-5-building-a-diversified-managed-futures-book","t":"13.5 Building a diversified managed-futures book","v":"Vol 13","x":"Futures contracts expire, so a continuous series must be stitched. Two conventions: panama (subtract the gap at each roll, preserving differences) and ratio (multiply, preserving returns). Panama-adjusted series can go negative far back in history, which silently breaks any percentage-return calculation; ratio-adjusted series distort abso","s":1},{"id":"13-5-building-a-diversified-managed-futures-book","t":"13.5 Building a diversified managed-futures book","v":"Vol 13","x":"Trend following on daily futures data matches your profile better than any other strategy family here, for four specific reasons: Low decision frequency. Positions update daily and change slowly. There is no intraday screen to watch, which removes the main occasion for Immoderation (15/20) to express itself. The rules are simple and fully","s":1},{"id":"vol13a","t":"Rates, Credit and the Instruments Beyond Equities","v":"Vol 13A","x":"Volume 13 builds a diversified managed-futures book and treats every contract as a price series with a carry and a trend. That abstraction works, and it hides structure that matters once you size positions or explain a loss. A ten-year note future and a copper future both have prices; only one of them has a duration, a convexity, and a return that decomposes cleanly into roll-down and yield change. This volume gives you the instrument-level knowledge for the non-equity part of a cross-asset book: the yield curve an","s":1},{"id":"13a-1-the-yield-curve-and-the-three-numbers-that-d","t":"13A.1 The yield curve, and the three numbers that describe it","v":"Vol 13A","x":"A yield curve is a price list: what it costs today to receive one unit of money at each future date. Zero rates, forward rates and discount factors are the same information written three ways, and knowing they are one object rather than three saves a great deal of confusion. Almost all of the curve' >13A.1 The yield curve, and the three numbers that describe it ¶ A zero-coupon bond paying 1 at has price , and the curve can be written equivalently as discount factors, zero rates or forward rates: The zero rate is the average of the forwards to that maturity, which is why the zero curve is always smoother than the forward curve and wh","s":"13a.1 the yield curve, and the three numbers that describe it >13a.1 the yield curve, and the three numbers that describe it ¶ a zero-coupon bond paying 1 at has price , and the curve can be written equivalently as discount factors, zero rates or forward rates: the zero rate is the average of the forwards to that maturity, which is why the zero curve is always smoother than the forward curve and why fitting the forward curve is the harder problem. a yield curve is a price list: what it costs today to receive one unit of money at each future date. zero rates, forward rates and discount factors are the same information written three ways, and knowing they are one object rather than three saves a great deal of confusion. almost all of the curve's movement is captured by three numbers: its level, its slope and its curvature. hedge the first two and most of a rates book's risk is gone. the third is where the interesting relative-value trades live. three panels showing a yield curve shifting in level, rotating in slope, and bulging in curvature, each with a shaded family of alternative shapes"},{"id":"nelson-siegel-svensson","t":"Nelson–Siegel–Svensson","v":"Vol 13A","x":">Nelson–Siegel–Svensson ¶ Factor Loading shape Interpretation PCA counterpart constant across maturities level — the long rate PC1, ~85–90% of variance decays from 1 to 0 slope — short minus long PC2, ~5–10% hump, peaking near curvature — the belly PC3, ~1–3% sets where the hump sits usually fixed, not fitted — Why the parametric form, when PCA gives the same three factors PCA on yield changes recovers level, slope and curvature almost exactly, and does so without assuming a functional form. Nelson–Siegel is noneth","s":1},{"id":"13a-2-duration-convexity-and-dv01","t":"13A.2 Duration, convexity and DV01","v":"Vol 13A","x":"Duration and convexity are the first and second derivatives of a bond's price with respect to its yield — which is a precise way of saying “how much do I lose if rates rise a little, and how does that answer change if rates rise a lot?” DV01 is the same information expressed in money rather than per >13A.2 Duration, convexity and DV01 ¶ Bond price as a function of yield is a Taylor expansion (Volume 0A.5), truncated after the second term: Measure Definition Use Macaulay duration PV-weighted average time to cash flow, in years intuition; the balance point of the cash flows Modified duration the percentage price sensitivity DV01 / PV0","s":"13a.2 duration, convexity and dv01 >13a.2 duration, convexity and dv01 ¶ bond price as a function of yield is a taylor expansion (volume 0a.5), truncated after the second term: measure definition use macaulay duration pv-weighted average time to cash flow, in years intuition; the balance point of the cash flows modified duration the percentage price sensitivity dv01 / pv01 price change per 1 bp yield move, in currency the working number — it is additive across a book convexity the error duration alone makes; always positive for a bullet bond key-rat duration and convexity are the first and second derivatives of a bond's price with respect to its yield — which is a precise way of saying “how much do i lose if rates rise a little, and how does that answer change if rates rise a lot?” dv01 is the same information expressed in money rather than percentages, which is the form you actually risk-manage in."},{"id":"convexity-is-a-real-return-not-a-correction-term","t":"Convexity is a real return, not a correction term","v":"Vol 13A","x":">Convexity is a real return, not a correction term ¶ Because convexity is positive, a bond gains more from a yield fall than it loses from an equal yield rise. Over a volatile period this is worth roughly per unit time — a genuine expected return that the market prices, which is why long-dated bonds trade at lower yields than a pure expectations model predicts. It is also why a duration-matched barbell (short and long maturities) is not equivalent to a bullet at the same duration: the barbell has more convexity and","s":1},{"id":"13a-3-futures-swaps-and-the-carry-decomposition","t":"13A.3 Futures, swaps and the carry decomposition","v":"Vol 13A","x":">13A.3 Futures, swaps and the carry decomposition ¶ A cross-asset trend or carry book holds futures, and the return of a futures position decomposes in a way that is worth stating precisely because it is the basis of every carry strategy in Volume 13.4: Market Carry is Sign, typically Driver Equity index dividend yield minus financing negative financing above dividends Bond future yield minus repo (‘roll-down’) positive when curve is upward-sloping curve shape FX forward interest rate differential either covered in","s":1},{"id":"swaps-in-the-amount-you-need","t":"Swaps, in the amount you need","v":"Vol 13A","x":">Swaps, in the amount you need ¶ An interest rate swap exchanges fixed for floating. Post-2022 the market is built on overnight rates (SOFR, €STR, SONIA) rather than the discredited IBOR fixings, and the practical consequences are that discounting is done on the OIS curve, that collateral terms materially affect valuation, and that the old single-curve framework is gone for good. For a systematic trader, swaps mostly matter as the instrument the rates curve is quoted in — you will trade futures and hold swap curves","s":1},{"id":"13a-4-credit-default-risk-and-spread-risk-are-diff","t":"13A.4 Credit: default risk and spread risk are different trades","v":"Vol 13A","x":">13A.4 Credit: default risk and spread risk are different trades ¶ A credit spread compensates for two distinct things, and conflating them is the most common error in credit for people arriving from equities. with the hazard rate and the recovery. The empirical fact worth carrying: for investment-grade credit, expected loss accounts for perhaps a fifth to a third of the observed spread. The remainder is compensation for illiquidity, for the systematic timing of defaults, and for spread volatility itself — the “cre","s":1},{"id":"13a-5-what-a-cross-asset-book-actually-needs","t":"13A.5 What a cross-asset book actually needs","v":"Vol 13A","x":">13A.5 What a cross-asset book actually needs ¶ Risk in common units. Everything in annualised volatility contribution or DV01. A book that reports notionals across asset classes is reporting nothing. Correct roll handling. A continuous futures series must be back-adjusted, and the adjustment method changes the answer. Ratio-adjust for percentage returns, difference-adjust for spread trades, and never compute returns from a raw front-month series — the roll gap is a fictitious return that will dominate any long sam","s":1},{"id":"vol14","t":"Crypto Market Structure, AMMs and MEV","v":"Vol 14","x":"Crypto markets offer genuinely different microstructure — continuous trading, transparent on-chain state, deterministic automated market makers — and correspondingly different risks that have no equivalent in regulated markets: exchange insolvency, smart-contract failure, bridge exploits, and the absence of any mechanism to unwind an erro","s":1},{"id":"14-1-the-constant-product-amm","t":"14.1 The constant-product AMM","v":"Vol 14","x":"A Uniswap-v2-style pool holds reserves x and y and enforces the invariant xy=k on every trade. A swap of Delta x in returns","s":1},{"id":"14-1-the-constant-product-amm","t":"14.1 The constant-product AMM","v":"Vol 14","x":"with f the fee (commonly 0.3%). The marginal price is p = y/x , and the price after the trade is p' = p (x/(x+ gamma Delta x))^2 . Slippage is therefore fully deterministic and computable in advance — a genuinely unusual property. The effective price paid, relative to the pre-trade marginal price, is","s":1},{"id":"14-1-the-constant-product-amm","t":"14.1 The constant-product AMM","v":"Vol 14","x":"An automated market maker replaces the order book with a formula. There is no queue and no counterparty choosing a price: there is a pool holding two assets and a rule that the product of the two quantities must stay constant. Anyone may trade against it, and the rule alone determines the price they Compare this to the square-root impact law of Volume 2: AMM slippage is linear in trade size relative to pool depth, not concave. Large trades are punished much more severely than in an order book, which is why aggregators split across pools and why order-book DEXs and CLOB-based chains exist.","s":"14.1 the constant-product amm compare this to the square-root impact law of volume 2: amm slippage is linear in trade size relative to pool depth, not concave. large trades are punished much more severely than in an order book, which is why aggregators split across pools and why order-book dexs and clob-based chains exist. an automated market maker replaces the order book with a formula. there is no queue and no counterparty choosing a price: there is a pool holding two assets and a rule that the product of the two quantities must stay constant. anyone may trade against it, and the rule alone determines the price they get. every property of these venues, good and bad, follows from that one equation. which makes providing liquidity a volatility position, not a yield. it pays steadily and loses in exactly the conditions that make it look most attractive beforehand. left: a constant-product hyperbola showing a swap moving along the curve. right: the impermanent loss curve, negative in both directions and steepening as the price ratio moves away from one"},{"id":"14-2-impermanent-loss-is-a-short-volatility-position","t":"14.2 Impermanent loss is a short-volatility position","v":"Vol 14","x":"A liquidity provider in a constant-product pool holds a portfolio whose value, relative to simply holding the two assets, is","s":1},{"id":"14-2-impermanent-loss-is-a-short-volatility-position","t":"14.2 Impermanent loss is a short-volatility position","v":"Vol 14","x":"This is not a fee or a bug; it is the payoff of a position that is systematically short volatility. The LP sells the asset as it rises and buys as it falls, which is the exact opposite of the long-gamma hedging profile in Volume 8. The LP is compensated in fees, so the position is profitable only when accumulated fee income exceeds realis","s":1},{"id":"concentrated-liquidity","t":"Concentrated liquidity","v":"Vol 14","x":"Uniswap v3 lets an LP allocate liquidity only within a price band [p_a, p_b] , which multiplies capital efficiency within the band and reduces to zero outside it. The virtual reserve formulation:","s":1},{"id":"concentrated-liquidity","t":"Concentrated liquidity","v":"Vol 14","x":"with L the liquidity parameter. The economics change qualitatively: a narrow band earns far more fees per unit of capital and concentrates divergence loss, and once price exits the band the position is entirely in the losing asset and earns nothing. Concentrated LP-ing is an actively managed short-volatility strategy with a rebalancing co","s":1},{"id":"14-3-perpetual-futures-and-the-funding-rate","t":"14.3 Perpetual futures and the funding rate","v":"Vol 14","x":"A perpetual swap never expires; it is tethered to spot by a periodic funding payment between longs and shorts:","s":1},{"id":"14-3-perpetual-futures-and-the-funding-rate","t":"14.3 Perpetual futures and the funding rate","v":"Vol 14","x":"An ordinary futures contract expires, and expiry is what drags its price back to the spot price. A perpetual never expires, so something else must do that job. That something is the funding rate: a payment made every few hours from whichever side is crowded to whichever side is not. It never appears paid typically every 8 hours. When the perp trades above spot, longs pay shorts; the mechanism drags the perp back toward the index. This creates the cash-and-carry basis trade : long spot, short perp, collect funding. It is genuinely market-neutral in price terms and decidedly not risk-free — the risks are exchange credit, liquidation o","s":"14.3 perpetual futures and the funding rate paid typically every 8 hours. when the perp trades above spot, longs pay shorts; the mechanism drags the perp back toward the index. this creates the cash-and-carry basis trade : long spot, short perp, collect funding. it is genuinely market-neutral in price terms and decidedly not risk-free — the risks are exchange credit, liquidation of an ordinary futures contract expires, and expiry is what drags its price back to the spot price. a perpetual never expires, so something else must do that job. that something is the funding rate: a payment made every few hours from whichever side is crowded to whichever side is not. it never appears on a price chart and routinely runs to tens of percent a year. it is a real, recurring, invisible cost. a backtest on perpetual prices that ignores funding is not slightly optimistic; it is measuring a different instrument. a perpetual future's premium oscillating around spot, with an explanation that funding flows from the crowded side to the other every eight hours"},{"id":"14-4-mev-the-mechanics","t":"14.4 MEV: the mechanics","v":"Vol 14","x":"Because pending transactions are public and block builders order them, value can be extracted by choosing that ordering. The main forms:","s":1},{"id":"14-4-mev-the-mechanics","t":"14.4 MEV: the mechanics","v":"Vol 14","x":"Sandwich attacks are the reason slippage tolerance is a security parameter, not a convenience setting: a transaction submitted with a 5% tolerance is an open invitation to be moved by up to 5%. The economically relevant quantity is loss-versus-rebalancing (LVR), the systematic amount AMM liquidity providers lose to arbitrageurs who trade ","s":1},{"id":"14-4-mev-the-mechanics","t":"14.4 MEV: the mechanics","v":"Vol 14","x":"The last function makes the trade-off concrete. A pool with 60% annualised volatility loses roughly 0.6^2/8 = 4.5 % per year to divergence; at a 0.3% fee it needs daily turnover of about 4% of TVL just to break even against holding. Whether a given pool clears that bar is an empirical question you can answer before committing capital.","s":1},{"id":"14-5-where-the-structural-edges-actually-are","t":"14.5 Where the structural edges actually are","v":"Vol 14","x":"Cross-venue basis and funding. Fragmentation across dozens of venues with real barriers to capital movement sustains spreads that would be arbitraged away in equities. The edge is real; the constraint is that it requires capital on multiple exchanges, which is exactly the exposure that has produced the largest historical losses. Funding-r","s":1},{"id":"14-5-where-the-structural-edges-actually-are","t":"14.5 Where the structural edges actually are","v":"Vol 14","x":"In order of historical damage: exchange failure (customer assets commingled or lost — not hypothetical, and recovery takes years if it happens); smart-contract exploit (an audited contract is not a safe one); bridge compromise (historically the single largest category of loss by value); stablecoin depeg (an algorithmic peg is a confidence","s":1},{"id":"14-5-where-the-structural-edges-actually-are","t":"14.5 Where the structural edges actually are","v":"Vol 14","x":"Crypto is continuously open, highly volatile, and socially loud. Each of those interacts badly with a specific facet in your profile: 24/7 markets remove the natural stopping point that protects against Immoderation (15/20); high volatility amplifies Anxiety (16/20) and Vulnerability (13/20); and the social intensity of the space rewards ","s":1},{"id":"vol15","t":"Data Engineering: Point-in-Time Discipline and Alternative Data","v":"Vol 15","x":"More apparently-profitable strategies are killed by a data defect than by a modelling error, and the defects are systematic rather than random — they nearly always make the backtest look better. A researcher who gets the data layer right and uses a linear model will beat one who gets it wrong and uses a neural network, reliably and by a w","s":1},{"id":"15-1-point-in-time-the-discipline","t":"15.1 Point-in-time: the discipline","v":"Vol 15","x":"A dataset is point-in-time if, for any historical date t , you can reconstruct exactly what was knowable at t — including values that were later revised. Most vendor data is not point-in-time by default; it is the current best view of history, which is a different and much more optimistic object.","s":1},{"id":"15-1-point-in-time-the-discipline","t":"15.1 Point-in-time: the discipline","v":"Vol 15","x":"The correct schema for any revisable series carries two time axes: the event date (what period the value describes) and the knowledge date (when you could first have seen it). Every query must filter on knowledge date. This is bitemporal modelling, and it is not optional.","s":1},{"id":"15-2-survivorship-and-the-biases-that-inflate-every-","t":"15.2 Survivorship and the biases that inflate every backtest","v":"Vol 15","x":"When a stock is delisted for cause, most databases simply end the series. A backtest that drops the name on its last observation implicitly assumes you sold at the last price — but a delisted-for-cause equity typically realises a large negative return around the event. Studies of CRSP delisting returns find averages in the range of −30% f","s":"15.2 survivorship and the biases that inflate every backtest when a stock is delisted for cause, most databases simply end the series. a backtest that drops the name on its last observation implicitly assumes you sold at the last price — but a delisted-for-cause equity typically realises a large negative return around the event. studies of crsp delisting returns find averages in the range of −30% f the ten-minute check: search your universe for a company you know was delisted or acquired. if it is absent, every cross-sectional result you have is overstated. twelve company lifespans over ten years, nine surviving and three delisted early, with a note that a free dataset contains only the survivors"},{"id":"15-3-entity-resolution-and-corporate-actions","t":"15.3 Entity resolution and corporate actions","v":"Vol 15","x":"Tickers are recycled, companies merge, share classes split. The only durable identifier is a permanent security ID (CRSP PERMNO, CUSIP with issue history, FIGI). Mapping across vendors on ticker alone produces silent mismatches that are extremely hard to detect after the fact, because the resulting series looks perfectly plausible.","s":1},{"id":"15-3-entity-resolution-and-corporate-actions","t":"15.3 Entity resolution and corporate actions","v":"Vol 15","x":"Corporate actions that must be handled explicitly, each with a distinct treatment:","s":1},{"id":"15-3-entity-resolution-and-corporate-actions","t":"15.3 Entity resolution and corporate actions","v":"Vol 15","x":"Splits and stock dividends — adjust price and volume by the factor; note that volume adjustment is frequently forgotten and breaks any ADV-based liquidity filter. Cash dividends — total-return series adjusts, price-return series does not. Momentum computed on the wrong one is contaminated by yield. Spin-offs — the parent's price drops wit","s":1},{"id":"15-4-alternative-data-what-to-ask-before-paying-for-","t":"15.4 Alternative data: what to ask before paying for it","v":"Vol 15","x":"Alternative datasets — card transactions, satellite imagery, web scrapes, app downloads, shipping manifests, geolocation — are marketed on their novelty. The questions that determine whether one is worth anything:","s":1},{"id":"15-4-alternative-data-what-to-ask-before-paying-for-","t":"15.4 Alternative data: what to ask before paying for it","v":"Vol 15","x":"What is the history? Under five years is usually untestable; a signal cannot be validated across a regime change that is not in the sample. Is the history point-in-time, or reconstructed? Backfilled “history” created by re-running today's pipeline over old raw data is not evidence, because today's pipeline was designed with knowledge of w","s":1},{"id":"15-4-alternative-data-what-to-ask-before-paying-for-","t":"15.4 Alternative data: what to ask before paying for it","v":"Vol 15","x":"Research on card-transaction and similar datasets finds that the predictive value of a given alternative dataset tends to erode quickly once the data becomes widely distributed — the mechanism is straightforward: the signal is a nowcast of a number that will shortly be public, and the value of a nowcast collapses once enough participants ","s":1},{"id":"15-5-a-research-data-architecture-that-scales","t":"15.5 A research data architecture that scales","v":"Vol 15","x":"Immutable raw layer. Store vendor files exactly as received, with the receipt timestamp, and never modify them. Every downstream table is derived and reproducible from this layer. Versioned transformations. Each derived dataset records the code version that produced it. When a result cannot be reproduced, this is the only thing that saves","s":1},{"id":"15-5-a-research-data-architecture-that-scales","t":"15.5 A research data architecture that scales","v":"Vol 15","x":"Before any strategy work, build: (1) a point-in-time price and volume store with correct corporate-action handling and delisting returns; (2) a daily universe table with a liquidity filter; (3) the assert_no_lookahead check wired into your test suite. That is perhaps two weeks of work and it is the difference between research that means s","s":1},{"id":"15-5-a-research-data-architecture-that-scales","t":"15.5 A research data architecture that scales","v":"Vol 15","x":"Data engineering rewards exactly the traits that make other parts of trading harder for you. Orderliness at 16/20 — the strongest single facet in your profile — is precisely the disposition that builds a correct bitemporal store and keeps it correct. Careful, patient, systematic work with clear right answers and no emotional volatility is","s":1},{"id":"vol16","t":"Event-Driven Backtesting: Architecture and Correctness","v":"Vol 16","x":"A vectorised backtest computes signals and returns as array operations. It is fast, it is the right tool for signal research, and it makes look-ahead almost undetectable. An event-driven backtest processes one event at a time through the same code path the live system uses. It is slower, and it is the only kind whose result you should ris","s":1},{"id":"16-1-the-architecture","t":"16.1 The architecture","v":"Vol 16","x":"A single event queue, processed strictly in timestamp order, connects five components. The critical design property is that the strategy sees exactly the same interface in backtest and in production — only the data source and the execution handler are swapped. Any divergence between the two is a source of bugs that appear only with real m","s":1},{"id":"16-1-the-architecture","t":"16.1 The architecture","v":"Vol 16","x":"A backtest is a claim that a set of rules, applied only to information available at the time, would have produced certain trades. The single design decision that makes that claim trustworthy is processing events strictly in time order, so that no line of code can reach a price that had not happened  Data handler — emits MarketEvent in timestamp order from historical storage (backtest) or a live feed (production). Strategy — consumes market events, emits SignalEvent . Holds no knowledge of position sizing or of the broker. Portfolio / risk — converts signals to OrderEvent , applying sizing and every limit from Volume 12.6. Execution ","s":"16.1 the architecture data handler — emits marketevent in timestamp order from historical storage (backtest) or a live feed (production). strategy — consumes market events, emits signalevent . holds no knowledge of position sizing or of the broker. portfolio / risk — converts signals to orderevent , applying sizing and every limit from volume 12.6. execution h a backtest is a claim that a set of rules, applied only to information available at the time, would have produced certain trades. the single design decision that makes that claim trustworthy is processing events strictly in time order, so that no line of code can reach a price that had not happened yet. vectorised backtests are faster and make that guarantee impossible to enforce; this architecture trades speed for a guarantee."},{"id":"16-2-the-fill-model-is-the-backtest","t":"16.2 The fill model is the backtest","v":"Vol 16","x":"Everything else can be right and a bad fill model will still produce a fictional result. Ordered from naive to defensible:","s":1},{"id":"16-2-the-fill-model-is-the-backtest","t":"16.2 The fill model is the backtest","v":"Vol 16","x":"For a passive strategy the queue model is not a refinement — it is the difference between a plausible result and a fantasy. The naive assumption “my limit order at the bid fills if the bar's low touched the bid” grants you a fill on every touch, whereas in reality you were behind a queue and filled only when the level traded through, whic","s":"16.2 the fill model is the backtest for a passive strategy the queue model is not a refinement — it is the difference between a plausible result and a fantasy. the naive assumption “my limit order at the bid fills if the bar's low touched the bid” grants you a fill on every touch, whereas in reality you were behind a queue and filled only when the level traded through, whic it is the cheapest experiment in this reference: re-run with the next model down and see how much of the performance was an assumption rather than a result. four fill models ranked from filling at the signal bar's own close, which is pure look-ahead, to queue-aware modelling from real order book data"},{"id":"16-3-correctness-checks-that-catch-real-bugs","t":"16.3 Correctness checks that catch real bugs","v":"Vol 16","x":"Cash conservation. At every step, cash + mark-to-market value of positions must equal starting equity plus cumulative realised P&L minus costs. Assert it every bar in debug mode; a drift means a fill was applied twice or a sign is wrong. Zero-signal test. Run with all signals forced to zero. Final equity must equal starting equity exactly","s":1},{"id":"16-3-correctness-checks-that-catch-real-bugs","t":"16.3 Correctness checks that catch real bugs","v":"Vol 16","x":"Do not represent cash or quantities as binary floats and compare them for equality. Accumulated rounding across a million fills produces positions like -1.4 times10^-13 shares that then trigger a “flatten” order, generating spurious trades and costs. Use integer share counts and integer cents, or Python's Decimal , and compare with an exp","s":1},{"id":"16-4-making-it-fast-enough-to-be-usable","t":"16.4 Making it fast enough to be usable","v":"Vol 16","x":"Research velocity is a real constraint: a backtest that takes an hour gets run once a day, and a strategy you can only test once a day takes months to develop. Practical ordering, cheapest first:","s":1},{"id":"16-4-making-it-fast-enough-to-be-usable","t":"16.4 Making it fast enough to be usable","v":"Vol 16","x":"Profile before optimising. cProfile then line_profiler . The bottleneck is almost never where you expect — it is usually DataFrame indexing inside a loop. Hoist data access out of the loop. Convert DataFrames to NumPy arrays once at the start. This alone is frequently a 10–50× speedup. Precompute everything that does not depend on state. ","s":1},{"id":"16-4-making-it-fast-enough-to-be-usable","t":"16.4 Making it fast enough to be usable","v":"Vol 16","x":"One caution: each of these steps moves you further from the code that will run live. The event loop that executes in production should be the simple, readable one. Optimise the research harness aggressively and the production path conservatively.","s":1},{"id":"16-4-making-it-fast-enough-to-be-usable","t":"16.4 Making it fast enough to be usable","v":"Vol 16","x":"Building the backtester is the most enjoyable part of this discipline for someone with your profile: it is a clean engineering problem with verifiable correctness, immediate feedback, and unlimited scope for elegance. That combination — high Orderliness, high Intellect, low Self-Efficacy about market outcomes — makes infrastructure work a","s":1},{"id":"vol17","t":"Production Infrastructure, FIX and Operational Risk","v":"Vol 17","x":"Operational failures have destroyed more trading capital than bad models. Knight Capital lost roughly $460 million in 45 minutes in 2012 because a deployment left stale code active on one of eight servers and reused a flag that had previously meant something else. No signal was wrong. The lesson is that the deployment process, the kill sw","s":1},{"id":"17-1-system-topology","t":"17.1 System topology","v":"Vol 17","x":"Market data handler — normalises venue feeds to an internal format, sequences and gap-fills, publishes to consumers. Owns the book state. Strategy processes — one per strategy, isolated, so a crash in one cannot take down the others. Risk gateway — every order passes through it; enforces limits outside strategy code so a strategy bug cann","s":1},{"id":"17-2-fix-in-the-amount-of-detail-you-need","t":"17.2 FIX in the amount of detail you need","v":"Vol 17","x":"FIX is a tag-value protocol: tag=value pairs separated by SOH (ASCII 0x01). A new order single ( MsgType=D ) looks like:","s":1},{"id":"17-2-fix-in-the-amount-of-detail-you-need","t":"17.2 FIX in the amount of detail you need","v":"Vol 17","x":"The message you must handle correctly is the ExecutionReport ( 35=8 ), which reports every state change. The fields that matter: 39 OrdStatus (0 New, 1 Partially filled, 2 Filled, 4 Cancelled, 8 Rejected), 150 ExecType, 14 CumQty, 151 LeavesQty, 6 AvgPx, 31 / 32 LastPx/LastQty.","s":1},{"id":"17-2-fix-in-the-amount-of-detail-you-need","t":"17.2 FIX in the amount of detail you need","v":"Vol 17","x":"1. Trusting your own order state instead of the broker's. The broker's ExecutionReport is authoritative. If you believe you are flat and the broker believes you are long 500, you are long 500. 2. Reusing a ClOrdID. It must be unique across all time, not just within a session. Include a date and a monotonic counter persisted to disk. 3. As","s":1},{"id":"17-3-idempotency-and-exactly-once-semantics","t":"17.3 Idempotency and exactly-once semantics","v":"Vol 17","x":"Networks partition, processes restart, messages duplicate. The system must be safe under retry, which means every operation is idempotent and every fill has a unique identity.","s":"17.3 idempotency and exactly-once semantics networks partition, processes restart, messages duplicate. the system must be safe under retry, which means every operation is idempotent and every fill has a unique identity. networks partition and processes restart, so this happens. it is one of the four things volume 0.9 insists on before an automated system is allowed near real money. two four-step chains: without a client order id a timeout and retry leaves the broker holding two hundred shares, with one it leaves a hundred"},{"id":"17-4-reconciliation","t":"17.4 Reconciliation","v":"Vol 17","x":"Reconciliation is comparing what you believe against what the broker and the exchange believe, and it must run continuously rather than at end of day. Three levels:","s":1},{"id":"17-4-reconciliation","t":"17.4 Reconciliation","v":"Vol 17","x":"When your position and the broker's disagree, the temptation is to trade the difference automatically. Do not. If the discrepancy is caused by a bug in your fill handling, the correcting trade doubles the error and the loop can run repeatedly. Halt, alert, and require a human to resolve. The cost of being flat for twenty minutes is bounde","s":1},{"id":"17-5-deployment-and-change-management","t":"17.5 Deployment and change management","v":"Vol 17","x":"Version everything, including config. Most production incidents are configuration changes, not code changes. Config belongs in source control with the same review as code. Never reuse a flag or field for a new meaning. This is the specific mechanism of the Knight Capital failure: a repurposed flag activated code that should have been dead","s":1},{"id":"17-6-monitoring-what-to-alert-on","t":"17.6 Monitoring: what to alert on","v":"Vol 17","x":"Two principles keep an alerting system useful. Alert on symptoms the user experiences (orders not reaching the market) rather than on causes (CPU load). And ruthlessly delete alerts that fire without requiring action — an operator who has learned to ignore alerts is worse than no alerting at all.","s":1},{"id":"17-6-monitoring-what-to-alert-on","t":"17.6 Monitoring: what to alert on","v":"Vol 17","x":"Every control in this volume exists to make good decisions automatic and bad ones difficult, which is exactly the design principle your profile calls for. Two specifics worth committing to: The kill switch must be a single, unambiguous action. In a moment of panic — the state Vulnerability 13/20 describes — a multi-step shutdown procedure","s":1},{"id":"vol18","t":"Backtest Overfitting, Multiple Testing and Deflated Performance","v":"Vol 18","x":"A sufficiently determined search will find a strategy with a Sharpe ratio of 3 in pure noise. This is not a hypothetical — it is a theorem, and the number of trials required is smaller than you would guess. Every technique in this volume exists to answer one question: given how much I searched, how much of this result should I believe? A ","s":1},{"id":"18-1-the-arithmetic-of-selection-bias","t":"18.1 The arithmetic of selection bias","v":"Vol 18","x":"Suppose you run N independent backtests on a strategy family with true Sharpe zero. The Sharpe estimate from T observations has standard error approxsqrt((1+S^2/2)/T) , which for S=0 is 1/sqrt(T) . The expected maximum of N independent standard normals is approximately","s":1},{"id":"18-1-the-arithmetic-of-selection-bias","t":"18.1 The arithmetic of selection bias","v":"Vol 18","x":"so the expected best annualised Sharpe found by chance, with T years of data, is roughly E[ max Z]/sqrt(T) . The table below is the one to internalise.","s":1},{"id":"18-1-the-arithmetic-of-selection-bias","t":"18.1 The arithmetic of selection bias","v":"Vol 18","x":"This is the arithmetic behind almost every disappointing live result. Run enough backtests on strategies with no edge whatsoever, and the best of them will still look excellent — not because you found something, but because you looked many times. The maximum of many random numbers is not itself a ra Read the 1,000-trial row: a decade of daily data and a thousand configurations produces an expected best Sharpe of about 1.03 from noise alone . A great many published and internally-celebrated strategies sit comfortably inside that band. And 1,000 trials is not a lot — a grid over four parameters with six values each is 1,296.","s":"18.1 the arithmetic of selection bias read the 1,000-trial row: a decade of daily data and a thousand configurations produces an expected best sharpe of about 1.03 from noise alone . a great many published and internally-celebrated strategies sit comfortably inside that band. and 1,000 trials is not a lot — a grid over four parameters with six values each is 1,296. this is the arithmetic behind almost every disappointing live result. run enough backtests on strategies with no edge whatsoever, and the best of them will still look excellent — not because you found something, but because you looked many times. the maximum of many random numbers is not itself a random number; it is reliably large. this section says how large."},{"id":"18-2-the-deflated-sharpe-ratio","t":"18.2 The Deflated Sharpe Ratio","v":"Vol 18","x":"Bailey and López de Prado's deflated Sharpe ratio converts an observed Sharpe into a probability that the true Sharpe exceeds zero, correcting for the number of trials, the sample length, and the non-normality of returns:","s":1},{"id":"18-2-the-deflated-sharpe-ratio","t":"18.2 The Deflated Sharpe Ratio","v":"Vol 18","x":"where hat gamma_3 is skewness, hat gamma_4 kurtosis, and S_0 is the expected maximum Sharpe under the null given N trials:","s":1},{"id":"18-2-the-deflated-sharpe-ratio","t":"18.2 The Deflated Sharpe Ratio","v":"Vol 18","x":"Two things fall out of the denominator that are worth noticing independently of the deflation. Negative skew increases the required Sharpe — which is correct, since a strategy that grinds out small gains and occasionally loses a great deal is riskier than its volatility suggests. High kurtosis does the same. This is the formal reason shor","s":1},{"id":"18-3-the-probability-of-backtest-overfitting","t":"18.3 The Probability of Backtest Overfitting","v":"Vol 18","x":"PBO estimates, via combinatorially symmetric cross-validation, how often the configuration that looked best in-sample underperforms the median out-of-sample. The procedure:","s":1},{"id":"18-3-the-probability-of-backtest-overfitting","t":"18.3 The Probability of Backtest Overfitting","v":"Vol 18","x":"Split the return matrix (time × configurations) into S contiguous blocks. For each of the binomSS/2 ways to split blocks into train and test halves, find the best configuration on train and record its rank among all configurations on test. PBO is the fraction of splits where that rank falls below the median.","s":1},{"id":"18-3-the-probability-of-backtest-overfitting","t":"18.3 The Probability of Backtest Overfitting","v":"Vol 18","x":"A PBO above roughly 0.5 means your selection procedure has no ability to identify a genuinely superior configuration — the in-sample winner is a coin flip out of sample. The important property is that PBO evaluates the selection process , not any single strategy, which is exactly the right unit of analysis when you have searched over many","s":1},{"id":"18-4-the-catalogue-of-biases","t":"18.4 The catalogue of biases","v":"Vol 18","x":"Remove your ten best days and re-plot the equity curve. If the strategy is unprofitable without them, you do not have a strategy — you have ten lucky days and a narrative. Do the same with the ten worst days to understand the tail you are exposed to. This takes two lines of code and eliminates a large fraction of apparently promising resu","s":1},{"id":"18-5-a-research-protocol-that-resists-self-deception","t":"18.5 A research protocol that resists self-deception","v":"Vol 18","x":"Split data into three, once. Development (say 60%), validation (20%), final holdout (20%). The holdout is opened once, at the end, and if the result fails you do not re-optimise — you abandon the strategy or you start over with fresh data. Write the hypothesis first. One paragraph, before any fitting: what economic mechanism produces this","s":1},{"id":"18-5-a-research-protocol-that-resists-self-deception","t":"18.5 A research protocol that resists self-deception","v":"Vol 18","x":"Minimum track record length answers this. For a strategy with a true annualised Sharpe of 1.0, zero skew and normal kurtosis, being 95% confident that the true Sharpe exceeds zero requires roughly (1.645/1.0)^2 approx 2.7 years of daily data. For a true Sharpe of 0.5, it is about 11 years. For 0.3, about 30 years. Sit with that. Most reta","s":1},{"id":"18-5-a-research-protocol-that-resists-self-deception","t":"18.5 A research protocol that resists self-deception","v":"Vol 18","x":"The trait combination that creates overfitting risk for you is not carelessness. It is Imagination 16/20 and Intellect 16/20 generating an inexhaustible supply of plausible hypotheses, together with Orderliness 16/20 making it pleasant to test all of them thoroughly. Thorough testing of an unbounded hypothesis set is precisely the failure","s":"18.5 a research protocol that resists self-deception the trait combination that creates overfitting risk for you is not carelessness. it is imagination 16/20 and intellect 16/20 generating an inexhaustible supply of plausible hypotheses, together with orderliness 16/20 making it pleasant to test all of them thoroughly. thorough testing of an unbounded hypothesis set is precisely the failure the counter in step 3 has to be incremented by the fitting function itself. a count you maintain by hand is a count you will under-report, and the deflation in step 4 depends on it entirely. five protocol steps chained left to right ending in opening a sealed holdout once, with a red feedback arrow underneath showing the loop researchers actually run"},{"id":"vol19","t":"Competitions, Interviews and Realistic Career Paths","v":"Vol 19","x":"Not because the prize money matters, but because they supply the one thing individual research lacks: an externally scored , out-of-sample verdict on your work, with no opportunity to adjust the result afterwards. That is an unusually honest feedback signal, and it is worth far more than another backtest.","s":1},{"id":"19-1-the-competition-landscape","t":"19.1 The competition landscape","v":"Vol 19","x":"Numerai's structure removes almost every source of self-deception: you cannot see the data's meaning, you cannot choose the universe, you cannot adjust after scoring, and performance is measured on a period that did not exist when you submitted. Participants consistently discover that their cross-validated models degrade substantially liv","s":1},{"id":"19-2-the-interview-process-stage-by-stage","t":"19.2 The interview process, stage by stage","v":"Vol 19","x":"Online assessment — timed probability, mental arithmetic, sequence and pattern questions. Speed matters more than depth. This stage is a filter, not an evaluation. Technical screen — probability and statistics, some coding. Expect Bayes, expectation with conditioning, martingales and stopping times, and basic combinatorics. Coding — data ","s":1},{"id":"the-four-question-archetypes","t":"The four question archetypes","v":"Vol 19","x":"Expectation with conditioning. “Expected number of coin flips to see HTH?” Set up first-step equations on a state machine. If you can do these fluently you have covered a large share of the probability questions asked. Martingale / optional stopping. “Random walk from 0, absorbing at -a and +b ; what is the probability of hitting b first,","s":1},{"id":"the-four-question-archetypes","t":"The four question archetypes","v":"Vol 19","x":"Not whether you compute the fair value correctly — they know you can. They are watching three things: do you widen your spread when someone repeatedly lifts your offer; do you update your fair value from the flow rather than defending your original number; and do you say “I don't know” when you do not know. Candidates who cannot admit unc","s":1},{"id":"19-4-the-honest-picture-for-an-independent-trader","t":"19.4 The honest picture for an independent trader","v":"Vol 19","x":"Capital is the binding constraint, not skill. A Sharpe-1 strategy on $50,000 generates around $5,000 of expected annual return at 10% volatility, with a very real chance of a losing year. That is not an income. Below roughly $250,000 of risk capital, systematic trading is a serious hobby or a research apprenticeship, and treating it as a ","s":1},{"id":"19-4-the-honest-picture-for-an-independent-trader","t":"19.4 The honest picture for an independent trader","v":"Vol 19","x":"Quitting a job to trade a small account full-time is the single worst structural decision available in this field, and it is common. It converts a variable, uncertain return stream into your only income, which forces you to take risk you would otherwise decline, precisely when a drawdown makes the pressure highest. Every element of the di","s":"19.4 the honest picture for an independent trader quitting a job to trade a small account full-time is the single worst structural decision available in this field, and it is common. it converts a variable, uncertain return stream into your only income, which forces you to take risk you would otherwise decline, precisely when a drawdown makes the pressure highest. every element of the di it is also why capacity (volume 19a.3) is worth computing early. a strategy that scales is a strategy somebody better resourced will eventually take from you. a map of infrastructure needed against capacity, showing high-frequency trading closed off, large funds occupying the high-capacity region, and a small uncontested corner available to an individual"},{"id":"19-5-building-evidence-that-someone-else-can-verify","t":"19.5 Building evidence that someone else can verify","v":"Vol 19","x":"One deep project, not five shallow ones. A complete pipeline — point-in-time data, a strategy with a stated mechanism, honest costs, deflated Sharpe, a live-forward record — is worth more than a portfolio of notebooks. Publish the negative results. A write-up explaining why a promising idea failed demonstrates exactly the judgement that i","s":1},{"id":"19-5-building-evidence-that-someone-else-can-verify","t":"19.5 Building evidence that someone else can verify","v":"Vol 19","x":"Your profile points clearly toward research, data and risk roles and away from trading-floor and client-facing seats. Gregariousness 8/20 and Friendliness 9/20 make a loud, socially dense trading floor genuinely costly for you, while Orderliness 16/20, Intellect 16/20 and Imagination 16/20 are close to the ideal profile for solitary, rigo","s":1},{"id":"vol19a","t":"The Business: Brokerage, Financing, Capacity, Regulation and Tax","v":"Vol 19A","x":"Every other volume in this reference treats returns as something a strategy produces. This one is about everything standing between a gross return and money in an account: who holds the assets, what the financing costs, how much capital the strategy can absorb before its own impact eats the edge, what rules apply to you, and what the tax treatment does to the compounding. These are not administrative details. A strategy with a gross Sharpe of 1.0 and 300% annual turnover, financed at an unfavourable rate and taxed ","s":1},{"id":"19a-1-where-the-money-is-held","t":"19A.1 Where the money is held","v":"Vol 19A","x":">19A.1 Where the money is held ¶ Arrangement Who you are Minimum, roughly What you get Retail broker an individual £0 simple, expensive financing, limited shorting, no cross-margin Retail with portfolio margin an individual, larger account $100k–$125k risk-based margin; materially more efficient Introduced / mini-prime a small fund or serious individual $1m–$10m prime services through an intermediary; better rates Full prime brokerage an established fund $25m–$100m+ financing, stock loan, cross-margin, capital intr","s":1},{"id":"19a-2-financing-borrow-and-the-costs-nobody-backte","t":"19A.2 Financing, borrow and the costs nobody backtests","v":"Vol 19A","x":">19A.2 Financing, borrow and the costs nobody backtests ¶ Cost Typical retail Typical institutional Applies to Commission 0–0.5 bp equities 0.1–0.3 bp every trade Spread crossed 1–5 bp liquid equities same every aggressive trade Market impact Volume 2A same size-dependent Long financing benchmark + 1.5–3% benchmark + 0.3–0.8% leveraged long positions Short rebate benchmark − 2–4% benchmark − 0.3–1% cash from short sales Stock borrow (general collateral) 0.3–1% 0.1–0.4% every short Stock borrow (hard to borrow) 5–10","s":1},{"id":"19a-3-capacity","t":"19A.3 Capacity","v":"Vol 19A","x":">19A.3 Capacity ¶ Capacity is the assets under management at which the strategy's own impact reduces net return to some threshold. It follows directly from the square-root law of Volume 2A. Per unit of capital, cost scales with , so net alpha declines and eventually crosses zero: with the AUM, annual turnover, the average position weight, the average daily volume of the traded names, and a constant absorbing the details. Solve for the at which net alpha hits your hurdle. Python Copy import numpy as np from scipy im","s":1},{"id":"19a-4-regulation-in-the-shape-that-affects-you","t":"19A.4 Regulation, in the shape that affects you","v":"Vol 19A","x":">19A.4 Regulation, in the shape that affects you ¶ This is jurisdiction-specific and changes; what follows is the structure of the questions rather than answers, and none of it is legal advice. The single most useful thing to internalise is the threshold structure: almost every regime is permissive for trading your own money and becomes demanding the moment you manage someone else's. Question Why it matters Where it bites Am I trading my own capital only? managing others' money triggers registration almost everywhe","s":1},{"id":"19a-5-tax-and-the-compounding-it-destroys","t":"19A.5 Tax, and the compounding it destroys","v":"Vol 19A","x":">19A.5 Tax, and the compounding it destroys ¶ Tax treatment is not a deduction at the end; it changes what strategy you should run. The mechanism is compounding: a strategy taxed annually on realised gains compounds on the after-tax amount, and over long horizons the gap against deferred treatment is very large. Structure Typical treatment Effect on a high-turnover strategy Taxable account, short-term gains taxed as income, annually severe — often 30–50% of the compounding Taxable, long-term gains lower rate, but r","s":"19a.5 tax, and the compounding it destroys >19a.5 tax, and the compounding it destroys ¶ tax treatment is not a deduction at the end; it changes what strategy you should run. the mechanism is compounding: a strategy taxed annually on realised gains compounds on the after-tax amount, and over long horizons the gap against deferred treatment is very large. structure typical treatment effect on a high-turnover strategy taxable account, short-term gains taxed as income, annually severe — often 30–50% of the compounding taxable, long-term gains lower rate, but r this calculation takes twenty minutes and belongs before the research, not after it. a high-turnover strategy has to clear a materially higher bar simply to match a patient one. three compounding curves from the same gross return over twenty years: untaxed, taxed once at the end, and taxed annually, with the annually taxed one ending at less than half"},{"id":"vol20","t":"Proofs: The Results Worth Deriving Once","v":"Vol 20","x":"Fourteen results that recur throughout this book, each derived rather than asserted. The value of working through a proof is not the theorem — you can look that up — but knowing exactly which assumption fails when the result stops holding in practice. Each derivation therefore ends with the assumption most likely to break.","s":1},{"id":"20-1-quadratic-variation-of-brownian-motion-equals-t","t":"20.1 Quadratic variation of Brownian motion equals T","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. For a partition 0=t_0< cdots<t_n=T with mesh | Pi | to0 , V_n = sum_i(W_t_i+1-W_t_i)^2 to T in L^2 .","s":1},{"id":"20-1-quadratic-variation-of-brownian-motion-equals-t","t":"20.1 Quadratic variation of Brownian motion equals T","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. Write Delta_i = W_t_i+1-W_t_i sim N(0, delta_i) with delta_i = t_i+1-t_i , independent across i . Then E[V_n] = sum_i delta_i = T , so V_n is unbiased. For the variance, use Var(Z^2) = 2 sigma^4 for Z sim mathcal N(0, sigma^2) :","s":1},{"id":"20-1-quadratic-variation-of-brownian-motion-equals-t","t":"20.1 Quadratic variation of Brownian motion equals T","v":"Vol 20","x":"The independence and exact Gaussianity of increments. With microstructure noise, observed price changes have a negative first-order autocorrelation, and the realised-variance estimator diverges as sampling frequency rises rather than converging — the volatility signature plot of Volume 4.5.","s":1},{"id":"20-2-it-isometry","t":"20.2 Itô isometry","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. For predictable theta with E int_0^T theta_t^2dt< infty , E big[( int_0^T theta dW)^2 big] = E int_0^T theta_t^2 dt .","s":1},{"id":"20-2-it-isometry","t":"20.2 Itô isometry","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. First take theta simple: theta_t= sum_i theta_t_i mathbb1_(t_i,t_i+1](t) with theta_t_i being F_t_i -measurable. Then I= sum_i theta_t_i Delta_i and","s":1},{"id":"20-2-it-isometry","t":"20.2 Itô isometry","v":"Vol 20","x":"For the cross terms with i<j , condition on F_t_j : the factor theta_t_i Delta_i theta_t_j is F_t_j -measurable and E[ Delta_j midF_t_j]=0 , so every cross term vanishes. For the diagonal, condition on F_t_i and use E[ Delta_i^2 midF_t_i]= delta_i , giving E[I^2]= sum_iE[ theta_t_i^2] delta_i = E int_0^T theta^2dt . Extend to general thet","s":1},{"id":"20-2-it-isometry","t":"20.2 Itô isometry","v":"Vol 20","x":"Predictability. If theta_t_i were allowed to depend on Delta_i — a look-ahead — the cross terms no longer vanish and the integral is not a martingale. This is the measure-theoretic content of the backtest look-ahead bug.","s":1},{"id":"20-3-it-s-lemma","t":"20.3 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. For dX= mu dt+ sigma dW and f in C^1,2 , df = (f_t+ mu f_x+ tfrac12 sigma^2f_xx)dt+ sigma f_x dW .","s":1},{"id":"20-3-it-s-lemma","t":"20.3 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof sketch. Taylor-expand over a partition:","s":1},{"id":"20-3-it-s-lemma","t":"20.3 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 20","x":"Now ( Delta X_i)^2 = mu^2 delta_i^2 + 2 mu sigma delta_i Delta W_i + sigma^2( Delta W_i)^2 . Summing: terms in delta_i^2 and delta_i Delta W_i vanish in the limit (the latter because sum delta_i Delta W_i has variance sum delta_i^3 to0 ), while sum sigma^2( Delta W_i)^2 to int sigma^2dt by 20.1. The third-order remainder R_i is o( delta_i","s":1},{"id":"20-3-it-s-lemma","t":"20.3 Itô's lemma","v":"Vol 20","x":"Path continuity. With jumps, ( Delta X)^2 picks up a contribution that does not vanish, and Itô's lemma gains a jump term: sum_s le t[f(X_s)-f(X_s^-)-f_x(X_s^-) Delta X_s] . This is why delta hedging fails across a gap.","s":1},{"id":"20-4-solution-of-geometric-brownian-motion","t":"20.4 Solution of geometric Brownian motion","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. dS= mu S dt+ sigma S dW Rightarrow S_t=S_0 exp[( mu- tfrac12 sigma^2)t+ sigma W_t] .","s":1},{"id":"20-4-solution-of-geometric-brownian-motion","t":"20.4 Solution of geometric Brownian motion","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. Let Y= ln S , so f(x)= ln x , f_x=1/x , f_xx=-1/x^2 . Itô with mu_X= mu S , sigma_X= sigma S :","s":1},{"id":"20-4-solution-of-geometric-brownian-motion","t":"20.4 Solution of geometric Brownian motion","v":"Vol 20","x":"Y is arithmetic Brownian motion, so Y_t = Y_0 + ( mu- tfrac12 sigma^2)t+ sigma W_t ; exponentiate. Taking expectations of S_t using E[e^ sigma W_t]=e^ sigma^2t/2 recovers E[S_t]=S_0e^ mu t , confirming the - tfrac12 sigma^2 is a property of the median path, not of the mean. blacksquare","s":1},{"id":"20-5-ornstein-uhlenbeck-solution-moments-half-life","t":"20.5 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: solution, moments, half-life","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. For dX= kappa( theta-X)dt+ sigma dW , Var[X_t mid X_0]=( sigma^2)/(2 kappa)(1-e^-2 kappa t) .","s":1},{"id":"20-5-ornstein-uhlenbeck-solution-moments-half-life","t":"20.5 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: solution, moments, half-life","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. Apply Itô to f(t,x)=xe^ kappa t : f_t= kappa xe^ kappa t , f_x=e^ kappa t , f_xx=0 , so","s":1},{"id":"20-5-ornstein-uhlenbeck-solution-moments-half-life","t":"20.5 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: solution, moments, half-life","v":"Vol 20","x":"Integrating from 0 to t and dividing by e^ kappa t : X_t= theta+(X_0- theta)e^- kappa t+ sigma int_0^te^- kappa(t-s)dW_s . The stochastic integral has zero mean, giving the conditional mean. By Itô isometry its variance is","s":1},{"id":"20-5-ornstein-uhlenbeck-solution-moments-half-life","t":"20.5 Ornstein–Uhlenbeck: solution, moments, half-life","v":"Vol 20","x":"Setting the deviation's decay e^- kappa t_1/2= tfrac12 gives t_1/2= ln2/ kappa .","s":1},{"id":"20-6-black-scholes-by-the-hedging-argument","t":"20.6 Black–Scholes by the hedging argument","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. V_t+rSV_S+ tfrac12 sigma^2S^2V_SS-rV=0 .","s":1},{"id":"20-6-black-scholes-by-the-hedging-argument","t":"20.6 Black–Scholes by the hedging argument","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. Hold one option and short Delta shares: Pi=V- Delta S . Over dt , with dS= mu S dt+ sigma S dW , Itô gives","s":1},{"id":"20-6-black-scholes-by-the-hedging-argument","t":"20.6 Black–Scholes by the hedging argument","v":"Vol 20","x":"Choose Delta=V_S . The dW terms cancel exactly and the mu terms cancel with them, leaving a deterministic d Pi=(V_t+ tfrac12 sigma^2S^2V_SS)dt . A riskless portfolio must earn the risk-free rate, else arbitrage: d Pi=r Pi dt=r(V-V_SS)dt . Equating and rearranging yields the PDE. blacksquare","s":1},{"id":"20-6-black-scholes-by-the-hedging-argument","t":"20.6 Black–Scholes by the hedging argument","v":"Vol 20","x":"Continuous costless rebalancing. In discrete time the dW cancellation is imperfect, leaving a residual proportional to tfrac12 Gamma S^2[( Delta S/S)^2- sigma^2 Delta t] — precisely the gamma–theta P&L of Volume 8.1. With transaction costs, continuous hedging has infinite cost, which is why real hedging is discrete and why the hedge error","s":1},{"id":"20-7-put-call-parity","t":"20.7 Put–call parity","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. C-P = S_0e^-qT-Ke^-rT , model-free.","s":1},{"id":"20-7-put-call-parity","t":"20.7 Put–call parity","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. At expiry, max(S_T-K,0)- max(K-S_T,0)=S_T-K in both cases ( S_T>K : S_T-K-0 ; S_T le K : 0-(K-S_T) ). Two portfolios with identical terminal payoffs must have identical value today, else buy one and sell the other for a riskless profit. Discounting: the payoff S_T is worth S_0e^-qT today (dividends leak out), and K is worth Ke^-rT ","s":1},{"id":"20-7-put-call-parity","t":"20.7 Put–call parity","v":"Vol 20","x":"No distributional assumption is used — only no-arbitrage and replicability. This is why parity violations in listed markets indicate a data problem, a borrow constraint, or an early-exercise feature, never a mispricing you can trade.","s":1},{"id":"20-8-optional-stopping-and-gambler-s-ruin","t":"20.8 Optional stopping and gambler's ruin","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. A symmetric random walk from 0 with absorbing barriers at -a and +b hits +b first with probability a/(a+b) , and the expected time to absorption is ab .","s":1},{"id":"20-8-optional-stopping-and-gambler-s-ruin","t":"20.8 Optional stopping and gambler's ruin","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. S_n is a martingale and tau is a.s. finite with bounded increments, so optional stopping gives E[S_ tau]=0 . With p=P(hit b) : pb-(1-p)a=0 Rightarrow p=a/(a+b) .","s":1},{"id":"20-8-optional-stopping-and-gambler-s-ruin","t":"20.8 Optional stopping and gambler's ruin","v":"Vol 20","x":"For the time, M_n=S_n^2-n is also a martingale (since E[S_n+1^2 mid mathcal F_n]=S_n^2+1 ). Optional stopping gives E[S_ tau^2]=E[ tau] , and","s":1},{"id":"20-8-optional-stopping-and-gambler-s-ruin","t":"20.8 Optional stopping and gambler's ruin","v":"Vol 20","x":"The trading content: with a=b the expected time to move a fixed distance grows as the square of that distance. This is why a drawdown twice as deep takes roughly four times as long to recover, and why time-under-water grows so much faster than intuition suggests (Volume 12.3).","s":1},{"id":"20-9-the-kelly-criterion","t":"20.9 The Kelly criterion","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. For continuous returns with drift mu , volatility sigma and risk-free rate r , the leverage maximising expected log growth is f^ ast=( mu-r)/ sigma^2 , and growth is negative for f>2f^ ast .","s":1},{"id":"20-9-the-kelly-criterion","t":"20.9 The Kelly criterion","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. Wealth under leverage f follows dV/V = [r+f( mu-r)]dt+f sigma dW . Apply Itô to ln V (as in 20.4):","s":1},{"id":"20-9-the-kelly-criterion","t":"20.9 The Kelly criterion","v":"Vol 20","x":"The growth rate is the drift g(f)=r+f( mu-r)- tfrac12f^2 sigma^2 . Then g'(f)=( mu-r)-f sigma^2=0 gives f^ ast=( mu-r)/ sigma^2 , and g''=- sigma^2<0 confirms a maximum. Substituting, g(f^ ast)=r+ tfrac12 S^2 where S=( mu-r)/ sigma . Setting g(f)=r and solving the quadratic gives roots f=0 and f=2f^ ast ; since g is a downward parabola, g","s":1},{"id":"20-9-the-kelly-criterion","t":"20.9 The Kelly criterion","v":"Vol 20","x":"For fractional Kelly f=cf^ ast , the excess growth is g(cf^ ast)-r = (2c-c^2) cdot tfrac12S^2 , so the fraction of maximum growth retained is 2c-c^2 : half Kelly keeps 75%, quarter Kelly keeps 44%. Volatility, however, scales linearly in c — which is the whole argument for betting fractionally.","s":1},{"id":"20-10-the-markowitz-solution","t":"20.10 The Markowitz solution","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. Minimising tfrac12w^ top boldsymbol Sigmaw subject to w^ top boldsymbol mu= mu_p and w^ top1=1 gives w^ ast= boldsymbol Sigma^-1( lambda_1 boldsymbol mu+ lambda_2 mathbf 1) .","s":1},{"id":"20-10-the-markowitz-solution","t":"20.10 The Markowitz solution","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. Lagrangian L= tfrac12 mathbf w^ top boldsymbol Sigma mathbf w- lambda_1( mathbf w^ top boldsymbol mu- mu_p)- lambda_2( mathbf w^ top mathbf 1-1) . First-order condition partial L/ partial mathbf w= boldsymbol Sigma mathbf w- lambda_1 boldsymbol mu- lambda_2 mathbf 1=0 gives the stated form. Substituting into the constraints and wri","s":1},{"id":"20-10-the-markowitz-solution","t":"20.10 The Markowitz solution","v":"Vol 20","x":"The last expression is a parabola in mu_p — the efficient frontier in mean–variance space, a hyperbola in mean–standard-deviation space. blacksquare","s":1},{"id":"20-10-the-markowitz-solution","t":"20.10 The Markowitz solution","v":"Vol 20","x":"boldsymbol Sigma^-1 must exist and be well estimated. With N ge T the sample covariance is singular and D is numerically meaningless. Even when invertible, boldsymbol Sigma^-1 amplifies the smallest eigenvalues, which are the noisiest — Volume 11.1.","s":1},{"id":"20-11-the-fundamental-law-of-active-management","t":"20.11 The fundamental law of active management","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof sketch. Let each of BR independent bets have a signal z_i correlated rho=IC with its realised return r_i , both standardised. Grinold's rule sets the position proportional to the conditional expectation, E[r_i mid z_i]= rho z_i . The per-bet expected return is then rho^2 and the per-bet variance of the position's return is approxima","s":1},{"id":"20-11-the-fundamental-law-of-active-management","t":"20.11 The fundamental law of active management","v":"Vol 20","x":"Independence. Breadth counts independent bets, not positions. Five hundred stocks in one sector driven by one factor is close to one bet, not five hundred, and the sqrt(BR) term is then wildly overstated. The transfer coefficient TC — the correlation between the ideal and implemented portfolios — must also be included, and constraints and","s":1},{"id":"20-12-standard-error-of-the-sharpe-ratio","t":"20.12 Standard error of the Sharpe ratio","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. For i.i.d. returns, Var( hat S) approx(1)/(T)(1+ tfrac12 S^2) ; more generally, with skewness gamma_3 and kurtosis gamma_4 ,","s":1},{"id":"20-12-standard-error-of-the-sharpe-ratio","t":"20.12 Standard error of the Sharpe ratio","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof sketch. hat S= hat mu/ hat sigma is a smooth function of the first two sample moments. The delta method gives Var( hat S) approx nabla g^ top Sigma_m nabla g where Sigma_m is the asymptotic covariance of ( hat mu, hat sigma^2) , whose entries involve the third and fourth central moments. Evaluating the gradient of g( mu, sigma^2)= m","s":1},{"id":"20-12-standard-error-of-the-sharpe-ratio","t":"20.12 Standard error of the Sharpe ratio","v":"Vol 20","x":"Numerically: with T=252 (one year daily) and a true annualised Sharpe of 1, se( hat S) approx 1.02 annualised. One year of data cannot distinguish a Sharpe-1 strategy from a Sharpe-0 one at any useful confidence — the result that drives Volume 18.5.","s":1},{"id":"20-13-var-is-not-subadditive","t":"20.13 VaR is not subadditive","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. There exist X,Y with VaR_ alpha(X+Y)>VaR_ alpha(X)+VaR_ alpha(Y) .","s":1},{"id":"20-13-var-is-not-subadditive","t":"20.13 VaR is not subadditive","v":"Vol 20","x":"Counterexample. Let X and Y be independent defaultable bonds, each paying 0 with probability 0.03 and 100 otherwise; take alpha=0.95 , and measure loss relative to par.","s":1},{"id":"20-13-var-is-not-subadditive","t":"20.13 VaR is not subadditive","v":"Vol 20","x":"Individually: P(loss=100)=0.03<0.05 , so the 95% quantile of loss is 0 . Hence VaR_0.95(X)=VaR_0.95(Y)=0 . Jointly: P(no default at all)=0.97^2=0.9409 , so P(at least one default)=0.0591>0.05 . The 95% quantile of the combined loss is therefore 100 .","s":"20.13 var is not subadditive individually: p(loss=100)=0.03<0.05 , so the 95% quantile of loss is 0 . hence var_0.95(x)=var_0.95(y)=0 . jointly: p(no default at all)=0.97^2=0.9409 , so p(at least one default)=0.0591>0.05 . the 95% quantile of the combined loss is therefore 100 ."},{"id":"20-13-var-is-not-subadditive","t":"20.13 VaR is not subadditive","v":"Vol 20","x":"So VaR(X+Y)=100 > 0 = VaR(X)+VaR(Y) : diversifying increased the measured risk. blacksquare Expected shortfall does not have this defect, because it averages over the whole tail rather than reading a single quantile.","s":1},{"id":"20-14-the-almgren-chriss-trajectory","t":"20.14 The Almgren–Chriss trajectory","v":"Vol 20","x":"Claim. Minimising E[C]+ lambdaVar[C] over liquidation paths yields x_k=X sinh( kappa(T-t_k))/ sinh( kappa T) .","s":1},{"id":"20-14-the-almgren-chriss-trajectory","t":"20.14 The Almgren–Chriss trajectory","v":"Vol 20","x":"Proof. From Volume 10.1, the objective in terms of the holdings path is (dropping schedule-independent terms)","s":1},{"id":"20-14-the-almgren-chriss-trajectory","t":"20.14 The Almgren–Chriss trajectory","v":"Vol 20","x":"Differentiating with respect to x_j for 0<j<N and setting to zero:","s":1},{"id":"20-14-the-almgren-chriss-trajectory","t":"20.14 The Almgren–Chriss trajectory","v":"Vol 20","x":"This is a discrete second-order linear difference equation — the discrete analogue of x''= kappa^2x — with characteristic roots e^ pm kappa tau , where cosh( kappa tau)=1+ tfrac lambda sigma^2 tau^22 tilde eta . The general solution is a combination of sinh and cosh ; imposing x_0=X and x_N=0 selects the sinh form stated. As lambda to0 , ","s":1},{"id":"20-14-the-almgren-chriss-trajectory","t":"20.14 The Almgren–Chriss trajectory","v":"Vol 20","x":"This volume is deliberately finite: fourteen proofs, each about a page. That structure is chosen with your profile in mind. Open-ended mathematical study is where Openness (84/120) will happily absorb unlimited time with no completion signal, and unfinished work is expensive when Anxiety runs at 16/20. A closed list you can finish and tic","s":1},{"id":"vol21","t":"The Codebook: Twelve Production Modules","v":"Vol 21","x":"Twelve modules, each solving a distinct problem, each self-contained enough to lift into a project. They are the pieces the earlier volumes assume exist: the research harness that counts your trials, the allocators, the volatility estimators, the risk daemon, the reconciliation job. Nothing here repeats code shown elsewhere in this book —","s":1},{"id":"21-1-research-harness-with-automatic-trial-counting","t":"21.1 Research harness with automatic trial counting","v":"Vol 21","x":"Volume 18 requires a trial count for the deflated Sharpe ratio, and Volume 6.7 notes that a count you have to remember to update will drift. This harness increments it as a side effect of fitting, and persists the record to disk so it survives a kernel restart.","s":1},{"id":"21-11-rust-rolling-window-statistics-kernel","t":"21.11 Rust: rolling-window statistics kernel","v":"Vol 21","x":"When the Python event loop is the bottleneck, the rolling statistics are usually the hot spot. This computes rolling mean and standard deviation in O(1) per step using Welford's online algorithm, which is numerically stable — the naive “sum of squares minus square of sum” loses catastrophic precision on price-level data.","s":1},{"id":"21-12-c-latency-histogram","t":"21.12 C++: latency histogram","v":"Vol 21","x":"Volume 9.4 insists on measuring percentiles rather than means. This records them in constant time with no allocation on the recording path, which is what makes it safe to call from inside a hot loop.","s":1},{"id":"21-12-c-latency-histogram","t":"21.12 C++: latency histogram","v":"Vol 21","x":"A twelve-module codebook can be read two ways. The one that helps is: take a module when you need what it does, understand it, adapt it, and move on. The one that does not is: build a beautiful personal library of all twelve, generalised and tested, before writing a strategy. The second is the more attractive option given Orderliness 16/2","s":1},{"id":"vol22","t":"The 101 Formulaic Alphas: Complete Catalogue","v":"Vol 22","x":"All 101 expressions below are the published formulas from Zura Kakushadze, 101 Formulaic Alphas (2016), arXiv:1601.00991 . They are reproduced verbatim, and all 101 are distinct — the tags on each row (fields used, operator families, lookback, neutralisation) are computed programmatically from the expression itself, so they cannot disagre","s":1},{"id":"vol22","t":"The 101 Formulaic Alphas: Complete Catalogue","v":"Vol 22","x":"Three things must be true before any expression here becomes a position, and none of them are supplied by the formula: a universe (which names, filtered how), a combination rule (these are weak individual signals meant to be blended), and a cost model . With holding periods under a week, turnover is high; Volume 2.3's impact arithmetic wi","s":1},{"id":"22-1-the-operator-and-data-language","t":"22.1 The operator and data language","v":"Vol 22","x":"Every formula is built from a small vocabulary. Time-series operators act along the time axis for a single asset; cross-sectional operators act across assets at a single point in time. Confusing the two is the most common implementation error.","s":1},{"id":"22-1-the-operator-and-data-language","t":"22.1 The operator and data language","v":"Vol 22","x":"Many expressions carry fractional windows — correlation(vwap, adv180, 17.9282) . These are artefacts of an automated search that optimised over a continuous parameter and reported the raw value. In implementation you round them. Their presence is also the most honest thing in the paper about how the alphas were produced: a parameter fitte","s":1},{"id":"22-3-two-worked-implementations","t":"22.3 Two worked implementations","v":"Vol 22","x":"Alpha#101 is the simplest expression in the set — an intraday close-to-open move normalised by the day's range, with a small constant preventing division by zero on a zero-range bar:","s":1},{"id":"22-3-two-worked-implementations","t":"22.3 Two worked implementations","v":"Vol 22","x":"Alpha#003 illustrates the more typical shape: a cross-sectional rank inside a time-series correlation, sign-flipped.","s":1},{"id":"22-5-the-complete-catalogue","t":"22.5 The complete catalogue","v":"Vol 22","x":"This catalogue is close to ideal study material for you and close to worst-case trading material. As study: 101 worked examples of signal construction, with enough variety to build real intuition about ranking, neutralisation and conditional logic — the kind of systematic, complete corpus that suits Orderliness 16/20. As trading material ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"Problem Bank: 106 Solved Problems","v":"Vol 23","x":"There are 106 problems here, and all 106 are different — different questions, different solutions, verified by an assertion that runs on every build. That is a smaller number than the source document claimed, and it is the number that is actually true. A bank of a hundred-odd genuinely distinct problems worked carefully is worth considera","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P001 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"You flip a fair coin until you see heads. What is the expected number of flips? Let E be the expectation. Condition on the first flip: with probability tfrac12 it is heads and we stop after 1; otherwise we have used one flip and are back where we started. E = tfrac12(1) + tfrac12(1+E) implies E = 1 + tfrac12 E implies E = 2. Generally, fo","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P002 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Expected number of fair coin flips to first see the pattern HH ? Use states by progress toward the pattern. Let E_0 = expected flips from no progress, E_1 = from having just seen one H. E_0 = 1 + tfrac12 E_1 + tfrac12 E_0, E_1 = 1 + tfrac12(0) + tfrac12 E_0. From the first: tfrac12 E_0 = 1 + tfrac12 E_1 Rightarrow E_0 = 2 + E_1 . Substitu","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P003 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Expected number of fair coin flips to first see HT ? Why does it differ from HH? States: E_0 (no H yet), E_1 (just saw H). E_0 = 1 + tfrac12 E_1 + tfrac12 E_0, E_1 = 1 + tfrac12 E_1 + tfrac12 (0). The second gives E_1 = 2 ; the first gives E_0 = 2 + E_1 = 4 . Why HT (4) beats HH (6). After an H, a failure has different consequences. Chasi","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P004 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"In Penney's game, player A picks a length-3 pattern, then B picks another, and they flip until one appears. Show B can always win, and give B's response to HHH . The game is non-transitive, so B — choosing second — always has an answer. B's rule: given A's pattern a_1a_2a_3 , choose overlinea_2 a_1a_2 (the complement of A's second symbol,","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P005 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"A stick of length 1 is broken at two uniformly random points. What is the probability the three pieces form a triangle? Let the break points be X, Y sim U(0,1) independent. A triangle exists iff every piece is shorter than tfrac12 (the triangle inequality with total length 1). Work in the unit square. If X<Y , pieces are X , Y-X , 1-Y , a","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P006 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two players alternate rolling a fair die; the first to roll a 6 wins. What is the probability the first player wins? Let p be the first player's win probability. On the first roll they win with probability tfrac16 . Otherwise (probability tfrac56 ) the second player is now in the first player's position, so the original player's chance be","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P007 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the probability that in a room of 23 people, two share a birthday? Why is the number so small? Complementary counting with 365 equally likely birthdays: P(no match) = prod_k=0^22(365-k)/(365) = frac365!342! 365^23 approx 0.4927, so P(match) approx 0.507 . The intuition trap is counting people instead of pairs : 23 people give bino","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P008 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"n people check coats and receive them back at random. What is the expected number who get their own coat, and its variance? Let X_i = mathbb1 person i gets own coat , X= sum X_i . Then P(X_i=1)=1/n , so by linearity E[X] = n cdot tfrac1n = 1 , independent of n . For the variance, note the X_i are not independent. For i ne j , E[X_iX_j] = ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P009 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"You draw cards one at a time without replacement from a shuffled deck. What is the expected number of draws until the first ace? The four aces split the other 48 cards into 5 gaps (before ace 1, between aces, after ace 4). By symmetry each of the 48 non-aces is equally likely to fall in any gap, so the expected number of non-aces before t","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P010 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Sample n i.i.d. U(0,1) variables. What is the expected value of the maximum? Of the k -th smallest? For the maximum M= max_i U_i : P(M le x)=x^n , so the density is nx^n-1 and E[M] = int_0^1 x cdot nx^n-1dx = (n)/(n+1). Generally the k -th order statistic U_(k) has a Beta(k, n-k+1) distribution, so E[U_(k)] = (k)/(n+1). The order statisti","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P011 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"A fair die is rolled repeatedly. What is the expected number of rolls to see all six faces? Coupon collector. After k distinct faces have appeared, the probability a roll gives a new face is (6-k)/6 , so the waiting time is geometric with mean 6/(6-k) . E = sum_k=0^5(6)/(6-k) = 6(1+ tfrac12+ tfrac13+ tfrac14+ tfrac15+ tfrac16) = 6 H_6 = 1","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P012 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"You have 100 doors, all closed. You walk past 100 times; on pass k you toggle every k -th door. Which doors are open at the end? Door d is toggled once per divisor of d , so it ends open iff d has an odd number of divisors. Divisors pair as (e, d/e) , which is a genuine pairing unless e = d/e , i.e. d = e^2 . So only perfect squares have ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P013 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"X, Y sim N(0,1) independent. What is P(X > Y) ? What is E[ max(X,Y)] ? By symmetry and continuity, P(X>Y)= tfrac12 . For the max, use max(X,Y) = tfracX+Y2 + tfrac|X-Y|2 . Now X+Y has mean 0, and D = X-Y sim N(0,2) , so E|D| = sqrt(2) cdotsqrt(2/ pi) = 2/sqrt( pi) . E[ max(X,Y)] = 0 + tfrac12 cdot(2)/( sqrt pi) = frac1sqrt( pi) approx 0.56","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P014 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"A drunk starts at 0 on the integers and takes pm1 steps with equal probability. What is the probability they ever reach +1 ? What is the expected time? The walk is recurrent in one dimension, so P(ever reach +1) = 1 . But the expected hitting time is infinite . Sketch: let T be the first hitting time of +1 . Conditioning on the first step","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P015 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Three prisoners A, B, C: one will be pardoned. A asks the warden to name a prisoner other than A who will be executed. The warden says B. Has A's probability of pardon changed? No — it remains 1/3 . This is the Monty Hall structure. Let the warden name B. Then P(warden says B mid A pardoned) = tfrac12 (he picks B or C at random), P(says B","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P016 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"You roll a fair die repeatedly and are paid the face value, but you may stop whenever you like. What is the optimal strategy and the value of the game (unlimited rolls, no cost)? Backward induction on the indifference value. If the continuation value is V , you should stop on a face f iff f ge V , so V = (1)/(6) sum_f=1^6 max(f, V). Guess","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P017 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"100 people board a plane. The first sits in a random seat; each subsequent passenger takes their own seat if free, otherwise a random free seat. What is the probability the last passenger gets their own seat? tfrac12 . Consider only seats 1 (the first passenger's) and 100 (the last passenger's). Every displaced passenger chooses uniformly","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P018 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"A test for a disease with prevalence 0.1 % has 99 % sensitivity and 99 % specificity. You test positive. What is the probability you have the disease? P(D mid+) = (0.99 times 0.001)/(0.99 times0.001 + 0.01 times0.999) = (0.00099)/(0.00099+0.00999) approx 0.090. About 9%. In 100,000 people: 100 have it, 99 test positive; 99,900 do not, and","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P019 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"X_1, dots, X_n i.i.d. U(0,1) . What is P(X_1 + X_2 + cdots + X_n > 1 for the first time at n) , and hence E[N] where N = min n : sum_1^n X_i > 1 ? The key fact: P(X_1+ cdots+X_n le 1) = (1)/(n!) , the volume of the simplex x_i ge 0, sum x_i le 1 in R^n . Then P(N > n) = P( sum_1^n X_i le 1) = 1/n! , and E[N] = sum_n ge0P(N>n) = sum_n ge0(","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P020 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two envelopes contain X and 2X . You open one and see 100$. Should you switch? Expected value of switching appears to be 125 . What is wrong? <p>The naive argument assigns probability \\tfrac12 each to the other envelope holding \\ 50 or 200$, giving tfrac12(50)+ tfrac12(200)=125 . The error is treating those as equally likely without a pri","s":"p020 — probability & combinatorics two envelopes contain x and 2x . you open one and see 100$. should you switch? expected value of switching appears to be 125 . what is wrong? <p>the naive argument assigns probability \\tfrac12 each to the other envelope holding \\ 50 or 200$, giving tfrac12(50)+ tfrac12(200)=125 . the error is treating those as equally likely without a pri"},{"id":"vol23","t":"P021 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"N balls are thrown independently and uniformly into N bins. What is the expected number of empty bins, and the expected maximum load? Empty bins. P(bin i empty) = (1-1/N)^N to e^-1 , so by linearity the expected count is N(1-1/N)^N approx N/e approx 0.368N . Maximum load. Each bin's load is approximately Poisson(1). The maximum of N such ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P022 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"X simPoisson( lambda) . Show E[X(X-1)] = lambda^2 and hence find Var(X) . E[X(X-1)] = sum_k ge2 k(k-1) frace^- lambda lambda^kk! = lambda^2 e^- lambda sum_k ge2 frac lambda^k-2(k-2)! = lambda^2 e^- lambdae^ lambda = lambda^2. Then E[X^2] = lambda^2 + E[X] = lambda^2+ lambda , so Var(X) = lambda^2+ lambda- lambda^2 = lambda. Mean equals va","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P023 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"A monkey types uniformly random letters from a 26-letter alphabet. What is the expected number of keystrokes until the string “ABRACADABRA” appears? Use the Conway leading-number / martingale (gambling team) argument. Imagine a gambler arriving before each keystroke betting 1 that the next letters spell the target, at fair odds 26$ per co","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P024 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"You have 12 identical-looking coins, one of which is counterfeit (heavier or lighter — you do not know which). Using a balance scale three times, identify the fake and whether it is heavy or light. Three weighings give 3^3 = 27 outcomes; there are 12 times2 = 24 possibilities, so it is feasible but tight — every weighing must be near-maxi","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P025 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"X, Y are jointly normal, each standard, with correlation rho . Find P(X>0, Y>0) . Write Y = rho X + sqrt(1- rho^2) Z with Z independent standard normal. The pair (X,Y) is spherically distributed after a linear map, so the orthant probability is proportional to the angle between the axes in the transformed frame. Standard result: P(X>0,Y>0","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P026 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"An urn has 1 red and 1 blue ball. You draw one at random and return it along with an extra ball of the same colour (Pólya urn). After n draws, what is the distribution of the fraction of red balls? The fraction of red is uniform on (0,1) in the limit, and after n draws the number of reds drawn is uniform on 0,1, dots,n . Proof sketch by e","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P027 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the expected number of rolls of a fair die needed to get a sum of at least 4? Let E_s be the expected number of further rolls when a remaining sum of s is still needed, with E_s = 0 for s le 0 . Conditioning on the next roll: E_s = 1 + tfrac16 sum_k=1^6 E_s-k. Working upward: E_1 = 1, E_2 = 1+ tfrac16 E_1 = tfrac76, E_3 = 1+ tfrac","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P028 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"You observe a Poisson process with unknown rate lambda for time T and see n events. A friend will observe the next T units of time. What is your predictive distribution for their count? With a Jeffreys prior pi( lambda) propto lambda^-1/2 , the posterior after n events in time T is Gamma(n+ tfrac12, T) . The predictive distribution for m ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P029 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"There are 100 prisoners and 100 boxes containing their numbers in random order. Each prisoner may open 50 boxes; all must find their own number for the group to survive. Naive strategy gives 2^-100 . Find a strategy with success probability above 30%. Strategy: prisoner i opens box i , reads the number inside, opens that box, and follows ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"P030 — Probability & Combinatorics","v":"Vol 23","x":"Let S_n be a simple symmetric random walk. What is E[ |S_n| ] for large n ? S_n is a sum of n i.i.d. pm1 , so Var(S_n)=n and by the CLT S_n/ sqrt n Rightarrow mathcal N(0,1) . For Z sim mathcal N(0,1) , E|Z| = sqrt(2/ pi) , so E|S_n| approx sqrt((2n)/( pi)) approx 0.7979sqrt(n). Exactly, for even n=2m : E|S_2m| = 2m binom2mm2^-2m , which ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S001 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"id=\"vol23\" aria-labelledby=\"vol23-h\"> Volume 23 · Problems Problem Bank: 106 Solved Problems On the count There are 106 problems here, and all 106 are different — different questions, different solutions, verified by an assertion that runs on every build. That is a smaller number than the source document claimed, and it is the number that is actually true. A bank of a hundred-odd genuinely distinct problems worked carefully is worth considerably more than any quantity of the same four problems renumbered. Each solu","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S002 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"For which theta is exp( theta W_t - tfrac12 theta^2 t) a martingale? What is this object used for? For every theta inR . Let Z_t= exp( theta W_t- tfrac12 theta^2t) . Apply Itô with f(t,x)=e^ theta x- theta^2t/2 : f_t=- tfrac12 theta^2 f , f_x= theta f , f_xx= theta^2 f , so dZ_t = (- tfrac12 theta^2 f + tfrac12 theta^2 f)dt + theta f dW_t","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S003 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"S_t is GBM with dS= mu S dt+ sigma S dW , B>S_0 , and tau_B = inf t: S_t = B . Derive E[e^- lambda tau_B] . Let X_t= ln(S_t/S_0) . By Itô, dX = nu dt+ sigma dW with nu= mu- tfrac12 sigma^2 , and tau_B is the first passage of X to b= ln(B/S_0)>0 . For any theta , M_t = exp( theta X_t - ( theta nu+ tfrac12 theta^2 sigma^2)t) is a martingale","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S004 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Compute E[ int_0^T W_t dW_t] and int_0^T W_t dW_t in closed form. From d(W_t^2) = 2W_t dW_t + dt (Itô on x^2 ), integrate: W_T^2 = 2 int_0^T W_t dW_t + T implies int_0^T W_t dW_t = tfrac12(W_T^2 - T). Its expectation is tfrac12(T-T)=0 , as required for an Itô integral of a square-integrable integrand. Note the contrast with ordinary calcu","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S005 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two assets follow correlated GBMs with correlation rho . What is the SDE for their ratio R_t=S^1_t/S^2_t , and what is its volatility? Apply the two-dimensional Itô formula to f(x,y)=x/y , with f_x=1/y , f_y=-x/y^2 , f_xy=-1/y^2 , f_yy=2x/y^3 , f_xx=0 : dR = (dS^1)/(S^2) - (S^1)/((S^2)^2)dS^2 - (1)/((S^2)^2)d[S^1,S^2] + (S^1)/((S^2)^3)d[S","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S006 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"X is OU with kappa=2 , theta=0 , sigma=0.3 , starting at X_0=1 . What is P(X_1 > 0.5) ? From the closed-form solution (Volume 1.6), X_1 is Gaussian with E[X_1] = 0 + (1-0)e^-2 = e^-2 approx 0.1353, Var[X_1] = (0.09)/(4)(1-e^-4) = 0.0225(1-0.0183) = 0.02209, sd = 0.1486. P(X_1>0.5) = 1- Phi ((0.5-0.1353)/(0.1486)) = 1- Phi(2.454) approx 0.","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S007 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that a delta-hedged long call position, rebalanced discretely at intervals Delta t , has P&L approximately tfrac12 Gamma S^2[( Delta S/S)^2 - sigma^2 Delta t] . Over one interval, expand the option value to second order in Delta S and first order in time: Delta V approx Theta Delta t + Delta Delta S + tfrac12 Gamma( Delta S)^2. The h","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S008 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"A stock follows GBM. What is P(S_T > S_0) , and why is it less than tfrac12 when mu=0 ? ln(S_T/S_0) sim mathcal N (( mu- tfrac12 sigma^2)T, sigma^2T) , so P(S_T>S_0) = Phi ((( mu- tfrac12 sigma^2)T)/( sigma sqrt T)). With mu=0 this is Phi(- tfrac12 sigma sqrt T) < tfrac12 . The reason is the distinction between mean and median. With mu=0 ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S009 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Derive the Black–Scholes call price from the risk-neutral expectation C = e^-rTE^ mathbb Q[(S_T-K)^+] . Under mathbb Q , S_T = S_0 exp((r- tfrac12 sigma^2)T+ sigma sqrt T Z) , Z sim mathcal N(0,1) . The option is in the money when Z > z^* = ( ln(K/S_0)-(r- tfrac12 sigma^2)T)/( sigma sqrt T) = -d_2. Split the expectation: e^rTC = int_-d_2^","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S010 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the quadratic covariation [X,Y]_T for dX= sigma_1 dW^1 , dY = sigma_2 dW^2 with d langle W^1,W^2 rangle = rho dt ? Prove the polarisation identity. [X,Y]_T = rho sigma_1 sigma_2 T . Polarisation. Quadratic variation is a quadratic form, so [X,Y] = tfrac12([X+Y,X+Y]-[X,X]-[Y,Y]). Here X+Y has instantaneous variance sigma_1^2+ sigma","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S011 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Prove that a non-negative local martingale is a supermartingale, and give a financial example where the distinction matters. Proof. Let M ge0 be a local martingale with localising sequence tau_n uparrow infty , so each M^ tau_n is a true martingale. For s<t , Fatou's lemma for conditional expectations gives E[M_t mid mathcal F_s] = E [ li","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S012 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"A spread follows an OU process with half-life 10 days and equilibrium standard deviation 1.0. You enter at z=2 . What is the expected time to revert to z=0 ? The conditional mean decays deterministically: E[X_t mid X_0] = X_0e^- kappa t with kappa = ln2/10 = 0.0693 per day. The mean reaches zero only asymptotically, so the right question ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S013 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that under the risk-neutral measure, the forward price F_t = S_te^r(T-t) is a martingale, and explain why this makes the forward the natural quantity to model. Under mathbb Q , dS = rS dt+ sigma S dW^ mathbb Q . Apply Itô to F(t,S)=Se^r(T-t) : F_t = -rSe^r(T-t) , F_S = e^r(T-t) , F_SS=0 : dF = -rSe^r(T-t)dt + e^r(T-t)(rS dt+ sigma S ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S014 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"dS = mu S dt + sigma S dW . Find the SDE followed by Y = S^n for integer n , and the growth rate of E[Y] . Itô with f(x)=x^n : f'=nx^n-1 , f''=n(n-1)x^n-2 . dY = (n mu S^n + tfrac12 sigma^2S^2 n(n-1)S^n-2)dt + sigma n S^n dW = Y[(n mu + tfracn(n-1)2 sigma^2)dt + n sigma dW]. So Y is GBM with drift n mu + tfracn(n-1)2 sigma^2 and volatilit","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S015 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"A gambler with wealth x plays a game with edge p> tfrac12 , betting a fixed fraction f each round. Using a martingale argument, find the probability of ever being ruined (reaching 0 ) under continuous approximation. Under the continuous (Kelly) approximation, log-wealth follows d ln V = (f mu - tfrac12f^2 sigma^2)dt + f sigma dW — arithme","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S016 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Explain why int_0^T sigma_t dW_t has a Gaussian distribution when sigma is deterministic, but not when sigma is stochastic. Deterministic sigma . Approximate by simple functions: the integral is a limit of sums sum sigma_t_i(W_t_i+1-W_t_i) of independent Gaussians with fixed coefficients, hence Gaussian, with variance int_0^T sigma_t^2dt ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S017 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"State the Girsanov theorem and use it to price a digital (cash-or-nothing) call paying 1 if S_T>K . <p><strong>Girsanov.</strong> If Z_T=\\exp(-\\int_0^T\\Lambda_t dW_t - \\tfrac12\\int_0^T\\Lambda_t^2dt)$ satisfies Novikov's condition and d mathbb Q/d mathbb P = Z_T , then widetilde W_t = W_t+ int_0^t Lambda_s ds is a mathbb Q -Brownian motion","s":"s017 — stochastic calculus & martingales state the girsanov theorem and use it to price a digital (cash-or-nothing) call paying 1 if s_t>k . <p><strong>girsanov.</strong> if z_t=\\exp(-\\int_0^t\\lambda_t dw_t - \\tfrac12\\int_0^t\\lambda_t^2dt)$ satisfies novikov's condition and d mathbb q/d mathbb p = z_t , then widetilde w_t = w_t+ int_0^t lambda_s ds is a mathbb q -brownian motion"},{"id":"vol23","t":"S018 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that for a Brownian bridge from 0 at time 0 to 0 at time T , Var(B_t) = t(T-t)/T . Where does this appear in trading? The bridge is B_t = W_t - tfractTW_T . Then Var(B_t) = Var(W_t) - 2 tfrac tTCov(W_t,W_T) + tfract^2T^2Var(W_T) = t - tfrac2t^2T + tfract^2T = (t(T-t))/(T). Maximised at t=T/2 with value T/4 . blacksquare Where it appe","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S019 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Derive the expected maximum drawdown of a Brownian motion with drift mu>0 and volatility sigma over [0,T] , at least to leading order. Let X_t = mu t + sigma W_t and D_T = max_0 le s le t le T(X_s - X_t) . For large T the drawdown process is approximately a renewal process of independent excursions below running maxima. From S015, the pro","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"S020 — Stochastic Calculus & Martingales","v":"Vol 23","x":"Why can you not hedge a jump risk with the underlying, even continuously? Quantify the residual. Delta hedging cancels the first-order term V_S Delta S . For a diffusion, Delta S is small over dt and the residual tfrac12V_SS( Delta S)^2 = O(dt) is a controlled, predictable cost — the theta. For a jump of size J (a finite, non-infinitesima","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T001 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"You observe 252 daily returns with mean 0.05 % and standard deviation 1 % . Is the mean significantly different from zero? Standard error of the mean is 0.01/sqrt(252) = 0.00063 , so t = (0.0005)/(0.00063) = 0.79, nowhere near significance ( p approx0.43 ). Note this is exactly the annualised Sharpe ratio in disguise: annualised return 12","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T002 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Derive the standard error of an estimated correlation coefficient, and state Fisher's transformation. For bivariate normal data with true correlation rho , the sample correlation r has approximate variance Var(r) approx ((1- rho^2)^2)/(n). The distribution is skewed and bounded, so confidence intervals built from this directly are poor ne","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T003 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that the OLS estimator hat beta = (X^ top X)^-1X^ top y is BLUE, and state exactly which assumption fails for financial time series. Gauss–Markov. Under y = X beta+ varepsilon with E[ varepsilon]=0 and Var( varepsilon)= sigma^2 I , consider any other linear unbiased estimator tilde beta = Cy with C = (X^ top X)^-1X^ top + D . Unbiase","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T004 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Why does R^2 always increase when you add a regressor? What should you use instead? OLS minimises the residual sum of squares over a larger parameter space when a column is added, and the previous solution remains available by setting the new coefficient to zero. So RSS cannot increase and R^2 = 1 - RSS/TSS cannot decrease — even for a co","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T005 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two strategies have Sharpe ratios 1.2 and 0.9 over the same 3 years. Is the difference significant? Use the Jobson–Korkie test with the Memmel correction. For correlated strategies with correlation rho between their return series, the variance of the Sharpe difference is Var( hat S_1- hat S_2) approx (1)/(T)[2(1- rho) + tfrac12(S_1^2+S_2^","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T006 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Explain the difference between Type I error, Type II error, and power, using a strategy-selection example. Strategy really works Strategy is worthless You deploy it Correct (power =1- beta ) Type I error, rate alpha You reject it Type II error, rate beta Correct The asymmetry that matters. A Type I error costs you real money: you trade a ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T007 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Derive the bias–variance decomposition and explain why a deliberately biased estimator can beat an unbiased one. For an estimator hat f of a true value f , with y = f+ varepsilon and Var( varepsilon)= sigma^2 : E [(y- hat f)^2] = underbrace(E[ hat f]-f)^2_bias^2 + underbraceE [( hat f - E hat f)^2]_variance + underbrace sigma^2_irreducibl","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T008 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the standard error of an estimated volatility, and how many observations do you need for 10% relative precision? For i.i.d. normal returns, (n-1) hat sigma^2/ sigma^2 sim chi^2_n-1 , giving Var( hat sigma^2)=2 sigma^4/(n-1) . By the delta method on sqrt( cdot) : Var( hat sigma) approx( sigma^2)/(2(n-1)) implies (se( hat sigma))/( ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T009 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"You test 100 signals at the 5% level and 8 are significant. How many are real? Under the global null, expect 100 times0.05 = 5 false positives, so 8 discoveries is barely more than chance. Benjamini–Hochberg. Sort p -values ascending; find the largest k with p_(k) le (k)/(m)q ; reject the first k . At q=0.10 and m=100 , the 8th smallest p","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T010 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Why is the sample maximum a biased estimator of a distribution's upper bound, and how do you correct it for U(0, theta) ? The maximum of n draws from U(0, theta) satisfies E[X_(n)] = (n)/(n+1) theta < theta — biased downward, always, because the maximum of a finite sample cannot exceed theta but is almost surely below it. The unbiased cor","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T011 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Derive the Kalman filter update equations from a Bayesian argument. State: x_t = Fx_t-1+w_t , w sim mathcal N(0,Q) . Observation: z_t = Hx_t+v_t , v sim mathcal N(0,R) . Predict. The prior for x_t given data through t-1 is Gaussian with hat x_t|t-1=F hat x_t-1, P_t|t-1=FP_t-1F^ top+Q. Update. Bayes with Gaussian prior and Gaussian likelih","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T012 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the difference between a p -value and the probability the null hypothesis is true? A p -value is P(data at least this extreme mid H_0) . The posterior probability of the null is P(H_0 middata) . These are different conditionals and can differ enormously. By Bayes, with prior odds pi_0/ pi_1 and Bayes factor B : (P(H_0 mid D))/(P(H","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T013 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Given a covariance matrix Sigma , prove that the minimum-variance portfolio weights are w = frac Sigma^-1 mathbf 1 mathbf 1^ top Sigma^-1 mathbf 1 . Minimise tfrac12 w^ top Sigma w subject to w^ top mathbf 1 = 1 . Lagrangian L = tfrac12 w^ top Sigma w - lambda(w^ top mathbf 1-1) . ( partial L)/( partial w) = Sigma w - lambda mathbf 1 = 0 ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T014 — Linear Algebra & Optimisation","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the condition number of a matrix and why does it determine how much you can trust Sigma^-1 mu ? kappa(A) = |A | |A^-1 | = lambda_ max/ lambda_ min for symmetric positive definite A . The governing inequality: a relative perturbation delta in the inputs produces a relative error in the solution of x = A^-1b bounded by ( | Delta x |","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T015 — Linear Algebra & Optimisation","v":"Vol 23","x":"State the Marchenko–Pastur law and use it to decide how many principal components of a correlation matrix are signal. For an N times T matrix of i.i.d. standardised noise with q=N/T le1 , the empirical eigenvalue density of the sample correlation matrix converges to rho( lambda) = (1)/(2 pi q lambda)sqrt(( lambda_+- lambda)( lambda- lambd","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T016 — Linear Algebra & Optimisation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Why is Cholesky decomposition preferred over eigendecomposition for generating correlated random numbers? To generate x sim mathcal N(0, Sigma) from z sim mathcal N(0,I) you need any L with LL^ top= Sigma ; then x=Lz . Cholesky gives lower-triangular L in O(N^3/3) operations, and applying it costs O(N^2/2) because of the triangular struct","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T017 — Linear Algebra & Optimisation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Prove that any correlation matrix must be positive semi-definite, and explain what it means when an estimated one is not. Proof. For any weight vector a in mathbb R^N , the portfolio Y = sum_i a_iX_i has variance Var(Y) = sum_i,ja_ia_jCov(X_i,X_j) = a^ top Sigma a ge 0, since a variance cannot be negative. Hence Sigma succeq0 , and the sa","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T018 — Linear Algebra & Optimisation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that PCA on returns is equivalent to finding the maximum-variance linear combinations, and explain why the first component is almost always the market. Maximise Var(w^ top r) = w^ top Sigma w subject to |w |=1 . The Lagrangian gives Sigma w = lambda w : the stationary points are eigenvectors, and the maximum is attained at the eigenv","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T019 — Linear Algebra & Optimisation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Derive the Sherman–Morrison formula and give a trading application. Claim. (A+uv^ top)^-1 = A^-1 - dfracA^-1uv^ top A^-11+v^ top A^-1u , valid when 1+v^ top A^-1u ne 0 . Proof. Multiply the right-hand side by (A+uv^ top) and let alpha = 1+v^ top A^-1u : (A^-1- fracA^-1uv^ top A^-1 alpha)(A+uv^ top) = I + A^-1uv^ top - fracA^-1uv^ top alph","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"T020 — Statistics & Estimation","v":"Vol 23","x":"Your backtest shows a 62% win rate on 400 trades. Is this evidence of skill? Under the null p=0.5 , the number of wins is Binomial(400, 0.5) with mean 200 and standard deviation sqrt(400 times0.25)=10 . You observed 248 wins: z = (248-200)/(10) = 4.8, p < 10^-5. Statistically overwhelming. And yet it is not evidence of profitability , for","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O001 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"A stock is at 100, and a 1-year at-the-money call costs 10. What is roughly the implied volatility? (Rates zero.) Use the ATM approximation. With S=K and r=q=0 , d_1 = tfrac12 sigma sqrt T , d_2=-d_1 , so C = S[ Phi(d_1)- Phi(-d_1)] = S[2 Phi ( tfrac sigma sqrt T2)-1] approx S phi(0) sigma sqrt T = fracS sigma sqrt Tsqrt(2 pi) approx 0.4 ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O002 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"You are long a straddle. The stock does not move for a week. Decompose your loss. Three separate effects, which people routinely conflate: Theta. The dominant term. You paid for a week of variance and got none. Over a week the loss is roughly tfrac12 Gamma S^2 sigma^2 Delta t — for a 1-year ATM straddle at sigma=25 % , about 1% of the str","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O003 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that a butterfly spread's price must be non-negative, and derive what this implies for the implied volatility smile. A butterfly long one call at K- Delta , short two at K , long one at K+ Delta has payoff max( Delta - |S_T-K|, 0) ge 0 , so its price must be ge0 : C(K- Delta) - 2C(K) + C(K+ Delta) ge 0. Dividing by Delta^2 and lettin","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O004 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"Why is an American call on a non-dividend-paying stock never exercised early? When does the argument fail? Early exercise gives S-K . The alternative — sell the option — gives at least its European value, and C_Eur ge S - Ke^-rT > S-K for r>0, T>0. The first inequality follows from the payoff being convex with a lower bound; the second be","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O005 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"What is the vega of a variance swap, and why is it constant? A variance swap pays N_var( sigma_R^2 - K_var^2) on realised variance sigma_R^2 . Its value is linear in variance, so ( partial V)/( partial ( sigma^2)) = N_var (constant), ( partial V)/( partial sigma) = 2 sigma N_var (linear in sigma). So variance-vega is constant, while volat","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O006 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"You are short 100 ATM calls with delta 0.5 each. The stock gaps up 10%. What happens to your hedge? You were hedged with 100 times100 times0.5 = 5,000 shares long (100 contracts of 100 shares). After a 10% gap, delta rises — say from 0.50 to about 0.70 for a medium-dated ATM option. You now need 7,000 shares, so you must buy 2,000 shares ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O007 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"Explain dispersion trading and identify the risk that closes it out. Index variance relates to constituent variances through the correlation matrix: sigma_I^2 = sum_i w_i^2 sigma_i^2 + sum_i ne jw_iw_j rho_ij sigma_i sigma_j. Implied index volatility is systematically lower than the weighted average of implied single-stock volatilities, b","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"O008 — Options & Derivatives","v":"Vol 23","x":"A stock trades at 50. What is the price of a European put with strike 50 expiring tomorrow, if daily volatility is 2%? Use the ATM approximation from O001, adapted to a put (identical by parity at ATM with zero rates): P approx 0.4 S sigma_daily = 0.4 times50 times0.02 = $0.40. Equivalently: the expected absolute move is S sigmasqrt(2/ pi","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M001 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"I roll two fair dice. Make me a market on the sum. The mean is 7 and the standard deviation is sqrt(35/6) approx2.42 . Quote around fair value with a spread reflecting your uncertainty and the adverse selection you face: “6.5 at 7.5” is a reasonable opening market. What is actually being tested. Not the mean — they know you can compute 7.","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M002 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"I roll a die. If you like the number you may re-roll, up to twice more. Make a market on your final score. Work backwards. With one roll left, the expected value is 3.5. With two rolls, re-roll anything below 3.5, i.e. 1, 2 or 3: V_2 = tfrac12(3.5) + tfrac16(4+5+6) = 1.75+2.5 = 4.25. With three rolls, re-roll anything below 4.25, i.e. 1–4","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M003 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"You quote a two-sided market. An informed trader lifts your offer. How should you update, and what does Glosten–Milgrom say the equilibrium spread is? Update. A buy is evidence the value is higher than you thought. With informed fraction mu , prior pi = P(V = V_H) , and uninformed traders buying or selling with probability tfrac12 each: P","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M004 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two players simultaneously name a number between 0 and 100. The winner is whoever is closest to two-thirds of the average. What do you name? Iterated elimination of dominated strategies. Nobody should name above tfrac23 times100 = 66.7 , since that can never win. Given that, nobody should name above tfrac23 times66.7=44.4 . Iterating driv","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M005 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"In a sealed-bid common-value auction for an oil tract, each of n bidders receives an unbiased private signal of the true value. Why do naive bidders lose money, and by how much should you shade? The winner's curse. You win when your signal is the highest , which means conditional on winning your signal is biased upward. Bidding your unbia","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M006 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"You can see the order book but not who is trading. How would you detect the presence of a large iceberg order? Signatures, in rough order of reliability: Instant replenishment. The displayed size at a level is consumed and immediately restored to the same quantity, repeatedly. Genuine independent liquidity would arrive with varying size a","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"M007 — Market Making & Game Theory","v":"Vol 23","x":"Design a fair way for two people to divide a continuously divisible cake when they value regions differently. Extend to three people. Two people: “I cut, you choose.” The cutter divides into two pieces they value equally (so they are indifferent); the chooser takes the piece they prefer (worth at least half by their own measure). Both rec","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R001 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two uncorrelated strategies each have Sharpe 1. What is the Sharpe of an equal-risk combination? Equal risk allocation, each contributing volatility sigma . Combined return is 2 mu ; combined volatility is sigma sqrt2 by independence. So S_comb = (2 mu)/( sigma sqrt2) = sqrt2 ( mu)/( sigma) = sqrt2 approx 1.41. Generally, n uncorrelated e","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R002 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"Your strategy has annualised Sharpe 1.5 in backtest. What is the probability of a losing year? Of a losing month? Assuming i.i.d. normal returns with annualised Sharpe S , over a horizon of t years the mean is S sqrt t standard deviations from zero, so P(loss over t years) = Phi(-S sqrt t). One year: Phi(-1.5) = 6.7 % One month ( t=1/12 )","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R003 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"Show that maximising the Sharpe ratio and maximising expected utility for a mean–variance investor give the same portfolio up to leverage. When does this break? Maximising U = w^ top mu - tfrac lambda2 w^ top Sigma w gives w_U = tfrac1 lambda Sigma^-1 mu . Maximising S(w) = dfracw^ top musqrt(w^ top Sigma w) : since S is scale-invariant (","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R004 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"You have a 20% drawdown. What return do you need to recover, and how long should it take? Recovery requires dfrac11-0.20 - 1 = 25 % . The asymmetry grows sharply: a 50% drawdown needs 100%, a 75% drawdown needs 300%. Expected time. With annualised return mu and volatility sigma , the drift in log terms is mu - tfrac12 sigma^2 and recoveri","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R005 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"Your portfolio has 10% annualised volatility. What is the 1-day 99% VaR under normality? Why will you breach it more than 1% of the time? Daily volatility is 0.10/sqrt(252) = 0.63 % . The 99% quantile of a standard normal is 2.326 , so VaR_99 % = 2.326 times0.63 % = 1.47 %. Why breaches exceed 1%: Fat tails. Daily equity returns have kurt","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R006 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"Explain why leveraged ETFs decay, and compute the drag for a 3× fund on an index with 25% annual volatility. A daily-rebalanced k times ETF delivers k times the daily return, not k times the period return. In continuous time, if the index follows dS/S = mu dt+ sigma dW , the fund follows dL/L = k mu dt+k sigma dW , so by Itô the log growt","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R007 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"You run 20 strategies, each with Sharpe 0.5, pairwise correlation 0.2. What is the portfolio Sharpe, and how many effectively independent bets do you have? With equal weights and equicorrelation rho : S_p = Ssqrt((n)/(1+(n-1) rho)) = 0.5sqrt((20)/(1+19(0.2))) = 0.5sqrt((20)/(4.8)) = 0.5 times2.041 = 1.02. Effective number of bets. Compari","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"R008 — Portfolio & Risk","v":"Vol 23","x":"A pension fund must not lose more than 10% in a year with 95% confidence. What volatility can it run? Under normality with expected return mu and volatility sigma , the 5th percentile is mu - 1.645 sigma ge -0.10 . With mu = 6 % : sigma le (0.06+0.10)/(1.645) = 9.7 %. But this is the wrong answer to give, and saying so is the point of the","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A001 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Design a data structure supporting insert, delete, and “query current maximum” on a sliding window of the last k prices, in O(1) amortised time. A monotonic deque holding indices whose values are strictly decreasing from front to back. The front is always the window maximum. Push x : pop from the back while the back's value le x (those ca","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A002 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"You receive a stream of trades and must maintain a running median price. How? Two heaps. A max-heap for the lower half, a min-heap for the upper half, kept balanced to within one element. Insert: push to the appropriate heap by comparing with the current median, then rebalance by moving one element if the sizes differ by more than one. O(","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A003 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Given n price series, find all pairs with correlation above a threshold, faster than the naive O(n^2T) . Step 1: reduce correlation to distance. Standardise each series to zero mean and unit norm. Then |x-y |^2 = 2(1- rho_xy), so high correlation is exactly small Euclidean distance, and the problem becomes near-neighbour search. Step 2: r","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A004 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Your backtest takes 4 hours. Walk through how you would make it take 4 minutes. Profile first. cProfile for the function-level picture, line_profiler for the hot function. Guessing is how people spend a day optimising something that was 3% of runtime. Then, in expected-payoff order: Hoist DataFrame access out of loops. df.loc[i, 'col'] in","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A005 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Design a system that stores 10 years of tick data for 5,000 symbols and answers “give me all trades for symbol X between times A and B” in under a second. Scale. Roughly 100k–1M ticks per symbol-day; 5000 times2520 symbol-days gives order 10^12 ticks, or tens of terabytes raw. Layout. Partition by date, then by symbol: /data/trades/date=2","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A006 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"How would you detect that a live strategy has stopped working, as quickly as possible without excessive false alarms? Framed correctly this is sequential hypothesis testing, not a threshold on P&L. CUSUM is the right primitive. Track S_n = max(0, S_n-1 + ( mu_0 - r_n - k)) and alarm when S_n > h . It is optimal in the Lorden sense: minimu","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A007 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Implement an order-matching engine's core. What data structures, and what are the correctness requirements? Structures. Two price ladders (bids descending, asks ascending). Each level holds a FIFO queue of orders. For a bounded tick range, an array indexed by price offset with a bitmap of occupied levels beats a tree — O(1) level access a","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A008 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two processes must agree on which is the active trading process. How do you avoid both being active simultaneously? This is leader election, and the naive approaches all fail. Why heartbeats alone fail: a missed heartbeat cannot distinguish “the other process died” from “the network partitioned”. In a partition, both sides conclude the ot","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A009 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Given a stream of prices, find the maximum profit from a single buy-then-sell, in one pass. Track the running minimum and the best profit seen: best = 0 lo = prices[0] for p in prices[1:]: best = max(best, p - lo) lo = min(lo, p) O(n) time, O(1) space. The key observation is that the optimal sell at time t pairs with the minimum before t ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"A010 — Algorithms & Systems","v":"Vol 23","x":"Your P&L calculation disagrees with the broker's by 3.47$. How do you find the discrepancy? Do not start from the total. Decompose until the difference localises. Split by symbol. One symbol, or many? A single symbol points to a corporate action or a contract-specification error; a spread across all symbols points to fees or FX. Split by ","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"E001 — Estimation & Reasoning","v":"Vol 23","x":"How many golf balls fit in a school bus? Structure, stated assumptions, order-of-magnitude answer. Bus interior: roughly 12 m long, 2.5 m wide, 2 m high Rightarrow 60 m³. Subtract seats and wheel wells, say 25% Rightarrow 45 m³ usable. A golf ball is 42.7 mm diameter; its bounding cube is 4.3^3 approx 79 cm³. Random packing of spheres ach","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"E002 — Estimation & Reasoning","v":"Vol 23","x":"Estimate the daily revenue of a market maker quoting a single liquid US equity. Build it from the microstructure quantities in Volume 2. Take a liquid large-cap: 20 million shares/day, 200 price, \\ 4 bn daily notional. Quoted spread 1 cent on a 200$ stock is 0.5 bp; half-spread 0.25 bp. A single market maker among many captures perhaps 5%","s":1},{"id":"vol23","t":"E003 — Estimation & Reasoning","v":"Vol 23","x":"A strategy backtests at Sharpe 2.0 over 5 years. What Sharpe should you expect live, and why? Expect meaningfully less — plausibly 0.7 to 1.2. The haircuts, roughly in order of size: Selection bias. If this was the best of 100 configurations, Volume 18.1 says roughly 1.1 of the Sharpe is the expected maximum from noise over 5 years. This ","s":1},{"id":"23-1-how-to-use-this-bank","t":"23.1 How to use this bank","v":"Vol 23","x":"Attempt before opening. The solutions are collapsed by default for a reason. Five minutes of genuine effort on a problem you cannot solve teaches more than an hour of reading solutions you can follow. Say it out loud. Interview problems are assessed on the reasoning you narrate, not the answer you arrive at. Practise talking through the s","s":1},{"id":"23-2-the-bank","t":"23.2 The bank","v":"Vol 23","x":"Two specific adjustments. Practise out loud, with another person, before it matters. The technical content here will not be your constraint — with Intellect at 16/20 you will find most of these tractable on paper. The constraint is the interview format: being watched, being interrupted, being wrong in real time. Self-Consciousness at 14/2","s":1},{"id":"volgl","t":"Glossary: Every Term in Plain Words","v":"Vol GL","x":"Every technical term in the reference, defined in words that assume nothing. Every one of them is also tappable wherever it first appears in a volume — the dotted underline means a definition is one tap away, so you never have to leave a paragraph to find out what a word means. If a definition here still leaves you stuck, that is information rather than a failure: it usually means a prerequisite is missing rather than that the term is hard. Follow the volume link on the entry.","s":1},{"id":"gl-1-every-term-alphabetically","t":"GL.1 Every term, alphabetically","v":"Vol GL","x":">GL.1 Every term, alphabetically ¶ 227 terms adverse selection Vol 2 The people who take your resting order are disproportionately the ones who know something you do not. It is why quoting a spread is not free money. Almgren–Chriss Vol 10 The standard answer to how fast to execute a large order: trade quickly and pay impact, or slowly and risk the price running away. It gives the optimal middle. alpha Vol 5 Return that is not explained by simply taking known risks. If a strategy makes 12% and its market exposure al","s":1},{"id":"vol24","t":"Execution Blueprint: A System Designed Around Your Profile","v":"Vol 24","x":"This volume takes the Big Five report you supplied and turns it into concrete design constraints for a trading system. Before it does, three honest caveats about how much weight that report can bear. Big Five facets predict behavioural tendencies with modest effect sizes. Typical correlations between a facet and any specific behaviour are","s":1},{"id":"24-1-your-profile","t":"24.1 Your profile","v":"Vol 24","x":"Two additional metrics from the report are relevant here: a Burnout Buffer of 44 (mid) , which is a sustainability constraint rather than a trait, and a Conflict Temperature of 65 . The report also identifies Emotionality as your top strength and Morality as the facet most likely to need support.","s":1},{"id":"24-2-the-honest-summary","t":"24.2 The honest summary","v":"Vol 24","x":"Orderliness 16/20 — your highest facet, and the single most useful trait in this entire discipline. Point-in-time data discipline, reconciliation, correctness checks, careful cost models: this is exactly the work that separates real research from plausible research, and it is work you will do well and find satisfying. Intellect 16 and Ima","s":1},{"id":"24-2-the-honest-summary","t":"24.2 The honest summary","v":"Vol 24","x":"One combination in your profile matters more than everything else here, and it is worth naming precisely: Orderliness 16  +  Self-Efficacy 11  +  Immoderation 15  +  Vulnerability 13. You will build a genuinely good system, doubt it precisely when it is working through a normal drawdown, and have difficulty resisting the urge to intervene","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-1-overriding-the-system-during-a-drawdo","t":"Failure mode 1 — Overriding the system during a drawdown","v":"Vol 24","x":"Driven by: Self-Efficacy 11 (doubting your own competence under pressure), Vulnerability 13 (panic under stress), Anxiety 16. Looks like: reducing size, skipping signals, or flattening the book during a normal drawdown, then missing the recovery. This is the most expensive failure mode available to a systematic trader, because it converts","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-1-overriding-the-system-during-a-drawdo","t":"Failure mode 1 — Overriding the system during a drawdown","v":"Vol 24","x":"Controls: Automatic de-risking ladder, in code, in advance. At −8% from high-water mark, halve size. At −15%, flatten and stop. Because the system reduces risk before you feel the need to, the intervention you would have made manually has already happened — correctly sized and at the right time. Read Volume 12.3 before you need it. A Shar","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-2-adding-to-a-losing-position","t":"Failure mode 2 — Adding to a losing position","v":"Vol 24","x":"Driven by: Immoderation 15 (short-term reward orientation, difficulty resisting an urge), interacting with the fact that many models genuinely say a wider spread is a better entry. Looks like: scaling into a diverging statistical-arbitrage spread, averaging down, revenge trading after a loss. This is the mechanism behind the majority of i","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-2-adding-to-a-losing-position","t":"Failure mode 2 — Adding to a losing position","v":"Vol 24","x":"Controls: Maximum position fixed at entry, enforced at the broker or gateway — not in your own script, which you can edit in thirty seconds. If a limit lives somewhere you can change it while emotional, it is a suggestion. Scaling-in disabled by default. If a strategy genuinely requires it, the schedule must be fully specified in advance ","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-3-research-that-never-converges","t":"Failure mode 3 — Research that never converges","v":"Vol 24","x":"Driven by: Imagination 16 and Intellect 16 generating hypotheses faster than any honest validation budget can absorb, with Orderliness 16 making it pleasant to test all of them thoroughly. Looks like: months of interesting work, hundreds of variants, an ever-rising trial count, and no strategy in production. Careful execution of an overfi","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-3-research-that-never-converges","t":"Failure mode 3 — Research that never converges","v":"Vol 24","x":"Controls: Pre-register every research cycle. Hypothesis, number of configurations, deflated-Sharpe threshold — committed to version control with a timestamp before the first fit. Exceed the stated trial count and the cycle ends; the result is discarded, not deflated. Automatic trial counting (Volume 21.1). A counter you have to remember t","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-4-infrastructure-as-procrastination","t":"Failure mode 4 — Infrastructure as procrastination","v":"Vol 24","x":"Driven by: Orderliness 16 combined with Self-Efficacy 11. Building a beautiful backtester has a definite right answer and immediate, verifiable feedback; running a strategy does not. When the uncertain thing feels threatening, the certain thing becomes very attractive. Looks like: refactoring the event loop for elegance, optimising a back","s":1},{"id":"failure-mode-4-infrastructure-as-procrastination","t":"Failure mode 4 — Infrastructure as procrastination","v":"Vol 24","x":"Controls: A stated completion criterion, written before you start building. The backtester is finished when it passes the six correctness checks in Volume 16.3 and runs your candidate in under two minutes. After that, further work on it is procrastination however defensible each individual improvement sounds. Alternate weeks between infra","s":1},{"id":"24-4-the-drawdown-protocol","t":"24.4 The drawdown protocol","v":"Vol 24","x":"Write this out, print it, and keep it where you will see it. The purpose of a protocol is to be followed at a moment when you will not want to think.","s":1},{"id":"24-4-the-drawdown-protocol","t":"24.4 The drawdown protocol","v":"Vol 24","x":"The diagnostic checklist — run in this order, and write the answers down: Is realised slippage in line with the model? (Most “broken” strategies are cost problems.) Is the signal's information coefficient still in its historical range? IC decays visibly long before P&L does. Have factor exposures drifted from what you tested? Is the drawd","s":1},{"id":"24-5-reporting-discipline","t":"24.5 Reporting discipline","v":"Vol 24","x":"How you observe your own results changes how you behave, and for your profile the default is harmful. Daily P&L on a Sharpe-1 strategy is negative 46% of the time (Volume 23, R002). Watching a number that disappoints you nearly every other day, with Depression at 14/20 and Self-Consciousness at 14/20, imposes a real cost and produces no i","s":1},{"id":"24-5-reporting-discipline","t":"24.5 Reporting discipline","v":"Vol 24","x":"Look at P&L monthly. Not daily, not per-trade. Set a fixed day. Report at the portfolio level , never per trade. A trend strategy is wrong 60% of the time by design; watching individual losses is watching the strategy work correctly and feeling it as failure. Track process metrics weekly instead — did the system run, did reconciliation pa","s":1},{"id":"24-6-cognitive-biases-weighted-for-you","t":"24.6 Cognitive biases, weighted for you","v":"Vol 24","x":"The useful observation is the last row but one. Most trading psychology material is written for overconfident traders, because most traders are. It will tell you to be humbler, size smaller and doubt yourself more — advice that is actively wrong for you. Your risk is the opposite: a good system abandoned too early. Read generic trading ps","s":1},{"id":"24-7-sustainability","t":"24.7 Sustainability","v":"Vol 24","x":"Your Burnout Buffer of 44 is reported as mid-range, and Activity Level at 9/20 with Depression at 14/20 suggests that energy is a genuine constraint rather than a matter of willingness. This has direct design consequences, and they are not soft ones — a schedule you cannot sustain produces a system that stops being maintained, and an unma","s":1},{"id":"24-7-sustainability","t":"24.7 Sustainability","v":"Vol 24","x":"Budget 8–12 focused hours a week, not 30. The curriculum in Volume 25 is paced to this deliberately. A twelve-month plan you complete beats a six-month plan you abandon in month three. Fixed hours, fixed days. Ambient availability — checking positions in the evening, thinking about a bug at midnight — is where the cost accumulates, and it","s":1},{"id":"24-7-sustainability","t":"24.7 Sustainability","v":"Vol 24","x":"Trading is unusually good at converting existing anxiety into a loop that feeds itself: uncertainty produces checking, checking produces more uncertainty, and losses are experienced as evidence about you rather than as variance. Your profile — Anxiety 16, Depression 14, Vulnerability 13, Self-Consciousness 14 — means this is a live risk f","s":1},{"id":"24-8-the-one-page-summary","t":"24.8 The one-page summary","v":"Vol 24","x":"Quarter-Kelly sizing, never more. A smaller book held for years beats a larger one abandoned. Position and loss limits enforced at the broker, not in your own code. Automatic de-risk at −8%, automatic halt at −15%. No discretion in either. Scaling into losers disabled at the system level. 24-hour mandatory delay on any limit increase or m","s":1},{"id":"vol25","t":"Twelve-Month Curriculum, Paced and Gated","v":"Vol 25","x":"Most quant curricula are ordered by subject and sized for someone with unlimited time and unlimited tolerance for open-ended work. This one is ordered by what unblocks the next thing and budgeted at 8–12 focused hours per week , which is the level your Burnout Buffer (44, mid) and Activity Level (9/20) suggest is sustainable for a year. T","s":1},{"id":"25-1-quarter-1-foundations-and-the-data-layer","t":"25.1 Quarter 1 — Foundations and the data layer","v":"Vol 25","x":"Objective: a correct, point-in-time data store and a backtester you trust. No strategy work at all. This is deliberate: everything after this quarter is worthless if this quarter is wrong, and nothing in it depends on the market cooperating.","s":1},{"id":"25-1-quarter-1-foundations-and-the-data-layer","t":"25.1 Quarter 1 — Foundations and the data layer","v":"Vol 25","x":"Backtester passes the zero-signal, constant-signal, shuffled-returns and reversed-time tests exactly. Cash conservation asserts hold on every bar. You can produce, for any historical date, the exact tradeable universe as it stood on that date. Delisted names are present in the history with terminal returns booked. A full backtest runs in ","s":"25.1 quarter 1 — foundations and the data layer backtester passes the zero-signal, constant-signal, shuffled-returns and reversed-time tests exactly. cash conservation asserts hold on every bar. you can produce, for any historical date, the exact tradeable universe as it stood on that date. delisted names are present in the history with terminal returns booked. a full backtest runs in  paced for consistency rather than intensity, and gated so that a missing foundation stops you rather than quietly accumulating. four quarters chained left to right: foundations and data, one strategy end to end, live at small size, then consolidate or specialise"},{"id":"25-2-quarter-2-a-first-strategy-end-to-end","t":"25.2 Quarter 2 — A first strategy, end to end","v":"Vol 25","x":"Objective: one simple, honest, complete strategy, paper-traded. Trend following on liquid futures, because Volume 13.6 explains why it fits your profile better than any alternative here: low decision frequency, fully specifiable rules, automatic de-risking, and no counterparty relationships required.","s":1},{"id":"25-2-quarter-2-a-first-strategy-end-to-end","t":"25.2 Quarter 2 — A first strategy, end to end","v":"Vol 25","x":"During paper trading you change nothing. Not a parameter, not a filter, not the universe. The purpose is to measure the gap between your model and reality, and any change during the period destroys the measurement. This will be uncomfortable — the urge to improve something you are watching every day is exactly the Immoderation pattern fro","s":1},{"id":"25-2-quarter-2-a-first-strategy-end-to-end","t":"25.2 Quarter 2 — A first strategy, end to end","v":"Vol 25","x":"Deflated Sharpe computed with an honest trial count from the automatic log, and above 0.95. Strategy remains profitable at 2× your modelled costs. Realised paper slippage within 50% of the model's estimate. Risk system tested by simulated breach: de-risk and halt both fire correctly. You have written one paragraph explaining why this stra","s":1},{"id":"25-3-quarter-3-live-at-small-size-and-deeper-researc","t":"25.3 Quarter 3 — Live at small size, and deeper research","v":"Vol 25","x":"Objective: real money, deliberately small, plus a second uncorrelated signal. The capital at risk in this quarter should be an amount whose total loss would be irritating rather than significant — its purpose is to teach you how you behave with real money, not to earn anything.","s":1},{"id":"25-3-quarter-3-live-at-small-size-and-deeper-researc","t":"25.3 Quarter 3 — Live at small size, and deeper research","v":"Vol 25","x":"The first two weeks of live trading will teach you more about your own psychology than the preceding six months of reading. Watch specifically for: checking positions outside your scheduled session, wanting to adjust a parameter after a losing day, and feeling a loss as a statement about you rather than about variance. Journal those react","s":1},{"id":"25-3-quarter-3-live-at-small-size-and-deeper-researc","t":"25.3 Quarter 3 — Live at small size, and deeper research","v":"Vol 25","x":"Eight consecutive weeks live with zero manual overrides. Zero unexplained reconciliation breaks. Two strategies running with measured correlation below 0.4. You looked at P&L monthly, as specified. (Be honest with yourself about this one.) Kill switch tested in production at least once.","s":1},{"id":"25-4-quarter-4-consolidate-or-specialise","t":"25.4 Quarter 4 — Consolidate, or specialise","v":"Vol 25","x":"Objective: depending on what the first three quarters revealed, either deepen the existing book or build the evidence for a role. These are genuinely different paths and you should pick one rather than attempting both.","s":1},{"id":"25-5-what-is-deliberately-excluded","t":"25.5 What is deliberately excluded","v":"Vol 25","x":"These are the volumes you should not spend time on this year, with the reason:","s":1},{"id":"25-5-what-is-deliberately-excluded","t":"25.5 What is deliberately excluded","v":"Vol 25","x":"These will be the most tempting items on the list, precisely because they are the most interesting. Excluding them is not a judgement about their value — it is a judgement about sequencing under a fixed weekly budget. Everything here is available next year. The data layer, the risk system and the habit of not overriding are not things you","s":1},{"id":"25-6-weekly-rhythm","t":"25.6 Weekly rhythm","v":"Vol 25","x":"Total: roughly 9–12 hours a week. If a week goes badly and you do four hours, that is a normal week, not a failure — the plan has twelve months of slack in it precisely because some weeks will.","s":1},{"id":"25-7-the-honest-expectation","t":"25.7 The honest expectation","v":"Vol 25","x":"At the end of twelve months, if everything goes well, you will have: a correct data pipeline, a backtester you trust, two or three uncorrelated low-turnover strategies running live at small size, a risk system that has been tested by real breaches, and a documented, honest record of your own decisions. What you will not have is meaningful","s":1},{"id":"25-7-the-honest-expectation","t":"25.7 The honest expectation","v":"Vol 25","x":"The most common way this goes wrong is not a bad model or a market that does not cooperate. It is someone with exactly your strengths — careful, imaginative, rigorous — spending three years building increasingly elegant infrastructure for a strategy they never quite ship, because shipping means finding out. The gates in this plan exist fo","s":1},{"id":"vol25a","t":"The Operating Manual: Protocols for the System You Will Actually Run","v":"Vol 25A","x":"Volume 24 turns your profile into design constraints : which failure modes to engineer against, which controls to build. Volume 25 turns it into a twelve-month schedule. This volume covers what happens between those two — the daily and weekly conduct of the work, and the specific decisions that recur. It inherits Volume 24's caveats in full, and they matter more here because the recommendations are more granular. Big Five facets predict behavioural tendencies with correlations around 0.2–0.4. A single self-report s","s":1},{"id":"25a-1-the-profile-read-as-a-system-rather-than-a-l","t":"25A.1 The profile, read as a system rather than a list","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.1 The profile, read as a system rather than a list ¶ Volume 24.1 gives the scores. What follows is the structure — the four combinations that do the real work, none of which is visible from any single facet. Combination The facets What it produces The build–doubt loop Orderliness 16 + Self-Efficacy 11 + Vulnerability 13 you will construct something genuinely good and disbelieve it under the first ordinary drawdown Amplitude, not just downside Neuroticism 86 and Cheerfulness 15 large swings in both directions. ","s":1},{"id":"the-one-your-report-flags-and-volume-24-does-not-d","t":"The one your report flags and Volume 24 does not develop","v":"Vol 25A","x":">The one your report flags and Volume 24 does not develop ¶ Your report identifies Morality at the 8th percentile as the facet most likely to need support, describing it as a tendency to avoid direct answers. In the IPIP-NEO this facet is about candour rather than ethics — low scorers are guarded and disclose selectively, which is neither unprincipled nor rare. In systematic trading it has three specific consequences, and they are worth naming precisely because they are invisible from inside: Under-reporting losses","s":1},{"id":"25a-2-the-daily-protocol","t":"25A.2 The daily protocol","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.2 The daily protocol ¶ Short by design. The largest single risk to your process is that a daily routine becomes a daily opportunity to intervene, and the second largest is that Orderliness 16 elaborates it until it consumes the morning. When What Time Why this and not more Start of session Confirm the system ran. Check the ops dashboard: fills reconciled, no errors, no stale data. 3 min process, not P&L — the only daily number you control Start of session Note today's planned work in one line. One item. 1 min ","s":"25a.2 the daily protocol >25a.2 the daily protocol ¶ short by design. the largest single risk to your process is that a daily routine becomes a daily opportunity to intervene, and the second largest is that orderliness 16 elaborates it until it consumes the morning. when what time why this and not more start of session confirm the system ran. check the ops dashboard: fills reconciled, no errors, no stale data. 3 min process, not p&l — the only daily number you control start of session note today's planned work in one line. one item. 1 min  25a.4 adds the missing control. a rule that fires only in drawdowns leaves the profitable side of your own volatility completely unguarded."},{"id":"25a-3-emotionality-as-an-instrument","t":"25A.3 Emotionality as an instrument","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.3 Emotionality as an instrument ¶ Your report puts Emotionality at the 80th percentile and names it your top strength. The standard trading advice would treat that as something to suppress. That advice is both impossible to follow and wrong about what the facet is. Emotionality in the IPIP-NEO is access to and awareness of your own feelings — emotional granularity, not emotional volatility. Those are different traits and you score high on both, which is unusual and exploitable. High granularity means you can d","s":1},{"id":"the-intervention-log","t":"The intervention log","v":"Vol 25A","x":">The intervention log ¶ One line, whenever you notice the urge to change something. It takes fifteen seconds and it is the single highest-value habit in this volume. Python Copy # research/intervention_log.py from dataclasses import dataclass, asdict from datetime import datetime, timezone import json from pathlib import Path LOG = Path ( \"results/interventions.jsonl\" ) @ dataclass (frozen= True ) class Intervention: ts: str trigger: str # what prompted it: \"down 4 days\", \"read a paper\", ... urge: str # what you wa","s":1},{"id":"the-state-vocabulary","t":"The state vocabulary","v":"Vol 25A","x":">The state vocabulary ¶ Granularity is the point, so use it. “Bad” is not a reading. These are, and each has a different correct response: State Typically follows What it usually means Correct response Restless / itchy a flat or quiet stretch boredom, not information nothing. This is Immoderation 15 looking for an outlet Dread before looking several down days anticipatory anxiety, not evidence follow the protocol; the number is what it is Certainty that it is broken a drawdown past ~5% Vulnerability 13 under stress","s":1},{"id":"25a-4-the-upside-protocol-the-control-volume-24-is","t":"25A.4 The upside protocol — the control Volume 24 is missing","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.4 The upside protocol — the control Volume 24 is missing ¶ Volume 24.3 gives four failure modes and every one of them fires in a drawdown. The profile combination in 25A.1 says that is only half the exposure. Here is the other half, in the same format. Failure mode 5 — Scaling up after a good run Driven by: Cheerfulness 15 (high positive affect after wins), Immoderation 15 (difficulty resisting an attractive urge), Self-Efficacy 11 (the recent evidence temporarily resolves a standing doubt, which is a powerful","s":1},{"id":"controls","t":"Controls","v":"Vol 25A","x":">Controls ¶ A size-increase ladder, pre-committed in writing, with a time lock. Position size may increase only on a scheduled review date, only by a pre-set increment (25% of current, never more), and only if the strategy has run at current size for a full quarter. Write the ladder once, while calm, and put it in version control. The same 24-hour delay that Volume 24.3 applies to de-risking, applied to any increase. Symmetry is the point. If the delay is only on the downside, the system is explicitly biased toward","s":1},{"id":"25a-5-environment-design-make-the-wrong-action-exp","t":"25A.5 Environment design: make the wrong action expensive","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.5 Environment design: make the wrong action expensive ¶ Every control in Volume 24 that depends on you choosing correctly in the moment is a control that will fail in the moment it is needed, because the moment it is needed is defined by your judgement being compromised. The reliable version of every rule is a structural one. The urge Willpower version (fails) Structural version (works) Check P&L “I won't look” P&L off the default dashboard; broker logged out; equity curve behind a script Override the system “","s":1},{"id":"25a-6-sustainability-and-a-burnout-buffer-of-44","t":"25A.6 Sustainability, and a Burnout Buffer of 44","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.6 Sustainability, and a Burnout Buffer of 44 ¶ Your report gives a Burnout Buffer of 44 (mid). Combined with Activity Level 9, Depression 14 and Vulnerability 13, this is the constraint most likely to end the project — not a drawdown, and not a failure of ability. Systematic trading is a multi-year undertaking with long unrewarded stretches, and the failure mode is simply stopping. Parameter Setting Reason Session length 90 minutes, timer, hard stop Activity Level 9: sustained pace beats intensity, reliably Se","s":1},{"id":"the-early-warning-signals-and-the-pre-committed-re","t":"The early-warning signals, and the pre-committed response","v":"Vol 25A","x":">The early-warning signals, and the pre-committed response ¶ Depression 14 and Vulnerability 13 mean the descent into burnout will be legible in behaviour before it is legible in feeling. Watch the behaviour. Each of these has a defined response, decided now rather than then: Signal Response, pre-committed Two sessions skipped in a week reduce next week's target by half. Do not compensate Reports not opened on schedule the strongest available signal. Halve position size and take three days off Research feels effort","s":1},{"id":"25a-7-working-alone-and-the-one-input-you-cannot-g","t":"25A.7 Working alone, and the one input you cannot generate","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.7 Working alone, and the one input you cannot generate ¶ Gregariousness 8, Friendliness 9, Activity Level 9 and low Morality (guardedness) together describe someone well suited to solitary work and structurally unlikely to seek outside input. Volume 24.2 correctly counts the first part as an advantage. This section is about the second part, which is a cost, and about the specific and narrow form of contact that is worth overcoming it for. What you cannot supply yourself Every failure mode in Volume 24.3 shares","s":1},{"id":"the-minimum-viable-version-designed-around-your-ac","t":"The minimum viable version, designed around your actual preferences","v":"Vol 25A","x":">The minimum viable version, designed around your actual preferences ¶ What follows is deliberately the smallest thing that works, because a recommendation to “build a network” will not survive contact with Gregariousness 8 and should not have to: One person, once a month, thirty minutes, asynchronous is fine. Not a community, not a Discord, not a mentor relationship. One competent person who will read a written summary and respond. Written suits you better than a call and is equally effective for this purpose. Sen","s":1},{"id":"25a-8-strategy-fit-scored-against-your-profile","t":"25A.8 Strategy fit, scored against your profile","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.8 Strategy fit, scored against your profile ¶ Not all systematic strategies interact with a given temperament equally. What follows scores the main families against your specific facets. The score is about sustainability for you — whether you will still be running it in three years — not about expected return, which nothing in this reference claims to predict. Strategy family Fit Why Cross-asset trend / managed futures Excellent Slow (Activity Level 9). Rule-based and well-documented (Liberalism 9 prefers esta","s":1},{"id":"25a-9-pre-mortem-how-this-ends","t":"25A.9 Pre-mortem: how this ends","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.9 Pre-mortem: how this ends ¶ A pre-mortem assumes failure has already happened and works backwards to the cause. Done in advance, it converts a diffuse worry into a set of named triggers with defined responses — which is the form in which Anxiety 16 becomes useful rather than corrosive. It is now three years on and the project has ended. Ranked by probability, here is why. # Cause of death Early warning The control 1 Attrition. No dramatic failure. Sessions thinned, then stopped. Buffer 44, Depression 14, and","s":1},{"id":"25a-10-the-card","t":"25A.10 The card","v":"Vol 25A","x":">25A.10 The card ¶ Print this. Keep it where you will see it during a drawdown, which is the only time it matters and the only time you will not want to read it. Operating card Look at P&L monthly. Not today. The system already did what I would do, sooner and better. A drawdown is the base rate, not evidence. A Sharpe-1 strategy is below its high-water mark most of the time and loses money in 16% of years. Down days are 46% of days. That is the design, not a malfunction. If I want to override: write the reason, wai","s":1},{"id":"vol26","t":"The Forge: Building a Strategy of Your Own","v":"Vol 26","x":"Every volume before this one is knowledge. This one is a workbench: a market whose secrets you set yourself, a language for writing signals, and a backtester that refuses to flatter you. It runs in this file, offline, with no libraries. The point is not that it is a good backtester, but that it is an honest one, and that you can take a strategy from nothing to a specification and a Python file without leaving the page.","s":1},{"id":"26-1-what-a-strategy-actually-is","t":"26.1 What a strategy actually is","v":"Vol 26","x":"A systematic strategy is six decisions and one discipline: universe, signal, shaping, sizing, risk, execution, and validation. The signal is the part that feels like having an idea and is the least decisive of the six. Sizing and costs decide the outcome. A mediocre signal sized well beats a good signal sized badly, every time.","s":"26.1 what a strategy actually is a systematic strategy is six decisions and one discipline: universe, signal, shaping, sizing, risk, execution, and validation. the signal is the part that feels like having an idea and is the least decisive of the six. sizing and costs decide the outcome. a mediocre signal sized well beats a good signal sized badly, every time. the seventh is not a decision, which is why it does not feel like part of the work. it is the only step that tells you whether any of the other six mattered. six decision boxes in a row producing a number, feeding into a seventh validation box that takes most of that number back"},{"id":"26-2-a-market-whose-answer-you-already-know","t":"26.2 A market whose answer you already know","v":"Vol 26","x":"The hardest thing about learning to evaluate a backtest is that you never know the truth. A generated market removes that problem: you choose whether an edge exists, then find out whether your method can tell. Four worlds: random walk, trending, mean-reverting, and two volatility regimes, with fat tails, GARCH clustering, jumps and a common factor available on top.","s":1},{"id":"26-3-the-laboratory","t":"26.3 The laboratory","v":"Vol 26","x":"Everything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, nothing is fetched, and your configuration and trial count persist between visits. Ten steps take someone who has never built a strategy from a first run to an exported Python file, with the trial counter rising as they go.","s":1},{"id":"26-4-the-language-a-signal-is-written-in","t":"26.4 The language a signal is written in","v":"Vol 26","x":"The signal box takes an expression evaluated over the whole history at once, with every operator looking backwards only. The vocabulary is the one from Volume 22, so the hundred and one published alphas can be pasted in unchanged. Anything written in braces becomes a tunable parameter. hold carries the last non-zero value forward; bar is the day index and nothing else.","s":1},{"id":"26-5-reading-a-result-honestly","t":"26.5 Reading a result honestly","v":"Vol 26","x":"Six questions in a fixed order: could this have been produced by nothing, how hard did I look, does it survive moving the parameters, does it survive not having seen the data, does it survive its own costs, and could I have held it. The signature of lookahead is an implausibly high Sharpe together with an implausibly small drawdown.","s":1},{"id":"26-6-why-the-trial-counter-cannot-be-optional","t":"26.6 Why the trial counter cannot be optional","v":"Vol 26","x":"If you try N independent strategies on data containing no edge, the best Sharpe you should expect rises with N: 1.19 at five trials, 2.00 at twenty-five, 3.26 at a thousand. A nine by seven sweep is sixty-three trials, not one. And the count follows the data, not the session.","s":1},{"id":"26-7-out-of-the-browser-and-into-a-repository","t":"26.7 Out of the browser and into a repository","v":"Vol 26","x":"Export writes the specification as JSON, the prices as CSV, and a Python file carrying the same causal operators, cost model and metrics. Run it and check the numbers agree. What the Forge omits: point-in-time data, a fill model, corporate actions and borrow, capacity, and portfolio construction. Every omission flatters.","s":1},{"id":"26-8-fifteen-things-to-build-in-order","t":"26.8 Fifteen things to build, in order","v":"Vol 26","x":"Roughly two evenings of work, ordered so that each failure prepares the next: seed variation, manufacturing a result from an empty market, sweeping a signal that cannot know anything, the lookahead signature, costs destroying a reversal, smoothing, fat tails and drawdown, volatility targeting, cross-sectional diversification, pasting a catalogue alpha, walk-forward, bootstrap, real data, and the export fidelity check.","s":1},{"id":"vol0c","t":"Probability, Deeper: The Machinery Every Later Volume Assumes","v":"Vol 0C","x":"Volume 0A gave you enough probability to read a regression. This volume gives you the objects the stochastic calculus, the risk models and the Bayesian chapters silently assume you already own — and it is short, because only seven of them do real work."},{"id":"0c-1-conditional-expectation-is-a-random-variable","t":"0C.1 Conditional expectation is a random variable","v":"Vol 0C","x":"The single most useful upgrade to your probability is to stop reading E[X | Y = y] as a number and start reading E[X | Y] as a random variable: a function of Y , which takes a different value on each outcome. Everything downstream depends on that shift. Once i","s":"0c.1 conditional expectation is a random variable the single most useful upgrade to your probability is to stop reading e[x | y = y] as a number and start reading e[x | y] as a random variable: a function of y , which takes a different value on each outcome. everything downstream depends on that shift. once it is a random variable you can take its expectation, and the answer is forced: the tower property e[ e[x | y] ] = e[x] . averaging the conditional averages recovers the unconditional average. more generally, for nested information 𝒢 ⊆ ℱ , e[ e[x | ℱ] | 𝒢 ] = e[x | 𝒢] — the coarser conditioning always wins. that identity is the engine behind a surprising amount of practical work. it is how you price by conditioning on a first step (volume 8b.4's binomial tree is the tower property applied recursively). it is why a martingale's expectation is flat in time — the defining property e[m t "},{"id":"0c-2-lotus-moment-generating-functions-and-what-they-buy","t":"0C.2 LOTUS, moment generating functions and what they buy","v":"Vol 0C","x":"You want E[g(X)] . The wrong instinct is to derive the distribution of Y = g(X) first. The law of the unconscious statistician says you never have to: LOTUS E[g(X)] = ∫ g(x) f X (x) dx , or ∑ g(x)p(x) in the discrete case. Integrate the function against the de","s":"0c.2 lotus, moment generating functions and what they buy you want e[g(x)] . the wrong instinct is to derive the distribution of y = g(x) first. the law of the unconscious statistician says you never have to: lotus e[g(x)] = ∫ g(x) f x (x) dx , or ∑ g(x)p(x) in the discrete case. integrate the function against the density you already have. no change of variables, no jacobian, no new distribution. this is not a technicality. almost every quantity you actually want is an expectation of a function of something whose density you know and whose transform you do not: an option payoff under a log-normal, a utility of terminal wealth, a squared error, a drawdown indicator. volume 8.1's black–scholes formula is a lotus integral of a hockey-stick payoff against a log-normal density, and nothing more. the moment generating function m x (t) = e[e tx ] is lotus applied to one particular function, cho"},{"id":"0c-3-the-four-inequalities-worth-knowing-by-name","t":"0C.3 The four inequalities worth knowing by name","v":"Vol 0C","x":"You will rarely be able to compute a probability exactly under realistic assumptions. You will very often be able to bound it. Four inequalities cover almost every case, and each has a trading reading. Inequality Statement What it needs Reading Markov P(X ≥ a)","s":"0c.3 the four inequalities worth knowing by name you will rarely be able to compute a probability exactly under realistic assumptions. you will very often be able to bound it. four inequalities cover almost every case, and each has a trading reading. inequality statement what it needs reading markov p(x ≥ a) ≤ e[x]/a for x ≥ 0 non-negativity and a mean the crudest possible tail bound, and it needs almost nothing chebyshev p(|x − μ| ≥ kσ) ≤ 1/k² a finite variance no return series can spend more than 1/25 of its days beyond five sigma — whatever the shape jensen e[φ(x)] ≥ φ(e[x]) for convex φ convexity only the gap is the value of optionality, and it is why the average of ratios is not the ratio of averages cauchy–schwarz |e[xy]| ≤ √(e[x²]e[y²]) second moments correlation is bounded by one, and the information ratio is bounded by the correlation you can achieve jensen is the one that earns"},{"id":"0c-4-order-statistics-and-why-the-maximum-is-the-interesting-one","t":"0C.4 Order statistics, and why the maximum is the interesting one","v":"Vol 0C","x":"Sort a sample and index it: X (1) ≤ X (2) ≤ … ≤ X (n) . These are the order statistics , and two of them do all the work — the maximum and the sample quantiles. The maximum has a one-line law. For independent draws from a distribution with CDF F , The law of t","s":"0c.4 order statistics, and why the maximum is the interesting one sort a sample and index it: x (1) ≤ x (2) ≤ … ≤ x (n) . these are the order statistics , and two of them do all the work — the maximum and the sample quantiles. the maximum has a one-line law. for independent draws from a distribution with cdf f , the law of the maximum p(x (n) ≤ x) = f(x) n — every draw must be below x , and independence turns the joint event into a product. differentiating gives the density, and the same argument on the complement gives the minimum: p(x (1) > x) = (1 − f(x)) n . two consequences run through the rest of this reference. the first is the whole of volume 18. if you run n independent backtests on data containing no edge, the sharpe you report is not a draw from the null — it is the maximum of n draws, and the maximum of n standard normals grows like √(2 ln n). that is a slow function, which i"},{"id":"0c-5-markov-chains-and-the-two-questions-you-ask-of-one","t":"0C.5 Markov chains, and the two questions you ask of one","v":"Vol 0C","x":"A Markov chain is a process whose next state depends on the present state and on nothing before it. The whole object is a transition matrix P, with P ij the probability of moving from state i to state j . Powers of P give multi-step transitions: the n -step ma","s":"0c.5 markov chains, and the two questions you ask of one a markov chain is a process whose next state depends on the present state and on nothing before it. the whole object is a transition matrix p, with p ij the probability of moving from state i to state j . powers of p give multi-step transitions: the n -step matrix is simply p n . you ask a chain two questions, and almost nothing else. the two questions where does it end up? the stationary distribution π solves πp = π with π summing to one — a left eigenvector of p with eigenvalue 1. for an irreducible aperiodic chain the distribution converges to π from any start, at a rate set by the second largest eigenvalue modulus. how long until it gets somewhere? expected hitting times solve a linear system by conditioning on the first step: h i = 1 + ∑ j p ij h j for states outside the target, with h = 0 on the target. that is the tower prop"},{"id":"0c-6-poisson-processes-and-the-exponential-clock","t":"0C.6 Poisson processes and the exponential clock","v":"Vol 0C","x":"A Poisson process is the canonical model of things arriving: trades, quote updates, defaults, jumps. It has three equivalent definitions and you should be able to move between them, because different problems make different ones easy. Definition Statement Easi","s":"0c.6 poisson processes and the exponential clock a poisson process is the canonical model of things arriving: trades, quote updates, defaults, jumps. it has three equivalent definitions and you should be able to move between them, because different problems make different ones easy. definition statement easiest for by counts n(t) ~ poisson(λt), with independent increments over disjoint intervals probability that k events happen in a window by gaps inter-arrival times are i.i.d. exponential(λ) waiting-time and simulation questions infinitesimally p(one arrival in dt) = λdt, p(two or more) = o(dt) deriving an sde or a generator the exponential gap is memoryless: having waited ten minutes tells you nothing about how much longer you will wait. that is a strong claim, and it is the assumption that fails first in real markets — volume 2.7's hawkes process exists precisely because arrivals clus"},{"id":"0c-7-the-multivariate-normal-and-the-three-facts-you-will-reuse","t":"0C.7 The multivariate normal, and the three facts you will reuse","v":"Vol 0C","x":"The multivariate normal is the distribution every portfolio calculation reaches for first. It is parameterised by a mean vector μ and a covariance matrix Σ, and three properties are why it is inescapable. The three facts 1. Closed under linear combination. If ","s":"0c.7 the multivariate normal, and the three facts you will reuse the multivariate normal is the distribution every portfolio calculation reaches for first. it is parameterised by a mean vector μ and a covariance matrix σ, and three properties are why it is inescapable. the three facts 1. closed under linear combination. if x ~ n(μ, σ) then w t x ~ n(w t μ, w t σw) . a portfolio of jointly normal assets is normal, with the variance formula of 0a.8. this is why mean–variance is coherent at all. 2. marginals are normal, and so are conditionals. conditioning is linear: e[x 1 |x 2 ] = μ 1 + σ 12 σ 22 −1 (x 2 − μ 2 ) , with conditional covariance σ 11 − σ 12 σ 22 −1 σ 21 — which does not depend on the observed value at all. 3. zero correlation implies independence. true for the joint normal and for essentially nothing else, which is why the assumption is so seductive and so dangerous. fact 2 i"},{"id":"vol0d","t":"Numerical Methods That Actually Bite","v":"Vol 0D","x":"Almost every number you produce is the output of an approximation. This volume covers the five that appear constantly in trading code — root-finding, quadrature, Monte Carlo, finite differences and constrained optimisation — and, for each, the specific way it goes wrong quietly rather than loudly."},{"id":"0d-1-root-finding-and-the-implied-volatility-problem","t":"0D.1 Root-finding, and the implied volatility problem","v":"Vol 0D","x":"Implied volatility is defined by inversion: find the σ for which the model price equals the market price. There is no closed form, so you solve f(σ) = BS(σ) − P mkt = 0 numerically. The choice of method is a small decision with a large operational consequence.","s":"0d.1 root-finding, and the implied volatility problem implied volatility is defined by inversion: find the σ for which the model price equals the market price. there is no closed form, so you solve f(σ) = bs(σ) − p mkt = 0 numerically. the choice of method is a small decision with a large operational consequence. method convergence needs fails when bisection linear — one bit per step a bracket with a sign change never, if the bracket is valid newton quadratic near the root the derivative — here, vega vega ≈ 0: deep in or out of the money secant superlinear (≈1.6) two starting points same flat-region failure, without needing vega brent superlinear, guaranteed a bracket practically nothing — it falls back to bisection newton is the textbook answer and the wrong production default. vega goes to zero for deep options, so the update step σ ← σ − f(σ)/vega divides by something near zero and th"},{"id":"0d-2-quadrature-when-to-integrate-and-when-to-simulate","t":"0D.2 Quadrature: when to integrate and when to simulate","v":"Vol 0D","x":"Every price is an integral. The only question is how you evaluate it, and the answer is decided almost entirely by dimension . Method Error in n points Dimension d Use for Trapezoid O(n −2 ) 1–3 Quick one-dimensional work Simpson O(n −4 ) 1–3 Smooth integrands","s":"0d.2 quadrature: when to integrate and when to simulate every price is an integral. the only question is how you evaluate it, and the answer is decided almost entirely by dimension . method error in n points dimension d use for trapezoid o(n −2 ) 1–3 quick one-dimensional work simpson o(n −4 ) 1–3 smooth integrands; the default in one dimension gauss–legendre spectral for smooth f 1–4 characteristic-function inversion (8a.2) monte carlo o(n −1/2 ) any high dimension, path dependence quasi–monte carlo ≈o(n −1 ) moderate smooth, moderate-dimension payoffs the one rule deterministic quadrature error grows exponentially in dimension — a grid of m points per axis costs m d evaluations. monte carlo error is o(n −1/2 ) regardless of dimension. that indifference is the whole reason simulation exists, and it is why the crossover is somewhere around four dimensions and not negotiable by cleverness"},{"id":"0d-3-monte-carlo-and-the-four-variance-reductions-worth-knowing","t":"0D.3 Monte Carlo, and the four variance reductions worth knowing","v":"Vol 0D","x":"A Monte Carlo estimate has a standard error, and reporting a simulated price without one is the numerical equivalent of reporting a backtest without an error bar. Compute it — the sample standard deviation of the payoffs divided by √n — and report it beside th","s":"0d.3 monte carlo, and the four variance reductions worth knowing a monte carlo estimate has a standard error, and reporting a simulated price without one is the numerical equivalent of reporting a backtest without an error bar. compute it — the sample standard deviation of the payoffs divided by √n — and report it beside the price. four techniques cut that error without more paths. they are not interchangeable. technique mechanism typical gain cost or risk antithetic variates pair each path with its mirror −z; average the pair 2–10× nothing if the payoff is monotone; no gain, or harm, if it is symmetric control variates subtract a correlated quantity whose exact value you know 10–100× needs a good control; the optimal coefficient must be estimated importance sampling sample where the payoff lives, reweight by the likelihood ratio order of magnitude for rare events a bad proposal makes th"},{"id":"0d-4-finite-differences-and-the-stability-condition-nobody-checks","t":"0D.4 Finite differences, and the stability condition nobody checks","v":"Vol 0D","x":"Feynman–Kac (1.8) turns a pricing expectation into a PDE. Solving it on a grid is the right choice in low dimension, for early exercise, and wherever you need stable Greeks — because the grid gives you neighbouring values, so delta and gamma are differences of","s":"0d.4 finite differences, and the stability condition nobody checks feynman–kac (1.8) turns a pricing expectation into a pde. solving it on a grid is the right choice in low dimension, for early exercise, and wherever you need stable greeks — because the grid gives you neighbouring values, so delta and gamma are differences of numbers you already have rather than separate simulations. scheme stability accuracy per step explicit conditional — see below o(δt) + o(δx²) cheap; no solve implicit unconditional o(δt) + o(δx²) one tridiagonal solve crank–nicolson unconditional o(δt²) + o(δx²) one tridiagonal solve the explicit stability condition an explicit scheme is stable only when the time step is small relative to the square of the space step — roughly δt ≤ δx²/(σ²x²) in the black–scholes variables. halving the space step forces you to quarter the time step. ignore it and the solution does n"},{"id":"0d-5-constrained-optimisation-in-practice","t":"0D.5 Constrained optimisation in practice","v":"Vol 0D","x":"Portfolio construction, model calibration and execution scheduling are all constrained optimisations, and the useful classification is not by algorithm but by what the problem is . Shape Example Solve with Quadratic objective, linear constraints Mean–variance ","s":"0d.5 constrained optimisation in practice portfolio construction, model calibration and execution scheduling are all constrained optimisations, and the useful classification is not by algorithm but by what the problem is . shape example solve with quadratic objective, linear constraints mean–variance with a gross cap (11.1) a qp solver; unique optimum convex, non-smooth term turnover or l1 penalties (21.4) proximal gradient, or an epigraph reformulation convex, conic constraints risk budgeting, robust optimisation second-order cone programming non-convex cardinality limits, integer lot sizes, model calibration heuristics, and no guarantee at all the three diagnostics to run on any solution 1. is it feasible? check every constraint explicitly on the returned weights, to tolerance. many solvers return the last iterate on failure and set a status flag nobody reads. 2. are the multipliers se"},{"id":"vol1a","t":"Measure, Numéraire and Optional Stopping","v":"Vol 1A","x":"Volume 1 gives you Itô and Girsanov as tools. This volume gives you the theory they belong to — the two fundamental theorems, the freedom to choose a numéraire, stopping times and early exercise, and what happens to all of it when the price can jump."},{"id":"1a-1-the-two-fundamental-theorems-and-what-each-actually-says","t":"1A.1 The two fundamental theorems, and what each actually says","v":"Vol 1A","x":"Two theorems carry the entire structure of derivative pricing. They are short, they are often quoted loosely, and the loose version costs people money. First fundamental theorem A market admits no arbitrage if and only if there exists at least one equivalent m","s":"1a.1 the two fundamental theorems, and what each actually says two theorems carry the entire structure of derivative pricing. they are short, they are often quoted loosely, and the loose version costs people money. first fundamental theorem a market admits no arbitrage if and only if there exists at least one equivalent martingale measure ℚ — a probability measure with the same null sets as the real-world ℙ, under which every discounted asset price is a martingale. second fundamental theorem the market is complete — every contingent claim can be replicated by trading — if and only if that measure is unique . read the pair as a dictionary. no arbitrage ↔ at least one ℚ. completeness ↔ exactly one ℚ. everything else follows. if ℚ exists you may price by expectation; if it is unique that price is enforced by a hedging argument, so a dealer who mis-quotes it can be arbitraged. if ℚ exists bu"},{"id":"1a-2-change-of-numeraire-the-trick-that-removes-half-the-algebra","t":"1A.2 Change of numéraire: the trick that removes half the algebra","v":"Vol 1A","x":"Discounting by the money-market account is a convention, not a law. Any strictly positive traded asset can serve as the unit of account, and each choice comes with its own measure under which prices divided by that asset are martingales. Choosing well can remo","s":"1a.2 change of numéraire: the trick that removes half the algebra discounting by the money-market account is a convention, not a law. any strictly positive traded asset can serve as the unit of account, and each choice comes with its own measure under which prices divided by that asset are martingales. choosing well can remove an entire stochastic factor from a problem. numéraire measure martingale under it use for money-market account risk-neutral ℚ discounted asset prices the default; equities with deterministic rates zero-coupon bond p(t,t) t-forward measure ℚ t forward prices to t anything with stochastic rates; removes the discount factor from the expectation annuity (pv of a swap's fixed leg) swap measure the forward swap rate swaptions — makes the swap rate a driftless lognormal, giving black's formula a second risky asset asset measure the ratio s₁/s₂ exchange options; reduces tw"},{"id":"1a-3-stopping-times-and-what-makes-a-rule-legitimate","t":"1A.3 Stopping times, and what makes a rule legitimate","v":"Vol 1A","x":"A stopping time τ is a random time whose occurrence is decidable from information available at that moment: for every t , the event {τ ≤ t} is in ℱ t . In trading language, you must be able to tell that you have stopped without seeing the future. Rule Stopping","s":"1a.3 stopping times, and what makes a rule legitimate a stopping time τ is a random time whose occurrence is decidable from information available at that moment: for every t , the event {τ ≤ t} is in ℱ t . in trading language, you must be able to tell that you have stopped without seeing the future. rule stopping time? why first time the price falls 10% yes observable as it happens fixed horizon of sixty days yes deterministic times are trivially stopping times first exit from a band yes both barriers are observable the day of the maximum of the path no you cannot know it is the maximum until the path is over one day before the crash no the definition requires the future the last two rows are not curiosities. they are the formal statement of lookahead. a backtest that exits \"at the top\" or sizes by realised volatility computed over the whole sample is using a non-stopping time, and the r"},{"id":"1a-4-american-options-free-boundaries-and-the-price-of-waiting","t":"1A.4 American options: free boundaries and the price of waiting","v":"Vol 1A","x":"An American option is the right to stop optimally. Its value is sup τ E ℚ [ e −rτ Payoff(S τ ) ] over all stopping times τ ≤ T — an optimisation over exercise rules rather than a plain expectation. That supremum is why there is no closed form, and why the nume","s":"1a.4 american options: free boundaries and the price of waiting an american option is the right to stop optimally. its value is sup τ e ℚ [ e −rτ payoff(s τ ) ] over all stopping times τ ≤ t — an optimisation over exercise rules rather than a plain expectation. that supremum is why there is no closed form, and why the numerical treatment differs in kind rather than degree. the free boundary at each time there is a critical price separating continue from exercise . that boundary is part of the solution, not an input — which is what makes the pde a free-boundary problem. inside the continuation region the black–scholes pde holds; on the boundary the value equals the payoff and, crucially, the delta matches the payoff's slope too. that second condition — smooth pasting — is what pins the boundary down. two classical facts are worth holding, because they settle most practical questions immed"},{"id":"1a-5-jump-diffusion-and-why-it-cannot-be-hedged-away","t":"1A.5 Jump diffusion, and why it cannot be hedged away","v":"Vol 1A","x":"Add a compound Poisson component (0C.6) to a diffusion and you have the standard model for a price that can gap: The jump-diffusion dS/S = (μ − λk)dt + σdW + (J − 1)dN , where N is Poisson with intensity λ, J is the random jump multiplier, and k = E[J − 1]. Th","s":"1a.5 jump diffusion, and why it cannot be hedged away add a compound poisson component (0c.6) to a diffusion and you have the standard model for a price that can gap: the jump-diffusion ds/s = (μ − λk)dt + σdw + (j − 1)dn , where n is poisson with intensity λ, j is the random jump multiplier, and k = e[j − 1]. the −λk in the drift is compensation : it removes the average contribution of the jumps so the drift means what it did before. the modelling consequence is immediate and severe. a delta hedge removes diffusion risk because the hedge and the option move together for infinitesimal changes. a jump is not infinitesimal: when the price gaps, the option's value and the hedge's value change by different amounts, and the difference is a loss you cannot rebalance away. with jumps of random size there is one hedging instrument and a continuum of possible jump outcomes, so the market is incom"},{"id":"1a-6-the-market-price-of-risk-and-how-p-and-q-differ-in-practice","t":"1A.6 The market price of risk, and how ℙ and ℚ differ in practice","v":"Vol 1A","x":"For an asset with dynamics dS/S = μdt + σdW, the market price of risk is Definition λ = (μ − r)/σ — excess return per unit of volatility. It is the Sharpe ratio of the asset, and it is exactly the drift adjustment Girsanov applies when moving from ℙ to ℚ: unde","s":"1a.6 the market price of risk, and how ℙ and ℚ differ in practice for an asset with dynamics ds/s = μdt + σdw, the market price of risk is definition λ = (μ − r)/σ — excess return per unit of volatility. it is the sharpe ratio of the asset, and it is exactly the drift adjustment girsanov applies when moving from ℙ to ℚ: under ℚ the drift becomes r, and the shift is λσ. two facts make this more than notation. first, in a complete market every asset driven by the same brownian motion must carry the same λ . if two assets share a risk source and offer different excess return per unit of exposure to it, you can go long one and short the other in the ratio that cancels the risk and keep the difference — an arbitrage. that single requirement is what forces the black–scholes pde (20.6) and what makes the risk-neutral drift the same r for every asset. second, the distinction between measures is "},{"id":"vol2b","t":"The Trading Ecology: Who Is On The Other Side","v":"Vol 2B","x":"Volumes 2 and 2A give you the mechanics and the mathematics of the book. This volume gives you the population around it — the taxonomy of traders, the anatomy of a spread, how blocks and auctions work, and the manipulations the structure invites. It is the part that decides whether a strategy has an"},{"id":"2b-1-the-taxonomy-who-trades-and-why","t":"2B.1 The taxonomy: who trades, and why","v":"Vol 2B","x":"Every order has a reason behind it, and the reason determines how dangerous it is to take the other side. The first division is between traders who want the position and traders who want the profit from trading . Type Motive Information content of their flow U","s":"2b.1 the taxonomy: who trades, and why every order has a reason behind it, and the reason determines how dangerous it is to take the other side. the first division is between traders who want the position and traders who want the profit from trading . type motive information content of their flow utilitarian — hedgers, indexers, pension flows, tax and liquidity sellers they want the exposure, or want rid of it, for a reason outside the market low. this is the flow you want to take the other side of informed — fundamental analysts, event traders, insiders they believe the price is wrong and will correct high. this is the flow that makes quoting expensive dealers and market makers earn the spread for supplying immediacy; want inventory near zero low in intent, but their quotes carry inventory information arbitrageurs enforce relationships between related prices informed about the relations"},{"id":"2b-2-liquidity-has-four-dimensions-and-they-trade-off","t":"2B.2 Liquidity has four dimensions, and they trade off","v":"Vol 2B","x":"\"Liquidity\" as a single word is useless. It is four separable properties, and a market can be excellent in one and terrible in another. Dimension Question it answers Measured by Immediacy How fast can I trade at all? Time to fill; whether a quote is standing W","s":"2b.2 liquidity has four dimensions, and they trade off \"liquidity\" as a single word is useless. it is four separable properties, and a market can be excellent in one and terrible in another. dimension question it answers measured by immediacy how fast can i trade at all? time to fill; whether a quote is standing width what does a small trade cost? quoted and effective spread depth how much can i do at that price? size at the touch, and down the book resiliency how fast does the book refill after i take it? half-life of spread and depth after a trade the first practical use is diagnostic. a market with a one-tick spread and no size behind it is narrow and shallow — cheap to test and expensive to use. a market with a wide spread and enormous depth is the reverse, and a large order may execute better there. quoting a \"tight market\" from the spread alone is how size gets mis-sized. which one"},{"id":"2b-3-the-spread-decomposed-into-its-three-costs","t":"2B.3 The spread, decomposed into its three costs","v":"Vol 2B","x":"A dealer's quoted spread is not a profit margin. It is the sum of three distinct costs, and knowing which is which tells you what a narrower spread would actually mean. Component What it pays for Behaviour Order processing Exchange fees, clearing, technology, ","s":"2b.3 the spread, decomposed into its three costs a dealer's quoted spread is not a profit margin. it is the sum of three distinct costs, and knowing which is which tells you what a narrower spread would actually mean. component what it pays for behaviour order processing exchange fees, clearing, technology, the desk roughly fixed per trade; falls with scale and competition inventory the risk of holding an unwanted position until it can be laid off rises with volatility and with the time to offset; shows as quote skew adverse selection the expected loss to counterparties who know more rises with information asymmetry; irreducible by competition the asymmetry that matters competition compresses order processing toward its marginal cost and compresses inventory cost toward the true cost of risk. it cannot compress adverse selection, because that component is a transfer to informed traders r"},{"id":"2b-4-auctions-the-opening-the-closing-and-why-they-exist","t":"2B.4 Auctions: the opening, the closing, and why they exist","v":"Vol 2B","x":"A call auction accumulates orders over a window and crosses them all at a single price — the price that maximises executable volume. A continuous market matches them one at a time as they arrive. The difference is not cosmetic. Call auction Continuous market L","s":"2b.4 auctions: the opening, the closing, and why they exist a call auction accumulates orders over a window and crosses them all at a single price — the price that maximises executable volume. a continuous market matches them one at a time as they arrive. the difference is not cosmetic. call auction continuous market liquidity concentrated at one instant spread over the session immediacy none — you wait for the cross high price discovery uses all accumulated interest at once incremental, order by order best for reopening after a gap; thin names; benchmark prints active names, continuous interest why the day begins and ends with one the open follows a period during which information accumulated and no trading occurred, so the first price must aggregate a great deal of interest at once — precisely what an auction does and what a continuous market would do badly, one order at a time, with t"},{"id":"2b-5-blocks-upstairs-markets-and-why-size-hides","t":"2B.5 Blocks, upstairs markets and why size hides","v":"Vol 2B","x":"A large order faces a dilemma that has no clean solution. To find the other side you must reveal that you want to trade. Revealing it moves the price against you before you have traded. This is the block trader's problem , and market structure is largely a set","s":"2b.5 blocks, upstairs markets and why size hides a large order faces a dilemma that has no clean solution. to find the other side you must reveal that you want to trade. revealing it moves the price against you before you have traded. this is the block trader's problem , and market structure is largely a set of imperfect answers to it. mechanism how it helps what it costs slicing into the continuous book each child order is small enough not to signal time, and exposure to the price moving for other reasons (10.3) upstairs negotiation via a broker the broker knows who holds the other side and reveals selectively information leaks to the broker and to whoever they ask dark pools and crossing networks no pre-trade display, so no signal until the trade prints uncertain fill; adverse selection from participants who probe auctions everyone arrives at once, so being present is not informative n"},{"id":"2b-6-manipulation-and-the-signatures-it-leaves","t":"2B.6 Manipulation, and the signatures it leaves","v":"Vol 2B","x":"Some order flow exists to mislead. You do not need to participate in it to be affected by it: a signal fitted to a book that contains manipulated flow will learn the manipulation, and then lose money when it stops or when it is aimed at you. Pattern Mechanism ","s":"2b.6 manipulation, and the signatures it leaves some order flow exists to mislead. you do not need to participate in it to be affected by it: a signal fitted to a book that contains manipulated flow will learn the manipulation, and then lose money when it stops or when it is aimed at you. pattern mechanism what a naive signal learns spoofing / layering large orders posted with no intent to trade, cancelled before execution that book imbalance predicts direction — with the sign reversed wash trading trading with oneself to fabricate volume that the name is liquid, and that volume confirms a move painting the tape trades timed near a close or fixing to set a printed price that a late-session move carries information marking the close pressure into the closing cross to move a benchmark or a mark that closing prices are clean marks (2b.4) cornering and squeezes control of deliverable supply "},{"id":"2b-7-fragmentation-internalisation-and-the-order-you-did-not-route","t":"2B.7 Fragmentation, internalisation and the order you did not route","v":"Vol 2B","x":"A modern equity market is not one place. The same name trades on many lit venues, several dark ones, and through wholesalers who never send the order to an exchange at all. That structure has a real effect on what you get. Where a retail order goes In several ","s":"2b.7 fragmentation, internalisation and the order you did not route a modern equity market is not one place. the same name trades on many lit venues, several dark ones, and through wholesalers who never send the order to an exchange at all. that structure has a real effect on what you get. where a retail order goes in several markets a retail marketable order is routed not to an exchange but to a wholesaler who executes it against their own inventory, typically at or slightly better than the displayed national best quote. the broker may be paid for that flow. the order is attractive to the wholesaler for exactly the reason of 2b.1: retail marketable flow is, on average, uninformed, so its adverse selection cost is low. the arrangement is a real trade with costs on both sides. you receive price improvement on the displayed quote and pay no commission. you do not interact with the lit book"},{"id":"2b-8-halts-limits-and-what-happens-when-the-mechanism-stops","t":"2B.8 Halts, limits and what happens when the mechanism stops","v":"Vol 2B","x":"Markets have brakes. They exist because a price falling through a thin book is not obviously transmitting information, and a pause lets participants who are not already in the market arrive. Mechanism What it does Typical trigger Limit up / limit down Blocks t","s":"2b.8 halts, limits and what happens when the mechanism stops markets have brakes. they exist because a price falling through a thin book is not obviously transmitting information, and a pause lets participants who are not already in the market arrive. mechanism what it does typical trigger limit up / limit down blocks trades outside a band around a recent reference price a percentage band, tightened at the open and close single-name pause halts one instrument, then reopens with an auction price persisting at the band edge market-wide circuit breaker halts everything for a period, or closes the session an index falling by set percentage thresholds volatility auction switches an instrument from continuous trading into a call auction an indicative price outside a tolerance what a halt does to a hedge a hedged position depends on both legs being tradeable. when one is halted and the other is"},{"id":"vol5a","t":"Cross-Sectional Method: Sorts, Fama–MacBeth and the Replication Problem","v":"Vol 5A","x":"Volume 5 gives you the factor model and the signal pipeline. This volume gives you the empirical method that the entire published literature is built on: sorted portfolios, cross-sectional regressions, and the standard errors that decide whether a result is real. It ends with the replication crisis,"},{"id":"5a-1-portfolio-sorts-the-workhorse","t":"5A.1 Portfolio sorts: the workhorse","v":"Vol 5A","x":"Almost every published cross-sectional result begins the same way. Take a characteristic — book-to-market, past twelve-month return, gross profitability, asset growth. At the end of each formation period, rank every eligible stock on it. Cut the ranking into g","s":"5a.1 portfolio sorts: the workhorse almost every published cross-sectional result begins the same way. take a characteristic — book-to-market, past twelve-month return, gross profitability, asset growth. at the end of each formation period, rank every eligible stock on it. cut the ranking into groups, usually deciles. hold each group for a period, record its return, then re-rank and repeat. the output is a time series of returns for each decile, and a time series for the spread portfolio that is long the top decile and short the bottom. the test is then a simple one: is the mean of the spread series different from zero? you compute a t -statistic on that mean, and — because a raw spread may just be loading on known risk — you also regress the spread on a factor model (5.1) and test whether the intercept, the alpha , is different from zero. step decision it hides define the universe excha"},{"id":"5a-2-breakpoints-and-weighting","t":"5A.2 Breakpoints and weighting","v":"Vol 5A","x":"Two mechanical choices decide more published results than any theory does. The first is where the decile boundaries fall. The second is how stocks are weighted inside each decile. Breakpoints If you cut the deciles using the whole universe, the boundaries are ","s":"5a.2 breakpoints and weighting two mechanical choices decide more published results than any theory does. the first is where the decile boundaries fall. the second is how stocks are weighted inside each decile. breakpoints if you cut the deciles using the whole universe, the boundaries are set by the mass of the distribution — and in most equity markets the mass is tiny companies. in a market with several thousand listed names, the smallest half of firms can be under three per cent of total capitalisation. deciles cut on all names therefore produce portfolios where nine of ten buckets contain almost no investable money. the standard correction is large-cap breakpoints : compute the cut points using only the large, liquid segment of the market, then assign every stock to a bucket using those boundaries. the extreme buckets are no longer forced to hold a tenth of the names, and the portfol"},{"id":"5a-3-bivariate-sorts-independent-and-dependent","t":"5A.3 Bivariate sorts: independent and dependent","v":"Vol 5A","x":"You have a candidate characteristic and a known predictor — usually size — that it is correlated with. The double sort is how you separate them without imposing a functional form. Independent (unconditional) sort Rank on the control and on the characteristic s","s":"5a.3 bivariate sorts: independent and dependent you have a candidate characteristic and a known predictor — usually size — that it is correlated with. the double sort is how you separate them without imposing a functional form. independent (unconditional) sort rank on the control and on the characteristic separately , using the full sample for each. form the intersection: a 5 × 5 grid of twenty-five portfolios. then average the characteristic spread across the control buckets. because the cuts are made independently, the cells are unequal in size, and if the two variables are strongly correlated some cells can be nearly empty. a 5 × 5 independent sort on two variables with a correlation of 0.7 will have corner cells holding a handful of names, and those cells will dominate the spread's variance. dependent (conditional, or sequential) sort rank on the control first. within each control bu"},{"id":"5a-4-fama-macbeth-the-two-pass-regression","t":"5A.4 Fama–MacBeth: the two-pass regression","v":"Vol 5A","x":"The sort runs out of room at two controls. The cross-sectional regression does not, and the standard way of running one on a panel of returns is the two-pass procedure of Fama and MacBeth. The procedure Pass one — cross-section, one period at a time. For each ","s":"5a.4 fama–macbeth: the two-pass regression the sort runs out of room at two controls. the cross-sectional regression does not, and the standard way of running one on a panel of returns is the two-pass procedure of fama and macbeth. the procedure pass one — cross-section, one period at a time. for each month t , regress that month's returns across all n stocks on their characteristics as known at the start of the month: r i,t = γ 0,t + γ 1,t x 1,i,t−1 + … + γ k,t x k,i,t−1 + ε i,t that gives you t separate estimates of each slope — one per month. each γ k,t is the return in month t to a portfolio with unit exposure to characteristic k and zero exposure to every other characteristic in the regression. pass two — time series of the slopes. average each slope over time and test it against zero: γ̄ k = (1/ t ) σ t γ k,t , t = γ̄ k ÷ [ s (γ k ) / √ t ] the elegance is in what the split accompl"},{"id":"5a-5-standard-errors-that-survive-a-panel","t":"5A.5 Standard errors that survive a panel","v":"Vol 5A","x":"A t -statistic is a mean divided by a standard error. In a panel of returns the mean is usually the easy part; almost every published mistake is in the denominator. Ordinary least squares assumes residuals are independent. Panel residuals are not, and they fai","s":"5a.5 standard errors that survive a panel a t -statistic is a mean divided by a standard error. in a panel of returns the mean is usually the easy part; almost every published mistake is in the denominator. ordinary least squares assumes residuals are independent. panel residuals are not, and they fail in two distinct directions: dependence where it comes from the fix time-series (autocorrelation) overlapping holding periods, persistent characteristics, slow-moving premia newey–west, or cluster by firm cross-sectional (contemporaneous) common factors: every stock shares the market fama–macbeth, or cluster by period both at once the usual case in equity panels two-way clustering, by firm and period newey–west a heteroskedasticity- and autocorrelation-consistent estimator. it computes the long-run variance of the series by adding weighted autocovariances up to a lag l , with weights declin"},{"id":"5a-6-outliers-winsorising-and-conventions","t":"5A.6 Outliers, winsorising and the conventions that hide decisions","v":"Vol 5A","x":"Accounting ratios have unbounded tails. A firm with book equity near zero has a book-to-market ratio in the thousands; a firm recovering from a collapse has a one-month return of 400 per cent. Ordinary least squares minimises squared errors, so one such observ","s":"5a.6 outliers, winsorising and the conventions that hide decisions accounting ratios have unbounded tails. a firm with book equity near zero has a book-to-market ratio in the thousands; a firm recovering from a collapse has a one-month return of 400 per cent. ordinary least squares minimises squared errors, so one such observation can move a cross-sectional slope more than the other two thousand stocks combined. treatment what it does cost winsorise at the 1st and 99th percentile replaces extreme values with the percentile value; keeps the observation distorts the tail you may actually care about; the cut is arbitrary trim (truncate) deletes extreme observations entirely loses data, and can be a selection bias if extremes are informative rank-transform to uniform, or normalise ranks uses only the ordering; outliers cannot dominate throws away magnitude information altogether robust regre"},{"id":"5a-7-persistence-decay-and-turnover","t":"5A.7 Persistence, decay and turnover","v":"Vol 5A","x":"A significant spread tells you a signal predicts next month . It says nothing about whether it predicts month six, and that question decides whether the strategy is tradeable, because turnover is where the alpha goes to die (2.3, 5.5). Three measurements, in o","s":"5a.7 persistence, decay and turnover a significant spread tells you a signal predicts next month . it says nothing about whether it predicts month six, and that question decides whether the strategy is tradeable, because turnover is where the alpha goes to die (2.3, 5.5). three measurements, in order characteristic persistence. correlate the characteristic with itself at lags 1, 3, 6, 12, 24 months. a signal that fully refreshes each month cannot be traded slowly, no matter how strong it is. value measures persist for years; short-horizon reversal persists for days. return decay. re-run the sort predicting returns at horizon h for a range of h , and plot the spread against horizon. this is the honest picture of how long you have. transition matrix. the probability that a stock in decile i this period is in decile j next period. the diagonal tells you how much of the portfolio you keep; t"},{"id":"5a-8-the-anomaly-zoo-and-replication","t":"5A.8 The anomaly zoo and the replication problem","v":"Vol 5A","x":"Several hundred characteristics have been published as predicting the cross-section of stock returns. They cannot all be true, and the reasons they are not are systematic enough to be listed. The multiple-testing arithmetic A t -statistic of 2.0 corresponds to","s":"5a.8 the anomaly zoo and the replication problem several hundred characteristics have been published as predicting the cross-section of stock returns. they cannot all be true, and the reasons they are not are systematic enough to be listed. the multiple-testing arithmetic a t -statistic of 2.0 corresponds to a 5 per cent chance of a false positive on a single, pre-specified test. test one hundred characteristics on the same data and you expect five to clear that bar by chance alone. the published literature is the surviving tail of an unknown but very large number of tests, most of which were never written up. correcting for this raises the hurdle substantially. under a bonferroni-style correction the threshold grows with the number of tests; under false-discovery-rate control it grows more slowly but still materially. the practical consequence, argued influentially in the literature, is"},{"id":"vol6a","t":"Statistical Learning, Properly","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Volume 6 is about applying machine learning to financial data without fooling yourself. This volume is the layer beneath it: the bias–variance decomposition, honest resampling, the regularised linear family, classification and its metrics, trees through boosting, kernels, and unsupervised methods. I"},{"id":"6a-1-bias-variance-and-what-a-model-is-for","t":"6A.1 Bias, variance, and what a model is for","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Every supervised model is an estimate of a function f that maps features to an outcome. The expected squared error of a prediction at a point x 0 decomposes exactly: The decomposition E[( y − f ̂( x 0 ))²] = [Bias( f ̂( x 0 ))]² + Var( f ̂( x 0 )) + σ² ε Bias ","s":"6a.1 bias, variance, and what a model is for every supervised model is an estimate of a function f that maps features to an outcome. the expected squared error of a prediction at a point x 0 decomposes exactly: the decomposition e[( y − f ̂( x 0 ))²] = [bias( f ̂( x 0 ))]² + var( f ̂( x 0 )) + σ² ε bias — how far the average fit, over many training sets, sits from the truth. this is the error from the model being too simple to represent the relationship. variance — how much the fit moves when the training set changes. this is the error from the model chasing the particular sample it saw. irreducible error σ² ε — the noise in y itself. no model reduces it, and no amount of data or cleverness touches it. flexibility trades the first against the second. as you add capacity — more features, deeper trees, higher polynomial order — bias falls and variance rises. test error is u-shaped in flexi"},{"id":"6a-2-resampling-cross-validation-and-bootstrap","t":"6A.2 Resampling: cross-validation and the bootstrap","v":"Vol 6A","x":"You cannot evaluate a model on the data that chose it. Resampling is how you construct honest held-out estimates without a second dataset. The validation-set approach Split once into train and test. Simple, and it wastes data: the estimate is high-variance bec","s":"6a.2 resampling: cross-validation and the bootstrap you cannot evaluate a model on the data that chose it. resampling is how you construct honest held-out estimates without a second dataset. the validation-set approach split once into train and test. simple, and it wastes data: the estimate is high-variance because it depends on which split you drew, and it is biased upward because the model was fit on less data than you will eventually use. k -fold cross-validation divide the data into k folds. fit on k − 1 and evaluate on the held-out fold, rotating through all k . average the errors. every observation is used for testing exactly once and for training k − 1 times. bias of the estimate variance of the estimate cost validation set (50/50) high — fits on half the data high one fit k = 5 or 10 low moderate — the standard compromise k fits loocv ( k = n ) almost none high — the n fits are n"},{"id":"6a-3-ridge-lasso-and-elastic-net","t":"6A.3 Ridge, lasso and elastic net","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Ordinary least squares has no way to express a preference for simplicity. Regularisation adds a penalty on coefficient size to the loss, buying a large variance reduction for a small increase in bias — the favourable trade whenever noise dominates (6A.1). Meth","s":"6a.3 ridge, lasso and elastic net ordinary least squares has no way to express a preference for simplicity. regularisation adds a penalty on coefficient size to the loss, buying a large variance reduction for a small increase in bias — the favourable trade whenever noise dominates (6a.1). method penalty added to rss behaviour ridge λ σ β j ² shrinks all coefficients toward zero; none reach it. handles correlated predictors by splitting weight among them lasso λ σ |β j | shrinks and sets some coefficients exactly to zero : it selects. among correlated predictors it tends to pick one arbitrarily elastic net λ[α σ |β j | + (1−α) σ β j ²] selects, but keeps correlated groups together. the usual default when predictors cluster why the absolute-value penalty selects write each as a constrained problem: minimise rss subject to the coefficient vector lying inside a region. for ridge the region i"},{"id":"6a-4-classification-and-its-metrics","t":"6A.4 Classification and its metrics","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Many trading problems are naturally classification: will this label hit the profit barrier before the stop (6.2)? Will this trade be filled? Is this position going to breach its limit? The modelling is straightforward; the evaluation is where practitioners go ","s":"6a.4 classification and its metrics many trading problems are naturally classification: will this label hit the profit barrier before the stop (6.2)? will this trade be filled? is this position going to breach its limit? the modelling is straightforward; the evaluation is where practitioners go wrong. the confusion matrix and what comes off it metric definition answers accuracy (tp + tn) / all how often am i right? — nearly useless when classes are imbalanced precision tp / (tp + fp) when i say yes, how often am i right? recall (sensitivity, tpr) tp / (tp + fn) of the real yeses, how many did i catch? specificity tn / (tn + fp) of the real noes, how many did i correctly pass on? f1 harmonic mean of precision and recall a single number when both matter equally — they rarely do accuracy is a trap in exactly the cases you care about if 2 per cent of days are large drawdowns, a model that pr"},{"id":"6a-5-trees-bagging-random-forests-boosting","t":"6A.5 Trees, bagging, random forests, boosting","v":"Vol 6A","x":"A decision tree partitions feature space by recursive binary splits, each chosen to reduce a loss — squared error for regression, Gini impurity or entropy for classification. Predictions are the mean or the majority within a leaf. A single tree is interpretabl","s":"6a.5 trees, bagging, random forests, boosting a decision tree partitions feature space by recursive binary splits, each chosen to reduce a loss — squared error for regression, gini impurity or entropy for classification. predictions are the mean or the majority within a leaf. a single tree is interpretable, handles interactions automatically, needs no scaling, and is very high-variance: change a few observations and the top split can change, which changes everything below it. every method in this section exists to fix that. method how it builds which error term it attacks bagging fit deep trees on bootstrap samples; average them variance. bias is unchanged random forest bagging, plus each split considers only a random subset of features variance, harder — the feature subsampling decorrelates the trees, which is what makes averaging work boosting fit small trees sequentially , each on the"},{"id":"6a-6-support-vector-machines-and-kernels","t":"6A.6 Support vector machines and kernels","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Among the infinitely many hyperplanes separating two classes, the support vector classifier chooses the one with the largest margin : the greatest distance to the nearest training point of either class. That is a substantive choice, and it is why the method ge","s":"6a.6 support vector machines and kernels among the infinitely many hyperplanes separating two classes, the support vector classifier chooses the one with the largest margin : the greatest distance to the nearest training point of either class. that is a substantive choice, and it is why the method generalises well on clean problems. real data is not separable, so the soft-margin formulation allows violations and penalises them. the tuning parameter c sets the exchange rate: c margin support vectors bias–variance small wide, many violations tolerated many higher bias, lower variance large narrow, violations expensive few lower bias, higher variance only the points on or inside the margin — the support vectors — determine the solution. points far on the correct side could be deleted without changing anything, which makes the classifier insensitive to outliers on the safe side, and entirely"},{"id":"6a-7-unsupervised-learning-pca-and-clustering","t":"6A.7 Unsupervised learning: PCA and clustering","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Unsupervised methods look for structure without a target. In finance they are used constantly — for dimension reduction, risk decomposition, regime detection and grouping instruments — and they are constantly over-interpreted. Principal component analysis PCA ","s":"6a.7 unsupervised learning: pca and clustering unsupervised methods look for structure without a target. in finance they are used constantly — for dimension reduction, risk decomposition, regime detection and grouping instruments — and they are constantly over-interpreted. principal component analysis pca finds orthogonal directions of maximum variance: the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, ordered by eigenvalue. the first k components give the best rank- k linear approximation of the data in squared-error terms. application what the components usually are yield curve level, slope and curvature — around 95 per cent of variation in three components (4.3) equity returns pc1 is the market; the rest are unstable and hard to name covariance cleaning random-matrix theory separates signal eigenvalues from noise (7.3) feature compression decorrelated inputs for a downstream model — at the c"},{"id":"6a-8-what-finance-breaks","t":"6A.8 What finance breaks","v":"Vol 6A","x":"Everything in this volume assumes things that are true of most datasets and false of financial ones. This section is the reconciliation, and it is the reason Volume 6 exists as a separate treatment rather than as an application chapter. Standard assumption The","s":"6a.8 what finance breaks everything in this volume assumes things that are true of most datasets and false of financial ones. this section is the reconciliation, and it is the reason volume 6 exists as a separate treatment rather than as an application chapter. standard assumption the financial reality the correction observations are i.i.d. returns are serially dependent; volatility clusters; labels from overlapping windows share information purged and embargoed cross-validation; sample uniqueness weights (6.5, 6.6) data can be shuffled shuffling trains on the future walk-forward and combinatorial purged cv (6.5) the relationship is stable regimes change; a working strategy changes the thing it trades on rolling refits; regime-aware validation; expect decay (5a.7, 11.4) signal-to-noise is moderate r ² of a few per cent is a strong result heavy regularisation; simple models; suspicion of "},{"id":"vol8b","t":"Derivatives Machinery: Futures, Swaps, Trees and the Valuation Adjustments","v":"Vol 8B","x":"Volumes 8 and 8A build the option-pricing apparatus and the volatility surface. This volume covers the rest of the derivatives world — futures mechanics and clearing, forward pricing and carry, swaps and OIS discounting, binomial lattices, exotics, interest-rate models, and the XVA family that turns"},{"id":"8b-1-futures-mechanics-margin-and-clearing","t":"8B.1 Futures mechanics, margin and the clearing house","v":"Vol 8B","x":"A futures contract is an exchange-traded agreement to buy or sell a standardised quantity at a fixed price on a fixed date. The economics look like a forward; the cash flows do not , and the difference matters. Daily settlement At the close of each session the","s":"8b.1 futures mechanics, margin and the clearing house a futures contract is an exchange-traded agreement to buy or sell a standardised quantity at a fixed price on a fixed date. the economics look like a forward; the cash flows do not , and the difference matters. daily settlement at the close of each session the exchange marks every position to the settlement price. gains are paid into the account, losses are taken out — variation margin — and the contract is effectively rewritten at the new price. a futures position is therefore a sequence of one-day forwards, not a single instrument held to maturity. margin what it is initial margin good-faith collateral posted at inception, sized to cover a plausible worst-case move over the close-out period. it remains your property and typically earns interest variation margin daily settlement of gains and losses. not collateral — a genuine payment"},{"id":"8b-2-forward-pricing-carry-and-convenience-yield","t":"8B.2 Forward pricing, carry and convenience yield","v":"Vol 8B","x":"A forward price is not a forecast. It is the spot price adjusted for everything you avoid or forgo by receiving the asset later rather than now — enforced by arbitrage where the asset can be stored and shorted. Cost of carry For an asset paying a continuous yi","s":"8b.2 forward pricing, carry and convenience yield a forward price is not a forecast. it is the spot price adjusted for everything you avoid or forgo by receiving the asset later rather than now — enforced by arbitrage where the asset can be stored and shorted. cost of carry for an asset paying a continuous yield q , with continuous storage cost u and continuous convenience yield y , over horizon t at risk-free rate r : f 0 = s 0 e ( r − q + u − y ) t each term is a cash flow you do or do not experience by waiting: r is the financing you avoid, q the income you forgo, u the storage you avoid, y the benefit of physical possession you forgo. asset the relation non-dividend equity f = s e rt dividend-paying index f = s e ( r − q ) t currency f = s e ( r d − r f ) t — covered interest parity storable commodity f = s e ( r + u − y ) t contango, backwardation and the roll contango is an upward-"},{"id":"8b-3-swaps-and-the-discounting-revolution","t":"8B.3 Swaps and the discounting revolution","v":"Vol 8B","x":"An interest rate swap exchanges a stream of fixed payments for a stream of floating payments on a notional that is never exchanged. It is the largest derivatives market in the world, and its valuation mechanics are the template for the rest. Two equivalent rea","s":"8b.3 swaps and the discounting revolution an interest rate swap exchanges a stream of fixed payments for a stream of floating payments on a notional that is never exchanged. it is the largest derivatives market in the world, and its valuation mechanics are the template for the rest. two equivalent readings two bonds. a payer swap is short a fixed-rate bond and long a floating-rate note. the value is the difference between them. a strip of forwards. each payment date is a forward rate agreement. the value is the sum of the discounted differences between the fixed rate and each forward rate. the par swap rate is the fixed rate making the swap worth zero at inception — a discount-factor-weighted average of the forward rates. it is a quoted market rate, and the curve of par swap rates is one of the primary inputs to curve construction. why collateralised trades discount on ois before 2008, o"},{"id":"8b-4-binomial-trees-and-lattice-pricing","t":"8B.4 Binomial trees and lattice pricing","v":"Vol 8B","x":"The binomial tree is the most useful teaching device in derivatives and remains the standard production tool for American exercise. It also makes the risk-neutral argument concrete in a way the continuous derivation does not. One step Over a short interval Δ t","s":"8b.4 binomial trees and lattice pricing the binomial tree is the most useful teaching device in derivatives and remains the standard production tool for american exercise. it also makes the risk-neutral argument concrete in a way the continuous derivation does not. one step over a short interval δ t the price either multiplies by u or by d . build a portfolio of δ shares and a short option such that both outcomes give the same value: that portfolio is riskless, so it must earn r . solving gives a price that can be written as a discounted expectation under the risk-neutral probability p = ( e r δ t − d ) / ( u − d ) note what is absent: the real probability of an up move, and any expected return on the stock. hedging removed them. that is the entire content of risk-neutral valuation (8.1), visible in two lines. calibration the standard cox–ross–rubinstein choice matches the first two mome"},{"id":"8b-5-exotics-a-working-taxonomy","t":"8B.5 Exotics: a working taxonomy","v":"Vol 8B","x":"Exotic is not a category so much as a list of departures from the vanilla payoff. The useful question about any exotic is not what it pays but what breaks when you try to hedge it . Family Payoff The hedging problem Digital (binary) Fixed amount if in the mone","s":"8b.5 exotics: a working taxonomy exotic is not a category so much as a list of departures from the vanilla payoff. the useful question about any exotic is not what it pays but what breaks when you try to hedge it . family payoff the hedging problem digital (binary) fixed amount if in the money delta is unbounded at the strike near expiry. hedged as a tight call spread, which is a deliberate over-hedge barrier (knock-in / knock-out) activates or extinguishes at a level delta and gamma flip sign across the barrier; the hedge must be unwound instantly at exactly the least liquid moment asian on the average price over a window easier, not harder — averaging lowers effective volatility and resists manipulation of the fixing lookback on the maximum or minimum reached expensive and strongly path-dependent; requires simulation chooser holder later elects call or put decomposes into vanillas by p"},{"id":"8b-6-jumps-and-numerical-methods","t":"8B.6 Jumps and numerical methods","v":"Vol 8B","x":"Diffusion models move continuously. Markets do not: earnings, central bank decisions, defaults and sovereign events move prices in discrete steps. Adding a jump component changes both the option price and the meaning of the hedge. Merton's jump-diffusion A Poi","s":"8b.6 jumps and numerical methods diffusion models move continuously. markets do not: earnings, central bank decisions, defaults and sovereign events move prices in discrete steps. adding a jump component changes both the option price and the meaning of the hedge. merton's jump-diffusion a poisson process with intensity λ triggers jumps whose log-sizes are normally distributed. when jump risk is assumed diversifiable, the price is a poisson-weighted sum of black–scholes prices, each with volatility and drift adjusted for the number of jumps in the period — a rare case where adding realism keeps a closed form. what jumps do consequence fatten the tails of the return distribution out-of-the-money options become more valuable; a smile appears at short maturities where diffusion smiles vanish (8a.1) break the continuous-hedging argument delta hedging cannot eliminate risk. the market becomes "},{"id":"8b-7-caps-floors-and-swaptions","t":"8B.7 Caps, floors and swaptions","v":"Vol 8B","x":"Interest rate options are the largest options market by notional, and they are quoted and hedged with conventions that differ from equity options in ways worth knowing before you look at a screen. Caps and floors A cap pays the holder whenever a floating index","s":"8b.7 caps, floors and swaptions interest rate options are the largest options market by notional, and they are quoted and hedged with conventions that differ from equity options in ways worth knowing before you look at a screen. caps and floors a cap pays the holder whenever a floating index fixes above a strike, on each reset date over its life. it is a portfolio of independent options — caplets — one per reset, each a call on the forward rate for its period. a floor is the corresponding put. a collar is long a cap and short a floor, usually struck so the premium nets to zero. because a cap decomposes additively, its price is the sum of caplet prices, and a cap's quoted flat volatility is a single number that reprices the whole strip — not the volatility of any individual caplet. stripping caplet volatilities out of flat cap quotes is a standard and slightly awkward calibration step. sw"},{"id":"8b-8-term-structure-models","t":"8B.8 Term structure models","v":"Vol 8B","x":"Black's model prices one instrument at a time. A term structure model describes how the whole curve evolves, which is what you need for anything with an exercise decision that depends on the future shape of the curve — callable bonds, Bermudan swaptions, mortg","s":"8b.8 term structure models black's model prices one instrument at a time. a term structure model describes how the whole curve evolves, which is what you need for anything with an exercise decision that depends on the future shape of the curve — callable bonds, bermudan swaptions, mortgage prepayment. short-rate models these model the instantaneous short rate r ( t ) and derive everything else from it. model dynamics character vasicek d r = κ(θ − r )d t + σd w mean-reverting, gaussian; analytic bond prices; permits negative rates — a defect in 1977 and a feature now cir d r = κ(θ − r )d t + σ√ r d w volatility scales with the rate level, so r stays non-negative under the feller condition; still analytic ho–lee d r = θ( t )d t + σd w first model to fit the initial curve exactly, via a time-dependent drift; no mean reversion hull–white d r = (θ( t ) − ar )d t + σd w vasicek plus a time-dep"},{"id":"8b-9-xva-the-adjustments-that-make-a-price-real","t":"8B.9 XVA: the adjustments that make a price real","v":"Vol 8B","x":"A textbook derivative price assumes no default, free funding and no capital charge. A bank's price assumes none of those. The gap between them is the XVA family, and every member is a real cash cost that somebody must be charged for. Adjustment What it prices ","s":"8b.9 xva: the adjustments that make a price real a textbook derivative price assumes no default, free funding and no capital charge. a bank's price assumes none of those. the gap between them is the xva family, and every member is a real cash cost that somebody must be charged for. adjustment what it prices sign to the dealer cva credit expected loss from the counterparty defaulting while the trade is in your favour a cost — reduces the value of your asset dva debit expected gain from your own default while the trade is against you a benefit, and a deeply uncomfortable one fva funding cost of funding an uncollateralised position at your own funding spread rather than the risk-free rate a cost, usually mva margin cost of funding initial margin over the life of the trade a cost; grew large after mandatory margin rules kva capital cost of holding regulatory capital against the position for "},{"id":"vol8c","t":"Trading Options: Spreads, Synthetics and Position Management","v":"Vol 8C","x":"Volumes 8, 8A and 8B build the pricing apparatus. None of them tells you what to trade. This volume is the practitioner's layer — expiration diagrams, put–call parity as a working tool, the spread taxonomy and what each structure is really a bet on, how to choose one from a view, and how to manage a"},{"id":"8c-1-expiration-p-and-l-and-reading-a-position","t":"8C.1 Expiration P&L, and reading a position","v":"Vol 8C","x":"At expiry every option is worth its intrinsic value and nothing else. That makes the expiration diagram — the payoff plotted against the underlying price — the one picture that is unambiguously true, and the right place to begin before any model is consulted. ","s":"8c.1 expiration p&l, and reading a position at expiry every option is worth its intrinsic value and nothing else. that makes the expiration diagram — the payoff plotted against the underlying price — the one picture that is unambiguously true, and the right place to begin before any model is consulted. the construction rule every expiry payoff is piecewise linear , with a kink at each strike in the position and nowhere else. so you can draw any structure, however complicated, from three facts: the slope far below every strike: (long stock) + (short puts) − (long puts), counted in shares. the slope far above every strike: (long stock) + (long calls) − (short calls). the change in slope at each strike: +1 per long call and per short put, −1 per short call and per long put, times the contract multiplier. then shift the whole line down by the net premium paid, or up by the net premium receiv"},{"id":"8c-2-put-call-parity-and-synthetics","t":"8C.2 Put–call parity and synthetics","v":"Vol 8C","x":"Put–call parity is the only relationship in options that holds without a model. It is pure arbitrage, and because of that it is the practitioner's most-used tool: it says that every option position has two other ways of being expressed , and you should trade w","s":"8c.2 put–call parity and synthetics put–call parity is the only relationship in options that holds without a model. it is pure arbitrage, and because of that it is the practitioner's most-used tool: it says that every option position has two other ways of being expressed , and you should trade whichever is cheapest. the relation for european options on a non-dividend-paying stock, with strike k and expiry t : c − p = s − k e − rt long a call and short a put at the same strike and expiry is long stock, financed. rearranged, it gives the synthetic for every leg: you want build it from long stock long call + short put (same strike, same expiry) short stock short call + long put long call long stock + long put short call short stock + short put long put short stock + long call short put long stock + short call — the covered write with a dividend before expiry, subtract its present value from"},{"id":"8c-3-vertical-spreads","t":"8C.3 Vertical spreads","v":"Vol 8C","x":"A vertical spread is long one option and short another of the same type and expiry at a different strike. It is the fundamental directional structure, and it is bounded at both ends — the far slopes are zero, so the maximum loss and maximum gain are both known","s":"8c.3 vertical spreads a vertical spread is long one option and short another of the same type and expiry at a different strike. it is the fundamental directional structure, and it is bounded at both ends — the far slopes are zero, so the maximum loss and maximum gain are both known before you trade (8c.1). spread construction cash view bull call spread long lower-strike call, short higher-strike call debit up, to a level bull put spread short higher-strike put, long lower-strike put credit up, or not down bear call spread short lower-strike call, long higher-strike call credit down, or not up bear put spread long higher-strike put, short lower-strike put debit down, to a level the debit and credit versions are the same position a bull call spread and a bull put spread at the same two strikes have identical expiry payoffs up to the parity constant (8c.2). they are the same bet. the choice"},{"id":"8c-4-volatility-spreads-straddles-to-condors","t":"8C.4 Volatility spreads: straddles to condors","v":"Vol 8C","x":"A volatility spread is delta-neutral at inception and takes a position on how much the underlying moves rather than which way. The family is small, and each member trades a different part of the distribution. Structure Construction Pays when Greek signature St","s":"8c.4 volatility spreads: straddles to condors a volatility spread is delta-neutral at inception and takes a position on how much the underlying moves rather than which way. the family is small, and each member trades a different part of the distribution. structure construction pays when greek signature straddle call + put, same strike a large move either way long gamma, long vega, short theta — the purest form strangle call + put, different strikes, both out of the money a larger move either way same signs, cheaper, needs more movement to pay butterfly long 1 low, short 2 middle, long 1 high — equal spacing expiry lands near the middle strike short gamma at the middle, long the wings; bounded everywhere condor as the butterfly but with two different middle strikes expiry lands anywhere in a range wider profit zone, lower peak iron butterfly / condor the same shape built from a put spread"},{"id":"8c-5-calendars-diagonals-and-forward-volatility","t":"8C.5 Calendars, diagonals and forward volatility","v":"Vol 8C","x":"A calendar spread is long and short the same strike in different expiries — normally short the near, long the far. A diagonal is the same idea with different strikes as well. Both trade the term structure of volatility rather than its level. What the calendar ","s":"8c.5 calendars, diagonals and forward volatility a calendar spread is long and short the same strike in different expiries — normally short the near, long the far. a diagonal is the same idea with different strikes as well. both trade the term structure of volatility rather than its level. what the calendar is buying options at different maturities do not decay at the same rate. theta scales roughly as 1/√ t , so the near leg loses time value much faster than the far leg. selling the near and buying the far therefore earns the difference in decay while the underlying stays near the strike. but the position is also long the far leg's vega and short the near leg's, so it is long forward volatility — the market's implied volatility for the period between the two expiries. that is what you are really trading, and it is not directly observable: it must be backed out of the two implied volatil"},{"id":"8c-6-ratio-spreads-and-the-risk-you-did-not-price","t":"8C.6 Ratio spreads and the risk you did not price","v":"Vol 8C","x":"A ratio spread buys one option and sells more than one of another strike, usually arranged so the premium nets to roughly zero. It is the most commonly mis-sold structure in options, because it looks like a free position with a favourable payoff and is in fact","s":"8c.6 ratio spreads and the risk you did not price a ratio spread buys one option and sells more than one of another strike, usually arranged so the premium nets to roughly zero. it is the most commonly mis-sold structure in options, because it looks like a free position with a favourable payoff and is in fact a short tail wearing that costume. read the far slope, always a 1×2 call ratio — long one call at k 1 , short two at k 2 — has a far slope of −1 . beyond the upper strike it is short one unit of the underlying, permanently and without a bound. the maximum shown profit occurs exactly at k 2 , which is why the diagram looks so appealing; what the diagram also shows, if you extend the axis far enough, is a line heading down forever. the same applies to the ratio backspread in reverse: long two, short one, with a far slope of +1 and a bounded loss in the middle. the backspread is the so"},{"id":"8c-7-choosing-a-structure-from-a-view","t":"8C.7 Choosing a structure from a view","v":"Vol 8C","x":"Most bad option trades are a correct view expressed in the wrong instrument. The discipline is to state the view in four dimensions before looking at a single quote. The four questions Direction — up, down, or no view? Magnitude — how far, and is that more or ","s":"8c.7 choosing a structure from a view most bad option trades are a correct view expressed in the wrong instrument. the discipline is to state the view in four dimensions before looking at a single quote. the four questions direction — up, down, or no view? magnitude — how far, and is that more or less than the implied volatility already prices? timing — by when, and is there a known event inside that window? volatility — do you have a view on implied volatility itself, separate from the move? a view that specifies only direction is a stock view, and should be expressed in stock. options are for views with two or more of these dimensions filled in; that is the whole reason they exist. view structure because up, unbounded, soon long call, or long stock you are paying for convexity you will actually use up, to about this level, by this date bull vertical you sell the part of the distributio"},{"id":"8c-8-managing-a-position-adjustment-and-gamma-rent","t":"8C.8 Managing a position: adjustment and gamma rent","v":"Vol 8C","x":"Positions move. The question is what to do about it, and the honest answer is that most adjustments are new trades pretending to be maintenance. Gamma rent A long-gamma position earns from rehedging: each time the underlying moves and you rebalance the delta, ","s":"8c.8 managing a position: adjustment and gamma rent positions move. the question is what to do about it, and the honest answer is that most adjustments are new trades pretending to be maintenance. gamma rent a long-gamma position earns from rehedging: each time the underlying moves and you rebalance the delta, you buy low and sell high mechanically, and the accumulated gain over an interval is approximately ½γ s ²σ² realised δ t . against that you pay theta, which is priced from implied volatility. the net is gamma rent : the running charge for holding convexity. read it as a lease. you are paying daily for the right to profit from movement. if the movement does not arrive, the lease still runs. if you are short gamma, you are the landlord — collecting daily and exposed to the day the tenant destroys the building. the adjustment decision situation the honest options the view is intact, t"},{"id":"8c-9-early-exercise-dividends-and-pin-risk","t":"8C.9 Early exercise, dividends and pin risk","v":"Vol 8C","x":"American options can be exercised at any time, which adds decisions the European mathematics does not contain. The rules are short and worth knowing exactly, because getting them wrong costs money with no market move required. When early exercise is rational A","s":"8c.9 early exercise, dividends and pin risk american options can be exercised at any time, which adds decisions the european mathematics does not contain. the rules are short and worth knowing exactly, because getting them wrong costs money with no market move required. when early exercise is rational an american call on a non-dividend-paying stock is never exercised early. exercising throws away the remaining time value and pays the strike sooner than necessary. sell the call instead; it is worth at least its intrinsic value. an american call may be exercised just before an ex-dividend date when the dividend exceeds the remaining time value of the corresponding put. you give up time value to capture the dividend. an american put may be exercised early when it is deep in the money and interest on the strike proceeds outweighs the remaining time value. very low rates make this rare; high "},{"id":"vol10b","t":"Transaction Cost Analysis: Measuring What Trading Actually Costs","v":"Vol 10B","x":"Volume 2 gives the microstructure, 2A the impact models and 10 the optimal trajectory. This volume is the measurement layer that makes any of it usable: what a trade costs and against what, how to build an impact model from your own fills rather than from a paper, how to produce a pre-trade estimate"},{"id":"10b-1-what-a-trade-costs-the-decomposition","t":"10B.1 What a trade costs: the decomposition","v":"Vol 10B","x":"\"Costs\" is a single word covering at least six distinct quantities that behave differently, scale differently, and are measured differently. Treating them as one number is why so many backtests survive contact with a cost assumption they should not have surviv","s":"10b.1 what a trade costs: the decomposition \"costs\" is a single word covering at least six distinct quantities that behave differently, scale differently, and are measured differently. treating them as one number is why so many backtests survive contact with a cost assumption they should not have survived (16.2). component nature scales with observable? commission and fees explicit shares or notional; often stepped exactly, on the invoice taxes and levies explicit notional, by jurisdiction and side exactly spread cost implicit half-spread per crossing, times participation in liquidity taking yes, against the prevailing quote market impact implicit roughly the square root of the fraction of daily volume (2.3, 2a) only by inference — you cannot observe the counterfactual price timing risk implicit, symmetric volatility times the square root of the trading horizon only as a variance, never "},{"id":"10b-2-implementation-shortfall-properly-defined","t":"10B.2 Implementation shortfall, properly defined","v":"Vol 10B","x":"Implementation shortfall is the difference between the return of the portfolio you intended and the return of the portfolio you got. It is the only cost measure that captures everything, because it is defined against the decision rather than against the market","s":"10b.2 implementation shortfall, properly defined implementation shortfall is the difference between the return of the portfolio you intended and the return of the portfolio you got. it is the only cost measure that captures everything, because it is defined against the decision rather than against the market. the definition let p d be the price when the decision was made, p j and q j the price and quantity of each fill, q the quantity intended, and p end the price at the horizon when the order was abandoned. for a buy: is = σ j q j ( p j − p d ) + ( q − σ j q j )( p end − p d ) + fees the first term is the execution cost on what you traded. the second is the opportunity cost on what you did not. fees are explicit. divide by q · p d to express it in basis points. the two terms are what make the measure honest. an algorithm that quietly fails to complete a difficult order looks superb on e"},{"id":"10b-3-benchmarks-and-how-each-one-is-gamed","t":"10B.3 Benchmarks, and how each one is gamed","v":"Vol 10B","x":"A benchmark is a definition of good execution. Choose it carelessly and you will get exactly the behaviour it defines, which is rarely the behaviour you wanted. Benchmark Measures What it rewards Failure Arrival price (implementation shortfall) Fills against t","s":"10b.3 benchmarks, and how each one is gamed a benchmark is a definition of good execution. choose it carelessly and you will get exactly the behaviour it defines, which is rarely the behaviour you wanted. benchmark measures what it rewards failure arrival price (implementation shortfall) fills against the price when the order arrived speed and completion rewards trading through the book on a bad day; noisy for small orders vwap over the order's interval fills against volume-weighted average price tracking the volume profile self-referential : a large order is a big part of the vwap it is judged against twap fills against time-weighted average even slicing ignores where the liquidity actually was participation-weighted price market vwap over exactly the interval you were active fair comparison at a chosen rate rewards being active only when it is easy close fills against the closing print"},{"id":"10b-4-building-an-impact-model-from-your-own-fills","t":"10B.4 Building an impact model from your own fills","v":"Vol 10B","x":"Published impact models tell you the shape. Your own fills tell you the level, and the level is what you need, because it depends on your broker, your venues, your instruments and your own information. The canonical functional form Cost in basis points, for an","s":"10b.4 building an impact model from your own fills published impact models tell you the shape. your own fills tell you the level, and the level is what you need, because it depends on your broker, your venues, your instruments and your own information. the canonical functional form cost in basis points, for an order of q shares in a name with average daily volume adv and annualised volatility σ, executed at participation rate ρ : cost = a · σ b · ( q / adv ) c · f (ρ) + half-spread with c near ½ — the square-root law — and b near 1. the participation term f (ρ) splits the total between the part that decays after you stop (temporary) and the part that does not (permanent). fitting is done in logs, where the model is linear in log σ and log ( q / adv ), so ordinary least squares recovers the exponents directly. the fitting procedure, and the traps in it define the dependent variable. cost "},{"id":"10b-5-pre-trade-estimates-and-the-cost-curve","t":"10B.5 Pre-trade estimates and the cost curve","v":"Vol 10B","x":"A pre-trade estimate turns the fitted model (10B.4) into a decision. Its output is not a number but a curve : expected cost against trading horizon, with the dispersion at each point. Reading the curve Plot expected cost on one axis against horizon on the othe","s":"10b.5 pre-trade estimates and the cost curve a pre-trade estimate turns the fitted model (10b.4) into a decision. its output is not a number but a curve : expected cost against trading horizon, with the dispersion at each point. reading the curve plot expected cost on one axis against horizon on the other. two lines matter: expected impact falls monotonically with horizon — slower is cheaper, with diminishing returns. timing risk , one standard deviation of the cost, rises as σ√ t . their sum for a given risk aversion has a minimum, and that minimum is the recommended horizon. change the risk aversion and the recommendation moves — which is correct, because the right horizon genuinely depends on how much cost variance you are willing to bear (10.1). add the third line — alpha decay over the horizon — and the recommendation usually shortens sharply. for a signal that halves in a day, pati"},{"id":"10b-6-post-trade-attribution","t":"10B.6 Post-trade attribution","v":"Vol 10B","x":"A realised cost is one draw from a distribution. Attributing it requires separating what the market did from what you did, and the tool is the pre-trade model used as a control. The cost-adjusted z-score For each order, compute z = (realised cost − predicted c","s":"10b.6 post-trade attribution a realised cost is one draw from a distribution. attributing it requires separating what the market did from what you did, and the tool is the pre-trade model used as a control. the cost-adjusted z-score for each order, compute z = (realised cost − predicted cost) ÷ predicted standard deviation this converts every order — different sizes, names, days, urgencies — onto one comparable scale. then: the mean of z across a desk's orders is its skill estimate. zero means the model's expectation is being met. the standard error of that mean is 1/√ n . with 100 orders you can detect a persistent bias of about 0.2 standard deviations, and no less. grouping z by venue, algorithm, time of day, order size decile or instrument sector shows where any bias lives. the z-score is the whole discipline. comparing raw basis points across different orders is comparing things that"},{"id":"10b-7-costs-inside-portfolio-construction","t":"10B.7 Costs inside portfolio construction","v":"Vol 10B","x":"A cost model that only produces reports is a compliance exercise. A cost model that enters the objective function changes what you hold, and that is where the money is. The objective with costs Instead of maximising expected return against risk alone, maximise","s":"10b.7 costs inside portfolio construction a cost model that only produces reports is a compliance exercise. a cost model that enters the objective function changes what you hold, and that is where the money is. the objective with costs instead of maximising expected return against risk alone, maximise w ′μ − (λ/2) w ′σ w − c ( w − w 0 ) where c (·) is the cost of moving from the current holdings w 0 to the proposed w . three consequences follow immediately, and all three match what experienced traders do by instinct: a no-trade region appears. small deviations from optimal are not worth the cost of correcting, so the solution is a region rather than a point (11.6, 5a.7). the solution depends on where you are. the optimal portfolio is no longer a function of forecasts alone; it depends on the current holdings, which is why path matters. position sizes shrink with size. because marginal co"},{"id":"vol11b","t":"Systematic Position Sizing: Forecasts, Scaling and the Diversification Multiplier","v":"Vol 11B","x":"Volume 11 gives the optimisation theory and 11A the covariance estimation. Neither tells you how a running system actually converts a forecast into a number of contracts. This volume is that pipeline: what a forecast means, how it is scaled and capped, how rules are combined, why a portfolio of corr"},{"id":"11b-1-the-pipeline-end-to-end","t":"11B.1 The pipeline, end to end","v":"Vol 11B","x":"Between \"the model says buy\" and \"send 47 contracts\" there are seven stages. Most systems have all of them; the ones that fail have them tangled together, so that a change to a signal silently changes the risk. The seven stages Raw forecast. The signal in its ","s":"11b.1 the pipeline, end to end between \"the model says buy\" and \"send 47 contracts\" there are seven stages. most systems have all of them; the ones that fail have them tangled together, so that a change to a signal silently changes the risk. the seven stages raw forecast. the signal in its natural units — a z-score, a slope, a probability, a spread in basis points. scaling. multiply so the forecast has a known expected absolute value, making forecasts from different rules comparable (11b.2). capping. clip the scaled forecast at a fixed multiple of its average, so no single extreme reading dominates. combination. weight the rules into one forecast per instrument (11b.3). volatility scaling. convert a forecast into a position by dividing by the instrument's volatility, so that one unit of forecast means one unit of risk in every instrument. portfolio scaling. apply instrument weights and t"},{"id":"11b-2-forecast-scaling-and-capping","t":"11B.2 Forecast scaling and capping","v":"Vol 11B","x":"A raw signal has arbitrary units. A moving-average crossover in a bond future and a value z-score in an equity are not on the same scale, and averaging them is meaningless. Scaling fixes this by defining what a forecast is . The standard forecast Choose a conv","s":"11b.2 forecast scaling and capping a raw signal has arbitrary units. a moving-average crossover in a bond future and a value z-score in an equity are not on the same scale, and averaging them is meaningless. scaling fixes this by defining what a forecast is . the standard forecast choose a convention — say, an expected absolute value of 10 — and multiply each raw signal by the constant that achieves it: scalar rule = 10 ÷ mean(|raw forecast|) estimated over a long history and pooled across instruments , not fitted per instrument. pooling matters: a per-instrument scalar fits noise and lets a rule appear to work in one market because its scalar was tuned there. the number 10 is arbitrary and any convention works. what matters is that the same convention applies to every rule, so that a forecast of 20 from any rule means the same strength of conviction, and rules can be averaged without on"},{"id":"11b-3-combining-rules","t":"11B.3 Combining rules","v":"Vol 11B","x":"Once every rule produces a forecast on the same scale, combining them is a weighting problem — and it is the same weighting problem as portfolio optimisation, with the same failure mode (11.1). Why the optimiser is dangerous here Rules are highly correlated wi","s":"11b.3 combining rules once every rule produces a forecast on the same scale, combining them is a weighting problem — and it is the same weighting problem as portfolio optimisation, with the same failure mode (11.1). why the optimiser is dangerous here rules are highly correlated with one another. two moving-average crossovers with adjacent lookbacks can correlate above 0.9. mean–variance optimisation over highly correlated assets with estimated means produces enormous long–short weights that are unstable between refits — the estimation error in the mean dominates the signal entirely. the result is a combination that backtests beautifully and behaves erratically live. any weighting scheme you use must therefore be judged on the stability of its weights , not on the sharpe ratio of the fitted combination. scheme when it is right caution equal weights rules of similar quality; short history"},{"id":"11b-4-the-diversification-multiplier","t":"11B.4 The diversification multiplier","v":"Vol 11B","x":"A portfolio of correlated positions has less risk than the sum of its parts. If you size each position to its own risk target and then hold all of them, the book runs below target — often far below. The diversification multiplier is the correction. The formula","s":"11b.4 the diversification multiplier a portfolio of correlated positions has less risk than the sum of its parts. if you size each position to its own risk target and then hold all of them, the book runs below target — often far below. the diversification multiplier is the correction. the formula for weights w and correlation matrix ρ of the sub-portfolios' returns, the realised risk relative to the sum of the individual risks is √( w ′ρ w ). the multiplier that restores the target is its reciprocal: dm = 1 ÷ √( w ′ ρ w ) with n equally weighted instruments all correlated at ρ, this simplifies to dm = 1 ÷ √(ρ + (1 − ρ)/ n ) which is 1 when everything is perfectly correlated, √ n when nothing is correlated, and approaches 1/√ρ as n grows. n ρ = 0.0 ρ = 0.3 ρ = 0.6 ρ = 0.9 2 1.41 1.24 1.12 1.03 5 2.24 1.56 1.24 1.06 10 3.16 1.68 1.27 1.06 30 5.48 1.78 1.29 1.06 ∞ ∞ 1.83 1.29 1.05 what that"},{"id":"11b-5-volatility-targeting-at-portfolio-level","t":"11B.5 Volatility targeting at portfolio level","v":"Vol 11B","x":"Volatility targeting scales the whole book so that its forecast risk equals a chosen number. It is the mechanism that does most of the work in a systematic system, and its effects are frequently misattributed to the signals (13.2). The scaling Position in inst","s":"11b.5 volatility targeting at portfolio level volatility targeting scales the whole book so that its forecast risk equals a chosen number. it is the mechanism that does most of the work in a systematic system, and its effects are frequently misattributed to the signals (13.2). the scaling position in instrument i , in contracts: n i = (forecast i ÷ convention) × ( c × τ × w i × dm) ÷ (σ i × value per point i ) where c is capital, τ the annual volatility target, w i the instrument weight, dm the diversification multiplier (11b.4), and σ i the instrument's forecast annualised volatility. every term is a decision made in a different place, which is exactly the point of keeping the pipeline separated (11b.1). what targeting actually does effect mechanism stabilises the return distribution risk per unit of time becomes roughly constant, so the sharpe ratio is estimated from a stationary serie"},{"id":"11b-6-buffering-rounding-and-minimum-size","t":"11B.6 Buffering, rounding and minimum size","v":"Vol 11B","x":"The last stage of the pipeline converts a continuous optimal position into an integer number of contracts and decides whether to trade at all. It is the least glamorous stage and among the most valuable, because it acts directly on turnover, and turnover is wh","s":"11b.6 buffering, rounding and minimum size the last stage of the pipeline converts a continuous optimal position into an integer number of contracts and decides whether to trade at all. it is the least glamorous stage and among the most valuable, because it acts directly on turnover, and turnover is where net return is lost (2.8, 10b.7). the buffer compute the optimal position n *. define a band of width b around it — commonly ten per cent of the average absolute position. then: if the current position lies inside [ n * − b , n * + b ], do nothing. if it lies outside, trade to the nearest edge of the band , not to the centre. trading to the edge rather than the centre is what makes the buffer work. trading to the centre means every excursion produces a full-size correction, and the turnover reduction largely disappears. buffer width turnover tracking to optimal 0% (no buffer) 100%, the b"},{"id":"vol12b","t":"Credit, Counterparty and the Anatomy of a Blow-Up","v":"Vol 12B","x":"Volume 12 measures market risk and Volume 12A models dependence. This volume covers the risk that someone does not pay: hazard rates and survival curves, credit default swaps and how they are bootstrapped, index and tranche products, securitisation, wrong-way risk — and a set of well-documented fail"},{"id":"12b-1-hazard-rates-and-survival-curves","t":"12B.1 Hazard rates and survival curves","v":"Vol 12B","x":"Credit modelling starts by describing when a firm defaults, not whether. The natural object is the hazard rate λ( t ): the instantaneous default intensity conditional on having survived to t . The three quantities and how they relate Survival probability to ti","s":"12b.1 hazard rates and survival curves credit modelling starts by describing when a firm defaults, not whether. the natural object is the hazard rate λ( t ): the instantaneous default intensity conditional on having survived to t . the three quantities and how they relate survival probability to time t : q ( t ) = exp(−∫ 0 t λ( s ) d s ) with a constant hazard λ, this is e −λ t , and the cumulative default probability is 1 − e −λ t . default time is exponential, so it is memoryless : having survived five years tells you nothing about the next year's hazard, which is exactly why a piecewise-constant term structure of λ is used in practice rather than a single number. a useful approximation for small spreads: λ ≈ s / (1 − r ) , where s is the credit spread and r the recovery rate. a 300 bp spread with 40 per cent recovery implies a hazard of about 5 per cent a year. structural versus reduc"},{"id":"12b-2-credit-default-swaps-and-bootstrapping","t":"12B.2 Credit default swaps and bootstrapping","v":"Vol 12B","x":"A credit default swap is insurance on a reference entity's default. The protection buyer pays a periodic premium; on a credit event the protection seller pays the loss on the reference obligation. Element Convention Premium leg Quarterly, on standardised coupo","s":"12b.2 credit default swaps and bootstrapping a credit default swap is insurance on a reference entity's default. the protection buyer pays a periodic premium; on a credit event the protection seller pays the loss on the reference obligation. element convention premium leg quarterly, on standardised coupons (typically 100 bp or 500 bp), with an upfront payment settling the difference from the fair spread credit event bankruptcy, failure to pay, and — for some entities — restructuring. determined by a regional determinations committee , not by the counterparties settlement an auction establishes a single recovery price, and contracts settle in cash against it accrual on default premium accrued from the last payment date to the event is paid why the auction exists physical settlement required delivering a defaulted bond. since cds notional outstanding can far exceed the deliverable bonds, t"},{"id":"12b-3-indices-tranches-and-correlation","t":"12B.3 Indices, tranches and correlation","v":"Vol 12B","x":"Credit indices — standardised baskets of equally weighted single-name CDS, rolled every six months — are the liquid instruments of the credit market. Tranches on those indices are where correlation becomes a traded quantity. What a tranche does A tranche is de","s":"12b.3 indices, tranches and correlation credit indices — standardised baskets of equally weighted single-name cds, rolled every six months — are the liquid instruments of the credit market. tranches on those indices are where correlation becomes a traded quantity. what a tranche does a tranche is defined by an attachment point and a detachment point on the pool's cumulative loss. a 3–7 per cent tranche absorbs losses only after the first 3 per cent have been suffered, and is wiped out at 7 per cent. the equity tranche (0–3) takes the first losses; senior tranches take the last. tranching redistributes, it does not remove the sum of all tranche losses equals the pool's loss, always. tranching does not reduce risk; it reallocates it, and in doing so changes its character . the equity tranche is a leveraged bet on idiosyncratic defaults. the senior tranche only ever loses in a scenario wher"},{"id":"12b-4-securitisation-and-the-machinery-of-2007","t":"12B.4 Securitisation and the machinery of 2007","v":"Vol 12B","x":"Securitisation pools illiquid loans into a special purpose vehicle and issues tranched claims against their cash flows. The economics are sound — it matches long-dated assets with investors who want them, and turns loans no one would buy individually into secu","s":"12b.4 securitisation and the machinery of 2007 securitisation pools illiquid loans into a special purpose vehicle and issues tranched claims against their cash flows. the economics are sound — it matches long-dated assets with investors who want them, and turns loans no one would buy individually into securities many will. the failure was not the structure. it was every input to it. the chain origination. a broker writes a mortgage, earning a fee and retaining no exposure. aggregation. a bank buys thousands and pools them in an spv. tranching. the pool issues senior, mezzanine and equity claims (12b.3). rating. an agency rates the tranches, using a model calibrated on historical mortgage data. re-securitisation. unsold mezzanine tranches from many deals are pooled and tranched again — the cdo-squared — producing new aaa paper out of bbb inputs. distribution. rated paper is sold to invest"},{"id":"12b-5-counterparty-exposure-and-wrong-way-risk","t":"12B.5 Counterparty exposure and wrong-way risk","v":"Vol 12B","x":"Counterparty credit risk is the risk that the other side of a derivative fails while the trade is in your favour. It differs from lending risk in a way that makes it harder: the exposure is stochastic and bilateral . You do not know today what you will be owed","s":"12b.5 counterparty exposure and wrong-way risk counterparty credit risk is the risk that the other side of a derivative fails while the trade is in your favour. it differs from lending risk in a way that makes it harder: the exposure is stochastic and bilateral . you do not know today what you will be owed at the moment of default, and the amount depends on the same market that determines whether they can pay. measure definition used for current exposure max(mark-to-market, 0), net of collateral held today's loss if they default now expected exposure (ee) expected positive exposure at a future date the profile that cva integrates against default probability (8b.9) potential future exposure (pfe) a high percentile — commonly 95th or 99th — of exposure at a future date limit setting effective epe time-weighted average of a non-decreasing version of ee regulatory capital what reduces exposu"},{"id":"12b-6-blow-ups-and-what-each-one-proves","t":"12B.6 Blow-ups, and what each one proves","v":"Vol 12B","x":"The documented failures are the most efficient risk curriculum available, because each one isolates a distinct mechanism. Read them as experiments already run at other people's expense. Case Mechanism Transferable lesson Metallgesellschaft (1993) Long-dated su","s":"12b.6 blow-ups, and what each one proves the documented failures are the most efficient risk curriculum available, because each one isolates a distinct mechanism. read them as experiments already run at other people's expense. case mechanism transferable lesson metallgesellschaft (1993) long-dated supply contracts hedged with short-dated futures rolled monthly. backwardation turned to contango and the rolls bled cash; the hedge was economically sound and could not be funded maturity and cash-flow mismatch kills solvent positions. model the funding path, not just the terminal value (8b.1, 8b.2) barings (1995) one trader controlled both dealing and settlement; losses were hidden in an error account and doubled into segregation of duties is not bureaucracy. independent position and cash reconciliation is the single highest-value control there is ltcm (1998) convergence trades at very high l"},{"id":"vol13b","t":"Expected Returns: The Premia, and How to Measure Them Forward","v":"Vol 13B","x":"Volumes 5, 13 and 13A trade the premia. This volume asks the prior question: what are they, why should they exist, and how do you estimate one without simply extrapolating the past? It covers the equity, bond, credit, illiquidity and volatility premia, the bad-times framework that explains all of th"},{"id":"13b-1-expected-return-is-a-discount-rate","t":"13B.1 Expected return is a discount rate","v":"Vol 13B","x":"An asset's price is the present value of its expected cash flows. Fix the cash flows and the price is determined by one number: the rate at which they are discounted. That rate is the expected return. The identity that reorganises everything Price high ⟺ disco","s":"13b.1 expected return is a discount rate an asset's price is the present value of its expected cash flows. fix the cash flows and the price is determined by one number: the rate at which they are discounted. that rate is the expected return. the identity that reorganises everything price high ⟺ discount rate low ⟺ expected return low. price low ⟺ discount rate high ⟺ expected return high. three consequences follow, and each one contradicts an instinct: a market that has risen has, other things equal, a lower expected return than before. past return and future return are mechanically linked with a negative sign, through the price. the historical average return is a biased estimate of the expected return whenever valuations changed over the sample. a period in which discount rates fell shows high realised returns because expected returns were falling. valuation ratios predict returns not b"},{"id":"13b-2-the-equity-risk-premium","t":"13B.2 The equity risk premium","v":"Vol 13B","x":"The equity risk premium is the expected return on equities in excess of a safe rate. It is the single most consequential number in finance and nobody knows it, which is worth stating plainly before any estimate. The building-block estimate Decompose the expect","s":"13b.2 the equity risk premium the equity risk premium is the expected return on equities in excess of a safe rate. it is the single most consequential number in finance and nobody knows it, which is worth stating plainly before any estimate. the building-block estimate decompose the expected return into terms you can observe or forecast separately: e[ r ] ≈ dividend yield + expected real growth in dividends + expected inflation + expected change in valuation the last term is where honesty is required. over long horizons the defensible assumption is zero — you cannot forecast a re-rating — and if you assume it is positive you should say so and say why. subtract the expected safe rate to get the premium. the discipline of the decomposition is that it makes each disagreement locatable. two people who differ about the premium usually differ about exactly one of these four terms, and once ide"},{"id":"13b-3-the-bond-risk-premium","t":"13B.3 The bond risk premium","v":"Vol 13B","x":"A long yield is not a forecast of short rates. It is a forecast of short rates plus a term premium — the compensation for bearing the risk of holding duration — and separating the two is the central problem of the fixed income premium. The decomposition y ( n ","s":"13b.3 the bond risk premium a long yield is not a forecast of short rates. it is a forecast of short rates plus a term premium — the compensation for bearing the risk of holding duration — and separating the two is the central problem of the fixed income premium. the decomposition y ( n ) = average expected future short rate over n + term premium ( n ) under the pure expectations hypothesis the second term is zero and a steep curve means the market expects rates to rise. empirically it is not zero: a steep curve predicts excess returns on long bonds , not subsequent rate rises, which is the classic rejection of the hypothesis. curve steepness is therefore a forward-looking measure of the bond premium, and one of the better-established predictive relationships in finance. driver of the term premium direction inflation uncertainty higher uncertainty raises the premium — nominal bonds are r"},{"id":"13b-4-the-credit-risk-premium","t":"13B.4 The credit risk premium","v":"Vol 13B","x":"A corporate bond yields more than a government bond. Only part of that difference is expected default loss, and the rest is what makes credit interesting. The decomposition of a spread Component What it compensates Expected default loss Probability of default ","s":"13b.4 the credit risk premium a corporate bond yields more than a government bond. only part of that difference is expected default loss, and the rest is what makes credit interesting. the decomposition of a spread component what it compensates expected default loss probability of default times loss given default (12b.1) default risk premium the uncertainty of default losses, and their timing — defaults cluster in bad times illiquidity premium corporate bonds trade infrequently and at wide spreads (13b.5) tax and structural in some jurisdictions, differential taxation of corporate coupons historically, expected default loss accounts for well under half of the average investment-grade spread . the remainder is premium and illiquidity, and that is the credit spread puzzle: spreads look far too wide for the defaults that actually occur. why the premium is larger than the losses defaults clu"},{"id":"13b-5-the-illiquidity-premium","t":"13B.5 The illiquidity premium","v":"Vol 13B","x":"Illiquid assets should offer higher expected returns, because investors must be paid to give up the ability to sell. The theory is sound. The measurement is where almost everything goes wrong. Two distinct things called the illiquidity premium The level premiu","s":"13b.5 the illiquidity premium illiquid assets should offer higher expected returns, because investors must be paid to give up the ability to sell. the theory is sound. the measurement is where almost everything goes wrong. two distinct things called the illiquidity premium the level premium. an asset that is permanently harder to trade should have a permanently higher expected return. this is a real and defensible premium, and it is the one worth harvesting if your capital is genuinely long-term. the liquidity risk premium. an asset whose liquidity disappears in bad times should pay more still, because it fails when you most need to sell. this is a distinct exposure and it is the one that hurts (12b.5). the two are frequently conflated. an asset can be permanently illiquid but reliably tradeable, or usually liquid and periodically untradeable. the second is far more dangerous, and the cr"},{"id":"13b-6-the-volatility-risk-premium","t":"13B.6 The volatility risk premium","v":"Vol 13B","x":"Across almost every liquid options market, implied volatility exceeds subsequently realised volatility on average. Selling options is therefore profitable on average, and the reason it is profitable is also the reason it is dangerous. Why the premium exists In","s":"13b.6 the volatility risk premium across almost every liquid options market, implied volatility exceeds subsequently realised volatility on average. selling options is therefore profitable on average, and the reason it is profitable is also the reason it is dangerous. why the premium exists insurance demand. investors are structurally long equities and want protection. someone must supply it, and they charge for doing so — the market clears above fair value in the same way that any insurance market does. volatility rises in bad times. a short volatility position loses precisely when equities fall and risk appetite drops, so it is a bad-times exposure and must be paid for (13b.7). the payoff is negatively skewed. small regular gains and rare large losses require a premium over the mean, for the same reason credit does (13b.4). expression what is harvested main risk delta-hedged short opti"},{"id":"13b-7-bad-times-the-framework-that-unifies-them","t":"13B.7 Bad times: the framework that unifies them","v":"Vol 13B","x":"Five premia have been described separately. They are one idea, and stating it makes the whole subject smaller. The unifying principle An asset earns a premium to the extent that it pays off badly in bad times. Not to the extent that it is volatile — volatility","s":"13b.7 bad times: the framework that unifies them five premia have been described separately. they are one idea, and stating it makes the whole subject smaller. the unifying principle an asset earns a premium to the extent that it pays off badly in bad times. not to the extent that it is volatile — volatility that arrives at harmless moments is not compensated. what matters is when the losses occur, relative to when the investor most needs money. formally this is the covariance of the asset's payoff with the marginal utility of consumption: assets that pay less when you are already poor must be cheaper today, which is to say they must offer a higher expected return. every asset pricing model in the field is an argument about what \"bad times\" means. premium pays off badly when equity recessions, earnings collapses, risk aversion spikes bond term (in an inflation regime) inflation surprises"},{"id":"13b-8-time-varying-expected-returns","t":"13B.8 Time-varying expected returns","v":"Vol 13B","x":"Expected returns move. Valuation ratios, curve steepness, credit spreads and volatility levels all predict subsequent returns at long horizons, in-sample and to a considerable degree out of it. The question is what that licenses. What it does license Planning.","s":"13b.8 time-varying expected returns expected returns move. valuation ratios, curve steepness, credit spreads and volatility levels all predict subsequent returns at long horizons, in-sample and to a considerable degree out of it. the question is what that licenses. what it does license planning. a forward-looking estimate beats a historical average for setting expectations, liabilities and savings rates. this is the largest and least controversial use. modest, slow tilts. adjusting exposure by a limited amount as premia move is defensible, if the adjustment is small relative to the estimate's uncertainty. rebalancing discipline. mechanical rebalancing already harvests part of the effect, without requiring any forecast at all. what it does not license market timing on a short horizon. the predictability lives at multi-year horizons. at a monthly horizon the r ² is near zero, and a strateg"}]
;
